Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_020,12,1._GENERAL_INTRODUCTION_AND_SUBJECT,1,content,text,1._GENERAL_INTRODUCTION_AND_SUBJECT,Sponsor's Functional Requirements,Scope and General Requirements,Plant design intent and compliance hierarchy,,,"Exhibit B Part 2 Sponsor's Functional Requirements (SFR), Exhibit B Part 1 Saudi Aramco and SEC technical specifications (Master Offtake Agreement), Fadhili Combined Heat and Power Plant (FCHPP), EPC term sheet, performance requirements, continuous commercial operation, high availability, redundancy, factors of safety, Good Industry Practices, environmental and statutory regulations, precedence: Exhibit B Part 1 over Part 2",False,General EPC/specification precedence and plant requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention or signal.,True,,medium,False,Contains plant-level technical requirements and compliance/precedence statements useful for retrieving overarching design basis/specification documents.,"This Exhibit B, Part 2 (Sponsor's Functional Requirements) defines the general and specific requirements for the Works in addition to the requirements in given in Exhibit B, Part 1, which is a strict copy of the Saudi Aramco and SEC technical specifications of the Master Offtake Agreement, of Fadhili Combined Heat and Power Plant (FCHPP). This Exhibit B, Part 2 must also be read in conjunction with the terms and conditions of the Agreement.
The intent of this specification is to define the scope of Works and activities to be undertaken under an Engineering Procurement and Construction (EPC) term sheet to procure for the Owner a modern, functional, well-designed power station meeting the performance requirements and being capable of continuous, efficient and reliable operation of the Plant with minimum maintenance.
The overall plant and individual systems and equipment supplied by the Contractor shall be of proven, robust and reliable design with appropriate levels of redundancy and factors of safety built-in and incorporate protective systems and devices.
It is not the intent of this specification to define all details of design and construction of the complete Works. However, it shall be recognised that the whole of the Works shall conform in all respects to high standards of engineering, design and workmanship, be in accordance with Good Industry Practices and shall be capable of performing in continuous commercial operation with very high availability whilst meeting the Contractor's guarantees and complying with all environmental and statutory regulations. No defect, error or omission in this document in relation to any design, specification or method set out herein shall relieve Contractor from his responsibility to provide a fully operational power generation Plant capable of delivering the defined capacities over the specified range of operating conditions.
In the event of any conflict with the requirements of Exhibit B, Part 2 SFR (Sponsor's Functional Requirements) and Exhibit B, Part 1 (Technical Specifications), Exhibit B, Part 1 will take precedence."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_021,13,2.2._General,"2, 2",content,text,2.2._General,General Design Requirements,Plant Design and Manufacture,"Design robustness, materials, and protection features",,,"high availability and reliability, power, steam and water supply, manufacturer's standard design, best current engineering practice, design dimensions and materials, severe service conditions, corrosion minimisation, fire risk minimisation, vermin ingress prevention, accidental contact with electrical live parts, dust and dirt ingress minimisation",False,Generic plant design robustness/materials/protection requirements; no ACC-related equipment or context.,True,,medium,False,"Provides general technical design requirements (materials, corrosion, fire, ingress protection) that can anchor retrieval of design criteria/specifications.","The Contractor shall provide a Plant designed to meet all design requirements and conditions given in this Exhibit B and elsewhere in the Agreement.
The entire design and manufacture shall conform to the best current engineering practice to provide high availability and reliability of power, steam and water at minimum cost. Each of the several parts of the Plant shall be of the manufacturer's standard design, provided that this design is in general accordance with this Exhibit B and the Agreement and shall use components proven to be satisfactory by previous experience.
The design dimensions and materials of all parts shall be such that they will not suffer damage as a result of stresses under the most severe service conditions. The materials used in the construction of the Plants shall be of the highest quality and selected particularly to meet the duties required of them. The plants shall be designed and constructed to minimise corrosion. Workmanship and general finish shall be of the highest class throughout.
All equipment shall be designed to minimize the risk of fire and damage that may be caused in the event of fire.
The equipment shall also be designed to prevent ingress of all vermin, accidental contact with electrical live parts and minimize the ingress of dust and dirt."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_022,13,2.3._Plant_operating_conditions,"2, 3",content,text,2.3._Plant_operating_conditions,Plant Operating Conditions,,,,,"operational and functional requirements, Exhibit B Part B1, Plant design incorporation requirements",False,Generic statement about meeting operational/functional requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,False,Introductory sentence with no specific operating conditions or technical parameters; insufficient retrieval anchors beyond cross-reference to Part B1.,low,True,Too generic to match specific MDL documents without additional technical details.,Generally the Plant design shall follow and meet the operational and functional requirements specified in Part B1 of Exhibit B and also the following requirements shall be incorporated in Plant design.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_024,14,2.3.2.Availability_and_reliability,"2, 3, 2",content,text,2.3._Plant_operating_conditions > 2.3.2.Availability_and_reliability,Availability and Reliability,Design Requirements,Single point failure avoidance and group independency,,,"recorded operational reliability evidence, key equipment and systems, group independency, single point failure avoidance, loss of total power, steam and water generation, auxiliary equipment failure impact minimisation, high reliability and availability",False,Availability/reliability design principles only; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention.,True,,high,False,Explicit availability/reliability design requirements and failure-mode avoidance are strong retrieval anchors for availability/reliability and redundancy design documents.,"The Contractor shall demonstrate during the design with necessary details and references of the recorded operational reliability of the key equipment and systems to be provided.
Throughout the design phase the Contractor shall take into account in his design the independency of each Group (if applicable) from the other groups and avoid any design leading to 'single point' failure causing the shutdown of the whole Plant or loss of total power, steam and water generation.
The Plant shall be designed to achieve a high degree of reliability and availability and such that the impact of a failure of any single piece of auxiliary equipment on the output of the Plant will be minimal."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_025,14,2.3.2.Availability_and_reliability,,list,list_item,2.3._Plant_operating_conditions > 2.3.2.Availability_and_reliability,Availability and Reliability,Design and Manufacturer Modifications,Redundancy and protection logic,,,"proven technology (no prototype), Gas Turbine (GT) manufacturer modifications, Steam Turbine (ST) manufacturer modifications, latest upgrades / safety modifications (TIL / ECN), margins for stringent load configurations, ambient conditions range, foundations design, settlements evaluation, unfavourable subsoil conditions, seismic requirements, critical components redundancy, on-site spare parts program, two-of-three logic for critical trip signals",False,Generic redundancy/proven technology/foundations/seismic and trip logic; no ACC-related terms.,True,,high,False,"Contains concrete technical reliability/redundancy requirements including GT/ST modification applicability, foundations/settlement considerations, seismic attention, and 2oo3 trip logic.","- all equipment shall be based on proven technology and shall not be a prototype;
- all fleet wide modifications in design and construction recommended by the Gas Turbine (GT) and Steam Turbine (ST) manufacturers after award of the Contract up to Plant final takeover shall be immediately informed to Owner and Contractor shall implement the same free of cost and without any time impact to the Owner. The details of modifications, schedule and mode of implementation shall be mutually discussed and agreed upon with the Owner
- all the latest upgrades / improvements / safety modifications announced by the manufacturer (TIL / ECN etc.,) before and during the contract period up to Plant final take over shall also be applicable for this contract and implemented by the Contractor free of cost and without any time impact to the Owner at shop or at site with the consent of The equipment installed at the Plant shall be designed with sufficient margins to cope with the most stringent load configurations within the range of ambient conditions
- considering any unfavourable subsoil conditions and any seismic requirements, special attention shall be paid to foundations design and settlements evaluation;
- the critical components (those components that are necessary to support the power and steam train of the Plant in order to reliably maintain its rated power production and steam production to comply with the required annual availability) shall be designed with adequate redundancy, in the design of systems, and shall be supported through the on-site spare parts program and
- two-of-three logic shall be used for critical trip signals"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_026,15,2.4._Plant_Design_Lifetime,"2, 4",content,"text, list_item",2.4._Plant_Design_Lifetime,Plant Design Lifetime,Design Life Criteria,Start/Stop Cycling and Creep-Life Design Basis,,,"design life 25 years from COD, creep region materials, pressure parts, HRSG, steam turbine, gas turbine, reserve boiler, main steam piping, warm/hot/cold starts, combined cycle mode starts per year, maximum 60 starts per year (water-steam cycle equipment), 10 cold / 18 warm / 28 hot starts per year per GT (average)",False,Plant design life and starts; mentions generic 'condenser' but not ACC/air-cooled condenser specifically.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit technical design-life and cycling requirements (25-year life from COD; creep-region components; start categories and annual start limits) that can anchor retrieval of design criteria, lifetime/cycling assessments, and equipment design basis documents.","The Plant shall endure throughout its service life the load changes defined in such a manner that no stresses exceeding the permissible limits will be caused in the components. The Plant shall be matched to the application so that with operation and maintenance in accordance with Good Industry Practice, an operational life of 25 years can be achieved from COD. A minimum Design life criteria of at least 25 years (from COD) shall be applied to materials operating in the creep region (pressure parts, steam turbine, gas turbine, HRSG, Reserve boiler, main steam piping etc.,…)
The Contractor shall state and specify the calculated number of Warm/Hot/Cold starts throughout the plant life incorporated in his design reflecting the assumed operation regime of the Plant.
Number of Plant starts in combined cycle mode per year for the Plant design shall be, as a minimum, as follows:
- maximum 60 starts per year for the water-steam cycle equipment (incl. HRSGs, steam turbine, and condenser) out of which, on average per Gas Turbine:
- -10 cold starts per year
- -18 warm starts per year
- -28 hot starts per year
- number of starts per year for the GTs: according to the Long Term Services Agreement (LTSA)
The Contractor shall use such design criteria as to enable a Plant operation of 25 years (from COD), assuming an operation and maintenance plan in accordance with the operating regime as proposed by the Contractor in this Agreement, the O&M Manuals and/or Good Industry Practice where this Agreement and/or the relevant O&M Manuals are silent. Without prejudice to main body of this Agreement, it is understood that certain components or parts thereof (including but not limited to parts of the turbines) are subject to wear and tear and/or usually have a shorter life span, thereby requiring servicing and/or replacement on a more frequent basis."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_027,"16, 17",3._SCOPE_OF_WORKS,3,content,"text, list_item",3._SCOPE_OF_WORKS,Scope of Works,EPC Scope Responsibilities,Plant and Support Facilities (Engineering to Performance Testing),,,"engineering and detailed engineering, site investigations and surveys, civil works, testing and pre-commissioning, commissioning, performance testing, generation and supply of electricity, steam and water, major equipment technical specifications (GT, HRSG, ST, reserve boiler, transformers, fuel gas system, DCMS), EIA Environmental Impact Assessment technical files, coordination with Saudi Aramco and SEC",False,Overall EPC scope and major equipment list (GT/HRSG/ST/etc.) without ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,True,,medium,False,"Broad but includes technical scope anchors (plant systems list, commissioning/performance testing, site investigations, EIA documentation) useful for matching to existing MDL deliverables across disciplines.","Contractor shall be fully responsible for engineering, design, detailed engineering, expediting, inspection, permitting, insurance, procurement, manufacturing, fabrication, factory testing, painting, packing, shipping, transport to site, site storage, erection, construction and erection, protection and preservation, testing and pre-commissioning, commissioning, and performance testing, required documentation, site investigations, site development and all related civil works of the complete Plant and all related support facilities and other services as specified or necessary for the generation and supply of electricity, steam and water and safe and reliable operation.
The Contractor shall include in his scope of work described in his proposal all the facilities, equipment, works and services necessary for complete, safe and prudent operation and maintenance of the Plant, even if certain essential works are not expressly stated in this Exhibit B, Part 2 and Part 1. The Contractor shall be responsible for supplying all equipment/systems, as necessary to make the Plant completely functional. Additionally, the Contractor shall provide unpriced purchase order copies including scope of services (both for the service and supply part) and technical specifications for all major equipment suppliers such as GT, HRSG, ST, Reserve Boiler(s), Transformers, Fuel Gas System, DCMS etc., to the Owner during detailed engineering stage.
In addition to the Scope of Work specified in this Exhibit B, Part 2 and Part B1, the minimum scope for work shall also include, but not limited to the following sections:
- delivery of technical files and engineering documentation as required by the Environmental Consultant who will be in charge of the EIA (Environmental Impact Assessment)
- site investigations and surveys
- coordination through the Owner of plans and activities with Saudi Aramco and SEC during design, construction and commissioning of the Project
- importation and export of goods required to complete the Works, inclusive of customs clearance in any jurisdiction
- management and control of the works, including control and supervision of the sub-contractors
- obtaining all Consents and Permits for activities at Site, related to labor, construction, handling, storage, commissioning, testing, as per Exhibit L
The Contractor shall be deemed to have included in the Scope of Services any additional services necessary to meet the Plant design, construction, performance, operation, maintenance, and environmental criteria, but which are not specifically identified in the present document, or in the EPC Term sheet."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_029,18,4.1._General,"4, 1",content,text,4.1._General,Codes and Standards,National Standards,,,,"codes and standards, national standards, Exhibit B Part 1",False,Codes and standards intro only; no ACC-related signal.,False,Fragment only introduces that national standards must be applied but does not list any standards or technical requirements.,low,True,Insufficient technical specificity (no named standards or parameters) to reliably retrieve MDL documents.,"In addition to the Codes and Standards specified in Exhibit B, Part 1, the following National Standards must be applied:"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_030,18,4.1._General,,content,"text, list_item",4.1._General,Codes and Standards,Good Industry Practice,"Design, Construction, Testing and Protective Features (General Requirements)",,,"coherence of codes and standards system, Good Industry Practice, monitoring and testing during design/manufacturing/erection/commissioning, protective devices and design features, safe reliable long-term operation, manufacturer's guidelines and specifications, construction utilities and supplies, engineering/design/construction safety personnel",False,Good Industry Practice general requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.,True,,medium,False,"General but contains technical governance anchors (codes/standards coherence, monitoring/testing across lifecycle, protective devices for safe long-term operation) that can map to design criteria/specification documents.","The Contractor is responsible for the coherence of the codes and standards system chosen as reference, especially when codes and standards are from various origins.
The Contractor's activities in relation to the Plant shall be carried out in accordance with Good Industry Practice. Such practices include, but are not limited to, ensuring that:
- adequate materials, resources construction utilities and supplies are available to meet the needs at the relevant time
- sufficient engineering, design, construction and safety personnel are available and are adequately experienced and trained to design, construct, start-up and test the Plant, as well as each of their component parts, properly, efficiently and within the applicable laws, manufacturer's guidelines and specifications
- appropriate monitoring and testing is done during the design, manufacturing, erection and commissioning to ensure equipment is functioning as designed and to provide assurance that it shall function properly
- appropriate protective devices and design features are provided to ensure that safe, reliable, long-term operation of the Plant can be achieved
- any equipment is operated in a manner consistent with the applicable laws, manufacturer's guidelines and specifications and is safe for workers, the general public and the environment"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_031,"18, 19",4.2._System_of_Units,"4, 2",content,text,4.2._System_of_Units,System of Units,Units for Documentation and Drawings,,,,"SI units, documentation calculations drawings measurements, non-standard units shown in brackets, British units for documentation sent to Saudi Aramco",False,System of units for documentation/drawings; no ACC-related content.,False,Administrative drafting/unit convention; no equipment/system technical requirements.,low,True,Primarily documentation/unit-formatting instruction; weak linkage to specific technical MDL deliverables.,"SI units shall be used for this project and all materials, fittings, components, items of Plant and equipment used in the Plant.
SI units shall be used in all correspondence, documentation, calculations, drawings, measurements etc. If reference has to be made to non-standard units, the SI units shall be quoted followed by the nonstandard units in brackets.
However, British units (with International System of Units (SI units) shown parenthetically) shall be used for all documentation sent to Saudi Aramco"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_032,"19, 20",4.2.1.Equipment_identification_system,"4, 2, 1",content,"text, list_item",4.2._System_of_Units > 4.2.1.Equipment_identification_system,Equipment Identification and Labelling,Equipment Numbering System,KKS Equipment Numbering and Plant Identification,,,"KKS equipment numbering system, maintenance management system (MMS) compatibility, name plates: manufacturer, type, serial number, construction year, main design data, labels in English; warning/safety notices Arabic and English, Safe Working Load markings Arabic and English; S.I. units, piping identification: colour strips and flow direction arrows, cable, control panel and instrumentation labelling, IEC standards conductor colour identification code, GTG package cable color codes per NEMA / ICEA, high voltage apparatus nomenclature per SEC / Saudi Aramco plant identification, coordinate GT OEM codes with general equipment identification system, interconnection drawings related to cubicle/equipment labelling standard",False,General plant-wide equipment identification/labeling and KKS numbering; no mention of ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or ACC components.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit technical requirements for plant-wide identification/numbering (KKS), piping/cable/instrument labelling, language/units, and applicable standards (IEC, NEMA/ICEA) plus SEC/Saudi Aramco identification alignment—useful to retrieve tagging/labeling specs and related drawings.","Equipment numbering shall be in accordance with a clear, concise and systematic numbering system that is applied consistently across all the plant areas and one that can be easily incorporated into the Owner's supplied maintenance management system (MMS). e.g. KKS equipment numbering system.. Contractor shall use KKS in order to make the identification compatible with the MMS. No alternate identification system is permitted.
All major equipment shall also be identified by name plates indicating manufacturer's name, type of equipment, serial number, construction year and main design data.
A consistent system of labelling shall be proposed by the Contractor for the approval of the Owner's Representative. The proposed material, size and exact inscription as well as proposals for the arrangement shall be submitted to the Owner's Representative for approval.
The labels shall be written in English. Warning and safety notices shall be in Arabic and English.
Safe Working Load indications on lifting equipment shall be in Arabic and English and in S.I. units.
A piping identification system subject to Owner's Representative's approval shall be applied for the Plant piping using colour strips around the pipes, as well as arrows showing flow direction.
A labelling system for the cables, control panels and instrumentation, shall be provided and shall be submitted to the Owner's Representative's for its approval .
The labelling system for cubicles and equipment inside cubicles shall be in accordance with manufacturer's standard. The interconnection drawings are related to this standard
As a minimum requirement, labels shall be provided for:
- colour identification code for conductors of all electrical cables shall comply with the required IEC standards however; for GTG package only, the cables may be provided pre-wired with color codes per NEMA / ICEA.
- all instruments, control switches, push buttons, indication lights, breakers, etc
- all construction units and all separate construction units (cubicle, panel desk, box, etc)
- all equipment inside cubicles, panels, boxes, etc(including terminal blocks)
- all cables installed (power, instrument, control, telephoneand special cables)
- all wiring (cables and inside panel wiring) shall be labelled
Contractor shall ensure that all numbering and nomenclature of high voltage apparatus will be in accordance with the Plant identification system of SEC / Saudi Aramco.
Contractor shall properly coordinate between the GT Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) codes and general equipment identification system."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_033,21,5.1._Site_location_and_area_/_layout,"5, 1",content,text,5.1._Site_location_and_area_/_layout,,,,,,,False,Site location and overall cogeneration plant layout reference only; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,False,Primarily references annex maps/layout drawings in the RFP without stating technical requirements or design criteria in this chunk.,low,True,Location/layout is only pointed to via annex references; insufficient technical retrieval anchors in the chunk text itself.,"The location in Saudi Arabia of the Fadhili Gas Complex of Saudi Aramco is shown in the map attached as Annex 1 to Part IV (Drawings and Diagrams).
The Complex is located in the vicinity of Saudi Aramco's gas fields, which are shown in Annex 2 to Part IV (Drawings and Diagrams) of the RFP.
The tentative Layout of the Cogeneration Plant can be seen in Annex 5a to Part IV (Drawings and Diagrams) of the RFP."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_034,21,5.3._Site_Conditions_and_Technical_Limits,"5, 3",content,"text, list_item",5.3._Site_Conditions_and_Technical_Limits,,,,,,,False,Generic reference to exhibits for site conditions/topography; no ACC-related terms.,False,"Only cross-references (Exhibit B, Formsheet H) and a fragmentary pointer to 5.4; no technical limits/conditions stated in the chunk.",low,True,Insufficient standalone technical content; likely a section transition/cross-reference.,"Refer to Exhibit B, Part 1 and Part IV Formsheet H.
- 5.4. Topography and soil conditions"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_036,21,5.6._Utilities_for_construction,"5, 6",content,text,5.6._Utilities_for_construction,,,,,,,False,Construction utilities responsibility statement; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.,False,"Administrative responsibility statement (contractor manages construction utilities) without technical utility requirements, capacities, or interfaces.",low,True,Does not provide technical parameters or design/engineering scope suitable for MDL retrieval.,"During the construction phase, all utilities for construction shall be managed by the Contractor themselves."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_037,22,5.7._Initial_stock_of_Chemicals_and_lube_oil,"5, 7",content,text,5.7._Initial_stock_of_Chemicals_and_lube_oil,,,,,,,False,Initial stock of chemicals/lube oil requirement; no ACC-related equipment or scope.,False,"Procurement/supply requirement for initial chemicals and lube oil stock (full tanks) up to COD; no technical specifications, types, or quantities provided.",low,True,Mentions materials to be provided but lacks technical specification details needed to match engineering MDL documents.,Contractor shall provide initial stock of all Process Chemicals including Lube oil (full tanks) up to COD.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_038,22,5.8._Environmental_protection_regulations,"5, 8",content,"text, list_item",5.8._Environmental_protection_regulations,Environmental Protection,Environmental Regulations and Standards,,,,"Equator Principles, Export Credit Agencies (ECAs) requirements, World Bank guidelines for air emissions, Laws of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, environmental standards and safeguards",False,Environmental protection regulations (Equator Principles/World Bank air emissions); no ACC mention.,True,,medium,False,"Contains explicit environmental compliance standards (Equator Principles, World Bank air emissions guidelines, Saudi laws) useful to retrieve environmental criteria/specification documents.","In addition to the requirements of Exhibit B, Part 1, the Plant shall also comply with environmental standards and safeguards required by local laws as well as those embodied in the 'Equator Principles', and if applicable, those required by the countries of any Export Credit Agencies ('ECAs') involved in the project,.
Shall also be applicable:
- The World Bank guidelines for air emissions
- The Laws of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_039,22,5.8.1.Environmental_and_Social_Impact_Assessment,"5, 8, 1",content,text,5.8._Environmental_protection_regulations > 5.8.1.Environmental_and_Social_Impact_Assessment,Environmental Protection,Environmental and Social Impact Assessment,EIA data inputs and change management,,,"Environmental and Social Impact Assessment (EIA), EIA study data provision, Exhibit B Part 1 requirements, EIA report material changes to plant",False,EIA data provision/change management; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,medium,False,Defines EIA/ESIA study support and potential plant changes driven by EIA findings—useful for retrieving EIA-related requirements and environmental studies.,"Owner will prepare an Environmental and Social Impact Assessment (EIA) as per the requirements of Exhibit B, Part 1. The Contractor shall provide all data as necessary for the EIA study. In case the EIA report suggests any material changes to the plant; Contractor shall discuss the same with the Owner and do the needful."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_040,"23, 24",6.1.1.General_requirements,"6, 1, 1",content,text,6.1.1.General_requirements,Power Generation,Gas Turbine Generator Package,General requirements and operating modes,Gas Turbine (GT),,"automatic start-up sequence from CCR, automatic unloading and shutdown sequence, outdoor weatherproof acoustic enclosures, gas fired heavy duty industrial single shaft, directly coupled to 60 Hz generator, operation on Sales Gas and Low BTU Gas, ultra-dry low NOx combustion system (ULN), DCMS interface for integrated HRSG control, loss of cooling water/main AC power to oil systems (safe coastdown), on-load and off-load compressor washing, ISO 10816 Zone B vibration, DIN ISO 7919-4 acceptance criteria (+25% A/B boundary), turbine inlet air cooling (evaporative cooler), turning gear interlocks with lube oil, jacking facilities for turbine and generator bearings",False,Gas Turbine Generator package general requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or exhaust steam/condensing system references.,True,,high,False,"Dense GT technical requirements (controls, fuels, emissions concept, vibration standards, inlet cooling, washing, turning gear, interfaces) provide strong anchors to retrieve GT package specs, control narratives, and mechanical/electrical design documents.","The GT shall be capable of being automatically started, and the generator synchronised to the electrical system and loaded to full load by an automatic start-up sequence from the Plant main central control room (CCR). The gas turbine shall be capable of being automatically unloaded from the full load, disconnected from the grid and shut down safely with an automatic sequence.
The GT shall be newly manufactured and shall be complete with ancillary plant, and shall comply with the following requirements:
The GT shall be located outdoors in weatherproof acoustic enclosures and shall be of the Gas fired, heavy duty, industrial, single shaft type directly coupled to a 60Hz generator.
The GT shall be capable of start-up, shutdown and continuous operation on both Sales Gas and Low BTU Gas throughout their complete range. Refer to Exhibit B, Part 1 for the fuel specifications.
The GT shall be provided with ultra-dry low NOx combustion systems for burning Sales Gas and Low BTU Gas. The GT shall comply with the emissions requirements specified elsewhere in Exhibit B, Part 1.
The GT control system shall incorporate an interface with the DCMS to facilitate integrated control of HRSGs throughout all operational manoeuvres.
GT shall also be capable of coming to rest without bearing or other damage on loss of cooling water or main AC power to its oil systems.
Permanent equipment for both on-load and off-load compressor cleaning shall be provided for gas turbine, allowing for the connection of common mobile washing equipment. One set of mobile washing equipment shall be supplied at each site. The Contractor shall detail on-load and offload procedures for wet cleaning of compressor.
The GT shall be equipped with either Start up motor or Static Start system. The gas turbine shall be provided with a full set of automatic control and protection equipment incorporating governor, temperature control and supervisory functions. Vibration levels shall conform with the ISO standard 10816, Zone B. ISO 7919 zone A/B can be used as a guideline. GT OEM package will meet A/B boundary +25% as stated in DIN ISO 7919-4 Acceptance criteria (Section 4).
The GT shall be equipped with turbine inlet air cooling by means of an evaporative cooler
Lifting gear, such as lifting beams, slings, jacks etc. required for removing complete compressor-turbine rotor, generator rotor, combustion and not gas parts, the upper portion of casings and other heavy equipment shall be provided. Turning gear system shall also be capable of manual turning.
Means shall be provided for powered turning of the rotors for a sufficient period following normal or emergency shutdown to ensure satisfactory cooling of components. The turning gear shall be interlocked such that loss of lubricating oil shall immediately stop the gear. It shall not be possible to start the turning gear without adequate lubrication oil supplies.
Jacking facilities shall be provided for all turbine and generator bearings if necessary."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_041,24,6.1.1.General_requirements,"6, 1, 1",content,text,6.1.1.General_requirements,Power Generation,Gas Turbine Generator Package,Generator end winding support and factory testing; operating modes,Generator,,"generator end windings wet ties, SRS wet tie support (full wrap/frap), OEM bump test, phase loop at factory, end winding frequency analysis report, island mode operation, black start, OEM service support during erection and commissioning, commissioning and operational test period, fine tuning ULN combustors for Sales Gas and Low BTU Gas",False,Generator end winding support/testing and operating modes; no ACC-related content.,True,,high,False,"Includes specific generator design/testing requirements and operating modes (island/black start) plus commissioning/testing scope—strong retrieval signals for generator datasheets, factory test reports requirements, and commissioning procedures.","The GT generator end windings shall use wet ties free of any vibration issues. The OEM shall perform bump test and phase loop at the factory. The generator shall be fitted with new design SRS, wet tie (full wrap/frap) support. OEM must ensure that the generator has undergone a bump test or full end winding frequency analysis report at the factory after assembly.
As a backup to the powered system, means shall be provided for turning of rotors by hand during maintenance and for emergency use.
The GT shall be suitable for island mode operation and black start.
Necessary OEM service support during erection, commissioning, and operational test period shall be provided.
Contractor should consider adequate time buffer and resources (including Gas Turbine OEM personnel as required) for timely commissioning and testing (including fine tuning of the ULN combustors for the Sales gas and Low - BTU gas) of the Gas turbines."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_042,24,6.1.2._Noise_limits,"6, 1, 2",content,text,6.1.2._Noise_limits,Power Generation,Gas Turbine Generator Package,Noise limits and acoustic enclosure,,,"weatherproof acoustic hoods/enclosures, noise limits, Exhibit B Part 1",False,GTG acoustic enclosure/noise limits; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention.,True,,medium,False,States requirement for acoustic enclosures to meet specified noise limits—useful for retrieving noise criteria and acoustic enclosure specifications.,"The gas turbine generator will be provided with individual suitable weather proof acoustic hoods/enclosures to meet noise limits specified in Exhibit B, Part 1."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_043,25,6.1.3._Frequency_variations,"6, 1, 3",content,text,6.1.3._Frequency_variations,Gas Turbine Generator,Electrical Grid Interface,Frequency Variations,,,"frequency variations, transmission system performance characteristics, GT capability, grid code compliance",False,Discusses gas turbine grid interface frequency variations; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention or signal.,True,,medium,True,Technical requirement is high-level and references Section 7 for details; limited specific parameters in this chunk.,Attention is drawn to the requirement in Section 7 (Frequency variations in transmission system performance characteristics). The Contractor should clarify the GT capability under these conditions and in accordance to grid code.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_044,25,6.1.4._Air_intake_systems_and_filtration,"6, 1, 4",content,text,6.1.4._Air_intake_systems_and_filtration,Gas Turbine Generator,Air Intake System,Inlet Air Filtration and Silencing,,,"F9 class filter, EN779-2012, high-level air inlet filter unit, intake silencing, two stage filters, pulse-clean type filtration, 2-year maintenance-free operation, pulse cleaning from plant compressed air system, sand water droplets dust contaminants filtration, interlocks for doors/hatches, rain hoods on horizontal inlets, noise limits",False,Gas turbine air intake filtration/silencing requirements; no ACC-related equipment or context.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit technical requirements for GT air intake filtration class/standard, configuration, maintainability, interlocks, and noise/silencing.","The gas turbine shall be equipped with a high efficiency of F9 class as per EN779-2012, high-level air inlet filter unit and intake silencing.
The gas turbine intake air filtration system shall consist of minimum two stage filters and of the pulse-clean type. The type of filter element selected shall be capable of operating without maintenance for a minimum two-year period before a media change is required as per manufacturer's standards under normal ambient and operating conditions and under all conditions as specified in Exhibit B, Part 1. It shall be possible to operate the pulse-clean system without shutting down or reducing power output on the associated unit. In case of GT shutdown, the pulse cleaning shall be from the Plant compressed air system. Necessary design and interconnection shall be made by the Contractor.
The selection and design of the air filtration system shall be such as to ensure that sand, water droplets, dust and other harmful contaminants in the ambient air be filtered to a level which will not be detrimental to the life of the gas turbine unit under the most adverse ambient conditions at the plant site when the plant is operating continuously at maximum output with the necessity for blade cleaning reduced to a minimum.
The number of access points and penetrations into the air inlet system for maintenance and inspection shall be minimised. Any door or hatch shall be capable of being securely air-tight locked, and interlocks shall be provided to prevent any attempted start with any door or hatch not properly closed.
Particular attention shall be paid to the structural materials and paint used for the construction of the air filtration plant in view of the extensive temperature deviations and climatic conditions. Rain hoods shall be provided on any horizontal air filter inlets.
Silencing shall be provided as required in the inlet system in order to meet the specified noise limits."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_045,26,6.1.5._Fire/Gas_detection_and_protection_systems,"6, 1, 5",content,text,6.1.5._Fire/Gas_detection_and_protection_systems,Gas Turbine Generator,Fire and Gas System,Fire Protection and Detection,,,"automatic fire protection system, auxiliary enclosures, cylinders cylinder racks piping valves nozzles detectors, fire detectors wired as class A loops, CO2 extinguishing agent, foam extinguishing agent, NFPA 750, interface to plant main fire protection and detection system",False,Fire/gas detection and protection for gas turbine generator installation; no ACC mention.,True,,high,True,"Strong technical anchors, but NFPA 750 reference with CO2/foam may need verification in source context.","The entire gas turbine generator installation, including all auxiliary enclosures, shall be protected from fire with a complete automatic fire protection system. The system shall be complete in all respects and shall include cylinders, cylinder racks, piping, valves, nozzles, detectors, fire alarm actuating system, and all other equipment necessary for the safe and efficient operation of the units.
The fire detectors shall be wired as class A loops.
The extinguishing agent shall be carbon dioxide (CO2) or foam as per requirements of NFPA 750.
The fire protection and detection system provided with the gas turbine generator shall interface, and be connected to, the Plant's main fire protection and detection system."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_046,26,6.1.6._Exhaust_ducting,"6, 1, 6",content,text,6.1.6._Exhaust_ducting,Gas Turbine Generator,Exhaust System,Exhaust Ducting to Waste Heat Recovery Boiler,,,"exhaust gas system to waste heat recovery boiler, back pressure 50 mbar, duct test points, exhaust gas distribution and temperature measurement, exhaust gas sampling, agreed test code, gas tight access doors, 600mm x 600mm access doors, door interlocks, silencing in ducting or stack, noise limits",False,Gas turbine exhaust ducting to waste heat recovery boiler; not ACC exhaust steam ducting and no ACC signal.,True,,high,False,"Explicit exhaust ducting design criteria, test provisions, access/interlocks, and noise/silencing requirements.","The gas turbines shall be provided with the exhaust gas system direct to the waste heat recovery boiler. The whole of the exhaust system shall be designed to withstand a back pressure of not less than 50 mbar.
A series of test points shall be arranged in the ducts at positions such that the distribution and temperature of the exhaust gas can be determined and the exhaust gas sampled in accordance with the agreed test code.
Gas tight access doors shall be provided to give access to ducts for inspection and maintenance. They shall be not less than 600mm x 600mm size. Interlocks shall be provided to prevent any attempted start with the doors open.
Silencing shall be provided as required in the ducting or stack in order to meet the specified noise limits."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_047,"26, 27",6.1.7._Maintainability,"6, 1, 7",content,text,6.1.7._Maintainability,Gas Turbine Generator,Maintainability,Maintenance Intervals and Overhaul Requirements,,,"recommended running hours between mandatory inspection/overhauls, normal and emergency trips and starts, fired hours part load base load peak load, Equivalent Hours formulae, self-sustaining condition definition for start, maintenance procedures within enclosure vs enclosure removed, modular construction to reduce downtime, major component weights, overhead lifting equipment capacity and clearance, special lifting equipment in maintenance tools, extent of dismantling for mandatory inspection/overhaul, lay-down area required, OEM marginal conditions and storage requirements (Attachment 5 and 6)",False,Maintainability requirements for gas turbine generator; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,True,,high,False,"Clear maintainability requirements including equivalent hours, lifting/clearance, dismantling scope, and OEM storage/conditions references.","The maintainability features incorporated in the design and construction of the gas turbine generator units shall include the following items:
The formulae and a chart showing the variation in recommended running hours between mandatory inspection/overhauls in relation to the number of normal and emergency trips and starts, fired hours on part load, base load and peak load continuous operation of fuel gas.
Equivalent Hours formulae (if applicable) are to be supplied to relate the emergency trips, at various loads, and starts to the total hours between maintenance inspections. For this purpose a start shall mean a runup to at least the self-sustaining condition.
Identification of maintenance procedures that can be performed within the enclosure, if provided, and which procedures are required to be performed with the enclosure removed.
Details of the features of the gas turbine generator unit, such as modular construction, that can reduce down time and allow ease of maintenance, thus improving plant availability.
The weight of individual major components shall be stated. If overhead lifting equipment is required, the capacity and type of equipment required and clearance around the enclosure for unimpeded operation and movement shall be stated. The necessary special lifting equipment shall be provided within the maintenance tools and equipment supplied.
Details of the extent of dismantling of the equipment for mandatory inspection/overhaul, and lay-down area required.
The Contractor shall fulfil all the requirements of the OEM as specified in Marginal Conditions and Storage requirements of Attachment 5 and 6, Enclosure - 3 to this Exhibit B, Part 2"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_048,"27, 28",6.2.1.General_requirements,"6, 2, 1",table,text,6.2.1.General_requirements,Steam Turbine Generator,General Requirements,Design and Operational Requirements,,,"continuous safe reliable operation, undue heating vibration noise limits, indoor installation, rapid start-up and shutdown capability, blade erosion minimisation due to exhaust steam wetness, exhaust hood spray cooling, low exhaust flow operation, interface with DCMS for integrated plant control, loss of cooling and/or main AC power to oil systems (no damage), automatic intervention to limit thermal stress, HP steam header feeds two identical condensing steam turbine-generator units, steam extraction to feed FGP (Fuel Gas Package), IEC60045 overloading requirements, fail-safe turbine main valves, on-load testing of emergency stop valves, simulated over-speed trip gear operation, TIL/ECN fleet-wide modifications/upgrades by manufacturer",False,"Steam turbine general requirements (exhaust hood spray cooling, DCMS interface); no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mentioned.",True,,high,False,"Contains explicit steam turbine-generator technical design/operational requirements, interfaces (DCMS), standards (IEC60045), and specific features (spray cooling, fail-safe valves, testing) useful for matching to existing STG specifications, control narratives, and valve/protection documents.","The ST and its ancillary plant and equipment shall provide continuous, safe, reliable and economical operation without undue heating, vibration, or noise. It shall be located indoors and designed for ease of operation and maintenance and shall have rapid start-up and shutdown capability. Appropriate concepts shall be included in the design so as to minimise blade erosion due to exhaust steam wetness.
An exhaust hood spray cooling shall be supplied, if necessary, to limit the exhaust temperature rise under conditions of low exhaust flow operation.
The ST shall incorporate an interface with the DCMS to facilitate integrated control of the Plant throughout all operational manoeuvres.
The ST shall also be capable of coming to rest without any damage on loss of cooling and/or main AC power to its oil systems.
The control system shall include features to provide automatic intervention to limit maximum thermal stress levels to established threshold values.
The steam turbine unit and all auxiliary plant shall be provided with all equipment necessary to monitor all significant operational data.
The HP steam header shall feed two identical condensing steam turbine-generator units. Each steam turbine shall be fitted with a steam extraction to feed the FGP.
Overloading requirements specified in IEC60045 shall be taken into account. The turbine main valves shall be of the fail-safe type.
On-load testing of emergency stop valves and of the simulated over-speed trip gear operation shall be possible.
All fleet wide modifications in design and construction recommended by the ST manufacturer after award of the Contract up to Plant final takeover shall be immediately informed to the Owner and the Contractor shall implement the same free of cost and without any time impact to the Owner. The details of modifications, schedule and mode of implementation shall be mutually discussed and agreed upon with the Owner.
All the latest upgrades/improvements/safety modifications announced by the manufacturer (TIL/ECN etc.) before and during the contract period up to Plant final take over shall also be applicable for this contract and implemented by the Contractor free of cost and without any time impact to the Owner at shop or at site with the consent of owner."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_049,28,6.2.2._Performance_Requirements,"6, 2, 2",content,"text, list_item",6.2.2._Performance_Requirements,Steam Turbine Generator,Performance Requirements,Performance Data and Curves,,,"base load operation, part load operation, performance degradation (output, heat rate), cold warm hot start-up times, start-up/shutdown curves, minimum stable generation, performance correction curves (heat rate/efficiency vs load/exhaust steam pressure), maintenance schedule (frequency type duration of outages), design review phase submittal",False,Steam turbine performance data/curves vs load/exhaust steam pressure; no ACC equipment or scope signal.,True,,high,False,"Defines specific performance curves, degradation, start-up times, and minimum stable generation—strong anchors to retrieve existing performance guarantees, correction curves, and operating/start-up documentation.","The Steam Turbine is expected to operate at base load and at part load and other operational loads as specified in Exhibit B, Part 1.
Contractor shall supply the following performance information during the design review phase if not already submitted in its proposal:
- performance degradation on output and heat rate
- cold, warm, and hot start up times
- start-up / shutdown curves;
- minimum stable generation
- performance correction curves of heat rate/efficiency vs load/exhaust steam pressure
- supplier recommended maintenance schedule (frequency, type and duration of outages)"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_050,29,6.2.3._Non-Return_Valves,"6, 2, 3",content,text,6.2.3._Non-Return_Valves,Steam Turbine Generator,Steam System Valves,Non-Return Valves at Steam Extractions,,,"fast-acting forced-closing non-return valves, steam extractions, close proximity to steam turbine",False,Non-return valves at steam extractions near steam turbine; no ACC mention.,True,,medium,False,"Short but clear technical requirement for extraction non-return valves; useful to match to steam extraction P&IDs, valve datasheets, and turbine extraction arrangements.",Proven fast-acting forced-closing non-return valves shall be provided at steam extractions in close proximity to the Steam Turbine.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_051,29,6.2.4._Protective_Devices,"6, 2, 4",content,"text, list_item",6.2.4._Protective_Devices,Steam Turbine Generator,Protection and Trips,Turbine Protective Devices,,,"local trip push button, remote emergency push button, low bearing oil pressure trip, bearing drain oil temperature trip, high bearing metal temperature trip, over-speed protection system (duplex), high back pressure trip, high exhaust temperature alarm, fire detection, axial shift protection, turbine shaft vibration, bearing metal temperatures alarm, on-load testing of protection devices",False,"Steam turbine protective devices (back pressure, exhaust temperature, vibration, etc.); no ACC-related context.",True,,high,False,"Explicit list of turbine protection/trip functions and on-load testing requirement—strong retrieval anchors for cause & effect, protection philosophy, control system design, and instrumentation lists.","The following turbine protection devices shall be included in the supply as a minimum:
- local trip push button
- remote emergency push button
- low bearing oil pressure trip
- bearing drain oil temperature or high bearing metal temperature trip
- over-speed protection system (duplex)
- high back pressure
- high exhaust temperature (alarm only)
- fire detection
- axial shift protection
- turbine shaft vibration
- bearing metal temperatures (alarm only)
Arrangements shall be provided for on-load testing of these devices."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_052,"29, 30",6.2.5._Bearings_&_Lubricating_Oil_System,"6, 2, 5",content,text,6.2.5._Bearings_&_Lubricating_Oil_System,Steam Turbine Generator,Auxiliary Systems,Bearings and Lubricating Oil System,,,"bearings accessible adjustable replaceable, lower halves removable without lifting shaft significantly, visible oil flow indication from each bearing pedestal, dial-type thermometers, pressure lubrication oil system (vents drains valves pipes instrumentation controls), loss of main lube oil pump while on load, momentary loss of AC power while on load, safe shutdown without any AC power, DC emergency lube oil pump, lube oil flushing cleanliness acceptance by Owner's Engineer, oil leakage containment (prevent entry to surface water drainage), IEC45 compliance, multiple oil coolers and filters (one out of service while operating), AC jacking oil pumps for steam turbine and generator",False,Steam turbine generator bearings and lube oil system; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains detailed technical requirements for STG bearings and lube oil system redundancy, flushing/cleanliness, environmental containment, and IEC45 compliance—high value for matching to lube oil system P&IDs, datasheets, and auxiliary system specifications.","The bearings of the turbine and generator shall be readily accessible, adjustable and replaceable without risk of damaging adjacent glands, and that the lower halves can be removed and replaced without lifting the shaft more than a small amount or disturbing major components.
Arrangements shall be made for visible indications of the oil flow from each bearing pedestal and mounting of dial-type thermometers.
A pressure lubrication oil system shall be furnished for the Steam Turbine Generator to supply oil at a suitable pressure as applicable to the Steam Turbine, shaft driven auxiliary equipment, gearing, generator, continuously lubricated couplings, governing and hydraulic systems. The system shall include all vents, drains, valves, pipes, instrumentation and controls as required.
The lubricating system shall cater for loss of the main lubricating oil pump whilst on load, momentary loss of AC power whilst on load (such as occurs during a change of supply from unit board to station board) and safe shutdown without any AC power at all. The provision of a DC type pump as emergency shutdown in the event of an AC failure is required.
Following erection, the lubricating oil system shall be flushed until the level of cleanliness demonstrated to Owner's Engineer is acceptable. Acceptance will not be unreasonably withheld.
Oil leakages shall be contained and prevented from entering the surface water drainage system
The arrangement of the lubricating oil system shall comply with IEC45, and in particular multiple oil coolers and filters shall be provided of sufficient capacity so that one (1) oil cooler or filter can be taken out of service while the turbine remains in operation. Emergency DC lubricating oil pump and AC jacking oil pumps shall be provided for both the Steam Turbine and its Generator."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_053,30,6.2.6._Barring_Gear,"6, 2, 6",content,text,6.2.6._Barring_Gear,Steam Turbine Generator,Barring Gear,Rotor turning during shutdown,,,"barring gear, rotate turbine generator rotors continuously, shutdown even cooling, automatic and manual engagement selectable, automatic disengage on turbine starting under steam, manual barring lever on turning gear motor, limit switch lockout electric turning operation",False,Steam turbine generator barring gear requirements; no ACC/air cooled condenser mention or context.,True,,high,False,"Explicit technical requirements for steam turbine generator barring/turning gear operation, interlocks, and modes.","Barring gear shall be provided to rotate the turbine generator rotors continuously during periods of shutdown to ensure even cooling. The barring gear shall be arranged for both automatic and manual engagement, which shall be selectable. The barring gear shall automatically dis-engage on turbine starting under steam.
Manual barring by means of a lever fitted to the turning gear motor shall be provided. A limit switch should lock out the electric turning operation if the lever is attached."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_054,30,6.2.7._Steam_Turbine_Control_&_Monitoring_Systems,"6, 2, 7",content,text,6.2.7._Steam_Turbine_Control_&_Monitoring_Systems,Steam Turbine Generator,Control and Monitoring,Thermal stress limiting and performance monitoring,,,"automatic intervention to limit maximum thermal stress levels, monitor and record performance, significant operational data recording, turbine drains faulty drain operation indication, DCMS",False,Steam turbine control/monitoring and turbine drains to DCMS; no ACC signal.,True,,medium,True,"Clear control/monitoring functional requirements, but DCMS acronym is not defined in provided abbreviations and scope is somewhat high-level.","The control system shall include features to provide automatic intervention in order to limit maximum thermal stress levels.
The Steam Turbine unit and all auxiliary plant shall be provided with all equipment necessary to monitor and record performance and all significant operational data.
For the turbine drains faulty drain operation shall be indicated on the DCMS."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_055,30,6.2.8._Steam_Turbine_Start_up_System,"6, 2, 8",content,text,6.2.8._Steam_Turbine_Start_up_System,Steam Turbine Generator,Start-up System,Steam bypass stations and bypass capacity,,,"steam start up systems, steam bypass stations, steam and metal temperature matching during turbine start-up, steam turbine bypass system, 100% maximum continuous rated flow, HRSG (Heat Recovery Steam Generator), valves wide opened, turbine or generator trip at full load, avoid blowing safety valves",False,Steam turbine start-up/bypass stations and HRSG bypass capacity; no ACC reference.,True,,high,False,Contains specific technical design requirement for steam turbine bypass system capacity and start-up temperature matching.,"The steam turbine steam start up systems shall include steam bypass stations to facilitate steam and metal temperature matching during turbine start-up. The steam turbine bypass system of each unit shall be designed to accommodate 100% of the maximum continuous rated flow of its respective HRSG or as specified in Exhibit B. Part 1 whichever is more stringent at valves wide opened, thus keeping the steam generator running at full load without blowing the safety valves in case of a turbine or generator trip at full load"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_056,31,6.2.9._Condensate_extraction_pumps,"6, 2, 9",content,text,6.2.9._Condensate_extraction_pumps,Water/Steam Cycle,Condensate System,Condensate extraction pumps,,,"minimum flow protection system, controlled condensate level, cavitation free operation, materials selection to obviate erosion and corrosion, stainless steel construction for parts in contact with feed water, renewable liners and sleeves, mechanical shaft seals, seal water coolers, electric drive motor 3 starts per hour, access via overhead crane or lifting beams without moving major pipework",False,Condensate extraction pumps in condensate system; no ACC/air cooled condenser mentioned.,True,,high,False,"Detailed pump design/materials, protection, sealing, motor start duty, and maintainability/access requirements suitable for matching pump datasheets/specs/layouts.","In addition to the requirements of Exhibit B, Part 1, the pumps shall be provided with a minimum flow protection system. The pumps shall operate under a controlled condensate level which shall ensure cavitation free operation. Care shall be taken in the selection of materials for the pumps to obviate erosion and corrosion of any part.
All parts of the pump in contact with feed water shall be of stainless steel construction. Parts subject to wear shall be fitted with renewable liners and sleeves.
The pumps shall be provided with mechanical shaft seals and seal water coolers, if required by the equipment manufacturer.
The electric drive motor shall be suitable for at least 3 starts per hour without degradation.
The condensate extraction pumps shall be accessible via overhead crane or suitable lifting beams, without the requirement to move major pipework."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_057,31,6.2.10._Condensate_tank,"6, 2, 10",content,text,6.2.10._Condensate_tank,Water/Steam Cycle,Condensate System,Condensate collection tank (hot well),,,"gravity drain from heat exchangers, condensate collection tank, hot well, instruments fittings and connections, drain to waste connection, accessible instruments and valves for operation adjustment and maintenance",False,Condensate tank/hot well description without any ACC linkage or ACC term.,True,,medium,False,Defines condensate tank/hot well functional arrangement and instrumentation/accessibility requirements.,"The condensate shall be drained by gravity from the heat exchangers into a condensate collection tank (hot well).
The tank shall be provided with all necessary instruments, fittings and connections including a drain to waste connection. All instruments and valves shall be arranged to be easily accessible for operation, adjustment and maintenance."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_058,31,6.2.11._Drain_pot_and_drain_pumps,"6, 2, 11",content,text,6.2.11._Drain_pot_and_drain_pumps,Air Cooled Condenser (ACC),Exhaust Steam Duct,Drain Pot and Drain Pumps,,,"exhaust steam duct drain pot, horizontal section drain arrangement, ACC supplier scope of supply, piping accessories valves filters instruments joints, 2 x 100% drain pumps, drain pump motors",True,Explicit ACC scope: depth_1 is 'Air Cooled Condenser (ACC)' and text mentions 'exhaust steam duct' drain pot and 'ACC Supplier scope of supply' with drain pumps.,True,,high,False,Defines ACC exhaust steam duct drainage arrangement and specifies drain pumps (2x100%) and included piping/accessories scope.,"The exhaust steam duct is drained through a drain pot arranged in the horizontal section of the exhaust steam duct.
Piping and accessories, valves, filters, instrument, joints, etc., shall be included in the ACC Supplier scope of supply.
2 x 100% drain pumps, complete with motors shall be provided."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_059,32,6.2.12._Vacuum_Raising_and_Maintaining_Plant,"6, 2, 12",content,text,6.2.12._Vacuum_Raising_and_Maintaining_Plant,Steam Turbine Generator,Condenser Vacuum System,Vacuum Raising and Maintaining Plant (Air Extraction),,,"air extraction plant, maintain vacuum in steam turbine condenser, start-up hogging evacuation equipment, HEI standards, extract air and incondensable gases, discharge air and gases to atmosphere, retain condensed water from air stream, standby capacity to prevent loss of vacuum, design duty air removal capability (dry air), valves glands and seats sealed for sub-atmospheric pressure, isolating valve and union coupling at each valve connection, demineralised water for sealing duty",False,Condenser vacuum/air extraction plant for steam turbine condenser; no ACC term or ACC-specific context.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit technical requirements for condenser vacuum/air extraction (hogging), HEI compliance, standby capacity, sealing against air ingress, and demineralised water usage.","The air extraction plant shall be designed to maintain vacuum in the steam turbine condenser. The start-up or hogging evacuation raising equipment shall be designed in accordance with HEI standards and shall be capable of quickly extracting air and incondensable gases from the turbines, condensers to raise vacuum from atmospheric pressure to a vacuum at the minimum delay that is possible and at a rate to commensurate with unit synchronization. Means shall be provided for discharging the air and gases to the atmosphere and retaining water condensed from the air stream.
Standby capacity shall be proposed to ensure no loss of vacuum as a result of plant failure.
The design duty air removal capability (with dry air) shall be consistent with or better than that theoretically determined using an internationally recognised standard (HEI or equivalent).
The glands and seats of all valves and any other areas subject to sub atmospheric pressure shall be suitably sealed to avoid the ingress of air. The connection at each valve shall incorporate an isolating valve and union coupling to facilitate removal for maintenance. Water used for this duty shall be demineralised water."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_060,32,6.2.13._Steam_Connections_and_Drains,"6, 2, 13",content,text,6.2.13._Steam_Connections_and_Drains,Steam Turbine Generator,Steam and Feedwater Piping,Steam Connections and Drains,,,"rapid detection and prevention of water induction into turbine, main extraction and exhaust steam lines, spray attemperators, gland sealing systems, turbine drain lines, ASME Standard TDP-1-1998, Owner-approved equivalent code",False,Generic steam/feedwater piping and turbine exhaust steam line drain protection; no ACC mention.,True,,high,False,Provides specific design/code requirement (ASME TDP-1-1998) for steam/feedwater piping features to prevent water induction into turbine.,"The steam and feed water piping systems shall incorporate recognised features for rapid detection and prevention of water induction into the turbine from main, extraction and exhaust steam lines, spray attemperators, gland sealing systems and turbine drain lines (to ASME Standard TDP-1-1998 requirements, or equivalent alternative code approved by Owner)."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_061,32,6.2.14._Noise_Limits,"6, 2, 14",content,text,6.2.14._Noise_Limits,Steam Turbine Generator,Noise Control,Noise Limits,,,"steam turbine generator noise level requirements, acoustic hoods, acoustic enclosures, acoustic treatment, provide and install to meet noise limits",False,Steam turbine generator noise limits/acoustic enclosures; no ACC signal.,True,,medium,False,States requirement for acoustic enclosures/treatment if standard turbine cannot meet noise limits; useful for retrieving noise/acoustic design documents.,"If the noise level requirements applicable to each Steam Turbine Generator cannot be achieved with the standard Steam Turbine alone, then suitable acoustic hoods/enclosures or treatment shall be provided and installed with the Steam Turbine Generator unit if required to meet noise limits."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_062,"32, 33",6.2.15._Turbine_Pipework_and_Drains,"6, 2, 15",content,text,6.2.15._Turbine_Pipework_and_Drains,Steam Turbine Generator,Turbine Pipework and Drains,Drainage System Operation and Monitoring,,,"safe and efficient start-up and operation, remote and local control, turbine drains fault indication on DCMS, clean drains collecting vessel, transfer pump for steam turbine drains",False,Turbine pipework and drains/clean drains vessel; no ACC/air cooled condenser reference.,True,,high,False,Defines operational/control requirements for turbine drain system including DCMS indication and provision of clean drains collecting vessel with transfer pump.,"The pipework and drainage system shall allow for safe and efficient start-up and operation of the Steam Turbine Generator, controlled remotely and locally. For the turbine drains, faulty drain operation shall be indicated on the DCMS.
A clean drains collecting vessel complete with transfer pump shall be provided to handle drains from the Steam Turbines."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_063,33,6.2.16._Maintainability,"6, 2, 16",content,"text, list_item",6.2.16._Maintainability,Steam Turbine Generator,Maintainability,Maintenance access and lifting provisions,,,"maintainability features, design review phase, major component weights, lifting facilities, overhead lifting equipment capacity, clearance around enclosure, special lifting equipment, mandatory inspection/overhaul dismantling extent, lay-down area in turbine hall, rotor and casing lay-down locations, maintenance materials manpower skill set",False,Maintainability requirements for Steam Turbine Generator lifting/laydown; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention or signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains specific maintainability requirements (lifting/clearances, dismantling extent, lay-down area, manpower/materials) for Steam Turbine Generator that can map to existing maintenance/access/layout documents.","Information regarding the maintainability features incorporated in the design and construction of the Steam Turbine Generator unit shall be provided during the design review phase. The following items shall be included:
- the weight of individual major components, and the lifting facilities required for removal. If overhead lifting equipment is required, the capacity and type of equipment required and clearance around the enclosure for unimpeded operation and movement shall be stated. Any necessary special lifting equipment shall be provided within the maintenance tools and equipment supplied
- details of the extent of dismantling of the equipment for mandatory inspection/overhaul, and lay-down area required
- the lay-down area in the turbine hall showing the location of the turbine components (e.g. rotor, casing, etc.) which are to be placed during maintenance
- maintenance Materials, manpower and skill set requirements to be stated"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_064,"33, 34",6.3.1._General_requirements,"6, 3, 1",table,"text, list_item",6.3.1._General_requirements,Heat Recovery Steam Generator (HRSG),General requirements,Design and operating cases,,,"ASME stamped, ASME standards, supplementary firing, exhaust gas temperature and mass flow rates, operating cases with/without supplementary firing, acid dew point, cold end corrosion, inspection doors and platforms, gas duct linings and insulation, no direct flame impingement, pinch point < 36°F (20°C), self-draining horizontal gas pass, chemical/acid cleaning after erection, ASME I, ASME B31.1",False,"HRSG general requirements (ducting, chimneys, supplementary firing, ASME); no ACC-related terms.",True,,high,False,"Provides explicit HRSG technical design/operation requirements, standards (ASME I/B31.1), supplementary firing constraints, corrosion/insulation/duct geometry criteria, and performance anchor (pinch point) useful for MDL retrieval.","Heat recovery steam generator (HRSG) shall be complete with ducting, chimneys, mountings, integral valves and pipes and all other items associated with the unit to allow reliable operation.
The HRSGs shall be of proven design and in accordance with the requirements of relevant ASME standards where applicable or equivalent international standard. The HRSG shall be ASME stamped. The HRSGs shall incorporate supplementary firing for meeting the steam requirements as set forth in Exhibit B, Part 1. HRSG shall be entirely suitable for the exhaust gas temperatures and mass flow rates of the gas turbine resulting after supplementary firing (at full load and all required loads and ambient conditions).
The HRSGs and all associated facilities (e.g. Gas supply system, steam supply system etc.) shall therefore be capable of accommodating the following operating cases:
- continuous operation of HRSG without supplementary firing
- continuous operation of HRSG with minimum supplementary firing enabling fast increase of steam generation
- within design limits operation of supplementary firing according to steam demand by steam generation control or by steam pressure control; continuous operation of HRSG with maximum supplementary firing
The HRSG shall be designed for cold end corrosion issues and so that exhaust gas temperature is above the acid dew point when the gas turbine is fired on Gas.
Inspection doors and platforms shall be provided to allow ready access to the HRSG gas path, tubing and other pressure parts.
Gas duct linings and insulation shall use materials with proven capability at the maximum gas temperatures anticipated, together with design margin to allow for uneven temperature distribution downstream of supplementary firing burners. The duct geometry downstream of the supplementary firing burners shall be designed so as to ensure that no direct flame impingement on heat exchange surfaces or other metallic surfaces can occur.
The pinch point for the HRSGs shall be optimised at the design point and shall be less than 36 0 F (20 0 C).
The HRSGs shall be designed for outdoor installation and all equipment and mountings shall be entirely weather-proof and suitable for the aggressive environment. The main pressure parts shall be arranged in the form of a self-contained construction and shall be of the self-draining, horizontal gas pass type.
The water side of the HRSG shall be chemical/acid cleaned after completion of erection in accordance with accepted chemical cleaning procedures.
Regarding HRSG pressure retaining parts material selection shall be in line with requirements and stipulations of ASME I (pressure vessels) and ASME B31.1 (power piping).
All fleet wide modifications in design and construction recommended by the HRSG manufacturer after award of the Contract up to Plant final takeover shall be immediately informed to the Owner and the Contractor shall implement the same free of cost and without any time impact to the Owner. The details of modifications, schedule and mode of implementation shall be mutually discussed and agreed upon with the Owner."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_065,"34, 35",6.3.1._General_requirements,"6, 3, 1",content,text,6.3.1._General_requirements,Heat Recovery Steam Generator (HRSG),General requirements,OEM upgrades and service support,,,"latest upgrades/improvements/safety modifications, TIL/ECN, implemented at shop or site, OEM service support, erection, commissioning, operational test period, plant final takeover",False,HRSG OEM upgrades/service support during erection/commissioning; no ACC signal.,False,Primarily contractual/administrative requirement about implementing manufacturer upgrades free of cost and providing OEM support; lacks specific technical design/performance criteria or engineering deliverable anchors.,low,True,Contains limited technical retrieval signals beyond generic OEM support and upgrade notices; mostly contract administration.,"All the latest upgrades/improvements/safety modifications announced by the manufacturer (TIL/ECN etc.) before and during the contract period up to Plant final take over shall also be applicable for this contract and implemented by the Contractor free of cost and without any time impact to the Owner at shop or at site with the consent of owner.
Necessary OEM service support during erection, commissioning, and operational test period shall be provided"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_066,35,6.3.2._HRSG_drums_and_headers,"6, 3, 2",content,text,6.3.2._HRSG_drums_and_headers,Heat Recovery Steam Generator (HRSG),Drums and headers,Steam drum internals and testing,,,"evaporative section steam drum(s), primary and secondary steam/water separators, mechanical carryover limit, moisture carryover < 0.1%, superheater moisture carryover not detectable, hydrostatic test at shop and site, 100% radiographic testing butt welds, alloy steel and carbon steel welds, 2 minute hold up time, NWL to low-low level (LLC)",False,HRSG steam drum internals/testing requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.,True,,high,False,"Includes concrete HRSG steam drum performance limits, separator requirements, NDE/testing requirements, and level/hold-up time criteria suitable for matching to HRSG design datasheets/specs and QA/testing documents.","HRSG evaporative section shall be provided with steam drum(s), each of adequate size to ensure stable operation under all normal load conditions and other single item trip conditions. Drum internals shall include primary and secondary steam and water separators to limit the mechanical carry over. The moisture carryover from the drum to the first stage of the superheater will be less than 0.1%. The moisture carryover after the first stage of the superheater should not be detectable.
Steam drum shall be tested hydrostatically at shop and at site. Butt welds for alloy steel and carbon steel will be 100% Radiographically tested.
Contractor shall ensure that the drum has as a minimum two (2) minute hold up time between normal working level (NWL) and low-low level condition (LLC = low-low cut off)."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_067,"35, 36","6.3.3._Superheater,_evaporator_and_economiser_banks","6, 3, 3",content,"text, list_item",6.3.3._Superheater _evaporator_and_economiser_banks,Heat Recovery Steam Generator (HRSG),Superheater / Evaporator / Economiser banks,Tube bank construction and materials,,,"horizontal and vertical tube pitch, tube bank supports shallow depth for cleaning, fins solidly attached לאורך fin length, superheater tubes solid drawn seamless, no electric resistance welded (ERW) tubes, minimum tube thickness 3.2 mm, 2.7 mm seamless tubes with calculations and availability evidence, LP superheater ERW allowed, LP superheater minimum thickness 2.4 mm, off-load water washing, local drain points and external hose connections, prevent sagging and vibrations",False,HRSG tube bank construction/materials and drain points for washing; no ACC mention.,True,,high,False,"Provides specific HRSG heating surface/tube material and thickness requirements, exceptions for LP superheater ERW, and maintainability/design features (washing, drains, vibration/sagging prevention) useful for retrieving HRSG mechanical design documents.","Each element and tube bank shall contain tubes on a horizontal and vertical pitch, adequately supported and of shallow depth to enable easy cleaning. Any fins used shall be solidly attached to the tube throughout the fin length.
All superheater tubes shall be solid drawn seamless and not electric resistance welded. The tubes shall be of minimum 3.2mm thickness. However a minimum of 2.7mm seamless tubes are acceptable, subject to the:
- contractor providing the necessary calculations and documentary evidence for Owner's approval during detailed engineering
- contractor confirming and providing evidence of commercial availability of these tubes in the market; and
- confirmation by the Contractor to HRSG standards and codes as asked for in this document
- As an exception to the above paragraph, it is acceptable only for the LP super heater tubes to be ERW (electric resistance welded) with a minimum tube thickness of 2.4mm subject to:
- contractor providing the necessary calculations and documentary evidence for Owner's approval during detailed engineering
- contractor confirming and providing evidence of commercial availability of these tubes in the market; and
- confirmation by the Contractor to HRSG standards and codes as asked for in this document
All the HRSG heating surfaces shall be designed taking into account the requirement for off-load water washing and a number of local drain points and external hose connections shall be provided.
The design shall incorporate measures to prevent sagging and vibrations occurring in the tube banks."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_068,36,6.3.4._Steam_temperature_and_output_control,"6, 3, 4",content,text,6.3.4._Steam_temperature_and_output_control,HRSG,Steam Temperature and Output Control,Steam Temperature Control at Interface,,,"steam temperature control equipment, HRSG outlet steam temperature, required steam temperature, interface to existing systems, FGP processing plant interface, acceptable temperature range, all generation and supply conditions",False,Steam temperature control at HRSG outlet interface; no ACC-related equipment or keywords.,True,,high,False,Defines HRSG steam temperature control requirement and interface temperature control to existing FGP plant systems.,Steam temperature control equipment shall be provided to ensure that the HRSG outlet steam temperature is the required steam temperature at the Interface to the existing systems of the FGP processing Plant within the acceptable temperature range at all generation and supply possibilities/conditions.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_069,"36, 37",6.3.5._Valves_and_accessories,"6, 3, 5",content,text,6.3.5._Valves_and_accessories,HRSG,Valves and Accessories,"Safety Relief, Stop, Vent and Drain Valves",,,"boiler and superheater safety valves, all steam circuits, ASME Section I, vent-pipe, silencer, drain arrangement, electrically operated relief valve, HP steam main before boiler stop valve, main steam stop valve with bypass valve, double isolation valves master/martyr, motorised vent and drain valves remote operation, pressurised main drains to HRSG blowdown vessel/tank, access doors and access platforms for HRSG inspection, steam headers to FGP interface point",False,HRSG safety/stop/vent/drain valves and blowdown vessel connections; no ACC signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains detailed HRSG steam system valve/accessory requirements including ASME code basis, valve types/locations, blowdown vessel tie-in, and interface routing.","Boiler and superheater safety valves for all steam circuits shall be provided and shall be specified in accordance with design code recommendations. Each safety valve shall be fitted with its own vent-pipe, silencer and suitable drain and shall be designed and constructed to prevent back-pressure from interfering with the proper operation of the valve. The safety valves shall be designed and installed according to ASME Section I
An electrically operated relief valve shall be provided on the HP steam main before boiler stop valve for the purpose of venting steam during start-up and pressure excursions. This valve may discharge into the silencer of one of the safety relief valves as long as this does not impair the operation of the safety relief valve. The electrically operated safety relief valve is in addition to spring operated safety relief valve before boiler stop valve.
One electric motor or hydraulically operated stop valve and one non-return valve, together with one motor operated drain valve shall be installed in all main steam lines. Main steam stop valve shall be provided with a smaller electric motor operated by-pass valve or other integral means to enable downstream warming and pressure equalisation. High pressure steam from the boilers shall be routed to steam headers to the FGP interface point.
Double isolation valves operating as master/martyr shall be provided on all boiler emptying, venting and drain lines. Main vent and drain valves shall be motorised for remote operation. In the case where motor operated drain/vent valves are provided, there will be one motorised valve and one manual valve in series.
All pressurised main drains in the boiler area shall be connected to the HRSG blowdown vessel/tank.
Adequate access doors and access platforms shall be provided in the upper and lower sections of the HRSG for internal inspection."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_070,37,6.3.6._Feed_Water_control_stations,"6, 3, 6",content,text,6.3.6._Feed_Water_control_stations,HRSG,Feedwater System,Feedwater Flow Control Stations,,,"feed water flow control to HRSG, feed regulating valve(s), 0% to 100% flow control range",False,Feedwater flow control to HRSG; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,True,,medium,False,States functional requirement for HRSG feedwater control valve(s) and control range.,The control of feed water flow to HRSG shall be achieved by feed regulating valve(s) capable of controlling between 0% and 100% flow.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_071,37,6.3.7._Supplementary_firing_system,"6, 3, 7",content,text,6.3.7._Supplementary_firing_system,HRSG,Supplementary Firing System,Grid Burners and Operating Capability,,,"supplementary firing system, grid burners, steam generation capacity over base ranges, RSC (Reference Site Condition), OP A and OP B operating points, operation at GT partial loads and base loads, uniform heat distribution to HRSG, proven burner design, igniters and flame monitors maintainable online",False,Supplementary firing system/grid burners for HRSG; no ACC-related content.,True,,high,False,"Defines supplementary firing system type (grid burners), operating envelope relative to GT load, and maintainability requirements for igniters/flame monitoring.","The supplementary firing system shall utilise grid burners.
The supplementary firing system shall be designed for a steam generation capacity as requested in Exhibit B, Part 1 over and above the base ranges at RSC, OP A and OP B points and various scenarios as specified therein.
Operation of supplementary firing should be possible at any case of GT partial loads and base loads.
The burners shall be designed to provide maximum efficiency and uniform heat distribution to the HRSG. The burner system shall be of a proven design and include equipment necessary for its safe, efficient and reliable operation.
All igniters and flame monitors shall be capable of being maintained or replaced whilst the unit is on line."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_072,37,6.3.8._Blowdown_equipment,"6, 3, 8",content,text,6.3.8._Blowdown_equipment,HRSG,Blowdown System,Continuous and Intermittent Blowdown; Cooling to Wastewater Interface,,,"HRSG blowdown facilities, intermittent blowdown, continuous blowdown, blowdown lines minimum 3% mass flow at MCR, blowdown water cooling, pre-treatment plant interface, wastewater piping interface, wastewater temperature limit 140°F (60°C), HRSG blowdown coolers",False,HRSG blowdown equipment and cooling to wastewater interface; no ACC mention or scope.,True,,high,False,Provides HRSG blowdown design basis including minimum blowdown capacity and wastewater interface temperature limit requiring blowdown coolers.,"All HRSG circuits shall be fitted with blowdown facilities which shall be designed to operate on both intermittent and continuous basis. The blowdown lines shall be designed for a minimum of 3% mass flow (at MCR).
Before being discharged to the pre-treatment plant (or) wastewater piping, the HRSG blowdown water shall be cooled so that the wastewater temperature at the interface point shall not exceed 140°F (60°C), for which necessary HRSG blow-down coolers shall be provided."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_073,"37, 38",6.3.9._HRSG_drum_local_water_level_gauges_and_remote_indicators,"6, 3, 9",content,text,6.3.9._HRSG_drum_local_water_level_gauges_and_remote_indicators,HRSG,Steam Drum Instrumentation,Drum Water Level Measurement and Indication,Steam Drum,,"HRSG steam drum, two local water level gauges each end of drum, fully (on line) maintainable gauges, bicolour type level gauge, ASME Section I, electronic water level gauging system, hydrastep type, LCD configurable (% and engineering units), hard-wired level indication to central control room, dedicated drum level measurements for drum level control, steam flow meter, feed flow meter, temperature and pressure compensation, level control under transients",False,HRSG steam drum water level gauges and instrumentation; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit HRSG steam drum level instrumentation requirements, standards reference (ASME Section I), and control measurement scheme anchors useful to retrieve existing I&C/HRSG instrumentation documents.","HRSG steam drum shall each be furnished with a minimum of two fully (on line) maintainable local water level gauges at each end of the drum and as per ASME Section I. These shall be of the bicolour type.
The steam drum shall also be fitted with an electronic water level gauging system of the hydrastep type, or alternative proven system including LCD type that is configurable to read in both percentage and engineering units. The system provided shall be complete with provision of hard-wired level indication in the central control room.
Dedicated drum level measurements shall be provided for drum level control which will also use steam flow and feed flow meters and temperature and pressure compensation appropriate to maintaining control of level within the defined range under all operating conditions and transients in normal operation."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_074,38,6.3.10._Stacks,"6, 3, 10",content,text,6.3.10._Stacks,HRSG,Exhaust System,Stacks,Flue Stack,,"> 60 m stack height, single flue stack per HRSG, air emissions assessment (Saudi Aramco/Owner confirmation), self-sustaining stack (lift vs losses), DIN / EN / BS / ASME STS-1, earthing bosses; earthed, clad and insulated for personnel protection, expanded metal personnel protection option, stack damper insulation option, efflux velocity ≥ 15 m/s, 60–100% GT load, supplementary firing, aircraft warning lights, permanent monitoring of stack outlet gas emissions, stack test points for exhaust gas distribution and temperature, agreed test code",False,HRSG flue stack requirements and emissions monitoring; no ACC-related mention.,True,,high,False,"Provides concrete stack design/performance criteria, applicable standards, insulation/earthing requirements, and emissions monitoring/test point requirements—strong retrieval anchors for stack design, emissions monitoring, and mechanical/civil documents.","Each HRSG shall have a single flue stack of > 60m height, subject to confirmation by Saudi Aramco/Owner based on the air emissions assessment. The diameter shall be such that it is self-sustaining, i.e. its lift due to temperature differential shall be equal to or greater than the sum of all losses.
The stacks shall be designed and constructed in accordance with DIN, EN, BS or ASME STS-1 or an equivalent approved standard if acceptable as per Exhibit B, Part 1. The stacks shall be provided with suitable earthling bosses and earthed.
The stacks shall be clad and insulated where required for personnel protection. Optionally expanded metal instead of insulation can also be provided where required for personnel protection in the Stacks. The style and finish shall be as approved by the Owner. Alternatively; the main stack can be insulated up to the stack damper if similar references in Saudi Arabia are provided by the Contractor and is as per the relevant standards.
The efflux velocity shall not be less than 15m/sec at normal operating loads (60-100% GT load with or without supplementary firing) and shall be designed throughout the life of the Plant.
Aircraft warning lights as required shall be provided on the Stacks to meet local regulations.
Provision shall be made for the permanent monitoring of stack outlet gas emissions in accordance with environmental requirements specified in Exhibit B, Part 1. In addition, the stack shall be provided with test points at positions such that the distribution and temperature of the exhaust gas can be determined in accordance with the agreed test code."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_075,"38, 39",6.3.11._Insulation,"6, 3, 11",content,text,6.3.11._Insulation,HRSG,Thermal Insulation,Duct and Casing Insulation,,,"refractory materials limited to minimum, gas turbine exhaust ducting, supplementary firing duct, HRSG structure support, adverse operating conditions, expansion, wind loading, duct and casing insulated internally, noncarcinogenic insulation, mineral wool, ceramic fibre, stainless steel liner plate, carbon steel casing, minimise insulation losses, safe working conditions",False,HRSG duct/casing insulation and refractory limits; no ACC context.,True,,high,False,"Contains specific HRSG insulation scope, materials, and structural/design considerations (expansion, wind loading) useful for retrieving insulation specifications and HRSG mechanical design documents.","The use of refractory materials, if required by the design, shall be limited to an absolute minimum in the area of the gas turbine exhaust ducting and supplementary firing duct.
The HRSG structure shall be designed to give adequate support to the HRSG body under the most adverse operating conditions taking into consideration expansion and wind loading. The duct and casing part of the HRSG shall be insulated internally. Insulation materials shall be noncarcinogenic such as mineral wool, ceramic fibre and stainless steel liner plate and carbon steel casing. The insulation shall be adequate to minimise insulation losses and permit safe working conditions."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_076,39,6.3.12._Cleaning_and_preservation,"6, 3, 12",content,text,6.3.12._Cleaning_and_preservation,HRSG,Cleaning and Preservation,Off-load Cleaning and Preservation System,,,"boiler off-load cleaning, filling system, preservation system",False,Boiler off-load cleaning/preservation system; no ACC signal.,True,,medium,False,"Although brief, it specifies provision of a boiler/HRSG off-load cleaning, filling, and preservation system—useful anchor for O&M/commissioning and boiler auxiliary system documentation.","A complete boiler off-load cleaning, filling and preservation system shall be provided."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_077,39,6.3.13._Steam_connections_and_drains,"6, 3, 13",content,text,6.3.13._Steam_connections_and_drains,Steam and Feedwater Systems,Piping,Steam Connections and Drains,,,"steam piping system, feedwater piping system, rapid detection of water carry over, prevention of water carry over",False,Steam/feedwater piping features for water carryover detection; no ACC mention.,True,,medium,False,"Provides a functional requirement for steam/feedwater piping design (water carryover detection/prevention), which can map to piping design criteria and steam system documents.",The steam and Feedwater piping systems shall incorporate recognised features for rapid detection and prevention of water carry over.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_078,39,6.3.13._Steam_connections_and_drains,,content,text,6.3.13._Steam_connections_and_drains,Steam System,Steam Connections and Drains,Live steam header and bypass operation,,,"live steam system design, HRSG operation without limitations at specified load, steam turbine bypass operation feeding FGP, HRSG stability during start/stop, turbine trip, load rejection, ST bypass mode, common header feeding both steam turbines, cold start heating measures, GT/HRSG unit start-up and stopping while other units in combined cycle, specific steam bypass station for smooth start-up/stop",False,Live steam header and steam turbine bypass operation to FGP; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit operational/design requirements for live steam system, common header configuration, and steam bypass station enabling GT/HRSG and ST operating modes.","The live steam system design shall permit the HRSG and the steam turbines to be operated without limitations at specified load. It shall also make possible the bypass operation of each steam turbine feeding directly the FGP. It shall also ensure the HRSGs stability during start and stop and when a turbine trip or the load rejection occurs or when the Plant is running on ST bypass mode.
With the common header configuration feeding both steam turbines, the proper heating measures for cold start of one ST rest of the plant being in full operation shall be provided.
Each GT shall be capable of continuous operation in combined cycle mode also in case of a part of the other GTs are not in operation. The stopping and start-up of one GT/HRSG unit while any of the other GT/HRSG unit(s) remain(s) in combined cycle mode shall be possible and is a normal operation mode. Specific steam by-pass station is to be provided to enable smooth start-up/stop of a GT/HRSG unit when the other unit(s) is (are) already in operation."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_079,40,6.4.2._Steam_turbine_by-pass,"6, 4, 2",content,text,6.4.2._Steam_turbine_by-pass,Steam Turbine,Steam Turbine Bypass,"Bypass system capacity, valves, and control integration",,,"ST bypass system capacity and design, continuous GT base load operation with ST tripped/not in operation, smooth LP steam pressure transient control, bypass valves control capability, fast action isolating valves, pressure reducing valves, de-superheating sections, modulating valves, atomizers, spray water injection, automatic operation during startup/shutdown, hydraulic or pneumatic actuation, bypass controls integrated with Plant DCMS, vibration damping measures, thermal shock, pressure waves, pressure reducing valve instabilities",False,Steam turbine bypass system valves/controls integration; does not reference ACC.,True,,high,False,"Provides detailed technical requirements for steam turbine bypass system components, operating scenarios, and control system integration.","The capacity and design of the ST bypass system shall enable continuous GT operation at base load also in the event ST has been tripped and/or when the ST is not in operation. The design of the bypass system, bypass valves and their control capability shall control a smooth LP steam pressure transient in the event of ST trips (and all scenarios 1-11 as mentioned in Exhibit B, Part 1) in order to continue to feed the FGP.
The bypass system shall include fast action isolating valves, pressure reducing valves with de-superheating sections and modulating valves with atomizers for injection of spray water.
The by-pass system shall be operated automatically during startup/shutdown, in the event of a trip or sudden ST load reduction or in case of lower electrical demand. They shall be dedicated special bypass valves specifically designed for steam bypass applications with hydraulic or pneumatic actuation. The steam turbine bypass controls shall be integrated with the overall Plant DCMS.
The Contractor shall indicate in his proposal his successful experience in using bypass systems of similar design and shall indicate the design aspects that prevent damage from vibration, thermal shock, and pressure waves resulting from shock waves at the different pressure reduction stages and pressure reducing valve instabilities. Special attention shall be given to effective vibration damping design measures."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_080,"40, 41",6.4.3._Reserve_Boiler(s),"6, 4, 3",table,text,6.4.3._Reserve_Boiler(s),Boiler Systems,Reserve Boiler,"Packaged boiler design, fuels, emissions, and cleaning",,,"Reserve Boiler packaged system, burners, fuel and combustion air delivery systems, burner management systems, feedwater systems, ASME I, ASME B31.1, ASME certified, dual fuel capability, Low BTU gas, Sales gas, individual CEMS, emission and stack limits (Exhibit B Part 1), forced draft fans, deaerator supply via dedicated feedwater pump, warm standby with steam bleed from main steam system, inspection doors and platforms, gas duct linings and insulation for maximum gas temperatures, outdoor installation weather-proof, chemical/acid cleaning after erection, creep region materials, minimum design life 25 years from COD",False,Reserve boiler packaged system requirements; no ACC-related content.,True,,high,False,"Contains clear technical scope for reserve boiler package including applicable codes, fuel capability, emissions monitoring, auxiliaries, installation, and cleaning/life requirements.","A Reserve Boiler(s) inclusive of, burner(s), fuel and combustion air delivery systems, burner management systems, electrical control and feedwater systems shall be specifically engineered as a packaged system for the facility
The Reserve Boiler shall be of proven design and in accordance with the requirements of relevant ASME standards where applicable such as ASME I (pressure vessels) and ASME B31.1 (power piping) and shall be ASME certified. The Reserve Boiler(s) shall provide reliable and consistent performance to the operating parameters as defined in Exhibit B, Part 1.
The reserve boiler shall have dual fuel capability to fire both Low BTU and Sales gas, all inherit safety devices, an individual CEMS and meet the emission and stack limits as per Exhibit B, Part 1. Forced draft fans shall provide necessary combustion air. Feedwater shall be supplied from the deaerator via a dedicated feedwater pump. The reserve boiler shall be kept in warm standby as per Exhibit B, Part 1 when in stand-by with a steam bleed from the main steam system.
Inspection doors and platforms shall be provided to allow ready access to the Reserve Boiler's gas path, tubing and other pressure parts.
Gas duct linings and insulation shall use materials with proven capability at the maximum gas temperatures anticipated.
The Reserve Boiler(s) shall be designed for outdoor installation and all equipment and mountings shall be entirely weather-proof and suitable for the aggressive environment.
The water side of the Reserve Boiler(s) shall be chemical/acid cleaned after completion of erection in accordance with accepted chemical cleaning procedures.
Materials operating in the creep region shall have a minimum design life criteria of at least 25 years (from COD).
All modifications in design and construction recommended by the Reserve Boiler(s)' manufacturer after award of the Contract up to Plant final takeover shall be immediately informed to Saudi Aramco and the Contractor shall implement the same free of cost and without any time impact to the Owner. The details of modifications, schedule and mode of implementation shall be mutually discussed and agreed upon with the Owner.
All the latest upgrades/improvements/safety modifications announced by the manufacturer (TIL/ECN etc.,) before and during the contract period up to Plant final take over shall also be applicable for this contract and implemented by the Contractor free of cost and without any time impact to the Owner at shop or at site with the consent of owner.
Necessary OEM service support during erection, commissioning, and operational test period shall be provided."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_081,"41, 42",6.5._Air_Cooled_Condenser,"6, 5",content,text,6.5._Air_Cooled_Condenser,Steam Cycle,Air Cooled Condenser (ACC),"ACC performance, layout, and materials",,,"correction curves for steam turbine gross power vs dry bulb temperature and relative humidity, ACC handling steam turbine bypass operation, 10% reserve for possible steam turbine overloads, ACC orientation/layout for adequate air circulation, ACC fan individually isolatable, cells on hot dip galvanised steel support structure, internal partition walls to reduce mal-distribution of air flow, full vacuum design, HEI Standards for Steam Surface Condensers, ASME Boiler and Pressure Vessel Code, forced draught type, minimise recirculation of cooling air, entire steam flow from bypass system discharged to ACC during ST trip transient, condense entire steam flow dumped by bypass system for indefinite time, finned tube bundles, welded carbon steel tube + carbon steel fins (hot dipped galvanized), aluminized steel tubing with aluminium fins acceptable only for single row type, wind walls around perimeter, high pressure spray tube cleaning, single-speed fans",True,"Section titled ""6.5 Air Cooled Condenser (ACC)"" with detailed ACC requirements (fans, cells, finned bundles, wind walls, full vacuum, bypass steam to ACC).",True,,high,False,"Strong technical requirements for ACC design code basis, bypass duty, layout, materials, and maintenance/access provisions.","Contractor shall provide correction curves for gross power output of the Steam turbine versus ambient conditions of dry bulb temperature and relative humidity. In addition, the ACC shall be capable of handling steam turbine bypass operation during start-up, shut-down and/or emergencies (shall include a reserve of at least 10% to cover possible steam turbine overloads). The orientation and layout of the ACC shall be such that the adequate air circulation is possible around the ACC.
Each ACC fan shall be individually isolatable. Access ways, galleries, platforms and ladders shall be provided for safe and efficient operation and maintenance including maintenance of fans, gearboxes, bearings etc. The ACC shall comprise a number of cells mounted on a hot dip galvanised steel support structure. Internal partition walls shall be located between each fan section to reduce mal-distribution of air flow. Lockable access doors shall be provided in the partition walls (normally kept closed and locked).
The ACC shall be of proven design with supplier references demonstrating the same. The ACC shall be designed for full vacuum to an approved heat exchanger design code as applicable such as Heat Exchanger Institute (HEI) Standards for Steam Surface Condensers, ASME Boiler and Pressure Vessel Code etc.
The ACC shall be of the forced draught type, located on a steel structure at ground level. It shall be capable of condensing steam from the steam turbine and turbine drain flash vessel (if provided) at all ambient conditions and at all loads.
The ACC shall be arranged to minimise the recirculation of cooling air.
During steam turbine trip transient/start-up phase, the whole steam flow from the by-pass system will be discharged to the ACC.
During the by-pass conditions, the ACC shall be able to maintain the pressure such that no limitation occurs for the steam turbine capabilities.
In addition to the requirements of Exhibit B, Part 1; the ACC shall be designed to be capable of condensing the entire steam flow dumped by the by pass system following a steam turbine trip, for indefinite time.
Finned tubed bundles shall be used within the ACC. The tube fin material combination shall be of Welded carbon steel tube + carbon steel fins (both hot dipped galvanized). In case Contractor proposes Aluminium coated (Aluminized steel) tubing with Aluminium fins then the same is acceptable only for single row type condensers.
Wind walls shall be installed around the perimeter of the ACC structure, minimally extending from fan deck level to the top of the heat exchanger bundles. Each ACC shall be equipped with systems such as high pressure spray to enable cleaning of the outer surfaces of the tubes.
Fans shall be of single-speed type. The Contractor shall develop the operating concept for ACC during the detailed design phase."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_082,"42, 43",6.5._Air_Cooled_Condenser,"6, 5",content,text,6.5._Air_Cooled_Condenser,Steam Cycle,Air Cooled Condenser (ACC),Condensate system arrangement and ACC performance corrections,,,"condensate extraction pumps, condensate control valve, bypass control valve with manual valve, steam and water side isolating valves, automatic operation, pump assembly/dismantling, wear vibration cavitation limits, pumps and motors at ground floor outdoors near ACC condensate tank, optimal suction requirements, basket strainer fine mesh on pump suction during start-up, replace with coarse mesh after commissioning, ACC cleaning system, corrective actions for wind screens or different fan design during warranty if restrictions/damages observed, correction curves of ACC performance vs wind velocity direction and ambient temperature",True,"ACC condensate system arrangement and performance corrections; mentions ""ACC"", condensate extraction pumps near ACC condensate tank, cleaning system, wind screens/fan design.",True,,high,False,Includes specific ACC auxiliary equipment and operational/performance correction requirements useful for matching to condensate/ACC design documents.,"The ACC shall be arranged together with condensate extraction pumps, condensate control valve including a bypass control valve with manual valve, in addition to steam (if applicable) and water side isolating valves. The ACC shall be arranged to operate automatically.
The design and workmanship shall be such that the pumps are readily assembled or dismantled and that they shall operate satisfactorily under all operating conditions without undue wear, vibration and cavitation.
The pumps and motors shall be located at ground floor level outdoors and close to the ACC condensate tank. The configuration must be based on the optimal suction requirements.
Basket strainer with fine mesh/screen shall be supplied on the pump suction to remove suspended solids in the condensate, particularly during start-up (commissioning period). After commissioning this shall be replaced by the Contractor with coarse mesh / screen.
In case load restrictions or damages to the ACC are observed before the end of the warranty period while the plant is operated within the specified design range, the Contractor shall improve the design with adequate means (e.g. wind screens, different fan design, etc) to avoid such restrictions or damages, at no cost to the Owner. The proposed corrective actions and the timing of installation shall be subject to Owner's approval.
An ACC cleaning system should be included.
The contractor should provide correction curves of ACC performance in function of wind velocity, direction and ambient temperature."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_083,"43, 44",6.5.1.1._ACC_STRUCTURAL_SUPPORTS,"6, 5, 1, 1",content,text,6.5._Air_Cooled_Condenser > 6.5.1.1._ACC_STRUCTURAL_SUPPORTS,Air Cooled Condenser,ACC Structural Supports,Structural steelwork and access structures,,,"integrated ACC structure, condenser fans and motors support, plenum chamber, access galleries, staircases platforms maintenance runway beams, ground foundation concrete supports, hot dip galvanized steelwork, fan deck at least 16 m high, air recirculation minimization, wind speed design loadings, stress analysis anchors guides restraints, seismic design requirements (Exhibit B Part 1), checker plate flooring handrailing open grid flooring stair treads",True,"Section explicitly titled “ACC STRUCTURAL SUPPORTS” under “Air Cooled Condenser”; requirements for ACC structure, fan deck, plenum chamber, platforms, foundations, wind/seismic loads.",True,,high,False,"Contains explicit ACC structural/civil design requirements (height, wind/seismic loads, galvanizing, access steelwork) useful to retrieve structural drawings/specifications/calculations.","An integrated structure carrying the condenser, fans and motors, plenum chamber, access galleries, staircases, platforms and maintenance runway beams shall be supplied and secured to ground foundation concrete supports. Steelwork, platforms, ladders and stairways shall be hot dip galvanized.
The height of the structure shall take account of the air velocity when operating with all fans in service. The ACC shall be designed with a fan deck at least 16m high to minimize air recirculation.
The structural steelwork shall be capable of supporting the ACC at all operating, maintenance and testing loads and shall comply with the wind speed design loadings given for the Site. Where stress analysis calculations are performed, the location of anchors, guides or restraints shall be based on the results of the final stress analysis. Such anchors, guides and restraints shall be designed for the maximum forces and moments encountered.
The civil engineering and structural works shall be designed in accordance with accepted codes and standards of skill, care and diligence.
The seismic design, both for structural sizing and operating behavior, shall comply with the minimum seismic requirements provided in Exhibit B, Part 1.
The design, fabrication, testing, installation and fixing method of all steelwork including structural members, checker plate flooring, handrailing, ladders, open grid flooring and stair treads shall be in accordance with recognized codes and standards for steelwork.
The necessary signalizations shall be foreseen.
Stairs shall be preferred to ladders wherever possible. Where ladders are used they shall be installed only in areas where only occasional access is required or where space limitations so dictate."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_084,44,6.5.1.2._MECHANICAL_AND_ELECTRICAL_REQUIREMENTS,"6, 5, 1, 2",content,text,6.5._Air_Cooled_Condenser > 6.5.1.2._MECHANICAL_AND_ELECTRICAL_REQUIREMENTS,Air Cooled Condenser,Mechanical and Electrical Requirements,"Fans, motors and drive train",,,"forced draught fan per cell, electric motor driven fan, reduction gear and shaft drive, noise limits per environmental regulations (Exhibit B Part 1), emergency stop buttons within 5 m, key reset emergency stop, 3NC contacts 230VAC or 220VDC, TEFC motor (totally enclosed fan cooled), IP55 minimum, insulation Class F, 10% operating margin on motor power output",True,"Under “Air Cooled Condenser > Mechanical and Electrical Requirements”; specifies ACC cell forced draught fans, motors, gear/shaft drive, emergency stops, platforms/ladders for maintenance.",True,,high,False,"Provides concrete ACC fan/motor/gearbox electrical and mechanical requirements (IP rating, insulation class, E-stop details, margins) suitable for matching to equipment datasheets/specs and electrical design documents.","Rotating parts and relevant transmission components shall be designed to limit the noise according to the environmental regulations and as specified in Exhibit B-Part 1, whichever is more stringent.
Each cell shall have a motor driven forced draught fan driven by an electric motor, connected to the reduction gear by a shaft. Fan electric motors shall have half shrouded, stay put, emergency stop buttons located within 5m of the motor location. These buttons shall require a key to reset. 3NC contacts are required (230VAC or 220VDC insulated)
The electric motors shall be totally enclosed and fan cooled, fitted with lubricated antifriction bearings and moisture resistant insulation, designed for continuous outdoor service, with mechanical protection IP55 as a minimum, insulation Class F.
The motor and the reduction gear shall be designed in order to withstand the maximum electrical power with adequate margin.
Platform and ladders must be made to allow periodical check, lubrication of motor bearings and of gearbox bearings.
The motors will be selected taking into account the driven load characteristic; the motors will have at least a 10% operating margin on power output."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_085,45,6.6.1._Feedwater_pumps,"6, 6, 1",content,text,6.5._Air_Cooled_Condenser > 6.6.1._Feedwater_pumps,Feedwater System,Feedwater Pumps,"Pump protection, minimum flow recirculation and NPSH",,,"boiler feedwater pumps, maximum continuous rating water flow to each drum, 3% higher than HRSG drum safety valve setting pressure, suction and discharge isolation valves, non-return discharge valve, minimum flow recirculation to feedwater tank, severe service minimum flow valve, cavitation minimization, orifice/backpressure device to prevent flashing, suction strainer with manual blowdown, differential pressure indication, automatic pump changeover on high strainer DP alarm, vibration monitoring and protection, transient analysis for pump suction, NPSH maintained at all times and loads, corrosion resistant steel wetted parts, closed cycle cooling water system for pump cooling",False,Feedwater pumps requirements; no mention of ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or ACC interfaces.,True,,high,True,"Strong technical requirements for feedwater pumps (valving, recirc, NPSH/transient analysis, materials, monitoring). Needs review due to hierarchy_context showing '6.5 Air Cooled Condenser' while section is 6.6.1.","The feedwater pumps shall comply with the requirements of Exhibit B, Part 1.
The boiler feedwater pumps shall be capable of supplying the maximum continuous rating water flow to each drum, at a pressure 3 percent higher than the setting of HRSG drum safety valve.
Suction and discharge isolation valves and a non-return discharge valve shall be installed for each pump. Each pump shall be protected during periods of low flow with independent, minimum flow recirculation paths returning to the feedwater tank. The energy of the recirculation fluid shall be reduced through a pressure drop across the minimum flow valve and through an orifice/backpressure device located at the inlet to the feedwater tank. The minimum flow valve shall be designed for severe service, with provisions to minimize cavitation. The orifice/backpressure device shall prevent flashing in the recirculation piping downstream of the minimum flow valve.
Each pump shall be fitted with a suction strainer complete with manual blowdown. Strainers shall be equipped with commissioning and operating inserts. The strainer shall have differential pressure indication, with automatic pump changeover on high strainer differential pressure alarm.
Vibration monitoring and protection shall be provided.
A transient analysis shall be performed for the pump suction to ensure that the feedwater tank volume, elevation, suction line sizing, and layout maintain net positive suction head (NPSH) at all times and loads.
The pumps shall withstand any possible thermal shock without damage or leakage. The operating speed range of the pumps shall avoid all natural structural resonances. All parts of the pump in contact with feed water shall be corrosion resistant steel. Cooling of the feed water pumps and auxiliaries shall be provided by the closed cycle cooling water system."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_086,"45, 46",6.6.2._Condensate_system,"6, 6, 2",content,text,6.5._Air_Cooled_Condenser > 6.6.2._Condensate_system,Condensate System,Condensate Return and Cooling,FGP condensate return integration and fin fan cooler,,,"condensate return from ACCs, condensate return from FGP, CPP treated condensate, deaerator/feedwater tanks, water/steam cycle balancing, FGP return condensate temperature <100°C, cooling prior to CPP, Fin Fan Cooler, N+2 modules, module isolation without impacting full condensate flow, CPP inlet temperature requirement 60°C at design conditions, condensate storage tank flashing at atmospheric pressure, condensate storage connection before/after CPP away from normal path",True,Condensate system explicitly includes “return from the ACCs” and mixing of ACC condensate with FGP return; ACC condensate routing described.,True,,high,False,"Defines condensate system interfaces (ACC/FGP/CPP), temperature constraints, fin fan cooler redundancy (N+2), and storage tank arrangement—useful for P&IDs, equipment specs, and system design documents.","Refer to Exhibit B -Part 1.
The condensate system includes return from the ACCs and also from the FGP. Condensate return from the ACCs shall mix with FGP return condensate which has been treated in the CPP. The final condensate shall be directed to the deaerator/feedwater tanks (with consideration of balancing the water in each water/steam cycle if necessary).
FGP return condensate is at high temperature (<100°C) and must be cooled prior to entering the CPP. A Fin Fan Cooler shall be considered with N+2 modules where any one module can be isolated for repair without impacting the full condensate flow. The fin fan cooler shall be designed to meet CPP inlet temperature requirements of 60 °Cat design conditions.
Because the FGP condensate return temperature is <100°C, it is not possible to consider the condensate storage tank as part of the normal operating cycle as the water will flash if it goes to atmospheric pressure. Condensate storage tank should therefore be connected either before or after the CPP where the condensate temperature is low. Additionally; condensate storage should be connected away from the normal path of the condensate.
The condensate coming from the ACC and from the condensate polishing system shall be directed to the deaerator/feedwater tanks (with consideration of balancing the water in each water/steam cycle if necessary)."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_087,46,6.6.2._Condensate_system,,content,text,6.5._Air_Cooled_Condenser > 6.6.2._Condensate_system,Cooling Water System,Closed Circuit Cooling Water,Cooling loads and scope statement,,,"closed circuit cooling water system, remove heat dissipated by plant components, cooling water will not be provided, gas turbine generators cooling requirements, sampling panels cooling, lube oil cooling, HRSG blowdown cooling (if necessary), miscellaneous cooling loads",False,Generic closed circuit cooling water system for GT generators/sampling/lube oil/HRSG blowdown; no ACC signal.,True,,medium,True,"Contains a clear requirement statement about a closed circuit cooling water system and its loads, but placement under 'Condensate system' may be a boundary/structuring issue.","The closed circuit cooling water system shall be provided to remove the heat dissipated by Plant components. Cooling water will not be provided. This system shall be used to meet cooling requirements associated with the gas turbine generators, as well as sampling panels, lube oil, HRSG blowdown (if necessary) and other miscellaneous loads."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_088,"46, 47",6.6.2._Condensate_system,,list,"list_item, text",6.5._Air_Cooled_Condenser > 6.6.2._Condensate_system,Air Cooled Condenser,Condensate system,Closed circuit cooling water system,,,"air-to-water fin fan coolers, redundant fans (n+2), cooling water pumps (2x100% or 3x50%), closed cooling water head/storage tank, chemical dosing system (corrosion inhibition), maximum continuous rating (MCR) operation, demin water makeup pump, automatic filling during black start, system pressure and volume control",True,"Depth_1 is “Air Cooled Condenser” and discusses cooling via fin fan coolers, pumps, head/storage tank—ACC-context section metadata indicates relevance.",True,,high,False,"Defines closed-circuit cooling water system configuration, redundancy philosophy, makeup water/treatment, and operating/design requirements useful for matching to cooling water/fin fan cooler/pump/tank/dosing system engineering documents.","- cooling of the closed circuit system shall be through air to water fin fan coolers (total number (n) of fans to be determined during detail design however; the design shall be inclusive of two additional redundant fans (i.e. n+2) including all margins.)
- two (2) x 100% or three (3) x 50% capacity cooling water pumps. These pumps will circulate cooling water from the fin fan heat exchangers to various locations and back to the heat exchangers.
- one (1) closed cooling water head/storage tank to provide makeup water and be capable of performing both functions of a head tank and storage/surge tank.)
- a chemical dosing system, including chemical addition tank to inhibit corrosion in the closed cooling water system
The cooling system shall be designed in accordance with relevant international codes for Fin Fan Coolers for operation of the plant at maximum continuous rating for the entire ambient temperature range specified in Exhibit B, Part 1.
Demin water pump shall be used as makeup water for filling the closed circuit cooling water system and for covering losses. If Feedwater is used, the necessary treatment facilities shall be provided by the Contractor. Contractor shall consider automatic filling method in case of requirement during black start.
The system shall be capable of providing the required system pressure and volume control."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_089,"47, 48",6.8.1._Fuel_Gas_systems,"6, 8, 1",content,"text, list_item",6.5._Air_Cooled_Condenser > 6.8.1._Fuel_Gas_systems,Fuel Gas Systems,Gas supply systems,Fuel gas supply to GT/HRSG/Reserve Boiler,,,"Sales gas and Low BTU gas firing, Delivery Point (Saudi Aramco supply), isolating valve and emergency stop valve (CCR closure), compressor section (3x33% + 1x33% hot standby), gas coolers (2x50% + bypass), filtering section (3x50% or 2x100%), fuel gas heating section (heat exchangers or water bath heaters), dew point heater (1x100%) for start-up, pressure reducing station (slam shut, active/monitor regulators), gas vent stack (cold vent stack or flare system), separate gas metering for HRSG supplementary firing and Reserve Boilers, gas backup metering facility",False,"Fuel gas supply system scope (valves, compressors, metering, heaters); no ACC-related terms.",True,,high,False,"Provides detailed scope and equipment lineup for fuel gas supply/conditioning/metering/pressure control to GT/HRSG/Reserve Boiler, including redundancy and control/ESD interfaces—strong anchors for retrieving fuel gas system P&IDs, equipment datasheets, and control narratives.","The Plant shall be designed to fire both Sales gas and Low BTU gas ('Gas'). Both of these gases will be supplied by Saudi Aramco at the Delivery Point as per the Fuel gas quality specifications and as per the conditions specified in Exhibit B, Part 1.
The scope of work for each of the Gas supply systems includes, but is not limited to, the following:
- on the inlet pipe, one isolating valve (manual) + one emergency stop valve which can be closed from the CCR as per MFS (Exhibit B, Part 1 of)
- if required by the gas turbines, one compressors section (3 x 33 % plus 1 x 33 % in hot stand-by Two gas coolers (2 x 50% ; + by-pass) used to avoid too warm gas during the start-up or low load operation (if necessary - to be examined)
- filtering section (3 x 50% (or) 2 x 100% filters)
- metering sections compliant with the (Exhibit B - Part 1)
- fuel gas heating sections (3 x 50% (or) 2 x 100% heat exchangers or water bath heaters - if necessary)
- if required; dew point heater (1 x 100%) to be used only for start-up
- necessary piping including piping between the Delivery Point and the GT/HRSG/Reserve Boiler
- gas pressure and temperature control facilities for all gas feed points as required
- pressure reducing sections with 3 x 50% or 2 x 100% lines; each line consists of one manual isolating valve, one gas operated slam shut valve, one gas operated active pressure reducing valve, one gas operated monitor pressure reducing valve, one check valve, one manual isolating valve. If necessary a smaller line shall be installed for start-up or low load.
- gas conditioning and filtering facilities
- separate Gas metering facilities for Gas supplied to HRSGs for supplementary firing and Reserve Boilers
- gas back-up metering facility
The Contractor shall supply all equipment, valves & fittings, controls, piping, drain collection and disposal, gas vent stack (cold vent stack or flare system), vents etc. as necessary to make the fuel gas supply system complete and functional.
The total Gas flow will be measured by the Gas Metering System to be provided by the Contractor.
Separate Gas meters shall be supplied by the Contractor to measure the Gas consumed for supplementary firing on HRSG and for Reserve Boilers."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_090,"48, 49",6.8.1._Fuel_Gas_systems,"6, 8, 1",content,text,6.5._Air_Cooled_Condenser > 6.8.1._Fuel_Gas_systems,Fuel Gas Systems,Gas conditioning and pressure control,"Filtration, separation, heating and control stations",,,"gas conditioning and filtering skid, ASME VIII filters/separators, solid filtration 3 µm @ 100% and 0.5 µm @ 99.5%, liquid filtration 3 µm @ 100% and 0.3 µm @ 99.5%, fuel gas condensate drain tank (truck-loaded disposal), level indicating transmitters and switches to control room, double seat ball valves (isolation), pneumatically operated control valves (pressure control station), high-pressure shutoff valve (minimize venting), triple redundant pressure transmitters, ESD valves upstream, extended capacity strainer with differential pressure alarm to CCR via DCMS, Modified Wobbe Index (MWI) control +/- 5%, scrubbers and downstream scrubber after heaters (moisture carryover protection), coalescent final filters (2x100%) per GT manufacturer, stainless steel piping downstream of last scrubber (if recommended)",False,Fuel gas conditioning/filtration/heating and control stations; no ACC-related signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains specific technical performance/standard requirements (ASME VIII, micron ratings, MWI +/-5%), instrumentation/control features (triple redundant PTs, DP alarm to DCMS), and equipment/drain routing—high-value retrieval signals for fuel gas conditioning skid, pressure control station, and I&C documents.","The Gas conditioning and filtering skid shall include carbon steel filters/separators installed downstream of Plant Gas Delivery Point. The filters/separators shall be fabricated per ASME VIII to remove solid particles of micron size 3µ with an efficiency of 100% filtrate rate and 0.5µ at 99.5% or better and liquid particles of micron size 3µ with an efficiency of 100% filtrate rate and 0.3µ at 99.5% or better, to the requirements of the gas turbine equipment supplier. Liquid drain from the filter separators shall be routed to the fuel gas condensate drain tank from where it will be truckloaded out for disposal. Level-indicating transmitters and switches shall provide level indication to the control room operator. The isolation valves for filter separators shall be double seat ball valves.
Gas pressure control stations shall be provided for each of the Plant power and steam Unit. The Gas pressure to the Plant power and steam units shall be controlled within the appropriate operating range via pneumatically operated control valves. The pressure control station shall be provided with necessary control valves. A pneumatically operated highpressure shutoff valve will also be provided to close upon high downstream Gas pressure and will minimize the amount of Gas vented to atmosphere when the safety relief valve lifts. Triple redundant pressure transmitters shall be provided downstream of the regulating station to provide a highly reliable Gas pressure control system that will minimize unnecessary turbine trips. The Gas System shall be provided with ESD valves upstream to the fuel gas system.
Extended capacity strainer shall be provided to remove impurities from the Gas. A pressure switch shall monitor the differential across the strainers will signal an alarm to the central control room via DCMS for the pressure drop across the strainer indicates cleaning is required.
If required, Gas heaters and heating water boosting pumps shall be provided in order to control Gas temperature to maintain variation of Modified Wobbe Index (MWI) within +/- 5% to enable the gas turbines to operate at full load for both the Gas qualities within the specified ranges in Exhibit B, Part 1.
Scrubbers shall be provided to remove liquid inside fuel gas. In case the gas pressure is lower than heating liquid, scrubbers will also be installed downstream to the fuel gas heaters to prevent any moisture carry over from any tube leak in fuel gas heater. Liquid drain from the scrubber shall be routed to a fuel gas scrubber condensate drain tanks from where it will be truck-loaded out for disposal.
For each gas turbine, 2 coalescent final filters (2 x 100%) shall be foreseen (if required by the gas turbine manufacturer).
Piping after last scrubber to gas turbine shall be of stainless steel, if recommended by the manufacturer."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_091,49,6.8.2._Additional_requirements,"6, 8, 2",content,"text, list_item",6.5._Air_Cooled_Condenser > 6.8.2._Additional_requirements,Fuel Gas Systems,Fuel gas quality testing,Gas analysis and buffer facilities,,,"fuel gas quality testing facilities, calorific heating value (HHV and LHV), on-line gas composition analysis, gas buffer station",False,Fuel gas quality testing facilities; no ACC mention.,True,,medium,False,"Specifies required fuel gas quality testing/analysis functions and mentions buffer station, which can map to existing gas chromatograph/analyzer and fuel gas quality monitoring documentation.","The Contractor shall provide the facilities required to test the quality of the fuel gas including:
- quality: calorific heating value (higher and lower)
- on-line gas composition analysis
- gas buffer station"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_092,49,6.8.3._Gas_Buffer_Station,"6, 8, 3",content,text,6.5._Air_Cooled_Condenser > 6.8.3._Gas_Buffer_Station,Fuel Gas Systems,Gas Buffer Station,Fuel changeover (Low BTU to Sales gas),,,"gas buffer station sizing, sudden interruption of Low BTU gas supply, smooth fuel changeover to Sales gas, Wobbe Index analysis upstream of buffer station, gas turbine control system reaction time, proven design, harsh climatic conditions, working temperatures and pressures, vessel class suitable for application, Exhibit B Part 1 requirements",False,Gas buffer station requirements for fuel changeover; no ACC-related signal.,True,,high,False,"Defines functional purpose and sizing/analysis basis (Wobbe Index, changeover dynamics) and vessel suitability requirements for the gas buffer station—useful for retrieving buffer vessel datasheets, sizing calculations, and control philosophy documents.","The gas buffer station shall satisfy the requirements as defined in Exhibit B, Part 1.
Generally, if there is a sudden interruption of Low BTU gas supply or sudden gas pressure reduction, the gas buffer station shall enable a smooth fuel changeover from Low BTU to Sales gas.
Incoming gas shall be analysed to determine the Wobbe Index upstream of the buffer station to identify the characteristics of the gas. The buffer station shall be sufficiently sized to allow the gas turbine control system sufficient time to react to the fuel quality changes and therefore enable the smooth fuel changeover from Low BTU to Sales gas.
The buffer station shall be of a proven design and suitable for the harsh climatic conditions, the working temperatures and pressures of the gas. The vessel class shall be suitable for the application."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_093,50,6.9._Demineralization_plant_/_distribution,"6, 9",content,text,6.9._Demineralization_plant_/_distribution,Water Treatment,Demineralization Plant,Ion Exchange and Regeneration,,,"Ion Exchangers alternative, VGB R 450 L product water quality, minimum time between regenerations 24 hours, regeneration time max 4 hours, common regeneration equipment for all trains, acid storage tanks, caustic storage tanks, neutralization pit capacity 1.5x regeneration effluent volume, demineralised water storage 24 hours (two tanks), tank vent CO2 trap, replaceable air filter, chemical storage capacity 14 days max consumption or 1.5x unloading tanker capacity, bund wall 110% of tank volume",False,Demineralization plant/ion exchange and chemical storage requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention or interface.,True,,high,False,"Contains specific technical requirements for demineralisation plant quality standard, regeneration cycle/timing, storage capacities, vent CO2 traps, and chemical storage/bunding.","Refer to Volume 1, section 4.4.10 of Exhibit B, Part 1 with consideration of the Ion Exchangers alternative.
The quality of the product water from the demineralisation plant shall be according to VGB R 450 L.
The minimum time between regenerations shall be 24 hours at full demineralization plant output while the regeneration time does not exceed 4 hours. The regeneration equipment is common for all trains (acid and caustic storage tanks, pumps, neutralization pit with a capacity equal to 1.5 times the regeneration effluent volume).
The demineralisation plant shall have at least a 24 hours storage capacity (two tanks).
Each tank vent is equipped with a CO2 trap with an air filter of a replaceable type (if necessary - to be finalized during detailed design).
Chemicals storage capacity related to demineralisation shall be 14 days of maximal consumption or 1.5 times the unloading tanker capacity. Tanks shall be equipped with bund wall for a volume equal to 110% of the tank."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_095,"50, 51",6.11._Chemical_dosing_system_for_water/steam_cycle,"6, 11",table,"text, list_item",6.11._Chemical_dosing_system_for_water/steam_cycle,Water/Steam Cycle Chemistry,Chemical Dosing System,Boiler and Feedwater Chemistry Control,,,"HRSG water chemistry parameters, ammonia (NH3) dosing, oxygen scavenging chemical (during malfunction), trisodiumphosphate (Na3PO4) dosing, fully automatic dosing for ammonia and oxygen scavenging, manual trisodiumphosphate dosing with on-line phosphate analyzer, IEC/TS 61370 steam quality parameters, conditioning of boiler within 30 minutes, 2 x 100% chemical dosing pumps, 1 chemical dosing tank per system, dosing tank working capacity 4 days per additive, AISI 316L (1.4404) dosing pump casing, AISI 316L injection pipes and valves, bulk storage capacity 7 days at full load, bulk storage sized for full standard road tanker plus 4 days, bunds and segregated chemical drains, ventilation and emergency eye wash/deluge shower",False,Chemical dosing system for HRSG water/steam cycle chemistry; no ACC-related equipment or references.,True,,high,False,"Defines chemicals, automation, capacities, materials, redundancy, standards, and ancillary safety/drain/ventilation requirements useful for matching to existing water/steam chemistry and dosing system documents.","The Plant shall be equipped with chemical dosing systems to control the boiler and Feedwater chemistry. The chemical dosing system to control the boiler and Feedwater chemistry will be operated based on chemistry parameters of HRSG water. Since the steam will be used in Saudi Aramco processing facilities, the chemical dosing agents chosen have to be agreed with Saudi Aramco. However, the following shall be considered as preliminary:
- ammonia NH3
- Oxygen scavenging chemical, only during malfunction
- trisodiumphosphate Na3 PO4
The Contractor may propose also other chemicals if he can demonstrate long term economical, successful and safe utilization in other plants. A fully automatic chemical dosing system shall be provided for ammonia and oxygen scavenging. For trisodiumphosphate, manual dosing is acceptable as long as on-line phosphate analyzer is implemented .
The operational parameters of quality of the steam leaving each HRSG shall meet the parameters specified in IEC/TS 61370. The make-up water quality shall be produced accordingly.
All chemical dosing systems shall be supplied and provided as complete systems.
Each injection shall allow the conditioning of the boiler within 30 minutes and maintain sufficient excess of the chemicals in the steam drum.
Each chemical dosing system shall comprise all equipment for the safe, reliable and efficient operation of the dosing systems, including bunds, segregated chemical drains, appropriate ventilation and emergency eye wash and deluge shower units.
Each chemical dosing system shall consist of two (2) x 100% chemical dosing pumps and one (1) chemical dosing tank.
The chemical dosing tank shall combine solution mixing and preparation and shall be made of stainless steel or synthetic material. It shall have a working capacity of 4 days of continuous use for each additive. The tank shall be provided with electric agitator as necessary.
The dosing pumps material shall be AISI 316L (1.4404) for the casing. Two (2) pumps per chemical shall be provided.
Injection pipes and valves are made of stainless steel AISI 316L (1.4404).
Bulk storage for each chemical shall be provided with a storage capacity of at least 7 days with the Plant at full load. For chemicals in liquid or gaseous state bulk storage tanks sized to unload a full standard road tanker shall be provided Plus 4 day's storage capacity. Required unloading arrangements and interconnections for auto transfer between the storage tanks and dosing tanks shall also be provided.
- scale Control -
Phosphate
- pH Control -
Ammonia"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_096,52,6.12._Sampling_system_for_water/steam_cycle,"6, 12",content,"text, list_item",6.12._Sampling_system_for_water/steam_cycle,Water/Steam Cycle Chemistry,Sampling and Analysis,Continuous Sampling System (HRSG steam and water circuit),,,"continuous extraction and monitoring of HRSG steam and water samples, sample cooler for each circuit, sample locations and analysers for chemistry limits, analyser measurements recorded on DCMS, manual sampling facility for each sample, feedwater analysers: dissolved oxygen (DO), pH, specific conductivity, cation conductivity meter, silica analyser, main steam analysers: pH, specific cation conductivity, silica analyser, degassed cation conductivity meter, sodium, saturated steam (drum) analysers: specific cation conductivity, pH analyser, degassed cation conductivity meter, drum water analysers: pH, specific conductivity, phosphate analyser (HRSG drum/blowdown), central sampling rack close to laboratory, continuous and intermittent sampling (lab analysis for intermittent)",False,Sampling/analysis system for HRSG steam and water circuit; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,False,"Provides detailed sampling system scope, required analysers, and DCMS recording interface—strong anchors for retrieving sampling rack/analyser and water chemistry documents.","The Plant shall be equipped with a system for the continuous extraction and monitoring of selected samples from the HRSG steam and water circuit. A sample cooler shall be provided for each circuit. The selection of sample locations and analysers shall ensure adequate observation and control of steam and water chemistry limits. The measurements from the analysers shall be recorded on the power station DCMS. A manual sampling facility shall be provided for each sample. As a minimum, the following analysers shall be provided:
- Feed water:
- -DO (Dissolved Oxygen),
- -pH & Specific Conductivity,
- -cation Conductivity meter,
- -silica Analyser
- Main steam
- -pH & specific cation conductivity
- -silica Analyser
- -degassed cation conductivity meter and sodium
- Saturated steam (drum)
- -specific cation conductivity
- -pH Analyser
- -degassed Cation Conductivity meter
- Drum water
- -pH & Specific Conductivity
- -phosphate analyser (HRSG drum sample (Blowdown water))
The sampling system shall control the water-steam-cycle by providing samples from different points of the process. Samples will be taken both continuously and intermittently. The samples to be taken continuously shall be received, cooled, analysed and values displayed and transmitted to the DCMS. Intermittent samples will be analysed in the laboratory.
The samples with coolers shall be collected to one location on a central sampling rack close to the laboratory."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_098,53,6.14.1._General_requirements,"6, 14, 1",content,"text, list_item",6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.14.1._General_requirements,Fire Protection,Fire Water and Fire Fighting Systems,General Requirements and Codes,,,"Saudi Aramco Standard SAES-B-017, NFPA requirements/guidelines, fire water storage minimum 2 hours, fire fighting pumping station, main fire water pump electric motor driven, main fire water pump diesel engine driven, jockey pumps, underground main loop encircling the Plant, hydrants and hose reel stations, sprinkler/deluge systems, dry end pre-action sprinkler on turbine and generator bearings, foam systems on oil and diesel storage equipment, sprinkler systems on main and auxiliary transformers, fixed CO2 systems, gas detection systems, fire alarm systems, smoke ventilation, wet rising mains, clean agent suppression systems, NFPA 2001, NFPA 850",False,"Fire protection general requirements (SAES/NFPA, fire water pumps, hydrants, sprinklers); no ACC mention.",True,,high,False,"Contains explicit fire protection system scope, equipment, and governing standards suitable for matching to fire water, sprinkler/deluge, detection/alarm, and suppression design documents.","For technical requirements refer to Exhibit B, Part 1.
The fire protection and fire-fighting system for the Plant shall be designed in compliance with Saudi Aramco Standard SAES-B-017 and NFPA requirements/guidelines. In case of discrepancy the Saudi Aramco requirements prevail on any other document.
The fire protection and fire fighting system shall include, among others
- Water storage as required by NFPA but not less than two hours
- one (1) Fire fighting pumping station comprising of at least one (1) main fire water pump (electrical motor), one (1) main fire water pump (Diesel engine driven), jockey pumps, accumulators (as applicable) piping and valves
- underground main loop encircling the Plant and hydrants and hose rail stations inside installations
- sprinkler / deluge systems according to local regulations
- dry end pre-action type sprinkler on turbine and generator bearings
- leak confinement and foam systems on oil and diesel storage equipment
- sprinkler systems on main and auxiliary transformers
- the necessary mobile/portable extinguishers
- separate fire compartments in the buildings
- fire endurance of building bearing structures and stair cases
- fixed wet pipe sprinkler systems
- fixed deluge systems
- fixed CO2-systems
- gas detection systems
- fire alarm systems
- smoke ventilation
- hydrants and hose reels
- wet rising mains
- portable fire extinguishers
Clean agent suppression systems required for critical areas shall be designed in accordance with NFPA 2001 and NFPA 850. Central bank system or Independent systems will be confirmed during detail design and are subject to the approval of the Owner."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_099,54,6.14.2._Separate_Fire_Compartments,"6, 14, 2",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.14.2._Separate_Fire_Compartments,Fire Protection,Fire Compartmentation and Life Safety,"Fire Barriers, Penetration Seals, and Means of Egress",,,"fire compartments, fire barriers, penetration seals in walls and floors, two exit routes from exit areas, exit stairways, emergency and exit lighting, panic bars, exit marking",False,Fire compartmentation and egress requirements; no ACC-related context.,True,,medium,False,Defines compartmentation and egress technical requirements that can map to architectural/fire safety and building layout documents.,"The Plant shall be divided into fire compartments. Special attention shall be paid to fire barriers and penetration seals in separating walls and floors all over the Plant.
From every exit areas there will be two exit routes to the exit stairways or directly outdoor or to another fire compartment.
Exit routes will be provided with emergency and exit lighting and panic bars. The exits and the access to them shall be marked."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_100,54,6.14.3._Fixed_Wet_Pipe_Sprinkler_Systems,"6, 14, 3",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.14.3._Fixed_Wet_Pipe_Sprinkler_Systems,Fire Protection,Sprinkler Systems,Fixed Wet Pipe Sprinkler Systems,,,"sprinkler opening temperature, system valve position indicator, alarm annunciation, control room indication, cable spreading rooms, cable tunnels, NFPA 850 clause 7.8.2.1, automatic sprinkler systems",False,Fixed wet pipe sprinkler systems for cable rooms/tunnels; no ACC signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains specific fire protection system requirements (wet pipe sprinklers), alarm/indication interfaces to control room, and NFPA 850 reference for cable areas.","When the temperature of fire exceeds the opening temperature of the sprinklers they shall open and spray water to extinguish the fire.
System valve position indicator shall be installed for alarm annunciation in the control room to indicate system operation.
Cable spreading rooms and cable tunnels shall be protected as per NFPA850 clause 7.8.2.1 with automatic sprinklers systems."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_101,54,6.14.4._Fixed_Deluge_Systems,"6, 14, 4",content,"text, list_item",6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.14.4._Fixed_Deluge_Systems,Fire Protection,Water Spray Systems,Fixed Deluge Systems,,,"deluge system, lubricating oil system, steam turbine bearings protection, main transformers, auxiliary transformers, open spray nozzles, deluge valve, manual by-pass valve, fusible plug detection, EFD (Electrical Fire Detector), SAES, pressure switches, alarm indications, control room",False,"Fixed deluge systems for lube oil system, turbine bearings, transformers; no ACC mention.",True,,high,False,"Defines deluge fire protection for specific plant areas/equipment, describes system configuration (open nozzles, deluge valve, bypass), detection methods, and control room alarm interfaces.","A fixed deluge system shall be installed for the following areas:
- lubricating oil system (if not protected by CO2 system)
- steam turbine bearings protection shall be proposed, automatic but with operator local manual remote activation
- main transformers
- auxiliary transformers
A deluge system employs open spray nozzles connected via a piping system to water supply mains through a deluge valve. The system shall be equipped with a manual by-pass valve located on a safe distance.
Detection shall be achieved by fusible plug or EFD (Electrical Fire Detector) in accordance with SAES. Other means may be approved by the Chief Fire Prevention Engineer.
Pressure switches shall be installed for alarm indications in the control room."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_102,"54, 55",6.14.5._Fixed_CO2_Extinguishing_System,"6, 14, 5",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.14.5._Fixed_CO2_Extinguishing_System,Fire Protection,Gaseous Extinguishing Systems,Fixed CO2 Extinguishing System,Gas Turbine compartment,,"GT compartment, dedicated fire detection, CO2 systems (Carbon Dioxide), automatic operation on fire detection, audible and visual indication, control room indication, local area indication",False,Fixed CO2 extinguishing system for gas turbine compartment; no ACC-related content.,True,,high,False,Provides equipment-area-specific requirement for dedicated CO2 and detection in GT compartment with automatic actuation and alarm indications.,"The GT compartment shall be provided with its own dedicated fire detection and CO2-systems.
CO2-systems shall operate automatically when detectors indicate fire.
In the event of alarm activating, an audible and visual indication shall be provided in the control room and in the concerned area."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_103,55,6.14.6._Gas_Detection_System_for_Natural_Gas,"6, 14, 6",content,"text, list_item",6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.14.6._Gas_Detection_System_for_Natural_Gas,Safety Systems,Gas Detection,Natural Gas Detection System,,,"hazardous areas monitoring, GT enclosure, gas pipe routes, pressure reduction station, LEL (Lower Explosive Limit) alarm levels, low LEL alarm increases ventilation rate, coincident high LEL alarm (two or more detectors), CCGT plant shutdown, gas supplies isolated, signals to control room (faulty/deactivated/first level/second level), uninterrupted electric power supply",False,Natural gas detection system for hazardous areas/GT enclosure; no ACC reference.,True,,high,False,"Contains clear technical requirements for natural gas detection coverage, alarm setpoint logic/actions (ventilation increase, shutdown/isolation), control room signals, and power supply continuity.","The hazardous areas (all the spaces where gas may be accumulated with particular attention to the GT enclosure as well as like areas of gas pipe routes and the pressure reduction station) shall be monitored by a gas detection system.
The gas detection system shall cause various alarms and controlling measures to be initiated at detected levels of LEL (Lower Explosive Limit - to be demonstrated by the Contractor and agreed with the Owner during the detailed design stage).
A low LEL alarm shall cause the ventilation rate of respective area to be increased to raise level to provide maximum dilution of the detected leak. A coincident high LEL alarm from two or more detectors shall cause the CCGT Plant to be shut down and gas supplies to be isolated.
The following signals shall be transmitted to the control room:
- equipment faulty
- equipment deactivated
- the first level alarm
- the second level alarm
The system shall have an uninterrupted electric power supply."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_104,55,6.14.7._Fire_Detection_Systems,"6, 14, 7",content,"text, list_item",6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.14.7._Fire_Detection_Systems,Fire Protection,Fire Detection and Alarm,Fire Detection Systems,,,"automatic detectors (type according to risk), manual call points, alarm bells, remote alarm annunciation, admin facilities, control and electrical building, equipment rooms, power block buildings, water treatment plant offices, workshop and warehouse, fire alarm/control panel, control room system control panel, audible and visual indication",False,Fire detection systems for buildings and facilities; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,False,"Specifies fire detection/alarm system components, coverage areas/buildings, and control room panel/annunciation requirements—useful anchors for matching fire & gas / F&G documents.","Fire detection system shall be installed to provide a high level of protection to both personnel and equipment. The system shall comprise automatic detectors (type according to the risk), manual call points, alarm bells, remote alarm annunciation and detection systems shall be installed for the following areas:
- admin facilities
- control and electrical building and all equipment rooms
- power block buildings
- water treatment plant offices, workshop and warehouse fire alarm/control panel.
Other facilities and outdoor areas shall be provided with manual call points.
In the event of a fire alarm being activated, an audible and visual indication shall be provided. System control panel shall be provided in the control room."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_105,56,6.14.8._Smoke_Ventilation,"6, 14, 8",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.14.8._Smoke_Ventilation,Fire Protection,Smoke Ventilation,Smoke vents and smoke exhaust fans,,,"roof and wall openings, windows and hatches, smoke vents, smoke exhaust fans, automatic operation by elevated temperature (fire), manual opening, remote control panel, smoke vent area ~1% of floor area",False,Fire protection smoke ventilation requirements; no mention of ACC/air cooled condenser or related equipment.,True,,high,False,Contains specific smoke ventilation design/operation requirements including control mode and vent area sizing basis.,"Depending on the case, roof and wall openings, windows and hatches as well as specially designed smoke vents and fans may be used.
Specially designed smoke vents shall be operated automatically by the elevated temperatures generated during a fire. They shall also be opened manually and from a remote control panel.
The total area of the smoke vents shall be approximately 1% from the bottom area of each smoke-ventilated space."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_106,56,6.14.9._External_Hydrants_and_Hose_Reels,"6, 14, 9",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.14.9._External_Hydrants_and_Hose_Reels,Fire Protection,Fire Water Distribution,External hydrants and hose reels,,,"hose reels ground floor levels, hose reels at floor and service platform levels, outside each protected stairwell, served by site fire water main, pressure at high level, external hydrants positioned around the plant, local regulations, mandatory norms and standards",False,External hydrants and hose reels/fire water distribution; no ACC-related signal.,True,,medium,False,Defines placement and supply basis for hose reels and external hydrants tied to fire water main and regulatory compliance.,"Hose reels for the ground floor levels shall be located in accordance to mandatory norms and standards.
Hose reels shall be installed at floor and service platform levels (outside each protected stairwell) throughout the height of all building in accordance to mandatory norms and standards. These hose reels shall be served by the site fire water main in order to provide the necessary pressure at high level to ensure acceptable operation.
External hydrants shall be positioned around the Plant in accordance with local regulations."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_107,56,6.14.10._Portable_Fire_Extinguishers,"6, 14, 10",content,"text, list_item",6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.14.10._Portable_Fire_Extinguishers,Fire Protection,Portable Fire Extinguishers,Extinguisher types and coverage,,,"NFPA compliance, local fire regulation, dry powder extinguishers, CO2 (Carbon Dioxide) extinguishers, water/foam extinguishers, portable foam unit, one per GT (Gas Turbine) area, risk-based selection",False,Portable fire extinguishers for plant/GT areas; no ACC mention or ACC-specific context.,True,,medium,False,Provides fire protection requirements referencing NFPA and specifies extinguisher types and minimum foam unit provision for GT areas.,"Fire extinguishers of an appropriate type shall be located throughout the areas as required by NFPA and local fire regulation.
The type of extinguishers will be determined according to the risk. The following types can be used:
- dry powder
- CO2
- water/foam
At least one portable foam unit shall be provided for each GT area."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_108,57,6.15.1._Emergency/black_start_diesel_generator,"6, 15, 1",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.15.1._Emergency/black_start_diesel_generator,Electrical Systems,Emergency Power,Emergency / black start diesel generator,,,"safe shutdown without grid power, black start capability, diesel engine coupled to generator, common steel base, integral fuel tank, self-contained enclosure, fast start automatic on normal power failure, essential loads supply, GT start-up to grid synchronization and minimum load, local control panel logic and interlocks, DCMS monitoring and start/stop, PLC control, automatic transfer switch, IP23DW minimum protection, terminal box IP54, installed indoors, class F insulation, class B temperature rise",False,Emergency/black start diesel generator electrical requirements; no ACC-related terms.,True,,high,False,"Contains clear functional scope (safe shutdown/black start), control interfaces (PLC/DCMS/ATS), and explicit technical ratings (IP classes, insulation/temperature rise).","Combined emergency/black start diesel generator(s) may be provided for safe shutdown and start-up of the plant without grid power. The units shall consist of a diesel engine directly coupled to generator, mounted on common steel base with a fuel tank and a self-contained enclosure. In case of failure of normal power supply, the emergency/black start diesel generator shall fast start automatically to supply power to the essential loads of the plant which are to be run to take the plant to a safe shutdown condition. Later, the emergency/black start diesel generator shall cater to the start-up of the GT up to grid synchronization and minimum load.
All operational logic and interlocks for the generators shall be provided in the local control panel and DCMS. The generators will be controlled by a PLC (local control panel). Additionally, each generator shall also be able to start / stop and be feedback monitored from the DCMS. The power from the generators is fed to the switchgear through an automatic transfer switch.
The diesel generators shall be of minimum IP Protection class IP 23DW and terminal box enclosure should be class IP 54. The diesel generator shall be installed indoors.
Emergency diesel generators shall be designed for class F insulation and shall be operated with class B temperature rise."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_109,"57, 58",6.15.1._Emergency/black_start_diesel_generator,,content,"text, list_item",6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.15.1._Emergency/black_start_diesel_generator,Mechanical Utilities,Compressed Air,Instrument air and service air system,,,"instrument air absolute priority, plant service air distribution loops, technically oil free air, DIN ISO 8573-1, ISA-S7.0.01-1996, redundancy 3 x 100% air compressors, 2 x 100% air chillers, 2 x 100% air dryers, air filters, air receiver 2 x 100%, interconnection upstream of air dryers, stainless steel distribution bus main, general service air, control and instrumentation air, compressor intake particles ≤3 microns, solid particles ≤1 micron, oil content ≤1 ppm w/w at 20C and line pressure, final dew point at line pressure lower of -10C or 10K below lowest ambient",False,Instrument/service air compressed air system specification; no ACC scope signal.,True,,high,True,"Strong technical compressed-air requirements with standards, redundancy, and air quality limits; however section labeling suggests possible boundary/placement issue (diesel generator vs compressed air).","Instrument air has an absolute priority and any perturbation / problem in the service air part of the system may not have any influence on the instrument air part of the system.
Complete Plant service air system for maintenance with sufficient distribution loops (e.g. at different levels of the Plant equipment).
Compressed air systems supplying technically oil free air to DIN ISO 8573-1 shall be provided, and sized to provide a reliable supply of instrument and service air as applicable in the correct quantity and conditions for the various users in each area. The quality of air shall be in accordance with ISA-S7.0.01-1996
The equipment shall be designed with appropriate redundancy and shall consist of 3 x 100% heavy duty air compressors (100% means normal maximum air consumption for plant), 2 x 100% air chillers, 2 x 100% air dryers, air filters, air receiver (2 x 100%), control system and pipework distribution system. Each control air receiver shall have sufficient capacity to enable the Plant to be shut down in a safe and controllable manner and enable standby compressor plant to come into operational service without reduction in air supply. An interconnection shall be provided between the instrument and service air pipelines upstream of the air dryers on each system. The air distribution bus main shall be of stainless steel.
The compressed air systems shall include (i) General Service Air and (ii) Control and Instrumentation Air, configured to ensure a sustained supply of compressed air to the plant.
The control and instrumentation air quality shall meet the following requirements:
- compressor intakes to contain particles no greater than 3 microns
- the product air shall:
- -·contain no desiccant fines
- -·contain no solid particles greater than 1 micron
- -·contain no more than 1 ppm weight/weight of oil at 20C and line pressure
- -·have a final dew point at line pressure of the lower of -10C or 10K below the lowest ambient temperature"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_110,58,6.15.3._Nitrogen_system_/_preservation_of_the_Plant,"6, 15, 3",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.15.3._Nitrogen_system_/_preservation_of_the_Plant,Plant Preservation,Nitrogen System,Short-term and long-term standby preservation,Boilers,,"plant preservation against corrosion, short term standby preservation, long term standby provisions, rack-mounted high pressure portable cylinder sets, nitrogen distribution lines to boilers, manifold at ground level for bottle racks, extended out-of-service maintenance conservation, cylinder number and capacity determined during engineering",False,Nitrogen system for plant preservation/boilers; no ACC mention.,True,,high,False,"Defines a nitrogen preservation system concept and key configuration elements (cylinders, manifold location, distribution to boilers) useful for matching preservation/nitrogen system design documents.","The Plant shall be designed in such a manner that preservation of all parts is enabled without any danger of corrosion. The Contractor shall provide a system for the preservation of the Plant for a short term standby and provisions for a long term standby.
Rack mounted high pressure portable cylinders sets and distribution lines to the boilers for conservation during extended out-ofservice periods for maintenance. The manifold for the connection of the racks of bottles will be installed at ground level. The number and capacity of the cylinders will be determined during the engineering phase."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_111,"58, 59",6.15.4.1._MAIN_OVERHEAD_CRANE_FOR_TURBINES_AND_GENERATORS,"6, 15, 4, 1",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.15.3._Nitrogen_system_/_preservation_of_the_Plant > 6.15.4.1._MAIN_OVERHEAD_CRANE_FOR_TURBINES_AND_GENERATORS,Lifting and Hoisting Equipment,Overhead Travelling Crane,Turbine and Generator Maintenance Crane,Steam turbine generator units,,"electric overhead travelling crane, steam turbine generator maintenance, local norms and standards / equivalent international standard, traverse for lifting generator stator and rotor assemblies, lifting beam attached to main hook, steady creep speed control, radio remote control, warning alarm for long travel motion, longitudinal isolated gantry conductors, crane rails and holding down bolts, nameplate manufacturer/date/serial/rated capacity",False,Overhead travelling crane for steam turbine generator maintenance; no ACC-related equipment or references.,True,,high,False,"Contains technical requirements and features for the turbine/generator overhead crane (controls, traverse, conductors, alarms, rails) suitable for retrieving crane specifications/layouts.","The Contractor shall provide electric overhead travelling crane for the maintenance of the steam turbine generator units.
The crane shall be designed and constructed in accordance with the requirements of applicable mandatory local norms and standards or equivalent international standard and shall be complete in all respects for safe and efficient operation.
If required a traverse, to facilitate the separate lifting of generator stator and rotor assemblies and component parts, shall be supplied. The lifting beam shall be attached to the main hook of the crane.
The control of each crane motion will be arranged to incorporate a steady creep speed.
The crane shall be complete in every respect including access platforms, ladders, supports, protective covers, motors, control gear and wiring with main isolator and longitudinal isolated gantry conductors, collectors/supports, and longitudinal crane rails complete with holding down bolts and any other auxiliary apparatus incidental thereto for the safe and efficient operation of the crane.
A warning alarm shall be mounted on the electrically operated crane bridge in an easily accessible position. The alarm shall be initiated by the crane operator from the radio remote control to give warning of impending long travel motion.
The crane shall be fitted with a nameplate showing the manufacturer's name, the date of manufacture, a serial or identification number and the rated capacity of the crane."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_112,"59, 60",6.15.4.2._OTHER_LIFTING_/_HOISTING_EQUIPMENT,"6, 15, 4, 2",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.15.3._Nitrogen_system_/_preservation_of_the_Plant > 6.15.4.2._OTHER_LIFTING_/_HOISTING_EQUIPMENT,Lifting and Hoisting Equipment,Monorails and Hoists,Maintenance lifting provisions and sizing criteria,,,"turbine hall overhead travelling crane, boiler feed pump overhead monorail(s), lifting access bay hoist with motor operated trolley for boiler top, >10 tons motorized trolley and motorized hoist, 5 to 10 tons geared trolley and manual hoist, <5 tons overhead steel beam monorail or eye-plate, turbine building crane main and auxiliary hooks, AISC Standard impact loads from cranes, monorail beam impact 25% of lifted load, crane/trolley stops to prevent over travel",False,"General lifting/hoisting equipment (cranes, monorails) for turbine hall/boiler areas; no ACC signal.",True,,high,False,"Provides explicit lifting/hoisting equipment scope and quantitative thresholds (5/10 tons, 25% impact) and standards anchor (AISC) useful for matching lifting design documents.","Complete lifting equipment required for operation, maintenance, removal, and reinstallation of the equipment shall be designed, specified, procured, developed, fabricated, furnished, delivered, and installed.
Scope shall include turbine hall overhead travelling crane, and boiler feed pump overhead monorail(s) and the monorails in other areas required for the maintenance of the equipment, including but not limited to bridge, trolley, rail sweeps, motors, motor starters, gears, brakes, wheels, stops, bumpers, handrails, cables, wiring, panels, lighting, conductors, power feed connectors, pendant controls, radio controls, hoists, hooks, ropes, limit switches, etc., as needed to make a complete system.
The lifting access bay in the boiler structure servicing the boiler structure floors shall be provided with an electric operated controlled hoist with motor operated trolley for boiler top.
For indoor auxiliaries and machinery where the weight of a single part to be handled exceeds 10 tons, a minimum of a monorail with motorized trolley and motorized hoist shall be provided.
For indoor machines or indoor equipment, where the weight of a single part to be handled is 5 tons to 10 tons, a minimum of a monorail with geared trolley and manual hoist shall be provided and where the weight of a single part to be handled is less than 5 tons, only an overhead steel beam (monorail) or eye-plate shall be provided for supporting a temporary geared trolley and manual hoist or lifting equipment.
The bridge and gantry cranes and trolleys shall be sized to lift the heaviest piece in the equipment to facilitate their erection and maintenance. The turbine building crane shall have both main and auxiliary hooks.
The bridge and gantry crane wheel loads shall be as specified by the crane manufacturer. Impact loads from cranes shall be in accordance with AISC Standard.
Monorail hoist loads shall include lifted load, hoist weight, trolley weight and impact. Impact for monorail beams shall be 25% of the lifted load in the vertical direction. Transverse and longitudinal loads from trolley to the trolley beam shall be in accordance with trolley manufacturer's recommendation. The cranes and trolley shall have stops to prevent over travel."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_113,"60, 61",6.16.1._Piping_and_tubing,"6, 16, 1",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.16.1._Piping_and_tubing,Piping and Tubing,Piping Design Requirements,Water-steam cycle drains/vents and piping layout,,,"NDT methods for pipe quality, pipe supports and flexibility, expansion bends or loops, Exhibit B Part 1 norms and standards, double shutoff valves for blow-down/drains/vents/sampling lines >40 bar(g), clean drains returned to process (water-steam cycle chemical purity), steam traps with soakaway pits (steam line drainage between FCHPP and FGP interface), dissimilar joining flanges within GTG Package only, steam mains continuous slope in direction of flow, venting valves grouped with drainage to drainage tank, warm up vent with silencer for steam headers between FCHPP and FGP interface (subject to approval), HP steam stop valves with by-pass valves, underground fire fighting piping in GRP or suitable material, water hammer considered in drain line location",False,Generic piping design requirements for water-steam cycle drains/vents and fire fighting piping; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,True,,high,False,"Contains multiple concrete piping design criteria (pressure threshold, slopes, drains/vents, flange rules, firewater underground material) and interface points (FCHPP/FGP/GTG) useful for retrieving piping specs and layouts.","The quality of the pipes shall be guaranteed with appropriate NDT-methods. In addition to the material used, also the location, correct dimensioning and quality of supports as well as flexibility of the pipework shall be taken into account when designing the piping and piping layout. Expansion bends or loops shall be provided where required.
The design of all pipes, bends and fittings shall comply with norms and standards as per Exhibit B -Part 1
Piping for high-pressure services shall be cylindrical and uniform in section.
All atmospheric blow-down lines, drains, vents, sampling lines etc. belonging to the water-steam cycle shall be equipped with double shutoff valves if pressure exceeds 40 bar(g).
When locating the valves and the measuring points (temperature sensors, pressure taps etc.) the movements of the pipes with respect to the steel construction shall be taken into consideration.
Generally, clean drains shall be returned back to the process but considering at all times the operational requirements for chemical purity of the water-steam cycle. Such drains include e.g. steam lines, turbine drains, by-pass lines etc. Alternatively, for steam line drainage between FCHPP and up to FGP interface point only, the use of steam traps with soakaway pits is acceptable
Flanges shall be the same materials as the joining pipe or fitting. Where unavoidable for dissimilar joining flanges within the GTG Package only (not applicable for interface between Contractor and GTG Package where flanges of similar material shall be used) dissimilar flanges may be used as per applicable codes.
All steam mains shall be erected with a continuous slope in the direction of the flow so that the condensed steam will flow towards the drainage points.
The pipes shall be equipped with necessary venting, draining and sampling points including valves. Venting valves shall be collected to one group and they shall be provided with drainage to the drainage tank. Subject to the approval of Saudi Aramco, warm up vent with silencer (if required) is acceptable only for steam headers between FCHPP and FGP interface point.
All HP steam stop valves shall be equipped with by-pass valves. In case Contractor is of the opinion that a bypass valve is not required for any particular valve then, the list of such valves along with sufficient technical details shall be provided to the Owner during detailed design stage for the Owner's approval.
All fire fighting system piping underground shall be in GRP or any other adequate material suitable for that function. Special caution shall be taken during installation and erection to avoid any internal tension and leaks.
The effect of water hammering shall be taken into account when designing the location of drain lines."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_114,"61, 62",6.16.1._Piping_and_tubing,"6, 16, 1",content,"text, table",6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.16.1._Piping_and_tubing,Piping and Tubing,Piping Materials and Supports,"Drain piping, supports, and material selection (Power and Water sections)",,,"drain pipes connected to drain header at angle in direction of flow, continuous slope toward discharge ends, drain source labels for pipes entering drain vessel, piping support structure avoids harmful vibrations, supports not welded to bottom flange of beams, high pressure drain pipes designed for same pressure/temperature as drained equipment, piping specification for each piping system for Saudi Aramco approval, steam and condensate alloy and carbon steel, chemical injection 316L stainless steel / A106GR.B.S./A53GR.B.E / MDPE (LP subject to approval), potable water underground GRP; above ground rubber lined carbon steel / 316L stainless steel, fire fighting underground HDPE; aboveground A53GR.B.E galvanised (NFPA), waste water common discharge lines GRP or A53GR.B.E, instrument air 304L stainless steel; service air 304L stainless steel, GRP above ground to comply with ANSI B31.3; design for full vacuum conditions (except gravity/nonpressurised), flow velocities as per Exhibit B Part 1",False,"Generic drain piping/supports/material selection table (steam/condensate, fire fighting, air, etc.) without any ACC mention.",True,,high,False,"Provides explicit piping material selections by service, standards (NFPA, ANSI B31.3), and support/drain design rules that are strong anchors for matching piping specifications and material class documents.","All drain pipes discharging to a drain header shall be connected at an angle to the header so as to discharge in the direction of flow in the header. All drainpipes shall have an approved continuous slope towards the discharge ends.
Labels indicating the source of drain for each respective pipe entering a drain vessel shall be attached to the pipe.
The piping support structure design shall be such that no harmful vibrations occur during operation of the Plant. The movements of the pipework during standstill (cold) and during the normal operation (hot) shall be taken into account. Supports shall not be welded to the bottom flange of beams.
Pipes and fittings shall be so supported that the pipes may expand and contact freely.
All high pressure drain pipes on the inlet of drain valves, together with the drain pipes, shall be designed for the same pressure and temperature as the valve, pipe or vessel which they drain and shall be subject to the same specification and tests relevant thereto.
The Contractor shall provide a piping specification for each piping system for Saudi Aramco's approval.
Except where particularly specified in other clauses of the Specification, the materials of construction of pipework shall be selected in accordance with the following duties.
This following table applies for the Power and Water sections of the plant:
Steam and condensate, Material = Alloy and Carbon steel. Chemical injection, Material = 316L stainless steel or A106GR.B.S./A53GR.B.E (if suitable) or MDPE may be considered for some LP piping applications subject to approval of SA.. Potable water (Drinking water) for underground: For above ground:, Material = GRP or rubber lined carbon steel 316L stainless steel. Fire fighting, Material = Underground : HDPE Aboveground : A53GR.B.E Galvanised (All in accordance with NFPA). Waste Water common discharge lines, Material = GRP or A53GR.B.E. Instrument air, Material = 304L stainless steel. Service air, Material = 304L stainless steel
Where GRP pipe is used above ground it is to comply with the requirements of ANSI B31.3. Where GRP pipe is used above ground design shall meet full vacuum conditions except for gravity flow and nonpressurised piping such as discharge outfall drain piping.
The flow velocities shall be as per Exhibit B, Part 1.
Pipe materials are subject to the Owner's approval."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_115,"63, 64",6.16.2._Pumps,"6, 16, 2",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.16.2._Pumps,Wastewater Treatment,Pumps,Pump design and testing requirements,,,"pump characteristics matched to pipe network, start/stop under any operating conditions, mechanical shaft seals service life 20,000 operating hours, bearings service life 40,000 operating hours, separate foundations for large pumps (incl. feedwater pumps), withdrawable impeller for single-stage pumps, wear rings on casing and impeller, lubricant supply monitoring by temperature rise, sealing water sight glasses, manual/automatic air release on pump casing high point, hoisting beams and dismantling facilities, performance tests incl. pump curves, NPSH curve, vibration measurements",False,Wastewater treatment pump design/testing requirements; no mention of ACC/air cooled condenser or related components.,True,,high,False,"Contains detailed pump mechanical design, maintainability, monitoring, and performance testing requirements (curves/NPSH/vibration) useful for matching to pump datasheets/specifications and test procedures.","The Contractor shall match his pump characteristics to the pipe system network to achieve the high pump efficiency and reliability.
In normal operation, it shall be possible to start and stop the pumps under any operating conditions without any special precautions such as venting or warming.
The service life of the mechanical shaft seals shall be at least 20,000 operating hours and 40,000 operating hours for the bearings.
To avoid vibration problems all large pumps, including feedwater pumps, shall be located on separate foundations.
The impeller of the single stage pumps shall be readily withdrawable from the pump casing without the need to transfer the pump and with minimum dismantling and disconnection work from the pipework.
The pump design shall ensure that alignment is maintained between the various assemblies by recesses, spigots and dowels and shall be such that all components liable to wear can be replaced.
Components of the multistage pumps shall be permanently marked with the manufacturer's number and where dowels are not used, permanently marked for correct assembly. The pump casing and the impeller shall have detachable wear rings.
Where grease points are necessary they shall be fitted with removable screwed plugs which shall be accessible without removing guards.
Bearings with bearings which require a continuous supply of lubricant shall incorporate a means of monitoring such a supply, by temperature rise as appropriate for the type of bearing employed; separate monitors being fitted for each bearing feed or housing.
If sealing water is needed, flow shall be shown by sight glasses.
Special care in the selection of materials shall be taken in order to avoid binding and electrolytic action between the shaft sleeve and the mechanical seal components, particularly where long periods of idleness are inherent in the duty cycle as in the case of standby and storm pumping.
The highest point on the pump casing shall be fitted with a manual air release cock having a removable handle or an automatic air release valve with a lock-able isolation valve as specified (or according to the Manufacturer standard ).
When required, pumps shall have necessary hoisting and dismantling facilities, like hoisting beams, for maintenance purposes.
The test performed to verify the trouble free operation, of pumps shall at least include verification of pumps performances and validation of pumps curves including required NPSH curve, vibration measurements, etc). Saudi Aramco and/or their representative shall be informed reasonably in advance and allowed to witness the tests."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_116,"64, 65",6.16.3._Valves_and_Actuators,"6, 16, 3",content,"text, list_item",6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.16.3._Valves_and_Actuators,Wastewater Treatment,Valves and Actuators,"Valve design, materials, marking and actuation requirements",,,"valve materials suitable for operational purpose and corrosion conditions, maintenance access without removing valves (except butterfly valve), steam valve upper structure spindle force vs temperature, permanent marking on valve body: DN, PN, material, manufacturer mark, certificate ID, flow direction, stop valves larger than DN400 equipped with actuators for main control room operation via DCMS, valve identification number and CLOSED-OPEN position markings, hand-wheel clockwise to close, control valve position indicated on DCS operating station, actuator stall torque at 10% overvoltage; adjustable torque switch both directions, multistage control valves for strong cavitation, self-sealing bonnets for high-pressure (>40 barg) gate valves, under-pressure stop valves require seal water supply or air-leakage minimization",False,Generic valve/actuator requirements in wastewater treatment context; no ACC or air cooled condenser signal.,True,,high,False,"Provides concrete valve/actuator technical requirements (DN/PN marking, DCS indication, cavitation design, high-pressure gate valve bonnet type, actuator torque/overvoltage) that can map to valve specifications and I&C interface documents.","The design, structure and material of the valves shall be suitable for their particular operational purpose.
Valves and devices shall be located in an ergonomically correct way in respect to the maintenance platforms considering that maintenance shall be possible without removing the valves (except butterfly valve) from the pipe.
The valve and seat material shall comply with the operational and corrosion conditions for which they are intended.
The upper structure of the steam valves shall be designed in the manner that, at different operational temperatures of the spindle and cover, there is sufficient spindle force left to maintain the seat pressure required. In motor-controlled valves the seat force shall not vary according to the temperature so that the operating mechanism could not open cold valve.
The valves and the accessories shall be mounted so that pipe reactions do not hamper perfect valve operation and tightness.
The valves shall be well accessible for maintenance and operation. The larger valves (e.g.. boiler safety valves) must allow for the use of a crane or a hoist during maintenance. When necessary, the valve shall be equipped with an extended spindle. The valve hand-wheel shall not be higher than 1700mm above the operating level.
The following minimum data shall be permanently marked on the valve body:
- DN and PN
- material
- manufacturer's mark
- identification marks of the certificates
- flow direction
Generally (subject to Owner's Design Review and wherever they are required to be operated during start-up, shut-down and operation) stop valves larger than DN400 shall be equipped with actuators in order to enable operations from the main control room via DCMS.
Each valve must display with a clear text giving the identification number of the valve and markings of the ""CLOSED-OPEN"" positions with the direction arrows, and local position indicators.
The direction of rotation of valve hand-wheels (when looking at the face of the hand-wheel) shall be clockwise to close valves.
The position of all control valves shall be indicated on the DCS operating station.
The electrical valve-actuator combination has to be designed in such a way that the actuator does not damage the valve even if wrongly controlled with stall torque at 10 % overvoltage. There shall be one adjustable torque switch for both directions to prevent damage in the event of jamming.
Valve sizes shall be chosen according to pipe sizes, with the exception of control valves.
In the case of strong cavitation the control valves shall be of the multistage construction type.
The bonnets of the high-pressure (> 40barg) gate valves shall be of self-sealing construction type.
Stop valves, which operate at under pressure, must have either seal water supply, or some other system that minimises air leakage through the stem packing.
Intake and outlet nozzles of globe valves shall be located on the same centre line of the valve casing."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_117,"65, 66",6.16.3._Valves_and_Actuators,"6, 16, 3",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.16.3._Valves_and_Actuators,Wastewater Treatment,Valves and Actuators,Electric actuator requirements and actuator selection criteria,,,"valves designed for full shut-off pressure difference, electric actuators with anti-condensation heaters for outdoor mounting, self-locking electric actuator, motor/control gear positioned to avoid steam/water leakage blowing onto equipment, hand operating facility with automatic disengagement when motor operates, adjustable mechanical position indicator (incl. extended spindles), criteria to select pneumatic vs electrical actuators (steam, water, air, gas, oil; indoors/outdoors; space; maintenance; safety position), blow-out and safety valves equipped with silencers",False,Generic electric actuator/valve selection criteria; no ACC-specific equipment or references.,True,,high,False,"Contains specific actuator functional requirements (heaters, self-locking, indicators, manual override) and selection criteria plus silencers for safety valves—useful retrieval anchors for valve/actuator specs and control philosophy.","All valves have to be designed for full shut-off pressure difference.
The electric actuators shall be provided with anti-condensation heaters when mounted outdoors.
The electric actuator shall be self-locking. The motor and control gear shall be placed in such a position relative to the valve that there is no possibility of leakage of steam or water from the valve joints or glands blowing on to the motor or control equipment. A hand operating facility shall be provided and be so arranged that the mechanism will disengage automatically when the motor operates.
The actuator shall have an adjustable mechanical position indicator so that it may readily be seen whether the valves are open or shut and in the case of those valves fitted with extended spindles, indicators shall be fitted both to the extended spindle and to the valve spindle.
The Contractor shall propose to Owner criteria to select either pneumatic or electrical actuator depending on the needs of the process in question (process media: steam, water, air, gas, oil), location of the valve (outdoors/ indoors), space available, maintenance needs, purpose of function (regular closing or control, fast closing or control, safety position requirement).
All blow-out and safety valves shall be equipped with efficient silencers."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_118,66,6.16.4._Heat_Exchangers,"6, 16, 4",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.16.4._Heat_Exchangers,Wastewater Treatment,Heat Exchangers,Plate heat exchanger design margin,,,"plate heat exchangers, tierods sized to permit 25% increase of exchanger surface, 25% surface expansion allowance",False,Plate heat exchanger design margin note; no ACC/air cooled condenser mention.,True,,medium,False,Short but explicit technical requirement for plate heat exchanger mechanical design allowance (25% surface increase) useful for matching to exchanger datasheets/specs.,"If applicable, plate heat exchangers shall be designed with tierods the length of which would permit an increase of 25% of the exchanger surface."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_119,"66, 67",6.16.5._Thermal_Insulation,"6, 16, 5",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.16.5._Thermal_Insulation,Wastewater Treatment,Thermal Insulation,"Insulation materials, temperature limits and installation requirements",,,"thermal insulation compliance with local norms/standards or equivalent international standard, lagging withstand maximum surface temperature without deterioration, demountable insulation boxes for valve boxes/manholes; access for thermo-elements, insulate hot pipe penetrations (e.g. blow-out piping of safety valves), non-combustible insulation material; ignition risk considered, personnel protection: exposed portions >60°C insulated so outside surface ≤60°C at ambient 35°C, boiler insulation two layers insulating wool mat; overlapped seams; aluminium sheeting cladding, turbine insulation two layers wool mat or spray-on; prevent moisture and oil penetration, prefabricated mineral wool elements for piping and vessels, outdoor cladding aluminium; indoor cladding galvanised steel; weatherproofing, asbestos forbidden",False,General thermal insulation requirements for plant/boiler/turbine; no ACC-specific reference.,True,,high,False,"Contains multiple explicit insulation design/installation criteria and temperature limits (60°C at 35°C ambient), material/cladding requirements, and asbestos prohibition—strong anchors for insulation specifications and HSE-related design criteria.","Thermal insulations shall be designed, selected and installed in compliance to mandatory local norms and standards, or if not applicable, equivalent international standard.
All lagging adjacent to hot surfaces shall be capable of withstanding the maximum surface temperature obtainable in service without deterioration or breakdown of the material
The valve boxes and the covers of manholes shall be easily dismantled and reinstalled. The same is also valid for the boiler parts, which shall be regularly checked. The demountable insulation boxes shall be strengthened. It shall be possible to change temperature thermo-elements without dismantling the insulation.
The penetrations of hot pipes (e.g. blow-out piping of safety valves) shall also be insulated. The risk of ignition of construction materials of the boiler/turbine house shall be taken into account, and the insulation material used shall be non-combustible.
All exposed portions of the Plant which are subject to a temperature exceeding 60°C and are likely to come in direct contact with personnel shall be provided with approved thermal insulation so that the temperature on the outside of the insulation does not exceed 60°C when the ambient temperature is 35°C.
The insulation of the boiler shall be made up of two layers of the insulating wool mat, when applicable, and the seams of the separate layers shall be overlapped. The cladding shall be aluminium sheeting. The insulation of the turbine shall be made up of either at least two layers insulation wool mat or spray-on insulation material, and it shall be covered so that it prevents moisture and oil penetration into the insulation material.
Lagging shall be properly secured in position and it shall not loosen or suffer damage when subjected to vibration. Turbine cylinders shall be provided with fixing points for the insulation.
The insulation works shall be designed so that insulation will not get wet during storage or installation.
Piping and vessels shall be insulated with prefabricated mineral wool elements. The insulation of all piping, vessels, valves and fittings shall be finally covered (e.g. aluminium for outdoor application or galvanised steel sheeting for indoor application) and completely weatherproofed on any outdoor installations.
In all cases, the use of Asbestos is forbidden."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_120,67,6.16.6._Vibration,"6, 16, 6",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.16.6._Vibration,Mechanical,Rotating Equipment,Vibration Limits and Acceptance Zones,,,"ISO 10816 Parts 1-7, vibration magnitude, Zone A, Zone B, combustion gas turbines (GT), air compressors, vertical pumps, Emergency Diesel Generator, ISO 8528-9, ISO 7919 zone A/B",False,"General vibration limits for rotating equipment (GT, compressors, pumps); no ACC fans or ACC context mentioned.",True,,high,False,Explicit technical requirement specifying vibration standards and acceptance zones for multiple rotating equipment types.,"All rotating equipment shall comply with the requirements for vibration magnitude stipulated in ISO 10816 Parts 1 - 7 as applicable. For combustion gas turbines, air compressors, and vertical pumps Zone B shall apply. Emergency Diesel Generator shall comply with ISO 8528-9, part 9.For all other equipment ISO 10816 parts 1-7, Zone A shall apply.
For only GT, ISO 7919 zone A/B is acceptable as a guideline."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_121,67,6.16.7.1._PAINTING_/_SURFACE_TREATMENT,"6, 16, 7, 1",content,"text, list_item",6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.16.6._Vibration > 6.16.7.1._PAINTING_/_SURFACE_TREATMENT,Materials and Corrosion Protection,Coating and Painting,Surface Treatment and Corrosion Category,,,"painting / surface treatment, ISO 12944, corrosion category C4, high durability, outdoor steel elements, civil structures, plant equipment, dust and sand storms, corrosion category C3, indoor steel structures, HRSG",False,Coating/painting corrosion categories for outdoor/indoor steel elements; no ACC mentioned.,True,,high,False,Defines coating/corrosion protection requirements with ISO 12944 categories for indoor/outdoor steel and equipment.,"As per Codes and Standards (refer to section 4), Exhibit B, Part 1 and Good Industry Practice.
The Contractor shall be aware of the corrosion problems to be faced on Site, especially with the equipment installed outdoors, including incidence of dust and sand storms. The coating specification provided by Contractor shall consider the following corrosion categories (according to ISO 12944):
- at least C4 with high durability for all other outdoor steel elements including the civil structures and the Plant equipment (e.g. HRSG, pumps, etc)
- at least C3 with high durability for all the indoor steel structures and plant equipment"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_122,68,6.16.7.2._GALVANIZATION,"6, 16, 7, 2",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.16.6._Vibration > 6.16.7.2._GALVANIZATION,Materials and Corrosion Protection,Galvanizing,Hot Dip Galvanizing Requirements,,,"hot dip galvanizing process, cold galvanising paint touch-up (two coats), JIS H8641 Zinc Hot Dip Galvanizing, BS 729, SIS 055900",False,Hot dip galvanizing requirements; no ACC-related signal.,True,,high,False,Clear technical specification for galvanizing method and governing standards.,"In addition to the requirements of Exhibit B, Part 1, galvanising work shall conform to and shall be performed by the hot dip process, unless otherwise specified. Two coats of cold galvanising paint shall touch up damaged parts of the galvanising surface.
The following standards or equivalent shall be applied; JIS H8641 Zinc Hot Dip Galvanizing, BS 729, Swedish Standard SIS 055900."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_123,68,6.16.7.3._CATHODIC_PROTECTION,"6, 16, 7, 3",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.16.6._Vibration > 6.16.7.3._CATHODIC_PROTECTION,Materials and Corrosion Protection,Cathodic Protection,Buried Metallic Pipes and Storage Tanks,,,"cathodic protection, sacrificial anode type, buried metallic pipes, metallic storage tanks, design life",False,Cathodic protection for buried pipes and tanks; no ACC mention.,True,,high,False,Explicit corrosion protection requirement identifying applicable assets and CP type.,"In addition to the requirements of Exhibit B, Part 1, cathodic protection (sacrificial anode type or equivalent as approved by the Owner) shall be applied for all buried metallic pipes and all metallic storage tanks for the design life."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_124,68,6.17.1._Welders,"6, 17, 1",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.17.1._Welders,Welding and Fabrication,Welder Qualification,Pressure Parts Welding QA/QC,,,"ASME BPVC Section IX, pressure part welders qualification certificates, unique welder identification number, permanent test records, radiographs, welding engineer supervision, consumables control, approved welding procedures, heat treatment, non-destructive testing (NDT), spectrographic material checks",False,Welder qualification requirements for pressure parts; no ACC/air cooled condenser reference.,True,,high,False,Contains specific technical/quality requirements tied to ASME Section IX and welding traceability/NDT controls.,"All welders and welding operators shall be qualified for the work for which they are engaged. Pressure Part Welders shall hold current welder's qualification certificates in accordance with the latest edition of the ASME Boiler and Pressure Vessel Code Section IX, 'Qualification Standard for Welding Procedures, Welders and Welding Operations' or approved equivalent.
In the case of pressure parts and pressure part attachment welds, each approved welder shall have a unique identification number which shall be indicated on all welds made by him and on all permanent test records applicable to those welds including radiographs.
All welders shall be under the supervision of a competent welding engineer who shall demonstrate, to the Owner's satisfaction, a complete system for the control and treatment of consumables, deployment of welders, use of approved procedures, heat treatment, non-destructive testing, including spectrographic material checks on weld and parent metals, repairs and general quality control."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_125,"68, 69",6.17.2._Welder's_Tests,"6, 17, 2",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.17.2._Welder's_Tests,Welding,Welder Qualification Testing,Sample Weld Test Specimens,,,"welder's tests, sample weld specimens, similar shape thickness chemical analysis, worst-case welding position, test piece preparation, examination standards, approved standard/code",False,General welder test requirements; no mention of ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or related equipment.,True,,medium,False,Defines requirements for welder testing specimens and examination per agreed standards/codes; useful for retrieving welding qualification/testing specifications.,"Sample welds shall be carried out on specimens of similar shape, thickness and chemical analysis to the material to be welded, and in a position which shall represent as far as possible the worst conditions under which actual welding shall take place, as laid down by the approved Standard/Code.
The method of preparation of the test pieces and the standards to be used in examination of them shall be according to the agreed Standard or Codes, or as may be agreed with the Owner. All expenses of every kind in connection with welder's tests shall be borne by the Contractor."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_126,69,6.17.3._Welding_Procedures,"6, 17, 3",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.17.3._Welding_Procedures,Welding,Welding Procedures,Procedure Qualification,,,"welding procedure qualification, repair welding procedures, governing design code, ASME Section IX, approved equivalent, submit qualified procedures to Owner, prior to commencement of welding",False,Generic welding procedure qualification (ASME IX); no ACC-specific signal.,True,,high,False,Explicit requirement for welding procedure qualification to ASME Section IX (or governing code) and submission prior to welding; strong retrieval anchors for welding procedure specifications.,"Welding procedures including those proposed in repair situations shall be qualified as required by the governing design code or, where no appropriate standard is thereby indicated, by Section IX of the ASME Code or approved equivalent and all qualified procedures shall be submitted to the Owner prior to the commencement of any welding."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_127,69,6.17.4._Welding_and_Heat_Treatment,"6, 17, 4",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.17.4._Welding_and_Heat_Treatment,Welding,Welding and Heat Treatment,Qualification and Applicable Codes,,,"ASME BPVC Section IX, qualification of welding procedures welders operators, pressure parts welding, shop and field welding, AWS D1.1, structural steel welds, electrodes and welding rods, list of electrodes and consumables",False,General welding/heat treatment codes for pressure parts and structural steel; no ACC reference.,True,,high,False,"Cites specific governing codes (ASME BPVC IX, AWS D1.1) and qualification/submittal requirements; strong technical anchors for welding specifications and QA/QC documents.","All welding procedures, welders and operators of machine welding equipment shall be qualified. All shop and field welding procedures, welders and operators of machine welding equipment for welding of pressure parts shall be qualified in accordance with the latest edition of the ASME Boiler and Pressure Vessel Code Section IX, 'Qualification Standard for Welding Procedures, Welders and Welding Operations' or equivalent.
All welds in structural steel shall be in accordance with AWS D1.1 and other applicable codes.
All electrodes and/or welding rods and the fabrication procedure shall be in accordance with the applicable codes and standards. Before any welding is commenced, the Contractor shall submit a list of all electrodes and consumables to be used to the Owner."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_128,69,6.17.4.1._PREHEATING,"6, 17, 4, 1",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.17.4._Welding_and_Heat_Treatment > 6.17.4.1._PREHEATING,Welding,Welding and Heat Treatment,Preheating,,,"preheating prior to welding, maintain temperature throughout operation, thermocouples, temperature indicating crayons, applicable codes and standards",False,Generic preheating prior to welding; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.,True,,medium,False,Provides preheat control/measurement requirements tied to codes/standards; useful for retrieving welding heat treatment and fabrication specifications.,"Where necessary, the work shall be heated prior to welding in an approved manner, and the temperature maintained throughout the operation. The metal temperature shall be checked by means of suitable thermocouples or temperature indicating crayons.
Prior to welding, the work shall be preheated, where necessary, in accordance with applicable codes and standards, and the temperature shall be maintained throughout the operation."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_129,"69, 70",6.17.4.2._POST_WELD_HEAT_TREATMENT,"6, 17, 4, 2",table,"text, list_item",6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.17.4._Welding_and_Heat_Treatment > 6.17.4.2._POST_WELD_HEAT_TREATMENT,Welding,Welding and Heat Treatment,Post Weld Heat Treatment (PWHT),,,"post weld heat treatment (PWHT) mandatory, exceptions austenitic stainless steels, plain carbon steel <0.25% C up to 30 mm, low alloy steel 0.5% Mo and 1.25% Cr, hardness traverse 240 HV10, fillet weld leg 8 mm, minimum preheat 200°C, use temperatures >400°C creep properties, soaking time 5 min/mm minimum 120 min, vanadium steels 180 min regardless of thickness, PWHT temperature above original tempering temperature reassessment, stress relieving after welding, IPB welds AWS or relevant international standards",False,General PWHT/stress relieving requirements; no ACC-related terms or interfaces.,True,,high,False,"Detailed PWHT requirements with material/thickness exceptions, hardness/preheat limits, soaking times, high-temperature (>400°C) considerations, and IPB weld standards—strong technical retrieval signals for welding/PWHT specifications and material requirements.","Regardless of the requirements of the specific code or specifications applicable to the design and/or construction of the plant and associated pipework, it is a mandatory requirement of this Specification that all welds shall be subject to appropriate post weld treatment with the following exceptions:
- austenitic stainless steels
- plain carbon steels containing less than 0.25 per cent carbon at thicknesses up to and including 30 mm
- low alloy steels containing ½ per cent molybdenum and 1¼ per cent chromium, ½ per cent molybdenum up to and including thicknesses of 12.5 mm provided that:
- -a hardness traverse across the weld zone gives no single result greater than 240 hv10
- -no fillet weld leg is greater than 8 mm
- -a minimum preheat of 200°c is used for welding
Plain carbon steels shall contain no more than 0.25% carbon.
All post weld heat treatments of materials and components for use at temperatures in excess of 400°C shall be so designed as to guarantee maximum creep properties in the parent materials and weldments.
The soaking times at the selected heat treatment temperatures shall be:
- low alloy steels not containing vanadium, 5 minutes per mm of thickness with a minimum time of 120 minutes
- low alloy steels containing vanadium and high (11 to 15 per cent) chromium steels containing vanadium, 180 minutes regardless of thickness
In the event that any post weld heat treatment is carried out at a temperature in excess of the original tempering temperature of the parent materials, a re-assessment of the design strength limit stresses for the materials will be required. This may, in the absence of acceptable mechanical test data for the specific condition, require re-determination of the relevant mechanical properties of the materials.
The requirements for post weld heat treatment do not affect or replace in any way the requirements for maintenance of welding preheats or post welding heating for the purposes of diffusing hydrogen from the weldment.
Post weld heat treatment, where applicable, shall be conducted in accordance with applicable codes and standards. Records of post weld heat treatment shall be made available for inspection when requested.
After completion of welding, fabricated parts shall be stress relieved in accordance with the applicable codes and standards.
In the case of IPB welds, AWS or other relevant international Standards shall apply."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_130,71,6.17.5._Site_Weld_repairs,"6, 17, 5",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.17.5._Site_Weld_repairs,Welding,Weld Repairs,Site Weld Repairs,,,"weld repair procedure submission, dimensioned sketches of excavated areas, Owner approval before repair, non-destructive testing (NDT) before and after weld, stress relief and repeat NDT, weld cracking cause investigation, weld repair of cast iron not permitted",False,Generic site weld repair and NDT requirements; no ACC signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit technical requirements for site weld repairs, NDT, stress relief, and restrictions (cast iron), useful to retrieve welding/NDT procedures and QA/QC records requirements.","Where repairs on major items of plant are found to be necessary by welding, dimensioned sketches of the excavated areas shall be prepared and the proposed repair procedure submitted to the Owner before any repair is commenced. Under no circumstances shall the repair be commenced until the agreement of the Owner has been obtained. The areas shall be subjected to a suitable non-destructive test and on completion of the weld will again be non- destructively tested. In the event that the repair requires all or any part of the item to be stress-relieved, the non-destructive tests shall be repeated on any part deemed to have been affected by the stress relief.
In the case of weld cracking, the Owner may require a full investigation to be made of the cause of the cracking prior to any approval of repair procedures. The cost of all such investigations and reports is to be wholly borne by the Contractor.
Weld repair of cast iron parts is not permitted under any circumstances."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_131,71,6.17.6._Welding_Electrodes_and_Consumables,"6, 17, 6",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.17.6._Welding_Electrodes_and_Consumables,Welding,Welding Consumables,Electrodes and Consumables List,,,"electrodes list submission, welding consumables list, submit to Owner before welding",False,General requirement to submit welding consumables list; no ACC mention.,False,"Primarily an administrative submittal requirement (list submission) with no technical specifications, standards, or acceptance criteria for consumables.",low,True,Mentions welding consumables but provides only a submission instruction; weak technical retrieval anchors for matching to engineering MDL documents.,"Before any welding is commenced, the Contractor shall submit a list of all electrodes and consumables to be used to the Owner."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_132,71,6.17.7._Inspection_of_Welding,"6, 17, 7",content,text,6.13._Waste_water_treatment > 6.17.7._Inspection_of_Welding,Welding,Inspection and Quality Control,Weld Inspection (NDT / Radiography),,,"weld inspection extent per applicable codes and standards, welder qualifications records, weld procedure records, weld inspection and repair reports, indexed welding records at works and site, X-ray photographs viewing facilities, radiography procedure and resolution agreed with Owner, image quality indicator (IQI), gamma-ray unit storage and safety regulations",False,Generic welding inspection/radiography recordkeeping; no ACC-related content.,True,,high,False,"Defines weld inspection/quality compliance, recordkeeping, radiography requirements (IQI), and gamma-ray safety/storage—strong technical QA/QC retrieval signals.","The extent of weld inspection and the final weld quality shall comply with the applicable codes and standards. Records of all welder qualifications, weld procedures, and weld inspection and repair reports shall be maintained. The Contractor shall maintain, both at works and on site, adequately indexed records of all welding, weld inspection and repairs, which shall be available to the Owner, together with facilities for viewing of X-ray photographs.
The views taken by X-ray shall be to a procedure and resolution agreed with the Owner and shall incorporate the appropriate image quality indicator.
Adequate safe storage facilities and appropriate safety regulations shall be provided for gamma-ray units, if used."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_133,72,"6.18._Galleries,_Platforms_and_Staircases","6, 18",content,text,6.18._Galleries _Platforms_and_Staircases,Civil Works,Access and Egress Structures,"Galleries, Platforms and Staircases",,,"industrial safety norms and standards, maintenance access to valves and meters, vertical ladders not allowed for normal operation access, escape ladders permitted, steelwork near hot air/gas radiation protected and insulated, galvanised open steel flooring, platform loading for maintenance, bolted clips for flooring, galvanised nuts and bolts, minimum platform width 1 m, GRP grating for high salinity areas, handrails and stairways galvanised, curbs/kicking strips on edges, relief valves grouped with access platform",False,General platforms/galleries/staircases for plant access; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or ACC structure referenced.,True,,high,False,"Provides detailed design/installation requirements for platforms, staircases, ladders, materials (galvanised/GRP), access/ergonomics, and safety—useful for retrieving structural steel/access platform design documents.","Galleries, staircases, platforms, other related steel constructions shall be designed according to the mandatory local norms and standards, or if not applicable, equivalent international standard (ISO) related to directions of industrial safety and in the way that all places that are necessary for maintenance (e.g. valves, meters) are easily accessible. Vertical ladders are not allowed as a fairway to places which must be entered during normal operation, but they shall be allowed for escape ladders, access to stack and for access to not frequently used equipment (e.g. remote sensors).
Steelwork in close proximity with hot air or gas or subject to radiation from any part of the Plant shall be adequately protected and insulated. On completion of the work, all necessary platforms, ladders and stairways for the Plant and ancillary equipment shall be provided to give safe access to all equipment, valves, and instrumentation.
Platforms shall be constructed using galvanised open steel flooring and shall cater for the relevant loading for maintenance. The flooring shall be bolted to the framework using approved bolted clips. The platform framework and the corresponding nuts and bolts shall be galvanised. The minimum width of the galleries for emergency exits and the minimum free height of these galleries shall be according to relevant standards. Exit routes shall be clearly marked with colours according to applicable norms and standards.
Platforms shall be provided around equipment as required for maintenance work, testing, inspection and safe operation. Platforms shall be sized to facilitate safe ergonomic operation of manual valves and equipment between waist and shoulder level of a standard man. Relief valves shall be grouped together and an access platform provided. Operating valves which cannot be serviced from a platform shall be operated by other approved means.
All platforms to be visited on a daily basis shall be provided with stairways, except where a permanent access ladder is the only feasible means of access. All interior and exterior platforms, gratings, checker plate, stairways and handrails shall be galvanised.
Platforms shall be constructed using galvanised open steel flooring or a suitable grade of GRP grating in areas of high salinity, to the relevant ISO, British Standards or ASME and shall cater for the relevant loadings for maintenance. Generally, no frequently accessed platform shall be less than 1m wide and all platforms shall be extended up to the equipment, valves and instrumentation that they serve.
All edges of floors, platforms and walkways shall be provided with curbs or kicking strips."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_134,74,7.1._Introduction,"7, 1",content,text,7.1._Introduction,Electrical System,Plant Electrical Scope,Cabling Systems and Plant Interconnections,,,"complete electrical system for the Plant, power to auxiliary buildings, interface works, cabling systems in the Facility, Plant interconnections cabling, cabling between items of equipment, design installation and commissioning of cabling, low voltage loads assessment, high voltage apparatus numbering per Plant Identification System of SEC/SA",False,Overall plant electrical scope and cabling systems; no ACC-specific equipment or term mentioned.,True,,medium,False,Defines electrical scope responsibilities (design/installation/commissioning) and cabling/interconnection coverage plus HV nomenclature reference—useful for retrieving electrical design basis and cabling documents.,"The scope covered by this section of the specification relates to all equipment not specifically covered in Exhibit B, Part 1. In some cases certain duplication may be apparent but has been included in order to provide completeness.
The Contractor shall provide the complete electrical system for the Plant, including power to the various auxiliary buildings, interface works and the mechanical equipment in his scope of supply. The Contractor shall be responsible for the design, installation and commissioning of all cabling systems in the Facility (including Plant interconnections), together with all cabling between items of equipment in his supply. The Contractor shall make his own assessment of the electrical requirements. This is particularly relevant where low voltage loads are concerned.
The numbering and nomenclature of high voltage apparatus will be in accordance with the Plant Identification System of SEC/SA."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_135,"74, 75",7.2.1._Design_requirements,"7, 2, 1",table,"text, table",7.1._Introduction > 7.2.1._Design_requirements,Electrical Systems,Auxiliary Power and Distribution,Voltage Ratings and Operating Voltage Tolerances,,,"auxiliary power systems single component/circuit failure, unitized electrical distribution philosophy, MV/LV transformer feeding LV switchgear, LV emergency switchgear interconnections, HV switchyard 380 kV, generator 18 kV, isolated phase duct 18 kV, MV switchgear 13.8 kV, MV auxiliaries motors >200 kW 13.2 kV, LV boards 480 V, LV auxiliaries 460 V, UPS 480 V charging / 120 V output, DC system 125 VDC, motor terminal voltage during start-up ≥85% (exceptional 80%), AC continuous voltage range 90%–110% nominal; short-time 80% during motor starting, DC operating range 110%–85% nominal",False,Electrical auxiliary power voltage ratings and tolerances; no mention of ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or ACC equipment.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit electrical system design criteria, voltage levels for major equipment, and voltage tolerance requirements useful for matching to electrical single line diagrams, switchgear/transformer datasheets, and design criteria documents.","Auxiliary power systems shall be arranged to minimize operational constraint and be capable of maintaining or rapidly restoring the full continuous power output of the generators and steam raising plant in the event of a single component or circuit failure, except for a generator unit.
The electrical distribution system shall follow a unitized philosophy but with necessary interconnections to allow for rapid restoration. LV Switchgear shall be fed from the MV/LV transformer and LV emergency switchgear and LV interconnections at the LV emergency switchgear.
Interconnectors between unit boards may be closed until the particular unit has developed sufficient voltage to be self-sustaining.
All electrical equipment shall be designed so as to withstand abnormal system voltages as defined in this Specification.
Rated voltage of electrical equipment
HV Switchyard, 1 = 380 kV. Generator, 1 = 18kV or as per OEM's specification. Isolated Phase Duct, 1 = 18kV or as per OEM's specification. MVswitchgears, 1 = 13.8kV. MV auxiliaries (motors > 200 kW), 1 = 13.2kV. LV boards, 1 = 480V. LV auxiliaries, 1 = 460V. - Motors 0.25 kW through 200 kW - All motor operated valves, 1 = . UPS system 3-phase (UPS), 1 = 480V (charging supply) /120V (output). DC system, 1 = 125 VDC. Emergency Lighting, 1 = 125 VAC (via 125VDC/125VAC inverters). Protection and Switchgear Controls, 1 = 125 VDC. Small Power, 1 = 230V 1-phase. Motors < 0.25 kW, 1 = 230V 1-phase. Cubicle Lighting, 1 = 230V 1-phase. Cubicle Anti-condensation Heaters, 1 = 230V 1-phase. Motor Anti-condensation Heaters, 1 = 230V 1-phase. Field Instrumentation, 1 = 230V 1-phase. General purpose, 1 = 230V 1-phase
All AC equipment, except where otherwise specified shall be capable of continuous operation at a voltage level in the range 90% to 110% of the nominal voltage, and short-time operation at 80% of the nominal voltage during motor starting without deterioration. However auxiliary systems shall be designed such that a motor terminal voltage on start-up shall not fall below 85% of nominal voltage. Under exceptional circumstances motor terminal voltage may reach 80% provided there is sufficient margin of torque to accelerate the respective drive. DC equipment shall be capable of normal operation in the range of 110 percent to 85 percent of nominal voltage without deterioration.
DC equipment shall be capable of normal operation in the range of 110% to 85% of nominal voltage without deterioration."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_136,"75, 76, 77",7.2.1._Design_requirements,"7, 2, 1",content,"text, list_item",7.1._Introduction > 7.2.1._Design_requirements,Electrical Systems,Design Requirements,"Continuity, Testing, Hazardous Areas, Motors and Switchgear Housing",,,"GTG MV system energized from unit auxiliary transformer or start up transformer, continuity of operation; transient/abnormal/load shedding scenarios, factory type and routine testing; type test certificates, safety isolation locking interlocking facilities, electrical equipment in hazardous areas; special enclosures per hazard classification, short-circuit current temperature rise no permanent damage, anti-condensation heaters for motors >20 kW and motorized valve actuators, motors class F temperature rise limited to class B, large motors ≥1 MW high efficiency design, station service and unit auxiliary transformers N-1 (one transformer out of service), MV/LV switchgear, DC switchgear, batteries/charger, UPS inverters in fully air-conditioned containments, sunshades for outdoor cubicles/terminal boxes/enclosures, digital fault monitoring and disturbance recording system (LV incomers/couplers, MV feeders except motor feeders, HV switchyard bays)",False,"General electrical design requirements (testing, hazardous areas, motors, switchgear housing); no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.",True,,high,False,"Provides concrete electrical design requirements (transformer redundancy, hazardous area enclosures, motor/heater criteria, housing/air-conditioning, monitoring/DR system scope) that can retrieve relevant electrical specifications and equipment documents.","During Plant Unit start up and shut down the gas turbine generator MV system shall normally be energized either from the unit auxiliary transformer or start up transformer.
The electrical equipment shall be designed to ensure continuity of operation of the main plant under all working conditions at the Plant Site. The plant shall also be capable of meeting all transient, abnormal and load shedding scenarios. In addition it shall be arranged to facilitate inspection, maintenance and repairs.
All electrical equipment shall be type and routine tested in factories. Type test will not be repeated if type test certificates of the same equipment type are available.
All reasonable precautions shall be taken in the design of equipment and of works to ensure the safety of personnel concerned with the operation and maintenance of works. Safety, isolation, locking and interlocking facilities shall comply with the relevant standards and requirements.
Electrical equipment located in hazardous areas shall be provided with special enclosures suitable for the hazard classification of the areas according to the guidelines provided in statutory regulations and codes.
The final temperature attained as a result of the passage of short circuit current shall not cause permanent damage or deterioration sufficient to reduce the normal operating characteristics below those specified.
Anti-condensation heaters shall be provided for motors above 20kW rating, motorized valve actuators, switchgear and control panels, except where located in air-conditioned rooms. Except for motors which have heaters operated by the motor controller, the other heaters shall be permanently connected without thermostats and energized via a local isolator supplied from a local distribution board.
Electric motors shall be designed for class F temperature rises, with acceptable temperature rises limited to class B over the entire range of ambient air temperatures.
Large motors of 1MW and above shall use high efficiency designs as far as possible.
Station Service and Unit Auxiliary transformers and switchgear shall be designed to allow normal operation with one transformer out of service. A change-over scheme shall allow transfer, without loss of output, between this running condition and normal system operation.
All MV and LV switchgears, DC switchgear equipment, batteries and charger, UPS inverters, distribution equipment and safe AC equipment shall be suitably housed inside fully air-conditioned containments.
Sunshades shall be provided for all cubicles, terminal boxes and enclosures mounted outdoors. OEM (Siemens) standard design only for GTG package for providing no sunshade is acceptable, if the equipment / enclosure is suitable designed for outdoor installation.
A digital fault monitoring and disturbance recording system shall also be provided, which shall cover the entire power station electrical system items, including
- incomers and couplers of all main low voltage (LV) switchgears
- all MV switchgear feeders except motor feeders (motors shall be monitored via the DCMS)
- all HV switchyard bays"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_137,77,7.2.1._Design_requirements,"7, 2, 1",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.1._Design_requirements,Electrical Systems,Protection and Control,Digital Protection Relays Testing During Normal Operation,,,"electronic digital protection relays, testing facilities for all functions during normal operation, no operational restrictions during testing, other protection functions remain active",False,Protection relay testing requirement only; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.,True,,medium,False,Specific requirement on protection relay type and online testing capability; useful for matching protection/control specifications and relay functional requirements.,All electrical protection relays shall be of the electronic digital type with testing facilities to enable testing of all functions during normal operation without imposing operational restrictions (i.e. leaving other protection functions active).
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_138,"77, 78",7.2.2._Generators_and_generator_excitation_systems,"7, 2, 2",content,"text, list_item",7.1._Introduction > 7.2.2._Generators_and_generator_excitation_systems,Electrical Systems,Generators and Excitation,"Generator Design, Earthing, Monitoring and Protection",,,"two pole 60 Hz generator, IEC 60034, short circuit ratio ≥0.5, withstand 1.3× rated voltage for 30 s during load rejection, sub-transient reactance X''d (saturated) minimum 12% (IEC basis), online condition monitoring stator winding insulation, search coil for rotor winding turn shorting, generator neutral earthed via distribution transformer and loading resistor, earth fault current limited to max 10 A, anti-condensation heaters for air circuits/windings/excitation/control cubicles; automatic when out of service, duplicated generator protection (two independent devices), ANSI 87G stator differential; 87TG overall differential, ANSI 40 loss of excitation; 32 reverse power; 46 negative phase sequence, ANSI 24 over excitation; 27 undervoltage; 81O/81U over/under frequency, ANSI 64G rotor earth fault; 78 out of step; 50BF breaker failure, electronic digital protective relays with self-monitoring/testing during normal operation",False,Generator design/earthing/protection requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mentioned.,True,,high,False,"Rich generator technical requirements (standards, electrical parameters, earthing limit, monitoring, detailed ANSI protection list) strongly supports retrieval of generator datasheets, protection philosophies, and excitation/protection specifications.","Generators shall be two pole, 60Hz designed, built and tested in accordance with IEC-60034. The generators shall have a high degree of reliability under steady state condition and shall be capable of confirming recovery under emergency conditions and during transient frequency fluctuations.
Generator shall have a short circuit ratio of not less than 0.5 and shall be capable of withstanding 1.3 times the rated voltage for 30 seconds during load rejection or similar events. The generator sub-transient reactance (saturated) shall be a minimum of 12% (IEC basis).
On line condition monitoring for stator winding insulation and search coil to detect shorting of rotor winding turns shall be provided.
Anti-condensation heaters shall be provided for air circuits, generator windings, excitation systems and control cubicles. Heaters shall be suitably sized to prevent condensation. Heaters shall be switched on automatically when the generators are taken out of service.
The generator neutral point shall be earthed through a distribution transformer and loading resistor to limit the earth fault current to a maximum of 10 Amps.
The following protection shall be provided and shall be duplicated to the extent so that at least two independent devices shall detect a fault.
- generator over excitation (ANSI code 24)
- phase under-voltage (ANSI code 27)
- generator over frequency (ANSI code 81O)
- generator under frequency (ANSI code 81U)
- stator differential (ANSI code 87G)
- overall differential (ANSI code 87TG), CT will be on neutral side
- neutral ground overvoltage (Stator Ground) (ANSI code 59N)
- current unbalance/Negative phase sequence (ANSI code 46)
- loss of excitation (ANSI code 40)
- reverse power/anti-motoring (ANSI code 32)
- monitoring and alarm of generator stator
- voltage transformer supervision (miniature circuit breakers will be used instead of fuses)
- rotor earth fault (ANSI code 64G)
- out of step (ANSI code 78)
- phase time overcurrent (ANSI code 51)
- phase overvoltage (ANSI code 59)
- stator ground protection (3 rd Harmonic) (ANSI code 27TN)
- phase distance (ANSI code 21)
- voltage restrained time overcurrent (ANSI code 51V)
- inadvertent energisation (ANSI code 50/27)
- stator earth fault stage 1 and 2 (ANSI 64-1 and 64-2)
- breaker failure protection (ANSI 50BF)
The Contractor shall include additional protection if considered necessary.
The protective relays shall be electronic digital type with selfmonitoring functions for testing during normal operating conditions without imposing operational restrictions."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_139,"78, 79, 80",7.2.2._Generators_and_generator_excitation_systems,"7, 2, 2",content,"text, list_item",7.1._Introduction > 7.2.2._Generators_and_generator_excitation_systems,Electrical Systems,Generators and Excitation,"Excitation System, Synchronizing Limits, Cooling and Controls",,,"generator withstand fault and abnormal conditions without damage, synchronizing with generator circuit breaker and 380 kV circuit breaker, synchronizing tolerances ±10° phase displacement; ±0.7% slip; ±15% voltage, generator instruments temperature/oil pressure/vibration wired to junction boxes, fire detection and protection for generator, gas turbine generators and related cooling systems temperature rise within class B limits, stator and rotor windings class F insulation, air cooled / closed air-water cooled / hydrogen cooled generator options, fast response dual automatic voltage regulator (AVR) with standby manual channel and auto follower, over/under excitation limiters; over-fluxing protection; power system stabiliser; reactive power control; field suppression, generator terminal voltage variation no-load 50%–110%; control down to 0% excitation for commissioning, excitation power source: PMG or power potential transformer or SEE transformer fed from 13.8 kV MV switchgear, brushless or static excitation; suitable for grid connection, AGC capability; interface details between SEC and plant finalized during detailed engineering, generator terminal enclosure (GTE) positive pressure if hydrogen filled, on-load brush replacement/adjustment capability",False,Generator excitation/synchronizing/cooling options; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser scope or interfaces referenced.,True,,high,False,"Contains detailed excitation system functional requirements, synchronizing tolerances, cooling/insulation constraints, and control features (AVR/AGC/PSS) that are strong anchors for matching generator/excitation/control documents.","The generators shall withstand the following fault and abnormal conditions without any damage to the stator, rotor, windings and other parts of the generator.
Synchronizing shall be possible with the generator circuit breaker and 380kV circuit breaker.
Synchronising with the combined conditions based on maximum individual synchronising check tolerances of:
- ± 10 degrees phase displacement
- ± 0.7 percent slip
- ± 15 percent voltage
Fire detection and protection shall be provided. Generator shall be provided with required instruments to monitor temperature, oil pressure, vibration etc. These shall be wired out to junction boxes for field connection.
Gas turbine generators and related cooling systems shall be designed to maintain the temperature rise within insulation class B limits throughout the entire operational range and throughout the ranges of ambient temperatures.
The generator stator and rotor windings shall have class F insulation system.
Contractor shall optimise the Generators and as per SFR requirement shall provide suitable air cooled, closed air water cooled or hydrogen cooled.
The generator excitation system shall be complete with a fast response dual automatic voltage regulator, standby manual channel with auto follower and automatic transfer facilities. Monitoring and fault detecting devices with over and under excitation limiters, over-fluxing protection, power system stabiliser, reactive power control, field suppression equipment, dedicated instrument transformers and other required devices shall be provided to ensure a complete and reliable excitation system.
It shall be possible to continuously vary the generator terminal voltage under no load conditions between 50% and 110%. Facilities shall be included to control down to 0% of normal excitation for the purpose of commissioning.
The excitation power shall be derived either from a permanent magnet generator or from a power potential transformer connected to the terminals of the generator or from a SEE transformer which is fed from the 13.8kV MV Switchgear. The excitation system shall be brushless or static as appropriate. It is very important that the generators and the excitation system are suitable for grid connection.
Generator shall have AGC capability with all necessary hardware. Interface details between SEC and the Plant shall be finalized during detailed engineering stage.
Features of the excitation system shall include but not limited to:
- rotor current limiters
- rotor angle limiters
- under excitation alarm
- over excitation alarm
- volt-Hertz limiter and protection
- dual automatic channel
- manual channel
- reactive power control
- power system stabilizer
- field failure detection
- rotating diode monitoring where applicable
Generator compartments should be dust free. Contractor shall ensure that positive pressure in the generator terminal enclosure (GTE) is achieved if the generator is Hydrogen filled.
The design of the system shall be such that it is possible to carry out on-load replacement, and/or adjustment of the brushes. All necessary tools and equipment shall be provided to facilitate this work."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_140,"80, 81",7.2.3._Generator_circuit_breaker,"7, 2, 3",content,"text, list_item",7.1._Introduction > 7.2.3._Generator_circuit_breaker,Electrical Systems,Generator Circuit Breaker,Isolated Phase Breaker and Switchgear,,,"IEEE (ANSI) C37.013, IEC 60056, IEC 60694, IEC 60298, IEC 60129, IEC 60044-1&2, IEC 60099-4, IEC 60358, three-pole isolated phase breaker, synchronising generator to grid, SF6 alternator breaker, motorized line breaker switch, motorized earthing switches (generator side / transformer side), voltage transformers (transformer side), current transformer (transformer side), over-voltage discharger (auto-valves), protection capacitor (transformer side), sealed casing welded to isolated phase busbar casing, flexible connections to isolated phase busbars, short-circuit withstand and interrupting capability, generator breaker failure protection (grid code tripping time), two independent opening windings, local and remote open/close control, open delta secondary (VT), blade-type earth switches with interlocks",False,Generator circuit breaker and isolated phase breaker/switchgear requirements; no ACC-related signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains detailed technical requirements and standards for generator circuit breaker (GCB) assembly, protection, interlocks, instrument transformers, and isolated phase busbar interface—strong anchors for matching electrical switchgear/GCB and protection documents.","In particular, the following codes and standards shall be applicable:
- IEEE (ANSI) c37.013
- IEC standards (especially IEC 60056, 60694, 60298, 60129, 60044-1&2, 60099-4 and 60358)
The Gas Turbine shall incorporate a three-pole isolated phase breaker for main alternator on/off switching. These breakers shall be used for synchronising the generator to the grid and shall be designed for the necessary operating conditions and interrupting rate. The generator breaker switchgear panels shall be fitted with the following elements for each pole:
- SF6 alternator breaker in series with a motorized line breaker switch, with the required interlocking devices
- motorized earthing switches, one on the generator side and the other on the transformer side, with the required interlocking devices
- voltage transformers on the transformer side
- current transformer on the transformer side (if required)
- over-voltage discharger (auto-valves) on the transformer side
- protection capacitor on the transformer side
- devices and auxiliary circuits for signals and operations, both electrical and mechanical
Each generator breaker shall include a single-pole (isolated phase) sealed casing. The breaker casing shall be prepared for welding to the isolated phase busbar conductor casings. They shall incorporate viewports to allow visual checking of moving components (isolation switches), without having to open the casing.
The connection of generator breaker system active parts to those of the isolated phase busbars shall be made by means of flexible connection parts.
The breaker shall conduct the maximum generator output current and shall be able to withstand and break the short-circuit currents considering the reestablishment voltages under the most critical operating conditions.
Generator breaker failure protection within the tripping time set in the grid code including balance of equipment tripping shall be provided.
The breaker shall not be able to close if sufficient power is not available for opening. Two independent windings shall be available for opening. One winding is sufficient for closing. It shall be possible to open and close the breaker both locally and remotely.
Voltage transformers shall be installed on the transformer side (for synchronization and protection), with the power and precision required. These transformers shall be wired to the assembly control cabinet. The necessary resistance for protection against transformer resonance shall be fitted inside with open delta secondary. Each of the voltage outputs shall be protected with breaker switches.
To protect against over-voltages and increase the breaker voltage arc extinction effect, capacitor shall be installed on transformer side.
Both sides of the line switch-breaker assembly shall be fitted with blade-type earth switches to permit maintenance work. To this end, the necessary interlocks shall be fitted to both sides (transformer and generator). These connections shall not have current switch ON/OFF capability."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_141,81,7.2.3._Generator_circuit_breaker,"7, 2, 3",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.3._Generator_circuit_breaker,Electrical Systems,Generator Circuit Breaker,Starting System Interface / GCB Omission Note,,,"gas turbine starting system, motorised breaker, remote operation, omission of GCBs for 2 STGs, Exhibit B Part 1",False,Note on motorised breaker and omission of GCBs for STGs; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention.,False,Primarily contractual/acceptance statement about possible omission of GCBs for two STGs with reference to agreement exhibit; minimal technical design detail.,low,True,Only limited technical anchor (motorised breaker if required by starting system); main content is commercial/contractual acceptance and cross-reference.,"If required by the gas turbine starting system, the assembly will also be fitted with a motorised breaker with remote operation possibility.
The Owner accepts omission of GCBs for 2(two) STGs subject to the Contractor satisfying the requirements as specified in Exhibit B, Part 1 of this Agreement."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_142,81,7.2.3.1._CONTROL_CABINET,"7, 2, 3, 1",content,"text, list_item",7.1._Introduction > 7.2.3._Generator_circuit_breaker > 7.2.3.1._CONTROL_CABINET,Electrical Systems,Generator Circuit Breaker,Local Operation and Control Cabinet,,,"local operation and control cabinet, control and monitoring components, Local-Remote switching, breaker operation, isolation switch operation, wiring interconnection terminals, remote signals, trips and alarms",False,Local operation/control cabinet for generator breaker system; no ACC-related equipment or context.,True,,medium,True,"Technical scope is clear (GCB local control cabinet and I/O), but limited detail; still useful for retrieving GCB control cabinet wiring/GA and control philosophy documents.","The generator breaker system shall include a local operation and control cabinet to house the associated elements.
- control and monitoring components
- 'Local-Remote' switching devices
- breaker and isolation switch operation
- wiring interconnection terminals (for local and remote signals, trips and alarms etc)"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_143,82,7.2.3.2._SF6_CONTROL_DEVICES,"7, 2, 3, 2",content,"text, list_item",7.1._Introduction > 7.2.3._Generator_circuit_breaker > 7.2.3.2._SF6_CONTROL_DEVICES,Electrical Systems,Generator Circuit Breaker,SF6 Gas Monitoring and Interlocks,,,"densiometer, SF6 gas compartment, gas pressure verification, maximum/minimum limit indication, gas low pressure/density alarm, gas very low pressure/density interlock, voltage-free contacts, manufacturer-established pressure/density limits",False,SF6 gas monitoring devices for breakers; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,False,"Provides specific technical requirements for SF6 density monitoring, alarms, and interlocks—good retrieval anchors for GCB SF6 system and protection/interlock documentation.","The breakers shall include a densiometer for each independent SF6 gas compartment that permits gas pressure verification with acceptable maximum and minimum limit indications.
These devices shall incorporate independent voltage-free contacts for performing the following functions:
- gas low pressure/density alarm
- gas very low pressure/density interlock
- the operating pressure and density limits shall be established by the manufacturer"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_144,"82, 83",7.2.4.1._GENERAL,"7, 2, 4, 1",content,"text, list_item",7.1._Introduction > 7.2.3._Generator_circuit_breaker > 7.2.4.1._GENERAL,Electrical Systems,Transformers,Oil-Filled and Dry-Type Transformer Requirements,,,"IEC 60076, IEC 60859, oil filled transformers, GSUT cooling ONAN/ONAF/ODAF, UAT cooling ONAN/ONAF, radiators separate or tank-mounted, on-load tap changer (GSUT), off-load tap changer (other transformers), tap range / impedance / losses for full output, oil conservator, oil level indication/alarm, silica gel breather, winding temperature alarm/trip, pressure relief alarm/trip, Buchholz relay gas and surge protection, oil temperature indicator alarm/trip, open concrete enclosure, oil/water catchment sump, oily water separator drainage system, earthing links captive type, dry type transformer IP23DW",False,Transformer (GSUT/UAT) oil-filled/dry-type requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mentioned.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit transformer design/testing standards, cooling types, tap changer requirements, protection devices (Buchholz, temperature, pressure relief), installation/enclosure and drainage interface—strong anchors for transformer specifications and layouts.","The design, construction, performance and testing of oil filled transformers shall be in accordance with IEC 60076. Transformer terminals and their provision will comply with IEC 60859.
Cooling shall be ONAN/ONAF/ODAF type for Generator Step-up Transformer (GSUT) and ONAN/ONAF type for Unit Auxiliary Transformer (UAT). The radiators may be mounted separate from the tank or mounted on the transformer tank.
Step-up transformer shall be equipped with on load tap changers. The other transformers shall be equipped with off load tap changers. The tap range, impedance and losses shall be selected to enable full output under normal conditions which allow for the highest and lowest system voltage operation and shall not be restricted over the specified ambient range.
All oil cooled transformers shall have the following as minimum:
- oil conservator
- oil level indication/alarm
- oil sampling device
- silica gel breather
- winding temperature/alarm/tripping
- pressure relief/alarm/tripping
- Buchholz gas and surge protection/tripping/alarm
- oil temperature indicator with alarm/trip contacts
Each transformer shall be fitted with a gas and surge actuated Buchholz relay of approved make and pattern, having alarm and tripping contacts which close simultaneously in response to both liquid surge and low oil level.
Approved facilities shall be provided for inspection, testing and maintenance access to the gas and oil-actuated relays and conservators associated with the transformers.
Transformers shall be located in suitable open concrete enclosures and including oil/water catchment sumps which discharge to the oily water separator drainage system.
All links contained within the transformer tank for earthing and other purposes shall be readily accessible and the links and their fixings shall be of the captive type.
Dry type transformers shall be located in transformer compartments/housings with a protection class of minimum IP23DW."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_145,"83, 84",7.2.4.2._GENERATOR_STEP-UP_TRANSFORMERS,"7, 2, 4, 2",table,"text, list_item",7.1._Introduction > 7.2.3._Generator_circuit_breaker > 7.2.4.2._GENERATOR_STEP-UP_TRANSFORMERS,Electrical,Power Transformers,Generator Step-Up Transformers,,,"single line diagrams, isolated phase busducts, HV star earthed / MV delta, vector group YNd1, transformer impedance minimum 14% (IEC basis), on-load tap changer (OLTC), Saudi Arabian Grid Code, tap range +/- 10 x 1.1%, IEC 60214, control cabinet IP56, two independent feeders (load-break switches), automatic switchover with time delay relay, restricted earth fault protection ANSI 51TN, transformer differential protection ANSI 87T, overcurrent protection ANSI 50/51, ground overcurrent protection ANSI 51G, overfluxing protection ANSI 24",False,Electrical requirements for generator step-up transformers/OLTC and protections; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention or interface.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit technical design requirements for generator step-up transformers including winding configuration, impedance, OLTC standards/range, cabinet IP rating, feeder redundancy, and protection functions—strong anchors for matching transformer specs, SLDs, and protection schematics in MDL.","Generator transformers shall be provided and shown on the single line diagrams. Generator transformers shall be connected to generator using isolated phase busducts. The generator transformer and the isolated phase busduct system shall be designed to transmit the maximum output from the corresponding unit without any limitation and under all ambient conditions.
The windings will be star in H.V, connected to earth directly, and delta in M.V. with a vectorial group of YNd1.
The generator transformer impedance shall be Minimum 14% (IEC basis).
Generator step up transformers shall be equipped with tap changers to be on load type. On load tap changer (OLTC) shall be provided as required by the Saudi Arabian Grid Code setting range shall be a minimum+/- 10 x 1.1% of rated voltage.
The OLTC shall be in conformity with IEC 60214 (or equivalent ANSI standard). OLTC shall have been type tested, and related type test certificates shall be supplied with the offer. Only designs which have been type tested in accordance with the relevant IEC standards will be accepted.
All equipment related to the OLTC shall be supplied by the original OLTC manufacturer. This is also applicable for tie-in resistors, if provided, as well as all related accessories. License products etc. are not acceptable for any equipment related to OLTC.
The OLTC shall withstand all kinds of through-fault currents without damage. All OLTC connected to uniformly insulated windings shall have at least same LI and AC voltage withstand levels as the related windings. The OLTC tank should be designed that the OLTC can be removed/replaced without removal of transformer windings/core and top cover of the main tank.
The motor drive, plus all auxiliary equipment for operation of the tap changer, shall be incorporated in a rigid control cabinet, protection class IP56 and shall be mounted onto the transformer tank in a convenient floor height. The driving gear shall be of the belt-type or equivalent dry-type gear. Oil filled driving gears are not acceptable. The electrical supply for the control of the cooling units and for motor drive of OLTC shall be provided by means of two independent feeders (load-break switches) installed in the control cabinet.
To cover the event of an electrical supply failure, automatic switchover from the first feeder to the second feeder is required.
Automatic transfer equipment shall include a time delay relay to prevent immediate transfer from normal to emergency source.
Step-Up Generator Transformer shall be provided with the following minimum electrical protections:
- restricted E/F protection (ANSI code 51TN)
- differential protection (ANSO code 87T)
- overcurrent protection (ANSI code 50/51)
- ground O/C protection (ANSI code 51G)
- overfluxing protection (external) ANSI 24"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_146,84,7.2.4.2._GENERATOR_STEP-UP_TRANSFORMERS,"7, 2, 4, 2",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.3._Generator_circuit_breaker > 7.2.4.2._GENERATOR_STEP-UP_TRANSFORMERS,Electrical,Power Transformers,Instrument Transformers (CTs),,,"protection CTs, metering CTs, CT class",False,Generic note on protection/metering CTs for transformers; no ACC signal.,True,,medium,True,"Technical but very brief; indicates requirement for protection/metering CTs on transformers, useful for retrieving protection/measurement design documents but lacks detail (ratios/classes/locations).","All transformers to be equipped with of the appropriate class of protection / metering CTs, as required."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_147,"84, 85",7.2.4.3._UNIT_AUXILIARY_TRANSFORMERS_(UAT),"7, 2, 4, 3",content,"text, list_item",7.1._Introduction > 7.2.3._Generator_circuit_breaker > 7.2.4.3._UNIT_AUXILIARY_TRANSFORMERS_(UAT),Electrical,Power Transformers,Unit Auxiliary Transformers (UAT),,,"UAT connected via isolated phase busduct system, sized for entire unit loads, biggest motor start-up within permissible voltage range, unit start-up duty, HV delta / MV star earthed by resistance, vector group Dyn11, manual tap changer (off-circuit), tap range +/- 2 x 2.5%, rated power and impedance justified by electrical calculations, transformer differential protection ANSI 87T, overcurrent protection ANSI 50/51 at 18 kV side, ground overcurrent protection ANSI 51G, ground detector relay ANSI 64, open delta VT on GCB, protection / metering CTs",False,Unit Auxiliary Transformer (UAT) electrical specs/protections; no ACC-related equipment or context.,True,,high,False,"Clear technical requirements for UAT electrical configuration, tap changer range, sizing basis, and protection/VT/CT interfaces—strong retrieval anchors for transformer datasheets, load flow/short circuit calculations, and protection schematics.","UAT shall be connected to the generator and step-up transformer through the isolated phase busduct system. UAT shall be sized to load the entire electrical loads for it's own unit normal operating allowing the biggest motor start-up and inside permissible voltage range, and during unit start up.
The windings will be delta in HV and star in MV, connected to earth by resistance, with a vectorial group of Dyn11. The HV winding will be provided of a manual tap changer with five positions (off-circuit tap changers, the tappings and range to be +/- 2 x 2.5% of the rated voltage.)
The Unit auxiliary transformers shall be designed for all the ambient conditions at site.
The UAT rated power and impedance should be justified with the electrical calculations.
UAT shall be provided with the following minimum electrical protections:
- differential protection (ANSO code 87T)
- overcurrent protection (ANSI code 50/51) at 18kV side
- ground O/C protection (ANSI code 51G)
- to protect IPB during generator start-up (breaker open), ground detector relay should be included (ANSI code 64) connected to open delta VT on GCB
All transformers to be equipped with the appropriate class of protection / metering CTs, as required."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_148,85,7.2.4.4._MV/LV_TRANSFORMERS,"7, 2, 4, 4",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.3._Generator_circuit_breaker > 7.2.4.4._MV/LV_TRANSFORMERS,Electrical,Power Transformers,MV/LV Transformers,,,"MV/LV transformer sizing for plant power requirements, feed main LV switchboard (bus tie closed, other incomer open), start-up of biggest motor within permissible voltage range, LV copper power busbars connection to LV switchboards, auxiliary transformer numbers/ratings based on load estimates per service/building, oil immersed three-phase outdoor transformer, dry type accepted for indoor installations, GTG package outdoor dry type cast coil transformers by GTG OEM (Exhibit B Part 1), LV neutral solidly grounded, off-circuit tap changing range ±2 x 2.5%",False,MV/LV transformer sizing and installation requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,True,,high,False,"Provides concrete technical criteria for MV/LV transformer selection, installation type (oil/dry), grounding, busbar interface, and tap range—useful for matching transformer specs, LV distribution design, and grounding documents.","The Contractor shall suitably size ML/LV transformers taking into account power requirements of the Plant.
Main MV/LV transformers should be sized to feed all the main LV switchboard (normal operation with bus tie closed and the other incomer open). MV/LV transformers shall be sized to load the entire electrical loads for it's own unit during normal operating allowing the start-up of the biggest motor and inside permissible voltage range.
Connection between MV/LV transformers and LV switchboards should be through LV copper power busbars.
The numbers and ratings of the various auxiliary transformers required around the plant are to be determined by the Contractor based on his load estimates for each service or building.
The LV transformers have to be designed as oil immersed, threephase power transformer for outdoor installation. The dry type can be accepted only for indoor installations; however as an exception only for GTG package, outdoor dry type cast coil rated transformers supplied by GTG OEM can be accepted subject to satisfying Exhibit B, Part 1 requirements.
The transformer neutral point on the low voltage winding shall be solidly grounded.
These transformers will step down from the MV to LV and be fitted with off-circuit tap changing facilities of range ±2 x 2.5% steps"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_149,86,7.2.5._MVsystem_neutral_earthing_resistors,"7, 2, 5",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.5._MVsystem_neutral_earthing_resistors,Electrical,Earthing and Grounding,MV Neutral Earthing Resistors,,,"neutral earthing resistors, metal grid type, rated for 10 seconds, bonded to station earthing system, naturally ventilated weatherproof cubicle, outdoor installation alongside respective equipment",False,MV neutral earthing resistors specification; no ACC-related mention.,True,,high,False,"Explicit technical requirements for MV neutral earthing resistors including type, short-time rating, bonding, enclosure/installation—good anchors for grounding/earthing and MV system design documents.","Where neutral earthing resistors are installed, they shall be of the metal grid type rated for 10 seconds, and bonded to the station earthing system. Each resistor shall be enclosed in a naturally ventilated protected weatherproof cubicle located outdoors alongside their respective equipment."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_150,86,7.2.6._MVand_LV_AC_switchboards,"7, 2, 6",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.6._MVand_LV_AC_switchboards,Electrical Systems,MV and LV AC Switchboards,Switchgear functional requirements,,,"redundant power supplies (control and alarm), Local/Remote selector switch (Switchgear/CCR), switchgear incomer, bus-section, feeder, interconnector circuits, check-synchronising facilities (MV main incomers), MV transfer high speed transfer with short parallel (UAT incomer/start-up incomer), electrical interlocking to prevent paralleling incoming supplies (LV), withdrawable type MV and main LV switchgear, fault levels and thermal withstand time per applicable standards",False,MV/LV switchboards functional requirements; no ACC scope signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains specific MV/LV switchgear design/functional requirements (redundancy, synchronising, interlocking, withdrawable type, fault/withstand criteria) useful for matching to switchgear specifications and electrical design documents.","All main switchboards, MCC, relay panels, control equipment etc. shall be provided with redundant power supplies for control and alarm purposes.
A two position (Local/Remote i.e. Switchgear/CCR) selector switch shall be provided at the switchgear for all switchgear incomer, bussection, feeder and interconnector circuits respectively for switchgear emergency control and isolated testing/CCR remote control.
Check-synchronising facilities shall be included for the main incomers of the MV systems (at least two different sources). MV transfer between UAT incomer and start-up incomer shall be high speed transfer with short parallel.
The LV switchgear system main incomers, interconnectors and bus-section circuits shall exclude check-synchronising facilities but shall be electrically interlocked to prevent the paralleling of two incoming supplies to a switchboard.
Circuit breakers supplied shall be identical to those used for the type tests for which certificates are submitted.
MV switchgear and all main LV switchgear shall be of withdrawable type. Fault levels and thermal withstand time for MV and LV switchboards shall be in accordance with the technical requirements of the appropriate standards."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_151,"86, 87",7.2.6.1._MVSWITCHGEAR,"7, 2, 6, 1",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.6._MVand_LV_AC_switchboards > 7.2.6.1._MVSWITCHGEAR,Electrical Systems,MV Switchgear,Design and standards compliance,,,"IEC 60298 compliance, metal-clad MV switchgear, IEC 60056 MV circuit breakers, withdrawable three-pole vacuum interrupter circuit breakers, nominal voltage 13.8 kV, internal arc classification IAC=AFRL (IEC 62271-200), short-circuit withstand 3 seconds, short-circuit current margin minimum 10% above calculations, minimum IP41 protection class, surge suppressers for switching surge voltages",False,MV switchgear design/standards; no ACC mention.,True,,high,False,"Provides concrete MV switchgear technical requirements (standards, ratings, arc classification, IP class, surge suppression) that can retrieve MV switchgear specs/datasheets and electrical calculations.","MVswitchgear and control gear shall comply with IEC 60298 and shall be metal-clad.
MV circuit breakers shall comply with IEC 60056 and shall be of withdrawable three pole circuit breakers, using vacuum interrupters type.
The MV switchgears shall be designed for a nominal voltage of 13.8 kV, to be internal arc certified IAC=AFRL according IEC 62271-200 and to withstand a short-circuit current of minimum 10% higher than required by calculations for a duration of three (3) seconds.
The mechanical protection class for all MV switchgears shall be minimum IP41.
Where operation of a switching device can give rise to surge voltages which would be detrimental to the insulation of the equipment controlled, surge suppressers shall be installed in the switchgear to control these voltages to a safe level."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_152,87,7.2.6.2._LV_SWITCHGEAR,"7, 2, 6, 2",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.6._MVand_LV_AC_switchboards > 7.2.6.2._LV_SWITCHGEAR,Electrical Systems,LV Switchgear,"Construction, components, and earthing",,,"IEC 60947-2 LV circuit breakers, withdrawable air circuit breakers, moulded case circuit breakers (MCCB), metal-clad multicubicle Form 4 construction, Form 3b for MCC cubicles, segregation of switchgear components, LV contactors class AC3/AC4, GTG OEM 480V AC MCC per NEMA (if IEC requirements satisfied), earthing bar minimum cross-section 240 mm2, spare columns with equipped reserve (two breakers), 20% free space reserve",False,LV switchgear construction/components/earthing; no ACC-related signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains LV switchgear technical specifications (IEC/NEMA references, form of construction, earthing bar size, spare capacity) suitable for matching to LV switchgear/MCC design documents.","LV circuit breakers shall comply with IEC 60947-2. Withdrawable air circuit breakers or moulded case circuit breakers shall be used, unless otherwise approved.
LV switchgear and control gear shall comply with the relevant IEC and/or NEMA standards. It shall be metal-clad and generally of multicubicle of Form 4 construction and Form 3b for MCC cubicles.
Panels for different feeder circuits shall be appropriately equipped with control, metering, alarm and protection devices based on Good Industry Practices and current technology.
The construction shall be such that the various components of the switchgear are segregated electrically from each other.
Moulded case circuit-breakers and miniature circuit-breakers may be used if they are properly selected to withstand the maximum specified short-circuit current level and evidence of acceptable performance is provided by the Contractor.
LV contactors used as motor starters shall be of the air-break type with arc shields and to class AC3/AC4 according to IEC standards. It is acceptable for the GTG OEM only to offer 480V AC MCC built according to NEMA standards provided IEC standard requirements are satisfied under NEMA standards.
An earthing bar with a minimum cross-section of 240mm2 shall run the full length of the switchboards. The Contractor will connect to this bar all metallic parts not forming part of the live circuits and all CT points to be earthed. Chassis of circuit-breakers shall be directly connected to the earthing bar.
Every low voltage switchboards shall have spare columns with equipped reserve (two breakers) and empty space (for future necessities a 20% free space of reserve must be included)"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_153,88,7.2.7._Busbar_and_busduct_systems,"7, 2, 7",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.7._Busbar_and_busduct_systems,Electrical Systems,Busbar and Busduct Systems,Phase isolated busduct design,,,"phase isolated busducts, aluminium enclosures, connections between turbine generators and generator transformer, unit auxiliary transformer connection, pressurised with instrument air (clean and dry), slight overpressure to avoid humidity ingress, enclosure earthed with drain plugs, natural radiation and convection cooling (no forced air-cooling), allowable temperatures per IEC/ANSI standards, outdoor busduct sun shades, steel support structures, flexible busbar terminations, rubber/neoprene bellows, MV switchgear to MV/LV transformers via cables",False,Phase isolated busducts between turbine generators/transformers; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,True,,high,False,"Defines busduct type, interfaces (GTG/transformers/UAT), pressurization medium, cooling, outdoor provisions, and termination details—strong anchors for retrieving busduct specifications and layouts.","Phase isolated busducts with aluminium enclosures shall be used for the connections between the turbine generators and generator transformer and for unit auxiliary transformer connection. These busducts shall be of pressurised with instrument air (clean and dry) to exclude dust and moisture. For connections between MV switchgear and MV/LV transformers cables shall be provided.
The outer enclosure shall be of the self-supporting type and kept at a slight overpressure to avoid ingress of humidity. The enclosure shall be earthed and fitted with drain plugs at the lowest points.
The generated heat shall be dissipated by natural radiation and convection. No forced air-cooling shall be provided.
The allowable temperatures under full-load operation at site conditions shall be as per the relevant IEC/ANSI Standards
Generator and MV (as applicable) busducts shall be pressurised with instrument air (cleaned and dried air), anti-condensation heaters are not necessary.
Outdoor section of busducts shall be provided with sun shades. Complete steel support structures shall be provided. Busducts shall be terminated using flexible busbars and rubber/neoprene bellows."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_154,"88, 89","7.2.8._Electrical_system_control,_indications_and_alarms","7, 2, 8",content,"text, list_item",7.1._Introduction > 7.2.8._Electrical_system_control _indications_and_alarms,Electrical Systems,"Electrical Control, Indications and Alarms",Monitoring and control via DCMS/ECMS from CCR,,,"control, monitoring and protection systems for MV, LV and DC systems, CCR monitoring and control of MV and LV AC systems (excluding LV sub-distribution), ECMS or Plant DCMS, Digital fault Monitoring System, energy meters via Plant DCMS, alarm facia/windows on Electrical Panels and Fire Protection Panels, manual and auto synchronising facilities, display of switchboard voltage and single phase current (main circuits), motors 55 kW and above repeated in CCR via DCMS, motor control via DCMS (exceptions for station minor plant), LV sub-distribution locally controlled; alarms to CCR via DCMS, dual redundant MODBUS serial link (SEC/SA approved) to ESF/switchyard signals, Sequence of Events (SOE) hardwire interface to DCMS",False,Electrical system control/monitoring via DCMS/CCR; no ACC-specific equipment or interfaces mentioned.,True,,high,False,"Contains specific electrical control/monitoring architecture and interfaces (CCR, DCMS/ECMS, MODBUS, SOE, synchronising, metering thresholds) useful for retrieving control system integration and electrical I&C documents.","The Contractor will be responsible for the design, manufacture, supply, installation, testing and commissioning of control, monitoring and protection systems associated with his electrical installation. Control and alarm facilities for the power plant electrical MV, LV and DC systems shall be provided.
The scope of supply shall comprise all necessary control, protection, monitoring and supervisory equipment, cabling and wiring, regardless of whether it is specifically mentioned or not, for safe and efficient operation of the Plant. The control and monitoring of the electrical system and other associated common systems shall include at least the following:
- control and monitoring of MV system via ECMS or Plant DCMS and Digital fault Monitoring System
- control and monitoring of auxiliary power distribution (LV, DC, UPS) via Plant DCMS and Digital fault Monitoring System
- energy meters via Plant DCMS
- alarm facia/windows to be provided on Electrical Panels and Fire Protection Panels
- auxiliary and marshalling cubicles via plant DCMS
MV and LV AC electrical systems (excluding LV sub-distribution systems) shall be monitored and controlled from the CCR including for all transformers, switchgear incomers, bus-section, feeders and interconnector circuits via the plant DCMS. Manual and auto synchronising facilities shall be included for these circuits. The voltage of each switchboard and single phase current of all main circuits (incomers, bus-sections, feeders and interconnectors) shall be displayed locally at each switchgear, and for motors of 55kW and above repeated in the central control room via the plant DCMS.
Motor circuits shall normally be controlled and monitored from the central control room via the plant DCMS, except for station minor plant needing only local control. For motors of 50kW and above the single phase current of each motor circuit shall be displayed locally at each switchboard.
Alternative locations of control of switchgear and motors shall be at the switchgear (under Permit to Work for operation or testing) or plant (for operation or maintenance).
LV sub-distribution systems and the DC systems shall be locally controlled only, but alarms shall be transmitted to the central control room via the plant DCMS.
The switchgear shall include all related circuit prominent coloured LED type status and alarm lamps. The associated protection relays shall also include LED status/alarm indicators or flags.
Communication between the plant DCMS and ESF including the switchyard signal shall be done via a SEC/SA approved method such as a serial link using dual redundant MODBUS protocol for monitoring major switchyard and electrical signals from plant DCMS.
Sequence of Events (SOE), GT and ST operation signals shall be through hardwire interface to the DCMS."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_155,"89, 90",7.2.9._Low_voltage_AC_and_DC_sub-distribution_boards,"7, 2, 9",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.9._Low_voltage_AC_and_DC_sub-distribution_boards,Electrical,Low Voltage Distribution,AC and DC Sub-distribution Boards,,,"IEC 60439, IEC 60937, IEC 60529, Form 2 construction, weatherproof enclosure IPW54, fault current withstand, single phase / three phase and neutral (AC boards), two-pole (DC boards), incomer MCCB isolator, removable MCBs per sub-circuit",False,Low voltage AC/DC sub-distribution board requirements; no mention of ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or related interfaces.,True,,high,False,Contains clear technical requirements and standards for LV AC/DC distribution boards including enclosure rating and protective devices.,"All distribution boards shall comply with IEC 60439, IEC 60937 and other related IEC standards and, for the enclosure, comply with IEC 60529. They shall be of Form 2 construction as a minimum.
All distribution boards situated outside shall be of the weatherproof enclosure type IPW54. They shall be capable of withstanding the associated fault current until the related protection operates.
The AC sub-distribution boards shall be either single phase or three phase and neutral types, and the DC sub-distribution boards shall be of the two-pole type. All distribution boards shall be rated for the full load current of the incoming supply and equipped with an incomer moulded case circuit breaker (MCCB) isolator and with removable miniature circuit breakers (MCBs) to provide overcurrent protection to each sub circuit."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_156,"90, 91",7.2.10._Electric_motors,"7, 2, 10",table,"text, list_item",7.1._Introduction > 7.2.10._Electric_motors,Electrical,Motors and Drives,Electric Motors,,,"IEC 60034, hazardous area certification, MV motor starting current ≤ 5.5× rated, LV motor starting current ≤ 6× rated, squirrel cage induction motor (deep slots/double cage), TEFC or water-cooled, IPW54 (outdoor minimum IPW55), motorised valves IP65, Class F insulation, temperature rise Class B, DC motors direct-on-line with series armature resistance, GTG OEM DC motors 125 VDC, MV motors emergency stop key-reset mushroom head; alarm to central control room, motor service voltage: MV 3-phase 60 Hz >~200 kW; 460 V 3-phase 60 Hz ≤~200 kW, voltage/frequency tolerance 58–62 Hz and 90–110% V; transient 130% V, torque at 80% nominal voltage for 5 s",False,"General electric motor specifications (IEC 60034, IP ratings, starting currents); no ACC/fan deck/fan motor or ACC context mentioned.",True,,high,False,"Provides detailed motor technical requirements (standards, starting limits, enclosure/IP, insulation class, voltage/frequency tolerances, DC motor specifics, E-stop requirements) useful for matching motor datasheets/specifications and electrical studies.","All motors shall be designed and manufactured in accordance with IEC 60034 and other related internationally recognised standards. Motors in hazardous areas shall be suitably certified.
NEMA is accepted, but Contractor shall ensure:
- the starting current of MV motors does not exceed 5.5 times the rated current
- the starting current for LV motors does not exceed 6 times the rated current
- and prove during detailed engineering that there is no disturbance to the electrical system due to LV motors start ups
Motors of the same type must be fully interchangeable and shall comply as far as applicable with IEC standard motor dimensions. AC motors shall be of the squirrel cage induction type provided with either deep slots or double squirrel cages.
All motors shall normally be of the totally enclosed fan-cooled type or water-cooled type, of protection class IPW54 according to IEC recommendations 60034 and 60529. Where motors are installed outdoors a weatherproof design (minimum IPW55) shall be chosen. Motorised valves shall be to protection class IP65.
Motors shall have Class F insulation but the temperature rise shall not exceed Class B.
DC motors and starters shall be designed for high reliability with direct-on-line starting using a permanently connected series resistance in the armature. Service voltage for GTG OEM DC motors can be 125 VDC.
Emergency stop buttons of the stay-put key-reset red button mushroom head type shall be provided adjacent to all MV motors and all lubricating oil pumps. These buttons shall directly trip the motors, prevent a start until reset and alarm their operation to the central control room. The buttons shall all use a common reset key. This requirement may be relaxed for certain essential loads in the GT package as stopping of lube oil to the generator and turbine will severely damage those units.
The service voltages of the motors will be:
- MV, 3-phase AC, 60Hz, for motors above about 200kW
- 460V, 3-phase AC, 60Hz, for motors below and including about 200kW Calculations of the voltage drop at start-up shall be made for each motor to additionally verify the capability to cross line starting
The rating of each motor shall be adequate to meet the requirements of its associated load, without infringing upon the service factor as per NEMA and/or ANSI standards
Motors shall be capable of operating continuously under rated output conditions at any frequency between 58 and 62 Hz and/or with any voltage deviation between 90% and 110% of the nominal voltage. A transient overvoltage of 130% of the nominal voltage shall also be sustainable. Further, the motors shall be capable of delivering the maximum torque as required by the driven machine when running at 80% nominal voltage for a period of 5 seconds without damaging, overheating or slipping."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_157,91,7.2.10._Electric_motors,"7, 2, 10",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.10._Electric_motors,Electrical,Motors and Drives,Motor Starting and Torque Requirements,,,"pullout torque 160% (continuous) / 200% (intermittent), direct-on-line starting (fixed speed AC motors), number of starts per hour per relevant standards, MV motor: two successive starts at operating temperature, LV motor: three successive starts at operating temperature or once every 20 minutes, Class B winding temperature limit, chiller drive start-up not to perturb electrical system, stagger/optimise chiller start-up",False,Motor starting/torque requirements and chiller start-up note; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,False,Contains explicit motor torque and start-duty requirements plus chiller start-up constraints that can map to motor specifications and electrical system studies.,"The pullout torque for continuously loaded motors shall be at least 160% of the rated motor torque and for intermittently loaded motors 200% of the rated motor torque.
Fixed speed AC motors shall be designed for direct on-line starting.
The number of starts in one hour for all motors shall be in compliance with relevant standards and shall be determined during the detailed design stage. At the end of the determined number of starts the temperature of the insulated windings shall not exceed the maximum permitted temperature for class B insulation, and the cage windings shall not suffer damaging temperature rise.
Each MV motor shall be capable of enduring two successive starts with the motor initially at operating temperature and the temperature of the winding at the end of start shall not exceed the Class B limit. Each LV motor shall be capable of withstanding three successive starts under the same conditions or once every twenty minutes without damaging temperature rise.
For the start-up of electrical drives of chillers should not perturb the electrical system, therefore. Contractor shall stagger and optimise the chiller start up process and its configuration adequately accordingly."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_158,92,7.2.11._DC_supply_systems_and_un-interruptible_power_supplies,"7, 2, 11",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.11._DC_supply_systems_and_un-interruptible_power_supplies,Electrical,DC Power and UPS,DC Supply Systems and UPS,,,"DC and UPS system sizing, 2×100% battery chargers, 1×100% Nickel cadmium batteries (VRLA lead-acid subject to approval), DC distribution panels, 2×100% AC inverters, UPS AC distribution panel with two coupled bus sections, 120 V Safe AC system: 2×100% inverters with static bypass; two coupled bus sections, UPS for gas turbines from Plant UPS, battery autonomy 1 hour (emergency consumers), bypass static switch, DC nominal 125 VDC, UPS nominal 120 V, chargers fed from 480 V 60 Hz, simultaneous battery charging and full DC load, recharge fully discharged battery ≤ 10 hours, on-line maintenance facilities",False,Plant-wide DC supply and UPS system sizing; no ACC-related equipment or scope referenced.,True,,high,False,"Defines UPS/DC architecture, redundancy, voltages, autonomy, and charger/battery performance—strong anchors for UPS/DC system design documents and datasheets.","The Contractor shall suitably size DC and UPS systems taking into account power requirements of the Plant. The Contractor will supply UPS and DC systems to meet all the site DC requirements consisting of at least two (2) x 100% battery chargers, one (1) x 100% Nickel cadmium batteries (use of lead acid batteries (VRLA type) instead of Ni-Cd batteries is subject to approval during design stage), DC distribution panels, two (2) x 100% AC Inverters, and AC Distribution panel for UPS consisting of two coupled bus sections. The 120V Safe AC system shall consist of 2 x 100% invertors each equipped with a static by pass switch and a related safe AC distribution consisting of two coupled bus sections. UPS power for the gas turbines shall be provided from the Plant UPS. AC un-interruptible power supplies are to be provided for equipment requiring continuity of supply or equipment that need supply during emergency operation, when the normal power supply is lost. The rated capacity of the batteries shall be adequate for discharge duration of 1 hour for the emergency consumers. The UPS system shall be also be provided with a bypass static switch, autonomy will be 1 hour.
DC nominal voltage level should be 125VDC and UPS nominal voltage level should be 120V. The chargers shall be designed for operation from the 480V, 60Hz supply. Each charger shall be rated to ensure simultaneously battery charging and the supply of the full DC load. Under normal operating conditions, charging of a fully discharged battery will take a maximum of 10 hours. Each battery charger shall be capable of fully charging the battery on initial installation. Battery chargers shall have facilities for on-line maintenance."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_159,"92, 93",7.2.12._Cables_and_installation,"7, 2, 12",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.12._Cables_and_installation,Electrical,Cables,Cables and Installation,,,"cable connection schematic diagrams, terminal diagrams, cable lists / cable schedule, cable route plans, IEC standards (GTG OEM may use NEMA if IEC requirements satisfied), XLPE power cables, max conductor temperature 90°C (continuous), fault temperature ≤ 250°C, PVC LV cables subject to approval, UV-safe insulation for sunlight-exposed trays, control cables multicore PVC insulated, analogue signal cables: common metal tape screen; twisted pairs; separately screened pairs for process computers, control & instrumentation cables armoured for outdoor; shielded, minimum conductor section 0.5 mm², unarmoured I&C cables outdoors acceptable if in conduit/trench/duct bank/tray/ladder, segregation from power cables per international standards, cable labelling; ferrule numbering at terminations, MV/LV cable ends sealed and protected, plug-in terminations per MV switchgear characteristics, installation: trays inside buildings; trenches/trays outside; direct buried armoured, cable trays sized for one-layer plus ≥25% spare by weight and dimensions, separate trays and marshalling boxes per voltage grade",False,"Generic cable design/installation requirements (XLPE, trays, segregation); no ACC-specific mention.",True,,high,False,"Contains explicit cable material/temperature ratings, shielding/armouring rules, installation methods, segregation, and required cable engineering outputs (lists/schedules/routes) useful for retrieving cable schedules, routing layouts, and electrical installation specs.","The Contractor shall undertake all relevant design and engineering of the cable systems for equipment in his supply, and shall prepare the cable connection schematic diagrams, terminal diagrams, cable lists, cable schedule and the cable route plans. All cables shall comply with the relevant IEC standards. It is acceptable for the GTG OEM only to offer cables according to NEMA standards provided IEC standard requirements are satisfied under NEMA standards.
All power cables shall be of cross-linked polyethylene (XLPE) and shall be capable of continuous operation at the highest system voltage specified with a maximum conductor temperature of 90°C, under rated conditions and a maximum temperature under fault conditions of not more than 250°C. Use of PVC cables for LV duty shall be subject to approval.
All power and control cables capacity sizing shall be in accordance with the temperatures given in Exhibit B, Part 1. Cables installed on cable trays that may be exposed to sunlight during operation shall have ultra violet safe insulation.
Control cables shall be multicore, PVC insulated types withstanding without deterioration the conditions prevailing at each installation location. Cables for analogue signals shall have a common screen of metal tape, cores shall be twisted by pairs, for process computers, each pair shall be separately screened. Control and instrumentation cables shall be armoured for outdoor applications and all cables shall be shielded, with a minimum conductor section of 0.5mm2, alternatively un-armoured cables for control and instrumentation is acceptable for outdoor application if laid on conduit, trench, duct bank, tray, ladder (i.e., with suitable protection). Cables above ground shall be laid in trays or on ladder racks or conduits and shall be segregated from power cables in accordance with International standards. Cables shall be labelled and conductors shall be identified at terminations by ferrule number or suitable proprietary method.
Every cable end of MV/LV power cables shall be sealed and protected in an approved manner consistent with the voltage levels, cable type, and place of installation.
""Plug-in"" cables terminations (if supplied) shall be in accordance with the characteristics of the MV switchgears.
MV and LV cables are generally to be laid in cable trays (inside buildings) and in cable trenches and/or cable trays (outside buildings). Any cables direct buried shall be armoured.
Cables running inside buildings or concrete trenches shall be laid on trays. The trays shall be of adequate strength and size to carry in one layer, the weight, number and configuration required for each tray plus additional cables, not less than 25% by weight and dimensions, in excess of those installed.
Segregation of the various services and voltage levels shall be achieved by use of separate trays and marshalling boxes (when required) separate for each voltage grade of cable used. ."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_160,93,7.2.12._Cables_and_installation,"7, 2, 12",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.12._Cables_and_installation,Electrical,Cables and Installation,Cable Routing and Raceway,,,"data highway communication link, spatially separated routing, galvanised steel conduits, Flame Retardant Low Smoke (FRLS) cables, IEC-332 Part 3, cable tray routing above grade, underground duct banks, cable trenches, MV and LV interconnection cables",False,Cable routing/raceway guidance for plant interconnection; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit technical requirements for cable types (FRLS, IEC-332-3) and routing methods (tray, conduits, duct banks/trenches) including MV/LV interconnection scope.","For the data highway communication link, spatially separated routing with the cables running in galvanised steel conduits of approved type shall be supplied and installed.
All power, control and instrumentation cables shall be Flame Retardant Low Smoke type and shall comply with IEC-332 Part 3.
Wherever practical, all electrical/instrumentation circuit shall be routed above grade via a cable tray. For the outdoor areas, underground duct banks and/or cable trenches shall be used where exposed raceway is not practical.
Additionally, Contractor shall consider the required MV and LV cables for the power plants interconnection, all the necessary works will be in Contractor scope (required cable trenches, cable supporting systems etc.) to carry out the interconnections."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_161,94,7.2.12.1._CABLE_TRAYS,"7, 2, 12, 1",content,"text, list_item",7.1._Introduction > 7.2.12._Cables_and_installation > 7.2.12.1._CABLE_TRAYS,Electrical,Cable Trays,Cable Tray Segregation and Installation Criteria,,,"heavy duty hot dipped galvanized epoxy coated ladder type cable trays, tray covers (indoor physical protection / outdoor sunlight protection), segregation: MV power / LV AC ≤600VAC / 230VAC-125VDC control / analogue low level & 24VDC instrumentation / fibre optic, continuous barrier separation, vertical configuration MV above LV above control above instrumentation, minimum vertical spacing 300mm, minimum clearance 200mm to beams/piping, cross member spacing max 300mm centers, unistrut type supports and hangers",False,Cable tray types and segregation criteria; no ACC-related keywords or context.,True,,high,False,"Provides detailed cable tray construction, segregation, spacing/clearance, and support system requirements useful for matching to cable tray layout/specification documents.","Cable trays shall be used to support cables.
Heavy duty, hot dipped galvanized and epoxy coated, ladder type with channel side cable trays shall be used for outdoor cable installations.
Within Indoor areas, tray covers shall be used where physical protection of cable is needed. For outdoor installation, tray covers will be used on the highest level to protect cables from direct sunlight and on other levels to protect from water etc. as required.
Cable tray shall be segregated, top to down, as follows:
- medium voltage power circuits
- low voltage AC power circuits not over than 600VAC
- 230VAC/125VDC control circuits
- analogue low level signal or other analogue signal and 24VDC signal from instrumentation/other devices
- fibre optical cable (as long as it is proven by Contractor that no interference will occur providing signal cable and fibre optical cable in same trays are acceptable)
Low level control cable may be run in the low voltage power cable tray or 230 VAC/125VDC control cable tray when separated by a continuous barrier to prevent mixing.
In a vertical configuration, low voltage power cable trays shall be located below medium voltage power cable trays; control cable trays shall be located below low voltage power cable trays; instrumentation cable trays will be located below control cable trays.
In general, average maintained minimum vertical spacing for cable tray shall be 300mm, measured from the bottom of the upper tray to the top of the lower tray.
At least a 200mm clearance shall be maintained between the top of a tray and beams, piping, etc., to facilitate installation of cables in tray.
The cross member or lung of cable tray shall be spaced maximum of 300mm on centers.
Complete support system for the cable trays including all hangers, braces, brackets, inserts, anchors, fabricated structures, hardware and incidental materials utilizing unistrut type or equal supports and hangers wherever possible shall be provided."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_162,95,7.2.12.2._RIGID_METAL_CONDUIT,"7, 2, 12, 2",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.12._Cables_and_installation > 7.2.12.2._RIGID_METAL_CONDUIT,Electrical,Conduits,Rigid Metal Conduit Requirements,,,"hot dipped galvanized rigid steel conduit (exposed), minimum conduit size 22mm (power/lighting), minimum conduit size 16mm (communication/other), clamp-type fasteners to cable tray, insulated conduit grounding bushing, solid insulated bushings for conduit 42mm and smaller, threaded hubs for sheet metal enclosures, conduit through concrete slabs: galvanized steel pipe extend 150mm above slab, PVC sleeve through slabs with mandatory galvanized steel pipe, unistrut type conduit hangers/supports, conduit body not used for grounding",False,Rigid metal conduit installation requirements; no ACC scope or references.,True,,high,False,"Contains specific conduit material, sizing, grounding/bushing, slab penetration, and support requirements suitable for retrieving conduit/raceway specifications and installation details.","In general, hot dipped galvanized, rigid steel conduit shall be used for exposed conduit. The minimum size for power and lighting circuits shall be 22mm, and for communication and other circuits will be 16mm.
The conduit shall be securely fastened to the cable tray with clamp-type fasteners, and the end of the conduit shall be equipped with an insulated conduit grounding bushing.
Solid insulated bushings shall be provided for all conduit 42mm and smaller when not terminated in a box.
For rigid conduits enter sheet metal enclosure, threaded hubs shall be used.
When conduit passes through concrete slabs, galvanized steel pipe which extended 150mm above the surface of slab shall be provided. Conduit should be made of galvanized steel and should extend 150mm when passing through concrete. Contractor can use PVC to cross concrete slabs, and galvanized steel pipe can pass through this PVC pipe. It is mandatory to install galvanized steel pipes.
Complete galvanized support system including all hangers, brackets, inserts, anchors, steel structures and hardware shall be provided.
Unistrut type or equal conduit hangers and supports shall be used.
Conduit body shall not be used for grounding purposes."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_163,95,7.2.12.3._LIQUID_TIGHT_FLEXIBLE_METAL_CONDUIT,"7, 2, 12, 3",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.12._Cables_and_installation > 7.2.12.3._LIQUID_TIGHT_FLEXIBLE_METAL_CONDUIT,Electrical,Conduits,Liquid Tight Flexible Metal Conduit,,,"liquid tight flexible conduit, between motor terminal boxes and rigid conduit, equipment subject to vibration/mechanical adjustment, galvanized steel flexible tubing, synthetic jacket extruded over tubing, minimum length 12x conduit diameter, minimum 300mm",False,Liquid tight flexible conduit requirements near motors/equipment; no ACC-specific mention.,True,,high,False,"Defines application and construction/length criteria for liquid-tight flexible conduit, a clear technical installation requirement.","Liquid tight flexible conduit will be used between motor terminal boxes, or other equipment subject to vibration and/or mechanical adjustment and the rigid conduit systems.
Liquid tight flexible conduit to be used will be a type fabricated of galvanized steel conduit flexible tubing with a synthetic jacket extruded over the tubing.
The length of this conduit is not less than twelve times the conduit diameter (trade size), minimum 300mm."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_164,"95, 96",7.2.12.4._DUCT_BANKS_AND_MANHOLES,"7, 2, 12, 4",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.12._Cables_and_installation > 7.2.12.4._DUCT_BANKS_AND_MANHOLES,Electrical,Underground Raceway,Duct Banks and Manholes,,,"underground raceway: heavy wall HDPVC conduit or hot-dipped galvanized steel conduit encased in concrete, minimum duct size 75mm, nonmagnetic spacers/supports, 75mm minimum conduit spacing (outer surfaces), reinforced concrete encasement, 75mm minimum concrete cover (sides/top/bottom), top of encasement 300mm below finished grade, ground wire along duct bank route, manholes as pulling points, maximum 90m between manholes, pulling eyes on manhole walls, precast or cast in situ manholes, manhole floor slope to sump or drain to drainage system, transition to galvanized steel conduit last 2.4m and terminate 150mm above grade, manhole required if bend exceeds 2.7m",False,Underground duct banks and manholes requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,False,"Provides detailed underground duct bank/manhole design and installation parameters (materials, spacing, cover, pulling lengths, transitions) useful for matching to underground electrical/civil interface documents.","In general, all underground raceway shall use heavy wall high density polyvinyl chloride conduit or hot-dipped galvanized steel conduit encased in concrete.
Where practicable, duct size shall be standardized and minimum may be 75mm .
Duct banks will be separated and supported by using nonmagnetic materials to keep 75mm minimum spacing between the outer surfaces of the conduits.
All duct banks shall be provided with a reinforced concrete encasement. Minimum of 75mm concrete covering on sides, top and bottom will be provided. No waterproofing of the encasement will be considered.
The top of the duct bank encasement shall keep a minimum distance of 300mm below finished grade where applicable.
Ground wire that follows routes of duct banks may be laid directly on top or side of duct bank encasements.
Manholes shall be provided in underground duct runs so that duct lengths will not exceed those which will develop pulling tensions greater than allowed by the cable manufacturers. The maximum length of a duct bank between manholes (pulling points) will not exceed 90m. Pulling eyes will be installed on walls of manholes.
Manholes will be of the precast or cast in situ type.
Floors of manholes shall have slope to a sump in one corner or a drain pipe to the drainage system.
Where a duct bank turns upward for continuation above grade, the last 2.4m (8 feet) of the underground portion may include a transition to heavy-wall hot-dipped galvanized steel conduit and terminate approximately 150 mm above grade. If a bend exceeds 2.7m then manholes shall be provided."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_165,"96, 97",7.2.13.1._OFFICE_TELEPHONE_AND_LAN_SYSTEM,"7, 2, 13, 1",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.12._Cables_and_installation > 7.2.13.1._OFFICE_TELEPHONE_AND_LAN_SYSTEM,Cables and Installation,Telecommunications,Office Telephone and LAN System,,,"office telephone and LAN system, administration building, workshop and stores building, gatehouse, main distribution frame (MDF), 1 Gbit/s Ethernet (1000 Base-x), ring main building connections, optical fibre with ST-connectors, patch bay / patch bays, Cat 7 data cable, underground ducts and joint boxes, switches and routers, virtual IP PBX compatibility, cable testing and test protocol",False,"Office telephone and LAN system for buildings (MDF, switches/routers, cabling); no ACC mention or interface.",True,,high,False,"Contains explicit technical requirements and components for site office telecom/LAN infrastructure including performance (1 Gbit/s), topology (ring mains), fibre termination, cabling types, and interfaces (virtual IP PBX, Owner LAN servers).","The Contractor shall provide and install a complete office telephone and LAN system for the administration building, workshop and stores building and gatehouse comprising a main distribution frame, switches, routers, a full site cabling network including underground ducts, joint boxes, external and internal cabling, telephone/data sockets, telephone handsets and all other appropriate equipment.
The Office LAN shall be designed to operate at 1 Gbit/s (1000 Base-x) Ethernet. The buildings shall be connected by ring mains which end in data distribution boxes. The optical fibres shall be equipped with the pigtails via ST-connectors which are connected to the patch bay.
The components of the Office LAN system to be provided by the Contractor shall include patch bays, cubicle for patch bays, LAN- board, data sockets, data cable cat 7, fibre optical cable, testing of the cables including test protocol, switches and routers. The virtual PBX and LAN servers are to be provided by others.
The Office telephone system shall be compatible with the requirements of local telecom company for a virtual IP PBX.
The Office LAN system shall be compatible with the requirements of the Owner's LAN servers."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_166,97,7.2.13.2._INTERNAL_TELEPHONE_SYSTEM,"7, 2, 13, 2",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.12._Cables_and_installation > 7.2.13.2._INTERNAL_TELEPHONE_SYSTEM,Cables and Installation,Telecommunications,Internal Telephone System,,,"internal telephone system, PABX, main distribution frame (MDF), site cabling network, underground ducts, joint boxes, telephone sockets and handsets, capacity 175 extensions, extendible to 100 (as stated), background noise suppressed units with acoustic hoods, weatherproof telephone units, factory testing, site testing and commissioning, system documentation (handbooks, drawings), spares, tools and test equipment",False,Internal telephone/PABX system requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,True,Strong telecom system technical scope including PABX capacity and installation/testing/commissioning; needs review because the phrase 'extendible to 100' appears inconsistent with 'capacity for 175 extensions' but is reproduced as written.,"The Contractor shall provide and install a complete internal telephone system for the site comprising PABX, main distribution frame, a full site cabling network including underground ducts, joint boxes, external and internal cabling, telephone sockets, telephone handsets and all other appropriate equipment.
The PABX shall be state of the art and shall have an equipped capacity for 175 extensions, extendible to 100 without major hardware or software changes.
The site telephone cabling provided shall be capable of supporting clear voice communications.
Telephone instruments shall be provided in all site strategic areas, with two telephones in the administration building and one telephone in the main gatehouse. These instruments shall be provided to suit the use and location and shall include basic wall and desk mounted units, background noise suppressed units with acoustic hoods for noisy plant areas and weatherproof units for external areas.
The Work shall include the design, supply, factory testing, delivery, installation, site testing and commissioning of the telephone system. The provision shall include full system documentation, including handbooks and drawings, spares, consumables, tools and test equipment."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_167,97,7.2.13.3._SIREN_SYSTEM,"7, 2, 13, 3",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.12._Cables_and_installation > 7.2.13.3._SIREN_SYSTEM,Cables and Installation,Communications and Alarms,Siren System,,,"station siren system, sound level 5 dB above ambient, loudspeaker type selection for environment, PVC multicore armoured cables, cable trays, separation from other services",False,Station siren system and wiring/cables; no ACC-related equipment or context.,True,,medium,False,Defines performance criterion (5 dB above ambient) and installation/wiring requirements for site siren system.,"A station siren system shall provide a sound level to a minimum of 5dB above ambient levels throughout the site.
Consideration shall be given to the type of loudspeakers required to be suitable for the environment to which they will be subjected.
Wiring to the sirens shall generally be by means of PVC multicore armoured cables clipped to cable trays. Consideration shall be given to separation from other services."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_168,98,7.2.13.4._CCTV,"7, 2, 13, 4",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.12._Cables_and_installation > 7.2.13.4._CCTV,Cables and Installation,Security Systems,CCTV,,,"CCTV (Closed Circuit Television), security requirements, SEC/SA standards, Exhibit B Part 1, regulatory requirements, process monitoring not required unless requested",False,CCTV security requirements per standards; no ACC mention.,True,,medium,True,"Provides standards/regulatory compliance anchors for CCTV but lacks technical detail (camera types, coverage, architecture); still useful for retrieving security/CCTV specification documents.","CCTV requirements for security shall be provided as per SEC/SA standards specified in Exhibit B, Part 1 and regulatory requirements. Process monitoring is not required unless specifically requested in SEC / SA standards specified in Exhibit B, Part 1 or in regulatory requirements."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_169,98,7.2.14._Lighting_requirements,"7, 2, 14",content,"text, table",7.1._Introduction > 7.2.14._Lighting_requirements,Electrical Building Services,Lighting,Illumination Levels and Glare Criteria,,,"lighting requirements table, CCR glare index 16, Control & Relay rooms service lux 400, offices/laboratories/first aid rooms service lux 300, workshops service lux 300, computer & electronic equipment rooms service lux 400, switchrooms/relay rooms service lux 300, turbine hall service lux 100/200, building corridors & stairways service lux 200, toilets service lux 150, transformer bays & boiler areas service lux 100, water treatment plant service lux 200, cableways and cable basements service lux 50, outdoor areas not listed service lux 50, roadways/vehicle yards/car parks service lux 30, site perimeter fences service lux 20, area between HRSGs service lux 30, access ladders/stairways/platforms service lux 50",False,"General plant lighting illumination levels table (CCR, turbine hall, HRSG area, etc.); no ACC referenced.",True,,high,False,"Contains explicit lighting design criteria (lux levels and glare index) by room/area, useful for matching lighting design criteria/specifications and area lighting layouts.","Lighting requirements are defined in the following table.
Control & Relay rooms, desks and control surfaces (controllable lighting levels) - GLARE Index of 16 to be applied in the CCR., Service LUX = 400*. Control & Relay rooms, desks and control surfaces (controllable lighting levels) - GLARE Index of 16 to be applied in the CCR., Illumination = . Offices, laboratories and First Aid Rooms, Service LUX = 300. Offices, laboratories and First Aid Rooms, Illumination = . Workshops (General), Service LUX = 300. Workshops (General), Illumination = . Gate House &Rest Rooms, Service LUX = 300. Gate House &Rest Rooms, Illumination = . Computer &Electronic Equipment Rooms, Service LUX = 400. Computer &Electronic Equipment Rooms, Illumination = . Switchrooms &relay rooms, Service LUX = 300. Switchrooms &relay rooms, Illumination = . Turbine Hall (general/operating station), Service LUX = 100/200. Turbine Hall (general/operating station), Illumination = . Building corridors &Stairways, Service LUX = 200. Building corridors &Stairways, Illumination = . Toilets, Service LUX = 150. Toilets, Illumination = . All indoor areas not specifically listed, Service LUX = 200. All indoor areas not specifically listed, Illumination = . Transformer bays &Boiler areas, Service LUX = 100. Transformer bays &Boiler areas, Illumination = . Water treatment plant, Service LUX = 200. Water treatment plant, Illumination = . Cableways and cable basements, Service LUX = 50. Cableways and cable basements, Illumination = . Outdoor areas not specifically listed, Service LUX = 50. Outdoor areas not specifically listed, Illumination = . Roadways vehicle yards &car parks, Service LUX = 30. Roadways vehicle yards &car parks, Illumination = . Site perimeter fences, Service LUX = 20. Site perimeter fences, Illumination = . Area between HRSGs, Service LUX = 30. Area between HRSGs, Illumination = . Access ladders, stairways &platforms, Service LUX = 50. Access ladders, stairways &platforms, Illumination = "
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_170,99,"7.2.15._Earthing_system_design,_bonding_and_lightning_protection","7, 2, 15",content,text,7.1._Introduction > 7.2.15._Earthing_system_design _bonding_and_lightning_protection,Electrical,Earthing and Bonding,Lightning Protection,,,"copper conductors, earth rods, earthing network, bonding of electrical equipment, motors transformers switchboards control boards relay boards, potential gradient control system, switchgear rooms, touch and step voltages, roof collectors and down conductors, earthing electrodes, NFPA lightning protection standards, earthing calculations",False,General earthing/bonding/lightning protection requirements; no ACC-specific scope or mention.,True,,high,False,"Clear technical requirements for plant earthing/bonding, potential gradient control, and lightning protection including NFPA reference and earthing calculations (touch/step voltages).","The scope of supply shall include all required copper conductors, and earth rods, connecting material, and all attachment material to make up a complete, safe and reliable system. All electrical equipment such as motors, transformers, switchboards, control boards, relay and auxiliary relay boards, and all other subsidiary electrical equipment, as well as all metal parts of the civil structure or the mechanical equipment which might be subject to earth fault currents shall be connected to this network.
The Contractor will provide a potential gradient control system for the switchgear rooms and all such locations where dangerous surface voltage gradients may occur.
Each building roof or civil structure protective cover shall be provided with a lightning protection system composed of roof collectors and down conductors of adequate dimensions and spacing connected to the earthing electrodes.
Metallic structures or equipment housings located on the roofs of buildings shall be connected to the lightning protection conductors, with the equipment earthed via the earthing network.
The lightning protection system shall be bonded to the Plant's earthing system and shall be according to the applicable NFPA and/or other internationally recognised standards.
It shall be the responsibility of Contractor to ensure that proper and safe earthing is provided for the plant; Earthing calculations should be carried out (dissipate electric currents into the earth and no danger to electrical shock - touch and step voltages values)."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_172,100,8.1._Degree_of_automation,"8, 1",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation,Plant Control and Automation,Degree of Automation,Central Control Room Operation Concept,,,"fully automated processes, main control room, Central Control Room (CCR) principle, high reliability and availability, minimum operator intervention, automated start-ups and shutdowns, single GT/HRSG operation, Distributed Control and Monitoring System (DCMS), interfaces to turbine control systems, minimize subcontrol systems, two-operator normal operation",False,Plant-wide automation/CCR/DCMS concept; no ACC-specific controls or interfaces mentioned.,True,,high,False,"Defines plant-wide automation concept, CCR operating philosophy, DCMS interfacing, and operator staffing/operation requirements—strong anchors for control system design documents.","All the processes shall be completely automated and they shall be controlled from the main control room.
The control and automation systems of the Plant shall be designed and realised using a proven, state of the art modern control system and computer technology as specified in Exhibit B, Part 1 and shall also fulfil the following requirements:
- the plant can be run efficiently and safely
- the plant shall be operated in the most cost effective manner
- the Plant operation is to be based on Central Control Room (CCR) principle
- the Plant shall be designed with a view to high reliability and availability with a minimum of operator intervention being necessary during the normal operation
- all the normal operations including start-ups and shutdown (exception can be given after long term outages) of single GT/HRSGs or whole Plant shall be performed by the operator in the permanently manned CCR without any dedicated field operations
The major Plant components such as gas turbines can have their own control systems with interfaces to the Distributed Control and Monitoring system (DCMS) so that it is fully transparent to the operator i.e. operator interface is identical irrespective of actual control system used. However, number of control systems interfaced to the main DCMS (subcontrol systems) is to be kept at a minimum. All these sub-systems shall be operated through the main DCMS.
There may be separate local control rooms where appropriate sub-processes can be monitored as may be necessary time-to-time for maintenance purposes but not permanently and all necessary data and information and control shall be managed from the Central Control Room (CCR).
The Plant automation and operating concept shall be designed to enable fully automated normal operation (including start-ups and shutdowns) with two operators carrying out all control and monitoring tasks in normal operation in the main control room."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_173,101,8.1.1._Software_security_management,"8, 1, 1",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.1.1._Software_security_management,Plant Control and Automation,Software Security Management,Backup and Escrow Provisions,,,"software package/module backup copy, secure repositories, software upgrade services, coded listings of software, on-site backup copies during project, software ownership and licensee details, escrow agreement, escrow agent proforma agreement, classification and listing of contract software",False,Software security/backup/escrow provisions; no ACC signal.,True,,medium,False,"Contains control-system software lifecycle/security requirements (backup, escrow, repositories, upgrade services) that can map to existing automation/cyber/software management specifications.","In the event of damage to a contract software package or module, a back-up copy shall be readily available to reinstate the system.
Safeguards shall be included to protect Owner against the eventuality of the Contractor or any sub-contractor and/or any system software authors and/or any suppliers ceasing to exist, or ceasing to be able to support the software for any part of this Agreement. The Contractor shall include for the provision of an escrow agreement if required by Owner.
The Contractor shall carry out the following:-
- classification and listing of all contract software
- provision of the back-up software required in this Specification
- the placing of the back-up software into secure repositories
- the establishment of software upgrade services
- the provision of coded listings of software where appropriate
- maintaining back-up copies of all software on site while project is active
- providing details of Ownership, licensee, for all software supplied
- providing details of proposed escrow agent and proforma agreement"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_174,"101, 102",8.1.2._Lifetime_system_maintenance_support,"8, 1, 2",table,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.1.2._Lifetime_system_maintenance_support,Plant Control and Automation,Lifetime Support and Maintainability,HMI / MMI Functional Requirements,,,"lifetime plant support for control systems, spare parts and engineering support, Middle East regional support office, Man Machine Interface (MMI), functional keyboards for plant operation, free configuration graphic pages, animated plant flow diagram displays, alarm summary pages by priority, trend display minimum 6 analogue variables, colour printer status reports and history, laser jet colour printer for HMI displays, event chronological recording, mass and power balance, operating point displays with limit curves",False,Control system lifetime support and HMI/MMI functional requirements; no ACC referenced.,True,,high,False,"Strong technical anchors for control system/HMI design, alarm management, trending, reporting/printing, and lifetime support requirements.","It is a requirement of this Contract that system support for all control systems proposed shall be available for the lifetime of the plant. Such support is to include spare parts and engineering, via an office established in the Middle East region close to the country of installation of the plant Man Machine Interface (MMI)
Functional keyboards shall be used for the Plant operation, while for all system service and management functions the ordinary alphanumeric keyboards may be used.
Free configuration graphic pages shall be available for the animated display of the Plant flow diagram, with the indication of the control parts and measurement points. These graphic displays shall be designed considering operating and monitoring of processes without having too many shifts of displays during critical phases (like start-up of the Plant).
A general summary page of all the alarms, organised in subpages and selectable according to priority levels, shall be available. The subdivision according to priority shall meet the requirement that the operator is warned with alarm signals of higher priority, requesting his intervention and his attention is not diverted by less priority signals not requesting any specific intervention or can be examined with longer times.
The graphic representation of the trends of freely selectable analogue variables (minimum 6 at the same time) shall be possible. The time basis could be freely selectable.
It shall be possible to obtain on a colour printer the status reports and the history of variables measured and/or calculated values, operator's actions, alarms, etc. from the operator's console.
A laser jet colour printer shall be provided for printing copies of the HMI video displays.
The automation systems of independent auxiliary plants and other minor systems dedicated to special supervision functions that can foresee local control panels, shall however supply the alarms and other important information of their plants on the man machine interface (MMI) in the control room. This information shall be in the same form as much as possible in all places of operation.
Information for the operator shall be available, such as:
- calculation of the Plant effectiveness
- mass and power balance
- storage and display of rating trends of the Plant in particular conditions
- storage of the Plant operating data, for historic file
- event chronological recording, for printing in case of malfunction
Graphic displays could contain the following animated and interactive representations:
- plant mimics, highlighting the Auto/Manual status and the position of the controlled parts, the controlled variables, the variables that can be modified, etc.
- control loop diagrams of complicated loops
- automation sequences, highlighting the progress status
- control stations to be used in particular operation conditions
- operating point displays: shall include, on an appropriate reference system in engineering units, the limit curves (with dynamic updating possibility) and the current operation point
- for each controlled part, the operation status and the presence of possible failures shall be highlighted, that could be distinguished through colour and blink attributes"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_175,102,8.1.2._Lifetime_system_maintenance_support,"8, 1, 2",content,text,8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.1.2._Lifetime_system_maintenance_support,Plant Control and Automation,Control System Interfaces,OPC Data Exchange Protocol,,,"OPC protocol, data exchange, office systems integration, plant management systems, MMS, inventory and procurement systems, document management systems",False,OPC protocol for data exchange to office/plant systems; no ACC mention.,True,,high,False,Explicit technical requirement for OPC-based integration between plant control systems and enterprise/office systems—useful for interface/control network documentation retrieval.,"OPC shall be used as common protocol for data exchange to office and plant systems (such as plant management systems, MMS, inventory and procurement systems, document management systems, etc.)."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_176,103,8.1.3._Engineer's_programming_terminal,"8, 1, 3",content,text,8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.1.3._Engineer's_programming_terminal,Degree of Automation,Engineer's Programming Terminal,Engineering Workstations and Software Tools,,,"engineering programming terminals, DCMS, GT control system, ST control system, online program modification, debugging utilities, forced I/O values (override inputs), plant database point tagging, background mode development, monitors and printers for program development",False,Discusses engineering programming terminals for DCMS/GT/ST control systems; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention or ACC-specific signals.,True,,high,False,Defines functional requirements for engineering/programming terminals and online debugging/forcing capabilities tied to DCMS and turbine control systems.,"Separate Engineering programming terminals shall be provided for the DCMS, GT, ST and other control systems to permit program modification, debugging etc., on-line. Operational utilities shall be provided to support data input-output, data storage, monitors and printers which shall be provided for the program development facilities.
It shall be possible to over-ride actual inputs with forced values for all points in the plant database. Points so forced shall be tagged to that effect. It shall be possible to develop and debug new application software packages allied to the process. This facility shall be accommodated in background mode. Debugging facilities shall permit simple and effective debugging of application programs."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_177,"103, 104",8.1.3._Engineer's_programming_terminal,,list,"list_item, text",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.1.3._Engineer's_programming_terminal,Degree of Automation,Engineer's Programming Terminal,DCMS Architecture and Interfaces,,,"Distributed Control System (DCMS), Safety-related system (SRS), PLCs for GT/ST and water treatment plant, operator workstations monitoring packaged PLCs, hardwired interface signals, Modbus, Profibus DP, remote I/O cabinets, control building electronics room, IP55 minimum (non-air-conditioned), IP42 minimum (air-conditioned I&C cabinets), IEC standards compliance",False,"DCMS architecture/interfaces (PLCs, Modbus/Profibus, I&C cabinets IP ratings); no ACC-related equipment or references.",True,,high,False,"Contains concrete I&C system architecture requirements, interfaces/protocols, cabinet location, and IP protection degrees useful for retrieving control system design documents/specs.","- main control system (DCMS)
- protection System (SRS)
- separate control systems (if necessary) for gas turbine, steam turbine, water treatment plant, etc. (PLCs)
- interface to other systems outside power plant
- control room devices for MMI (Man Machine Interface)
- field instrumentation and data acquisition
- CEMS
The Plant shall be controlled and monitored via a Distributed Control System (DCMS). The usage of separate programmable logic controllers (PLCs) or other separate and specialised control systems shall be minimized. Typical and acceptable reasons for a specialised control system can be high speed of response as typically required at gas turbine and steam turbine control, or special safety requirements, which may require a separate safety-related system (e.g. for boiler protection).
If specialised separate control systems or pre-packaged PLCs are supplied, they shall be monitored and operated solely by the operator workstations of the DCMS. The interface to the DCMS shall be either of conventional hardwired type (small amount of signals) or of a well-known communication data link type (e.g. Modbus or Profibus DP).
The cabinets containing the control system electronics shall be placed in a room in the control building. Electronic cabinets dedicated to signal acquisition (remote I/O- cabinets) could be installed at field. For all the equipment not installed in air-conditioned environment, provisions shall be made for an adequate protection degree (IP 55 minimum).
All indoor I&C cabinets installed in air conditioned environment shall be of min. IP 42 protection ( IP 41 acceptable for cabinets located far from any source of water dripping - doors, windows, etc)
The design of the automation system shall generally comply with IEC standards."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_178,104,8.3.1._General_requirements,"8, 3, 1",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.1._General_requirements,Degree of Automation,General Requirements,DCMS Automation Functions,,,"DCMS automation functions, open loop drive controls, sequence controls, equipment protection (pumps, valves), closed loop control, man-machine interface (displays, trends), alarm annunciation, self-diagnostics, signal conditioning and power supply for field equipment, LAN interface, redundancy (processors, communication buses, supplies), operator terminal authorisation",False,"General DCMS automation functions (controls, alarms, redundancy); no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.",True,,high,False,Lists required DCMS functional scope and redundancy/authorization requirements that map to control system functional design/specification documents.,"Control and monitoring of main processes shall be carried out with the Distributed Control and System (DCMS). The DCMS shall take care of all automation functions, such as:
- open loop drive controls, group controls, and sequence controls
- equipment protection of individual drives (pumps, valves, etc.)
- closed loop drive controls and co-ordinating controls
- man-machine interface as process displays and trend reports
- alarm annunciation
- self-diagnostics
- process interface and power supply for field equipment, signal conditioning
- interface to external systems as PLCs, computers, local area network (LAN), etc
- back-up functions
- engineering functions
The availability and reliability level of the DCMS shall not compromise the one of the Plant. For this purpose, adequate redundancy of processors, communication buses, supply devices, etc. shall be used. The following requirements shall be taken into consideration by the Contractor:
- all processes can be controlled and monitored via any operator terminal (display capacity shall be high enough)
- however, each operator terminal can be authorised to carry out the control and monitoring only of the specified processes (authorisation shall be possible)"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_179,"105, 106",8.3.1._General_requirements,"8, 3, 1",list,"list_item, text",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.1._General_requirements,Degree of Automation,General Requirements,"Redundancy, Availability and Protection Logic",,,"redundant operator stations/monitors, redundant system buses, redundant process control stations, max 32 channels per I/O card, signal isolation (between channels and from field), online replacement of I/O cards/CPU/power supplies, module-level diagnostics (video pages/prints), failure containment between units, 2oo3 (2 out of 3) logic for critical trips, gateway stations to Ethernet, application program backup and auto-reload, AGC capability for Load Dispatch Center (LDC)",False,"DCMS redundancy/protection logic and AGC; no mention of ACC or ACC subsystems (fans, bundles, steam duct, vacuum, etc.).",True,,high,False,"Provides specific redundancy, I/O design constraints, protection voting logic, diagnostics, and AGC/LDC interface requirements—strong retrieval anchors for I&C design documents.","- should an operator station, terminal, or monitor be out of operation, all functions can be carried out via other stations, terminals and monitors (each disturbed piece of equipment is replaced by a working one)
- system buses are mainly redundant
- process control stations are in general redundant
- the concentration of signals per I/O card, depending of the type of signal/cards, shall not exceed 32 channels per card and the single signals shall be isolated among them and from the field
- in the case of changing of redundant devices ( input/output I/O cards, power supplies, CPU,…) or in case of partial power failure of the system for maintenance during operation, no changes to the previous conditions of the controlled plant shall be made. In general, the replacement of failed components shall be possible when the system is in operation. The systems shall enable the identification of malfunctions at least at module level, through detailed video pages and prints
- the organisation of the different units forming the system (control units, data acquisition units, computerised operation stations etc.) shall be such not to spread out a possible failure to the other units
- a single failure of a redundant component does not cause a spurious intervention of the protection system nor make it ineffective and, in any case, the failure of a single component does not cause the protection system to become ineffective
- when redundant field sensors are foreseen (duplicated or triplicate), each of them shall be acquired by an input card different from that of the other redundant sensors
- the processing of the main protections and critical trips shall be made according to 2 out of 3 logic
- there are gateway stations and appropriate protocols to connect the system to external systems and Ethernet network
- back-up function stores application programs and reloads them automatically into the stations after a power supply failure
- engineering tools to allow the user to make changes, maintenance, tests of the programs and extensions to the system are included in the delivery
The Distributed Control Systems (DCMS) and Instrumentation systems shall be designed and furnished to achieve the automatic safe and efficient operation of the Plant and equipment provided by the Contractor.
DCMS Plant interfaces shall be as per Section 2.4 of Exhibit B-1, Part I, MFS of this Agreement.
With respect to the Load Dispatch Center, the control system of the Power Units shall be equipped with automatic power generation control capability (AGC) for automatic and manual regulation of the real and reactive power output of the Plant and shall be capable of remote AGC operation by LDC
The DCMS has to provide all the required man-machine and machine-machine interfaces for monitoring and control of the Plant."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_180,"106, 107",8.3.1._General_requirements,"8, 3, 1",table,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.1._General_requirements,Degree of Automation,General Requirements,Supervision Functions and Data Access (OPC),,,"Central Control Room (CCR) data buses, acquisition/filtering/validation of analog and digital inputs, sequence control, interlocking (combinatory) control, analog control (open/closed loop), supervision information (status, alarms), package control system remote monitoring (images, alarms, hardware buttons), redundant OPC server, OPC DA, OPC HDA, licensed up to 5000 signals, spare parts availability 25 years from COD",False,Supervision functions and OPC server requirements for DCMS; no ACC-related signals.,True,,high,False,"Defines DCMS supervision/control functions plus explicit OPC DA/HDA server requirements and licensing limits, which are strong signals for control system architecture and interface documents.","All Plant equipment and system controls shall be achieved by Distributed Control System (DCMS) stations, local control systems which interface with DCMS, stand-alone control systems and hardwired circuits. With respect to local control systems associated to GTs and ST, the HMI station enabling a local control (e.g. screens in the PCC) will not be available after the Commercial Operation Date.
The system shall enable the following fundamental functions:
- acquisition, filtering, validation and conversion of both digital and analog input values originating from the process
- processing of all input values using:
- -sequence control performing succession of logic operations depending on previous orders and presence of criteria
- -combinatory (or interlocking) control that combines authorization criteria for sending orders that switch on/off one or several equipment, that realizes security actions and that generates the necessary alarms
- -analog control (open and closed loop) that keeps the process parameters at preset values (that are functions of the load of the Plant within the limits contractually foreseen)
- creating the output information for digital (on/off) or modulating actuators;
- working out of supervision information (including but not limited to status indications, alarms) for control room operators and for maintenance, planning or statistics
The automation stations (and other control stations) shall be connected to a number of operating stations in the Central Control Room (CCR) by data buses. Data handling systems such as archiving and interfaces with other systems shall also be connected to the system.
Control/supervision units receive data from their relevant packages control systems to perform remote control and/or monitoring of those packages. This includes sets of images, alarms and hardware buttons.
A redundant OPC server with DA and HDA protocols and the necessary license to provide access to all and every data available in the DCMS system shall be part of the scope of supply. The data access of the OPC-clients to the OPC-server is limited and licensed for up to 5000 signals.
The good working of the OPC server shall be demonstrated with an OPC client which shall also be part of the scope of supply.
For the DCMS system, Contractor shall ensure the availability of a supply of spare parts for a period of 25 years from COD. For a period of 5 years the equipment shall be functionally compatible for plug in main equipment, for the remaining time the equipment is functionally compatible and as far as possible compatible for plug in main equipment."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_181,107,8.3.2._Availability_-_Reliability_-_Maintenance,"8, 3, 2",content,text,8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.2._Availability_-_Reliability_-_Maintenance,Plant Control and Operational System,DCMS,"Availability, Reliability and Maintenance",,,"DCMS 24 hours a day operation, scheduled partial outages, degraded mode operation, module replacement maintenance, redundant critical sensors, 3 out of 3 I/O connection, redundant communication cards, redundant power supply cards, segregation of redundant modules to avoid common mode fault, maintenance interval 24 months",False,DCMS availability/reliability/maintenance requirements; no ACC mention.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit DCMS availability/maintenance and redundancy requirements (I/O voting, redundant cards, maintenance interval) useful for matching control system design/architecture and RAM-related MDL documents.","The DCMS is operating on a 24 hours a day basis. Verifications, checking and maintenance operation may only happen during the scheduled partial outages of the Plant (while maintenance is performed the system will still be operational in degraded mode). Beyond the outage periods no preventive maintenance shall be required.
Repairing is to be performed during operation of the Plant and shall consist only in module replacement (with minimum needs in wiring disconnection).
After detection and localization of a faulty module maintenance may be performed without disturbance on overall operation of the system.
Critical sensors will be redundant or connected in a 3 out of 3 manner to individual I/O's: only loss of both sensors or both I/O's would lead to shut down of controlled system. Critical cards ( communication cards, power supply cards or devices, ..) shall be fully redundant. Contractor shall describe the principles of the segregation of redundant modules/devices to avoid any common mode fault.
The period between two successive maintenance actions (checks, controls and maintenance) cannot be shorter than 24 months."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_182,107,8.3.3._Typical_functions_of_the_DCMS,"8, 3, 3",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.3._Typical_functions_of_the_DCMS,Plant Control and Operational System,DCMS,Control logic functions and signal priority,,,"control logic class typical diagrams/functions, signal classification priority, protection controls, interlock signals, manual controls, automatic controls",False,Generic DCMS control logic signal priority list; no ACC context.,True,,medium,False,"Defines DCMS functional organization and signal priority classes, which can anchor retrieval of control philosophy/functional design documents.","For each control logic class typical diagrams/functions shall be defined by the Contractor. Moreover, inside each control logic, the signals shall be classified and processed according to the following proposed priority list :
- protection controls
- interlock signals
- manual controls
- automatic controls"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_183,"107, 108",8.3.4._Execution_times_of_the_main_DCMS,"8, 3, 4",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.4._Execution_times_of_the_main_DCMS,Plant Control and Operational System,DCMS,Control system performance and execution times,,,"process alarms VDU within 1 s, audible alarm within 1 s, time stamp resolution 1 s, status display change within 2 s, SOE display within 2 s, SOE time tagged 10 ms, command from CCR/LCR status change within 1 s, analogue sampling better than 250 ms, binary sampling better than 100 ms",False,"DCMS execution time/performance criteria (alarms, SOE, sampling); no ACC-related references.",True,,high,False,"Provides explicit DCMS performance timing requirements (alarms, SOE, sampling, CCR/LCR commands) that strongly match control system performance/specification documents.","The process requirement will determine the executing time for each control function. The process requirement dictates how fast the execution time for each control/protection function shall be.
The following performance criteria shall be achieved by the system, under all normal conditions of operation and under all emergency conditions:
- Process alarms (digital): VDU display and audible signal within 1 second from plant initiation and time stamped to no greater than 1 second resolution.
- Status display: VDU display change within 2 seconds of step change of plant transducer signal.
- SOE: VDU display within 2 seconds, all time tagged to 10ms including SOE designated points and electrical points.
- Command from CCR/LCR (local control room): status change at plant within 1 second of operator initiation.
- Analogue sampling : at plant level better than 250 ms.
- Binary sampling : at plant level better than 100 ms."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_184,108,8.3.5._Checking_of_measurements_to_DCMS,"8, 3, 5",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.5._Checking_of_measurements_to_DCMS,Plant Control and Operational System,DCMS,Signal validation and measurement checking,,,"validation function for field signals, analogue signal validity limits check, thermoresistance cut-off detection, thermoresistance not supplied detection, thermocouple open circuit check, signal filtering, derivative check between samplings, deviation check between redundant measures, binary input contact consistency control, changeover contact",False,Signal validation/measurement checking to DCMS; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains concrete DCMS input validation/diagnostics functions for analogue and binary signals, useful for matching I&C design/specification content.","Before processing the signals coming from the field they shall be submitted to a validation function. For analogue measurements, implementing of the following functions shall be taken into consideration by the Contractor:
- check of the signal validity limits
- cut-off and not supplied thermoresistance detection
- open circuit check (for thermocouples)
- filtering when needed
- derivative check between two subsequent samplings when applicable
- check of deviation between redundant measures
For important and protective binary input contacts (changeover contact) the system shall perform a consistency control."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_185,"108, 109",8.3.6._DCMS_control_functions,"8, 3, 6",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.6._DCMS_control_functions,Plant Control and Operational System,DCMS,"Control functions, modes, interlocks and sequences",,,"automatic operation minimizes operator action, mode transfer equalisation and tracking, bumpless changeover, manual/automatic mode return decision, fail-safe actuator position on control chain failure, fail-safe actuator position on auxiliary power failure, equipment-level interlocks and protections, sequential functions for starting and stopping, CCGT plant sequences, manual controls for automatic sequences failure",False,"Generic DCMS control functions, modes, interlocks and sequences for CCGT; no ACC-specific mention.",True,,high,False,"Defines DCMS control philosophy elements (mode handling, fail-safe behavior, interlocks, sequences for CCGT) that can retrieve control philosophy and sequence/interlock documentation.","The following requirements shall be taken into consideration by the Contractor :
- the control functions shall not require in their normal automatic operation any action from the operator on final regulation parts, limiting the operator's manual interventions to the minimum
- transfers from an operation mode to the other one shall be made with automatic equalisation systems and tracking functions (bumbles changeover)
- when a control loop switches to manual due to unpredictable irregular events, the operator shall have possibility to decide whether to return back to automatic mode
- the final controlling actuator, in case of failure of the control chain or failure in auxiliary power, shall be driven to a position, which does not risk the Plant safety and enable to continue the service, if possible
- at single equipment control level, interlocks and protections shall be provided to assure a safe operation, even when the remaining part of the automatic equipment at higher levels is not operating
- the DCMS shall perform the sequential functions relevant to the starting, stopping, etc. of the CCGT Plant and functional/machinery area
- for all the automatic sequences, the corresponding manual controls shall be foreseen at functional unit level, operating unit or control of the single equipment level, to be used in case of failure of the sequences."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_186,109,8.3.7._DCMS_supervision_functions,"8, 3, 7",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.7._DCMS_supervision_functions,Plant Control and Operational System,Degree of Automation,DCMS Supervision Functions,,,"CCGT plant control, process supervision video functions, alarm management, status and events presentation and recording, event sequence supervision, operator interface control room",False,DCMS supervision functions for overall CCGT plant; no mention of ACC/air cooled condenser or related equipment.,True,,high,False,"Defines DCMS supervision/operator interface functions (video, alarms, event/status recording) relevant to control system design and HMI/alarm philosophy documents.","The supervision shall enable the operator to check the information required for the CCGT Plant control in all the operation conditions, offering him a complete range of means, including:
- video functions for the process supervision
- management of alarms
- presentation and recording of status and events
- supervision of event sequence
The information available to the operator's interface in the control room shall always be clear and sufficient to enable the operator to make decisions in a short time."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_187,109,8.3.8._DCMS_event_chronological_recording,"8, 3, 8",content,text,8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.8._DCMS_event_chronological_recording,Plant Control and Operational System,Degree of Automation,DCMS Event Chronological Recording,,,"protective fast signals, events record chronological order, protection logics actuation, digital signals only, 10 millisecond resolution",False,Event chronological recording for protective signals; no ACC-related terms or context.,True,,high,False,"Provides specific requirement for event recording/sequence of events (SOE) including 10 ms resolution, useful for control system/SOE specification retrieval.","Protective, fast signals shall be put in chronological order in the events record in order to originate the actuation of one or more protection logics.
This function is proposed to be performed only on digital signals with 10 millisecond resolution."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_188,"109, 110",8.3.9._Measurement_supervision,"8, 3, 9",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.9._Measurement_supervision,Plant Control and Operational System,Degree of Automation,Measurement Supervision,,,"field signal non-reliability, invalid measurements, double measurements deviation monitoring, triple measurements deviation monitoring, signal out of scale, control loop switch to manual mode, tolerance limit (calibrated), manual exclusion of measurement channel",False,"Generic measurement supervision logic (invalid signals, deviation monitoring); no ACC/air cooled condenser signal.",True,,high,False,Defines measurement validation/voting and control-loop fallback behavior (manual/auto) for double/triple measurements—strong control/I&C functional requirement anchors.,"The operator shall be kept alerted on the status of field signal non-reliability (invalid measurements or non-consistent contacts).
In case of double or triple measurements, information shall also be obtained when an excessive deviation is detected between measurements.
The management of the measures dedicated to control functions is proposed to follow the criteria listed below:
- in case of an invalidity of a single measurement (signal out of scale), information shall be given to the operator and in case the measurement is connected to a control loop, the loop shall be switched into manual mode
- in case of invalidity of one of the double measurement, the valid measurement shall remain used for the control (which remains in automatic) and an information to the operator shall given
- moreover, for double measurements, the deviation between the two channels shall be monitored and when the same exceeds the tolerance limit (that could be calibrated), after an appropriate time, an alarm shall be given to the control room and the concerned control loop shall be switched to manual
- in case deviation existing among the three measurements, taken two by two, shall be monitored. Whenever the detected deviation exceeds the tolerance limit (that could be calibrated), after an appropriate timing, the invalidity of the measurement channel deviation from the remaining two shall be declared. This shall automatically select the calculation of the mean on the two valid channels left (which shall be treated as double measure) and shall originate an alarm signal and the display of comparisons exceeding the limit set
- in case of double or triple measurements any measurement(s) could be manually excluded"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_189,"110, 111",8.3.10._Alarm_function,"8, 3, 10",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.10._Alarm_function,Plant Control and Operational System,Degree of Automation,Alarm Function and Process Information System,,,"audible and visual alarm devices, alarm acknowledgement, alarm chronological pages (event time, identification code, description, status/value, threshold exceeded), self-diagnostic messages operator console, Performance Monitoring System (power and heat rate calculations), corrections to reference conditions, off-design simulation, equipment performance monitoring (GT, ST, generator, HRSG, pumps/motors), Vibration Monitoring System, Generator monitoring (partial discharge, rotor winding short turn), TRD 508 lifetime calculation (high creep stresses), data transfer interface to Maintenance Management System",False,Alarm function and process information system for major equipment (GT/ST/HRSG etc.); ACC not mentioned.,True,,high,False,"Contains detailed alarm philosophy requirements plus process information/performance monitoring, vibration monitoring, generator monitoring, TRD 508 lifetime module, and MMS interface—strong retrieval signals for control/monitoring system documents.","The following requirements shall be taken into consideration by the Contractor:
- each alarm shall always enable sound and visual devices
- the alarm displaying on the monitor shall be complete with all the indications useful for the acknowledgement of the alarm
- self-diagnostic messages relevant to the malfunction of each component of the system shall appear on the screens of the operator's console
- alarm chronological pages shall include, in the form of a table, the alarm messages that shall contain the following information :
- -event time also in cases the alarm is initiated by a subsystem
- -identification code
- -description
- -current status or value
- -description of irregular conditions or threshold value exceeded
- the presence of alarms shall also be signalled out of the alarm chronological video pages, using colour and light signals
- the acknowledgements could be made either from alarm chronological pages or from the other video pages, limited to alarm conditions clearly highlighted on the page itself
- acknowledgements of alarms in sub-automation systems shall work with the main DCMS
One part of the Plants DCMS system will be the process information system. The system shall comprise
- Performance Monitoring System : On-line near-real-time thermodynamic process including power and heat rate calculations, corrections to reference conditions and means for off- design simulation for online near-real-time equipment performance monitoring. All major equipment must be included, for examples, ST, GT, Generator, HRSGs, main pumps/motors.
The system will be designed to allow the monitoring of all its components in a general way and particularly of:
- -the status of each plug-in module and its position in the racks;
- -the status of the actuators;
- -the status of the sensors and associated signals (among others for analog signals : out of range for transmitters, open transducer detection for thermocouples, Pt100 Ω and variation of resistance value);
- -feedback of order completion within specified time;
- -every power supply protection device.
- Vibration Monitoring System : for turbine/generator packages, boiler feedwater pumps fuel gas compressors and all critical pumps at 13.8kV level and as further requested elsewhere in this document.
- Generator Monitoring Systems , e.g., for partial-discharge in stator and detecting of rotor winding short turn which will be independently provided with dedicated rack mounted display in the central control room as per generator manufacturer experience and design criteria.
A module for lifetime calculation according to TRD 508 for components operating in a region of high creep stresses shall be part of the system. The system shall also serve as an extensive history database having state-of-art trending and reporting features.
The system shall also have data transfer interface to Maintenance Management System."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_190,111,8.3.10._Alarm_function,,content,text,8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.10._Alarm_function,Plant Control and Operational System,External Interfaces,Load Dispatch / SCADA Integration,,,"integration work, Offtaker Authority Load Dispatch System, SCADA, Project Site limitation, meters located in GIS, coordination of Owner",False,SCADA/load dispatch integration scope; no ACC references.,True,,medium,False,Specifies scope for integrating site systems with Offtaker load dispatch/SCADA including GIS-located meters; useful for retrieving interface/control integration documents.,"The scope of Contractor shall include: all integration work in Offtaker Authority's Load Dispatch System such as for SCADA if any, but limited to Project Site (including the meters located in GIS) under coordination of Owner"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_191,"111, 112",8.3.10._Alarm_function,,content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.10._Alarm_function,Degree of Automation,Alarm Function,Input Signals to FDM,,,"FDM input signals, ambient air conditions (dry bulb temperature, relative humidity, barometric pressure), natural gas LHV (kJ/kg), natural gas pressure and temperature at plant supply point, grid frequency (Hz), generator power factor, required plant net power output (MW), number of GT/ST/HRSG units in operation, gross power output per gas turbine (MW), gross power output steam turbine (MW), gas turbine evaporative coolers status, gas turbine air preheater status, steam and feedwater supplied to FGP (t/h)",False,"FDM input signals list (ambient, fuel, GT/ST/HRSG operation); no ACC/air cooled condenser mentioned.",True,,medium,False,"Provides explicit control/monitoring signal list and operating parameters that can match I&C signal lists, control narratives, and automation design documents.","Necessary input signals to FDM shall be part of the Contractor's scope of supply and shall include but not be limited to:
- ambient air conditions (in dry bulb temperature oC, relative humidity %, barometric pressure bara)
- natural Gas lower heating value (LHV in kJ/kg)
- natural Gas pressure and temperature at Plant supply point (bar g, °C)
- grid frequency (in Hz)
- generators power factor (-)
- required Plant net power output (in MW) as per dispatch instruction
- type of Fuel ( low BTU or Sales )
- number of gas turbine units in operation (-)
- number of steam turbine units in operation (-)
- number of HRSG units in operation (-)
- gross power output of each gas turbine unit (in MW)
- gross power output of the steam turbine unit (in MW)
- gas turbine evaporative coolers in operation, for each gas turbine unit (onoff)
- gas turbine air preheater in operation, for each gas turbine unit (on-off)
- steam and feedwater supplied to FGP ( in t/h )
The entire signal list shall be agreed during detail engineering."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_192,112,8.3.12._Plant_Information_(PI)_System,"8, 3, 12",content,text,8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.12._Plant_Information_(PI)_System,Degree of Automation,Plant Information (PI) System,Historian Server,,,"historian server, PI server license, ~10,000 tags, 5 licences for PI-Client, interface to DCMS, Ethernet network, tags configured in DCMS",False,Historian/PI system server requirements; no ACC-related signal.,True,,high,False,"Defines PI historian system scope, tag count, client licenses, and DCMS Ethernet interface—strong anchors for control system architecture and PI integration documents.","The Contractor shall supply an historian server. The system must consist of PI server license with app. 10,000 tags and 5 licences for PI-Client. The system must interface to DCMS via Ethernet network and ability to read data from it. All required tags are configured in DCMS."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_193,112,8.3.13._Reserve_capacity,"8, 3, 13",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.3.13._Reserve_capacity,Degree of Automation,Reserve Capacity,DCMS / Control System Sizing,,,"reserve capacity criteria, 20% free space for racks and I/O cards in I/O cabinets, 10% free channels in multi-channel I/O units, 20% reserve capacity in process control stations, 30% memory for application programs, minimum 20% increase of control logic and loops without increasing processing time, hardware reserve capacity based on active usage, system capacity based on technically accepted maximum capacity",False,Control system reserve capacity sizing; no ACC context.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit control system sizing and spare capacity requirements useful for retrieving I/O lists, cabinet layouts, system sizing, and DCMS architecture documents.","The system shall be designed so that sufficient reserve capacity (on the average) is included, the following criteria shall be taken into consideration by the Contractor:
- 20% of free space for racks and I/O-cards in the I/O cabinets
- 10% of free channels in multi-channel I/O units
- 20% of reserve capacity in process control stations and other system stations
- 30% memory for application programs
The system resources shall enable minimum 20% increase of the control logic and loops, without increasing the processing time.
Hardware reserve capacity is calculated from the actual hardware in active usage. The system capacity is calculated from the technically accepted maximum capacity where the system still runs without problems."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_194,113,8.4.1._General_requirements,"8, 4, 1",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.4.1._General_requirements,Degree of Automation,Safety Related System (SRS),Emergency Shutdown System Requirements,,,"Safety Related System (SRS), emergency shutdown system, independent from DCMS, IEC 61508, SIL 3 certificate, fault tolerant, redundant, risk analysis allocation of safety functions, major property damage SIL 2, logic controller response time 500 ms, proposed response time 1 s, applies to gas turbines, steam turbines, HRSG, fuel systems",False,Safety Related System (SRS) requirements for GT/ST/HRSG/fuel systems; ACC not included.,True,,high,False,"Clear functional safety requirements (IEC 61508, SIL levels, independence, response time) and protected equipment scope—strong retrieval signals for SRS/SIS design basis and safety studies.","The following requirements shall be taken into consideration by the Contractor.
The Safety Related System (SRS) shall be an emergency shutdown system that shuts down part of the Plant whenever the safety limits have been exceeded. The SRS shall include all the sensors, logic, and final elements needed for the safety function.
The SRS is required for the gas turbines, steam turbines, HRSG and the fuel systems.
The SRS system shall fulfil the requirements of IEC 61508 or equivalent:
- the SRS shall be independent from the DCMS. A fault at DCMS side may not affect the functions of safety system
- the SRS system shall be fault tolerant so that no single fault inside the SRS will cause a loss of a safety function
- the SRS shall be redundant. A single fault inside the SRS system may not cause a spurious trip of the plant/unit
- the SRS shall have a certificate that it fulfils the integrity level 3 of the IEC 61508 or equivalent. The requirements of the certification body shall be followed
- the safety functions and the safety integrity functions shall be allocated to each SRS system based on risk analyse of the process
- the major property damage shall be protected using SIL level 2 (IEC 61508). In case there is a risk of losing several human lives, the SIL level shall be 3
- response time of the logic controller: the maximum allowed response time for the safety-related functions of the logic controller is proposed to be 500 ms, that is 50 % of the proposed response time of 1 s
- in case the risk analyses indicates faster than 1 s response time, the SRS system shall fulfil that requirement"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_195,"113, 114",8.4.2._Safety_Systems,"8, 4, 2",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.4.2._Safety_Systems,Degree of Automation,Safety Systems,Fail-safe Protection and Redundancy Concept,,,"multichannel techniques, 2 out of 3 redundancy principle (2oo3), fail-safe protection for GTs, STs, HRSGs, triplicated measurements, exceptions: absolute bearing casing vibration, external binary trip signals, 1 out of 2 controller redundancy (1oo2), current switch-on to actuate, de-energize to trip, complete selectivity of protection (power distribution subdivided), binary and analog outputs neutralized during CPU initialization/start-up, continuous monitoring/validation of critical signals, UPS power redundancy for DCMS, single failure does not prevent protection, single fault does not trigger spurious protection",False,Fail-safe protection and redundancy concept for GT/ST/HRSG; no ACC/air cooled condenser mention.,True,,high,False,"Defines detailed safety/protection redundancy architecture (2oo3, 1oo2), fail-safe philosophy, power selectivity, and UPS/DCMS resilience—useful for SIS/protection design and architecture documents.","The critical protection functions shall be realized using multichannel techniques with the 2 out of 3 redundancy principle.
Multichannel technique is provided for ""Fail-safe"" protection which will be limited to such systems which jeopardize the safety of persons. Such features are limited to fail safe systems of GTs, STs and HRSGs. All important, fail safe relevant measurements will be triplicated (exception: absolute bearing casing vibration, external binary trip signals). The fail-safe protection system itself shall be executed as a 1 out of 2 controller redundancy.
The system works preferably according to principle of current switch-on to actuate and de-energize to trip.
A single failure shall not prevent a protection to be activated.
A single fault shall not trigger a protection if not required.
Measures shall be taken so as to ensure that orders of such critical functions are duly sent to the actuators, in particular but not restricted:
- power distribution will be sufficiently subdivided in order to prevent a fault from having repercussions on different functions: complete selectivity of protection will be achieved
- binary and analog outputs are neutralized during initialization and start-up of the CPU's
- each signal used for critical functions shall be validated by continuous monitoring of its value and/or the variation thereof in relation to other values/signals: associated digital information is made available for any further functional processing
- interruption of a single external AC power supply won't impair operation of the DCMS (UPS power for the DCMS is redundancy provided)"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_196,114,8.4.2._Safety_Systems,,list,"list_item, text",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.4.2._Safety_Systems,Degree of Automation,Safety Systems,Safety-related control system I/O segregation for redundancy,,,"redundant elements, redundant measurements, I/O card segregation, input card segregation, redundant devices I/O identification in lists, firmware security features, applicative configuration security features",False,Generic safety-related control system I/O segregation for redundancy; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention or context.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit technical requirements for redundancy handling and I/O card segregation in the safety-related control system, useful to retrieve SRS/ESD design and I/O list documents.","- Redundant elements (motors, valves etc.) shall not be controlled from the same I/O card; the I/O corresponding to redundant devices shall be put in evidence in the lists supplied by the Contractor;
- Redundant measurements shall not be acquired on the same input card; the I/O corresponding to redundant measurements shall be clearly put in evidence in the lists supplied by the Contractor.
The Contractor shall inform the Owner's Representative how these particular points are met and shall also describe all other security features that are built in the system. with clear distinction between firmware built in security features and others that have to be realized by applicative configuration being mentioned."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_197,115,8.4.2._Safety_Systems,,content,text,8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.4.2._Safety_Systems,Degree of Automation,Safety Systems,Plant protection / ESD protective logic and voting,,,"plant protection system, Saudi Aramco ESD standard SAES-J-601, protective logics, field instrument 2 out of 3 configuration, 2oo3 voting",False,Plant protection/ESD logic per SAES-J-601; no ACC-related equipment or signals.,True,,high,False,Defines governing standard (SAES-J-601) and 2oo3 voting configuration for protective logic—strong anchors for ESD/SIS design basis and logic documents.,"The plant protection shall be developed through an appropriate system. This one will respect Saudi Aramco ESD standard SAES - J -601and shall develop all protective logics, from field instrument 2 out of 3 configuration."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_198,115,8.4.3._User_interface,"8, 4, 3",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.4.3._User_interface,Degree of Automation,User Interface,"SRS/DCMS shutdown, bypass, alarms and status indication",,,"manual shutdown independent of DCMS and SRS, manual emergency trip hardwired push button to SRS input, bypass switches key locks, SRS status information (non-purged, purged, fire, trip initiated, safe state), criteria bypassed indicated and alarmed, voting status 2/3, processor fault / I/O fault status, loss of energy status and alarm, manual acknowledge of trip, process alarms displayed via DCMS alarm displays",False,"SRS/DCMS shutdown, bypass, alarms and status indication requirements; no ACC reference.",True,,high,False,"Provides concrete functional requirements for SRS user interface, alarms, bypass management, and manual trip—useful for retrieving HMI/alarm philosophy and SIS/ESD interface documents.","The following requirements shall be taken into consideration by the Contractor:
- there shall be also manual means of shutting down the process. This shall be independent from the DCMS and SRS
- the manual emergency trip shall be hardwired from a push button to an input of the safety related control system. This function shall be totally independent of the DCMS
- if any bypass operations of the SRS are allowed, the bypass switches shall be protected by key locks
- the SRS status information is proposed to include :
- -process state: non-purged, purged, fire, trip initiated, safe state
- -criteria by-passed shall indicated and alarmed with a repeating alarm
- -status of the voting ( 2/3 ) for logic part , the comparison fault status of the logic: normal, processor fault, I/O fault
- -status of all measurements and final elements connected to the SRS
- -status and alarm of all loss of energy
- manual acknowledge of a trip is proposed before process operation is allowed
- the process alarms shall be displayed via the normal DCMS alarm displays
- if a safety function is not causing automatic trip but initiates an alarm, then this alarm shall be done in SRS system and indicated independently from the DCMS system"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_199,"115, 116",8.4.4._Signal_interfaces,"8, 4, 4",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.4.4._Signal_interfaces,Degree of Automation,Signal Interfaces,SRS/DCMS signal segregation and sharing philosophy,,,"field signals wired directly to SRS from field, DCMS to/from signals via bus connection, safety functions not to use signals coming from DCMS, instruments not shared between control/monitoring and plant protection, shared arrangement subject to Owner/Engineer approval, HW shared signals between DCMS and SRS, DCMS fault shall not cause fault at SRS",False,Signal interface/segregation philosophy between SRS and DCMS; no ACC scope signal.,True,,high,False,"Clear technical interface requirements for wiring, bus communications, and signal sharing between DCMS and SRS—strong retrieval anchors for interface diagrams and control/safety philosophy docs.","The following requirements shall be taken into consideration by the Contractor:
- field signals shall be wired to the safety related control system (SRS) directly from the field
- signals from/to the DCMS system shall be transferred using the bus connection. Safety related functions shall not use a signal that is coming from the DCMS system and which might in special cases prevent the safety function
- in general, the philosophy shall be that instruments are not shared between control/monitoring systems and plant protection systems. In cases where this is not practical or there is sufficient technical justification (e.g. measurement redundancy) in doing so, a shared arrangement may be used subject to the approval of the Owner/Engineer. In case signals shall be HW shared between DCMS and SRS the fault in DCMS side shall not cause a fault at SRS side"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_200,116,8.4.5._Grounding,"8, 4, 5",content,text,8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.4.5._Grounding,Degree of Automation,Grounding,SRS logic cabinet grounding,,,"SRS logic cabinet, metal parts potential grounded, IEEE standard grounding",False,Grounding requirement for SRS logic cabinet; not tied to ACC.,True,,medium,True,Technical grounding requirement is present but vague (no specific IEEE standard number); still useful for retrieving grounding/earthing requirements for SRS cabinets.,All the metal parts of the SRS logic cabinet shall be potential grounded according to IEEE standard..
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_201,116,8.4.6._Safety_Integrity_requirements,"8, 4, 6",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.4.6._Safety_Integrity_requirements,Plant Control and Operational System,Safety Instrumented System / Safety Requirements,Safety Integrity Level (SIL) and IEC 61508 assumptions,,,"IEC 61508, Safety Integrity Level (SIL), SRS central part SIL equals maximum SIL from risk analysis, logic controller 1-year test interval, field equipment 1-year test interval, low demand shutdown system, testing during normal process operation",False,SIL/IEC61508 safety integrity requirements for SRS; no ACC mention.,True,,high,False,Defines SIL/IEC61508 basis and proof test interval assumptions for SRS integrity calculations; strong retrieval anchors for SIS/SRS design basis and safety requirements documents.,"The following requirements shall be taken into consideration by the Contractor:
- for safety integrity definitions standards IEC61508 shall apply
- Safety Integrity Level of the central part of the SRS shall be the same as the maximum SIL level stated at the risk analyse.
- when calculating the integrity level it shall be assumed that :
- -the central logic controller has a one year test interval
- -the field equipment has a one year test interval
- -the SRS is a 'low demand shutdown system'
- -if there is a need of test period shorter than 1 year, it shall be possible to do these tests during normal process operation"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_202,116,8.4.7._Fault_tolerances_of_the_SRS,"8, 4, 7",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.4.7._Fault_tolerances_of_the_SRS,Plant Control and Operational System,Safety Instrumented System / Safety Requirements,"SRS fault tolerance, safe state philosophy, and signal monitoring",,,"logic controller dual power supplies, loss of both supplies causes trip of fuel supply (GT), field circuits dual power supplies, de-energised output signals safe state (tripped), one-fault tolerant safety functions, self-testing diagnostics / multi-channel structure, second fault causes trip, binary signals energised = safe state, analogue 4–20 mA signal range monitoring, MCC contactors emergency usage, current cutoff capability over-dimensioned (divide by 0.6)",False,"SRS fault tolerance and safe-state philosophy (mentions GT fuel trip, MCC contactors); no ACC-related context.",True,,high,False,"Contains explicit SRS/SIS architecture and fail-safe requirements (power redundancy, fault tolerance, safe state wiring, 4-20 mA monitoring) useful for matching control/protection system design documents.","The following requirements shall be taken into consideration by the Contractor:
- the logic controller shall have a two power supplies, one of which shall be enough for normal operation. Total loss of both supplies shall cause trip of fuel supply (GT) and related plant sections.
- field circuits shall be fed from two power supplies, one of which shall be enough for normal operation. Total loss of both supplies shall cause trip of fuel supply (GT) and related plant sections. The de-energised state of output signals shall be the safe state (tripped).
- the logic controller shall be one-fault tolerant when counting the safety functions. Fault tolerance is achieved by self-testing (diagnostics) or by multi-channel structure. Second fault (of same function) shall cause a trip.
- all binary (Boolean) data shall be wired so that a safe state is an energised state.
- all analogue (4...20 mA) signals shall have a signal range monitoring.
- the MCC contactors shall be suitable for emergency usage; the current cutoff capability shall be over dimensioned by dividing 0.6."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_203,117,8.4.8._Field_devices,"8, 4, 8",content,"text, list_item",8.1._Degree_of_automation > 8.4.8._Field_devices,Plant Control and Operational System,Plant Protection Systems,Protection field devices and instrument sharing philosophy,HRSG protection field devices,,"HRSG protection field devices proven-in-use (3 years), probability-to-fail-on-demand (PFD) values, smart transmitters write protection, instruments not shared between control/monitoring and plant protection, measurement redundancy justification for sharing, HW shared signals between DCMS and SRS, DCMS fault shall not cause SRS fault",False,Plant protection field devices for HRSG and instrument sharing philosophy; no ACC signal.,True,,high,False,Specifies proven-in-use/PFD requirements and segregation/sharing rules between DCMS and SRS for plant protection instrumentation; strong anchors for I&C/SIS documentation.,"The following requirements shall be taken into consideration by the Contractor for Plant protection systems:
- HRSG protection field device types shall be proven on use, for at least three (3) years' service. There shall be available the probability-to-fail-ondemand values for each device
- smart transmitters shall have a write protection to prevent modification from remote location
- In general, the philosophy shall be that instruments are not shared between control/monitoring systems and plant protection systems. In cases where this is not practical or there is sufficient technical justification (e.g. measurement redundancy) in doing so, a shared arrangement may be used subject to the approval of the Owner/Engineer. In case signals shall be HW shared between DCMS and SRS the fault in DCMS side shall not cause a fault at SRS side."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_204,117,8.5._Time_synchronization,"8, 5",content,text,8.5._Time_synchronization,Plant Control and Operational System,Time Synchronization,GPS-based master clock and time distribution,,,"GPS-based time synchronisation system, master clock, common time for DCMS and separate automation systems, interfaces for time distribution, time synchronisation accuracy 1 ms, data acquisition and logging",False,GPS-based time synchronization for DCMS/automation systems; no ACC reference.,True,,high,False,Defines GPS time sync system scope and 1 ms accuracy requirement across DCMS/automation/protection systems; useful for control system architecture and network/time-sync documents.,"GPS-based time synchronisation system shall be included to the scope. This system will give the common time for DCMS and each separate automation system.
The master clock shall provide real time reference to the DCMS and all other plant control, monitoring and protection systems by suitable interfaces capable of achieving a time synchronisation accuracy of 1 ms among these systems for their data acquisition and logging activities."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_205,"117, 118",8.6._Power_supplies_for_automation_systems,"8, 6",content,"text, list_item",8.6._Power_supplies_for_automation_systems,Plant Control and Operational System,Automation System Power Supplies,"Redundancy, UPS feeds, and single-failure survival",,,"selective drop of power supply to affected control circuit, DCMS redundant power supply system, monitoring of supply unit outputs and malfunction identification, signalling in control room, survival to single failure principle, two separate 230 V AC single phase from redundant UPS dedicated for DCMS/control devices, one 230 V AC single phase from normal plant service network for non-critical cabinet auxiliaries (lighting), instrumentation/actuator power distribution rules (separate from signal cable), redundant supply of process stations, emergency supply unit, data saving routine, non-volatile memories to retain calibration and preset values",False,"Automation system power supply/UPS redundancy requirements; generic, no ACC mention.",True,,high,False,"Provides detailed DCMS/control power architecture requirements (redundant UPS feeds, monitoring, single-failure survival, process station ride-through) suitable for retrieving electrical/control power design documents.","The following requirements shall be taken into consideration by the Contractor:
- in general, supply systems shall be designed in such a way that a power failure occurring in one of the components of a control circuit causes the selective drop of the power supply only to that circuit
- DCMS shall be equipped with redundant power supply system, suitable to be connected to different supply sources.
- it shall control power lines and perform all voltage sublevels and distributions requested for the internal architecture
- the outputs of all the supply units shall be monitored and each malfunction identified
- provisions shall be made for the relevant signalling in the control room
- the internal distribution of supplies shall be designed to meet the principle of survival to the single failure
- for the power supply of the automation system equipment, the following shall be available :
- -two separate 230 V AC voltage single phase, supplied from redundant UPS (Uninterrupted Power Supply) dedicated for DCMS/control system devices ( to be detailed later ))
- -one single 230 V AC voltage single phase supplied from one normal Plant service network for non-critical DCMS/control system cabinet auxiliaries ( lighting )
- the electrical supply that the system distributes to the instrumentation and actuators shall be taken from these sources. The supply of the instruments, when not sent on the same cable with the signal shall be distributed by the system which is acquiring the measurement signal
- the supply of process stations shall be redundant and made in such a way that they are not affected by possible failures of one of the sources. In any case, process stations shall be equipped with appropriate systems (emergency supply unit, data saving routine, non-volatile memories, etc.) enabling to keep the calibration and pre-set values unchanged, even in case of power failure, safeguarding the most significant internal variables and the codes of processing programs"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_206,"118, 119",8.7._Plant_Instrumentation,"8, 7",table,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation,Plant Instrumentation,Control and Instrumentation Philosophy,Instrumentation Standards and Signal Interfaces,,,"standardization of measurement and control equipment, minimum diversity of type and manufacture, IP54 protection degree, IP65 protection degree, EEx d / EEx i explosion proof, 4-20 mA analogue signals, 22-50 VDC status and alarm signals, two-wire smart transmitters (HART), Fieldbus communication, DCMS I/O hardwired, functional safety hardwired transmitters, SIL rated transmitters and switches, instrument range 60-75% of span, double pole changeover contacts, mercury switches prohibited",False,General plant-wide control & instrumentation philosophy and signal standards; no mention of ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or ACC-specific equipment.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit instrumentation design requirements (IP ratings, signal standards, HART/Fieldbus, DCMS I/O, functional safety/SIL separation) useful to retrieve I&C specifications and design criteria documents.","A consistent control and instrumentation philosophy shall apply throughout the plant and shall be implemented in terms of a range of equipment exhibiting a minimum diversity of type and manufacture. The objective shall be to standardize all measurement and control equipment throughout the plant (except for the GT/ST/HRSG/Package suppliers) in order to rationalize operation, maintenance and reduce spares holding.
Generally equipment shall be supplied from one composite range of measurement and control equipment as marked by a reputable manufacturer of international standing and shall have operational use on similar projects. The equipment shall be of modern compact design incorporating the latest developments in proven technology.
Minimum protection degree IP 54 shall be provided for equipment in normal environment. IP 65 shall be provided in dusty environments, for open buildings, for outdoor installation and field installed instrument or explosion proof equipment following Eexd or Eexi when required.
All components shall be designed for the local ambient conditions. Equipment shall be designed to withstand the worst site conditions without deterioration in its functionality beyond the typical wear and tear. Field equipment shall have an appropriate degree of environmental protection.
The instruments shall not be shared between control/monitoring systems and plant protection systems. In cases where this is not practical or there is sufficient technical justification (e.g. measurement redundancy) in doing so, a shared arrangement may be used subject to the approval of the Owner/Engineer. In this case, the field signals shall be connected directly to the plant protection system where the pre-processing of the signals shall be carried out, thus avoiding the possible dependence of the protection system on the related control system for its inputs.
Analogue signals shall be standardized at 4-20 mA in the field. Direct current plant status and alarm signals shall be standardized on a DC voltage level between 22 to 50V. Instruments without separate power supply shall be chosen (excepted where impossible). Power supplies for current loops shall have a dedicated fused link for protection and isolation.
Two wire programmable smart type transmitters (e.g. HART) shall be provided. Transmitters used for control and monitoring may be hardwired to the DCMS I/O system or connected by a proven 'Fieldbus' communication system. However, transmitters used in functional safety systems shall be hardwired.
All transmitters shall have local indication of the measured value either built-in or equipped with separately mounted local indicator. Transmitters (except temperature transmitter and radar level transmitters) shall be provided with test connections and instrument isolating valve manifolds where appropriate.
Instrument ranges shall be chosen such that the nominal operating point lies between 60% and 75% of the measurement span.
Transmitters shall be used wherever possible instead of switches however, switches may be used with the approval of the Owner/Engineer in cases where their use can be technically justified. Switches shall be of the heavy-duty type with double pole changeover contacts. Mercury switches shall not be used. Transmitters and switches used for functional safety shall have traceable and appropriate SIL ratings."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_207,119,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation,"8, 7",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation,Plant Instrumentation,Signal Standards,Pneumatic Transmission and Control Signals,,,"pneumatic transmission signals, pneumatic control signals, 0.2 to 1.0 bar, 3-15 psi",False,Generic pneumatic signal standard (0.2–1.0 bar); no ACC reference.,True,,medium,False,Defines a specific pneumatic signal standard range that can match instrumentation design criteria/specifications.,"Pneumatic transmission and control signals where offered, shall be of the 0.2 to 1.0 bar (3-15psi) standard range."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_208,120,8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments,"8, 7, 1",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Environmental Protection and Mounting,,,"field electrical items IP65, NEMA Type 4, pneumatic instruments IP54, NEMA Type 2, outdoor local equipment panels IP54, local instrument racks, stainless steel tubing/pipe, centralised location for cable routing, seal chambers, diaphragm seals, corrosive/viscous/congealing services",False,General field instrument environmental protection and mounting requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,False,"Provides concrete field-instrument installation and protection requirements (IP/NEMA, racks, tubing material, diaphragm seals) suitable for retrieving instrument installation specs/details.","All electrical items located in the field shall be weather-proof to IP65 or NEMA Type 4 whereas pneumatic instruments shall be weatherproof to IP54 or NEMA Type 2. Any local equipment panels used outdoors shall be weather-proof to IP54 or equivalent. For explosion proof equipment specified standards shall apply.
Components that are not line or vessel mounted shall be installed on local instrument racks and connected to the measurement source with stainless steel tubing or pipe. These racks shall be configured in a manner to group multiple devices in the same plant area to a centralised location for cable routing and to facilitate equipment servicing.
Seal chambers or diaphragm seals shall be used where fluid services are corrosive, viscous, congealing, deposit forming, or dangerous. Seals shall be located as close as possible to the primary isolating valve. Instruments in liquid services, which solidify at ambient temperature, shall also be provided with diaphragm seals."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_209,120,8.7.1.1._PRIMARY_ISOLATION_AND_INSTRUMENT_VALVES,"8, 7, 1, 1",content,"text, list_item",8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.1._PRIMARY_ISOLATION_AND_INSTRUMENT_VALVES,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Primary Isolation and Instrument Valves,,,"primary isolation valve at tapping point, two valves in series, above 25 bar(g) line pressure, hazardous services, lockable open/shut valves, blow down valves, direction of rotation marked, differential pressure equalising valves, secondary isolation valve at instrument, manifold fitting with integral test connections, instrument isolating valves rated at least 100°C, GTG package root valves not offered",False,Generic requirements for primary isolation and instrument valves; no ACC context.,True,,high,False,"Contains specific requirements for instrument root/isolation valves, DP equalising valves, pressure/temperature thresholds, and locking/marking—strong anchors for instrument hook-up and valve/manifold specifications.","The primary isolation valve shall be located at the tapping point of the process and rated for the process conditions (e.g. temperature, pressure etc.) of the main line.
Two valves in series shall be provided for:
- applications above 25bar(g) line pressure
- hazardous services
All primary isolating and blow down valves shall be capable of being locked in the open or shut position. The direction of rotation shall be clearly marked on the hand-wheel.
Instruments measuring differential pressure shall be provided with equalising valves downstream of the instrument isolating valves to permit zero checking.
In addition to the primary isolation valve a secondary isolation valve shall be provided at the instrument itself. The secondary isolation valve may be a part of a manifold fitting with integral test connections. The instrument isolating valves shall be located on the connecting pipe at the measuring device end of the line.. Instrument isolating valves shall be rated to at least 100°C and the pressure of the main process line.
Only for the GTG Package, equipment: Root valves are not offered at the tapping point of the process. Isolation valves are offered where possible (does not compromise safety) on instruments."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_210,"121, 122",8.7.1.2._INSTALLATION_REQUIREMENTS,"8, 7, 1, 2",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.2._INSTALLATION_REQUIREMENTS,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Installation Requirements,,,"BS 6739 compliance, 316L stainless steel tubing, dedicated tapping point per instrument, instrument isolating valves, vent/test valves, instrument manifolds, compression type tube fittings, impulse line slope at least 10%, pneumatic piping in perforated cable trays, support spacing 0.6 m, steam and water sampling tubing 12 mm or more, control air tubing 6 mm or more, galvanised steel air mains, no open blow down/drain inside panels, vent/drain to safe position, high temperature sample tubing insulation, valve/manifold temperature not exceed 55°C, lockable isolating valve upstream of filter regulator, access from floor level or permanent platforms, mounting brackets not attached to pipework/handrails",False,"General instrumentation installation requirements (BS 6739, tubing, vents/drains, access); no ACC mentioned.",True,,high,False,"Rich installation criteria with explicit standard reference (BS 6739), materials (316L), tubing sizes, slopes, support spacing, temperature limits, and mounting/access rules—high value for matching instrumentation installation specifications and typical details.","The installation of all instrumentation shall comply with the guidelines given in BS 6739 or an international equivalent standard. The piping/tubing to instruments shall be of stainless steel or a material corresponding to the piping class of the line to which it is connected. Erosion and corrosion problems shall be prevented by the use of stainless steel (316L) where appropriate.
Each tapping point shall be dedicated to only a single instrument.
All measuring devices shall be provided with instrument isolating valves, vent/test valves and instrument manifolds where appropriate to enable them to be disconnected from the connecting pipe work without having to disconnect or drain the pipe work. Instrument impulse lines shall use compression type tube fittings.
The impulse lines shall be arranged so as to avoid any gas bubbles entering piping for liquids (and liquids entering gas piping). Unless otherwise specified on the erection drawings, the slope of the impulse lines from the process shall be at least 10% of length (to the connection point when the instrument is placed higher than this connection point, and to the instrument when the instrument is placed lower than the connection point);
If necessary, pneumatic piping shall be laid in perforated cable trays supported at least every 0.6m.
All instruments and their piping shall be fixed in a way that prevents vibrations being generated or transmitted and the Contractor shall be taken into the fact that process piping and equipment may be subject to expansion or contraction.
The Contractor shall maintain a distance sufficient with the other equipment.
Primary process instrument and sampling tubing for steam and water systems shall be 316L stainless steel 12mm or more. Control air tubing shall be 6mm or more, 316 SS or sheathed copper. Air mains maybe galvanised steel;
There shall not be any open blow down or drain connections inside panels or local equipment housings. All instrument vent/drain points shall be directed to a safe position away from maintenance personnel. Sample tubing systems carrying high temperature samples shall be insulated in areas which require personnel protection.
The arrangement of pipe work and connections shall be such that the temperature of the instrument isolating valve or instrument valve manifold does not exceed 55°C.
For pneumatic connections, each individual air user shall be provided with a lockable line size isolating valve immediately upstream of a dedicated filter regulator.
It shall be possible to read and access all indicating instruments and controls from floor level or permanent platforms. Enough clearance shall be provided around instruments and controls to access and service them without having to dismantle nearby equipment or pipe work.
Instrument mounting brackets or posts shall not be attached to pipe work, removable flooring or handrails. The mounting fixture shall not be directly affixed to machinery or equipment that may be subject to vibration."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_211,122,8.7.1.2._INSTALLATION_REQUIREMENTS,"8, 7, 1, 2",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.2._INSTALLATION_REQUIREMENTS,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Installation Requirements,,,"instrument installation hook-up sheets, separate bill of material (BOM) for hook-ups, test and calibration points, maintenance (breakdown/routine/periodic/outage) for control and instrumentation systems, local configuration of instruments and actuators (site or factory), portable HART communicators, radio frequency radiation immunity, electric/magnetic interference (EMI) from electrical equipment",False,"Generic hook-up sheets, calibration points, HART/configuration and EMI requirements; no ACC signal.",True,,high,False,"Contains explicit technical requirements for instrument installation documentation, calibration access points, configuration tools (HART), and EMI/RF immunity affecting I&C design and installation deliverables.","Typical instrument installation hook up's sheets shall be made in order to define how instruments will be installed. Separate bill of material information shall be prepared to accompany these details.
Sufficient test and calibration points shall be provided to enable staff to carry out breakdown, routine, periodic and outage maintenance of the control and instrumentation systems.
Full details of local configuration of instruments and actuators, whether on Site or out at factory, shall be provided and all software and hardware (such as portable HART communicators/any special device/laptop) as necessary for configuration shall also be provided.
The presence of radio frequency radiation from portable radio transceivers or fixed installations such as radar and electric/magnetic interference from electrical equipment shall not be detrimental to the performance of the control and instrumentation equipment."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_212,"122, 123",8.7.1.2._INSTALLATION_REQUIREMENTS,,content,"text, list_item",8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.2._INSTALLATION_REQUIREMENTS,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Installation Requirements,,,"instrument calibration and linearity testing, reference instrument accuracy 2x higher, calibration standard 2x more accurate, gauging and stamping certificate, certificate age not older than 12 months, five-point calibration (0/25/50/75/100%), switching thresholds verification (H, HH, L, LL), hysteresis recording, mA deviation for analog measurements, static pressure deviation (DP instruments)",False,General instrument calibration/testing procedure; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,True,,high,False,"Provides detailed calibration/testing acceptance criteria and calibration sheet content for field instruments, useful for retrieving I&C testing/commissioning specs and calibration procedures/templates.","In general, each instrument covered by the Contract shall be tested regarding calibration and linearity, which tests shall be carried out by the Contractor.
The reference instruments used for calibration shall have accuracy at least two times higher than that of the instrument that is being calibrated.
The reference instruments used in calibration shall have been recently compared to a calibration standard which is also at least two times more accurate than the instrument to be calibrated. Recently means that the certificate of ""gauging and stamping"" must be available, and not be older than 12 months.
Each instrument covered by the Contract shall be tested for five readings in both directions: 0%, 25%, 50%, 75% and 100% of the set range. Switching thresholds shall be verified in two directions, and switching thresholds shall be adjusted in function of the thresholds (H, HH in upward direction; L, LL in downward direction).
During calibration no account shall be taken of shift in point zero as may result of installation of the instrument.
At least the following information shall be given in the calibration sheets:
- accuracy required of the instrument to be verified;
- verified readings in two directions;
- %deviation, and mA deviation for analog measurements;
- static pressure deviation (differential pressure instruments);
- verified switching thresholds and their type (H, HH, L, LL, ...) in two directions;
- hysteresis.
Instruments delivered by the suppliers with an appropriate calibration sheet may be, in case of doubt, readjusted.
Recalibration will be required only if technically justified."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_213,123,8.7.1.2._INSTALLATION_REQUIREMENTS,,content,"text, list_item",8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.2._INSTALLATION_REQUIREMENTS,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Installation Requirements,,,"post-installation complementary calibration and checks, impulse line filling, zero-shifting adjustment for manometers, pressure transmitters calibration (0% and 100%) both directions, pressure switches threshold adjustment, level measurement bottles filling, ultrasonic level switch calibration, mass flow meter calibration, analyzer calibration",False,"Post-installation calibration/checks for instruments (impulse lines, analyzers, flow meters); no ACC context.",True,,high,False,"Lists specific post-installation calibration/check activities for multiple instrument types, supporting retrieval of commissioning/calibration procedures and instrument installation details.","After installation of the equipments, the Contractor shall perform at least the following complementary calibration and checks:
- filling of the impulse lines, zero-shifting adjustment for manometers, p transmitters, pressure switches, with thresholds adjustment;
- post calibration on two points (0% and 100%) in two directions, for p or p transmitters;
- filling of the bottles for level measurements/switches;
- calibration of ultrasonic level switches, mass flow meters, and other specific measurements;
- calibration of analyzers."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_214,"123, 124",8.7.1.4._PRESSURE_INSTRUMENTS,"8, 7, 1, 4",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.4._PRESSURE_INSTRUMENTS,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Pressure Instruments,,,"pressure transmitter overrange 150% nominal input range, DP transmitter withstand full rated line pressure as differential input, siphon loops for process temperature >70°C, impulse tubing cooling below 70°C, snubbers/pulsation dampers for pulsation, pressure gauge over-range protection, vent/test valves, blow-out devices for >25 barg, Bourdon-tube pressure gauges, liquid-filled pressure gauges 1% precision, avoid excessive vibration locations, differential pressure measurement on critical filters/strainers with control room alarms",False,"Generic pressure instrument requirements (DP transmitters, siphon loops, gauges); no ACC-specific mention.",True,,high,False,"Contains concrete technical criteria (overrange %, temperature threshold, pressure threshold, gauge type, DP monitoring/alarm requirements) that map to instrument datasheets/specifications and P&ID/I&C design documents.","Pressure transmitters shall withstand at least 150% of nominal input range without damage or calibration change. All differential pressure transmitters shall be capable of withstanding without damage or calibration change, full rated line pressure as a differential input.
Siphon loops shall be used for pressure gauges and transmitters employed in applications where the temperature of the process medium exceeds 70°C. Alternatively, use of impulse tubing from isolation valve to transmitter and cooling of process fluid in the impulse tubing below 70°C is also permitted. However, the length of impulse tubing shall be sufficient enough to cooldown the process medium. Moreover, where pressure measurements are subject to pulsation, snubbers (pulsation dampers) shall be fitted.
Pressure gauges with low ranges shall be fitted with over-range protection in applications where a higher containment pressure is involved. Gauges shall be fitted with vent/test valves as standard, together with blow out devices when used in applications greater than 25barg. Pressure gauges with Bourdon-tube and spring-type mechanism shall generally be used.
Pressure gauges shall be liquid filled type with 1% precision or better.
Where pressure measurements are subject to pulsation, snubbers (dampers) shall be fitted. Installation of pressure instruments at locations subject to excessive vibration shall be avoided.
Filters and strainers required for reliable, safe and continuous operation of the Plant (including main air filters) shall have differential pressure measurements providing readings and alarms in the control room. All other non-critical filters and strainers shall be provided with a suitable local monitoring device."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_215,124,8.7.1.5._TEMPERATURE_INSTRUMENTS,"8, 7, 1, 5",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.5._TEMPERATURE_INSTRUMENTS,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Temperature Instruments,,,"RTD vs thermocouple selection (accuracy/reliability/range), Type K thermocouples, PT100 4-wire RTDs, protective sheath for air/inert gas/radiant measurements, thermowells for steam/water/hazardous applications, head-mounted temperature transmitters, cold junction compensation, extension/compensating cables, thermocouple disconnection head clear of pocket and lagging",False,"Generic temperature instrument requirements (RTD/TC, thermowells); no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.",True,,high,False,"Defines preferred temperature sensor types and installation requirements (thermowells, transmitters, cabling, cold junction compensation), useful for retrieving temperature instrument specs/datasheets and installation standards.","The selection of Resistance Temperature Detectors (RTDs) or thermocouples for temperature measurement shall be based on accuracy, reliability and measurement range considerations. Type K thermocouples and PT100 4-wire RTDs are preferred for normal applications.
Suitable protective sheath shall be used for sensing elements when used for air, inert gas and radiant temperature measurements. For steam, water and hazardous applications, thermowells shall be employed.
Head mounted temperature transmitters are preferred where feasible. Transmitters for thermocouples shall incorporate cold junction compensation and when mounted remotely from the sensing element shall use suitable extension cables or compensating cables.
Where thermocouple assemblies are supplied, a disconnection head shall be arranged clear of the pocket and associated lagging and shall be sufficiently long to prevent excessive temperature of the head assembly."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_216,125,8.7.1.6._FLOW_INSTRUMENTS,"8, 7, 1, 6",content,"text, list_item",8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.6._FLOW_INSTRUMENTS,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Flow Instruments,,,"primary device carrier material same as pipeline, butt-welded carrier for high pressure applications, flanged connections, differential pressure flow transmitter, tapping point isolation valves, 3-valve manifold / 5-valve manifold, tariff/performance metering calibration certification, ISO 5167-1, ISO 5168 uncertainty assessment, ISO 9951 turbine meters, ASME PTC 22 fuel gas performance flow metering (GT OEM), annubar, Pitot tube with Δp-cell, mass flow meters, vortex flowmeter, orifice / nozzle / venturi, electromagnetic flowmeter, straight length per manufacturer both sides, density/temperature/pressure correction measurements near flow meter",False,"General flow instrument requirements (ISO 5167, DP transmitters, turbine meters) with no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention or context.",True,,high,False,"Contains detailed technical requirements for flow measurement devices, installation, manifolds/valves, metering purpose, and applicable ISO/ASME standards suitable for matching instrumentation specifications/datasheets.","The carrier for the primary device for high pressure applications shall be of similar material to the pipeline in which it is mounted and shall be butt-welded into the pipeline. Other applications shall use flanged connections wherever possible.
Flow transmitters using differential pressure devices shall be provided with tapping point isolation valves, 3 valve manifolds and drain valves or 5-valve manifolds, as required.
Meters used for tariff/performance metering purposes shall be supplied, complete with adjacent pipe sections where necessary, with calibration certification from an agreed laboratory. Where density, temperature or pressure corrections to tariff quality performance monitoring signals are required, such measurements shall be made close to the flow meter location.
The flow measurement system shall be designed, built and installed to ISO 5167-1 or an equivalent international standard. It is acceptable only for the GT OEM to apply fuel gas performance flow metering as per ASME PTC 22. The uncertainty of the measurement systems shall be assessed in accordance with ISO 5168 and the relevant parts of ISO 5167. Turbine meters shall comply with the relevant requirements of ISO 9951.
Unless specified otherwise in Exhibit B, Part 1, the flow measurements transmitter used for various services shall be:
- gaseous fluids: annubar or Pitot tube with ∆p-cell, mass flow meters or Vortex. For flow measurements not dedicated to settlement system (metering), orifices are allowed
- liquid or vapour: flow orifice, nozzle, venturi, electromagnetic
- straight length specified by Manufacturer (to be foreseen at both sides) shall be respected"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_217,"125, 126",8.7.1.7._LEVEL_INSTRUMENTS,"8, 7, 1, 7",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.7._LEVEL_INSTRUMENTS,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Level Instruments,,,"level measurement displacement, level measurement differential pressure, local level indication direct measuring devices, remove level device without vessel out of service, tight shut-off isolation valves pressure rating to vessel, external chambers for level switches, isolation valves drains vents for chambers, level gauges top and bottom isolating valves, full bore drain valve, plugged vent, flanged connections same rating as vessel, float switches glandless, test facilities without removal from process",False,Generic level instrument requirements; no ACC-related equipment or references.,True,,high,False,"Provides specific design/maintenance and installation requirements for level transmitters, switches, gauges, and isolation arrangements—useful for retrieving level instrumentation specs and typical installation details.","Level measurement shall generally be made by either displacement or differential pressure measurement, however other techniques may be considered.
Direct measuring devices shall be used for local indication of level. Unless otherwise approved by the Owner it shall be possible to remove any level measurement device without the vessel or other instruments being taken out of service.
It shall be possible with both the displacer type and differential pressur type of transmitter to isolate the transmitter from the process by suitable tight shut-off valves conforming to the pressure rating of the vessel to which they are connected and allow removal of the transmitter for maintenance without causing shutdown of the process.
Switches used for level detection shall be fitted within external chambers, which are provided with isolation valves, and suitable drains and vents.
Level gauges shall be fitted with top and bottom isolating valves with a full bore drain valve at the bottom and plugged vent at the top. Flanged connections which have the same rating as the vessel shall be used.
Float switches shall be glandless. Switches used for level detection shall provide facilities for testing the mechanical and electrical operation of the switch without its removal from the process."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_218,126,8.7.1.8._ANALYTICAL_INSTRUMENTS,"8, 7, 1, 8",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.8._ANALYTICAL_INSTRUMENTS,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Analytical Instruments,,,"process fluid analytical instruments, in-line analyser or nearby installation, air-conditioned container analyser, sampling systems, sample delay time consistent with control requirements, analyser close to sample take-off point, reduce sampling lag by increasing sample velocity, continuous / infrequent / interrupted cycle operation, one year consumables and operating spares, reference gases and solutions for calibration, spare sensors for recalibration/rejuvenation",False,Generic analytical instruments/sampling systems; no ACC signal.,True,,high,False,"Defines analyser installation approach (sampling systems, lag/delay constraints) and spares/consumables requirements—strong anchors for analyser/sampling system documentation.","Chemical or physical properties of process fluids and substances shall be measured by analytical instruments that are installed either directly in the process line or vessel or in the vicinity of the plant area (e.g. in an airconditioned container) and connected by means of sampling systems. The delay time between the sample being withdrawn from the process and being available to the analyser shall be consistent with the measurement and control requirements of the particular process. The preferred method of keeping sampling lags to acceptable limits shall be by locating the analyser close to the sample take-off point. When this is not practicable the distance velocity lag of the sampling system shall be reduced by increasing the velocity of the sample.
Analytical instruments shall be selected to operate either continuously, infrequently or on a continuously interrupted cycle as most suited to the measurement and the intended application.
All analytical instruments shall be supplied with a minimum of one year's supply of consumables and operating spares at normal usage. This shall include as a minimum, reference gases and solutions for instrument calibration as well as spare sensors required for replacement or removal for recalibration or rejuvenation at regular intervals."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_219,127,8.7.1.9._VIBRATION_INSTRUMENTS,"8, 7, 1, 9",table,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.9._VIBRATION_INSTRUMENTS,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Vibration Instruments,,,"vibration monitoring system rotating machines, GTG vibration monitoring, STG vibration monitoring, boiler feed pumps vibration monitoring, critical pumps at 13.8 kV, rotating machines ≥200 kW vibration devices, DCS indication/warning/alarm, non-contacting proximity transducers, radially mounted 90° apart same transverse plane, absolute vibration bearing pedestal, seismic transducers velocity/accelerometer, relative and absolute vibration bandwidth frequency spectrum, ISO 10816",False,Vibration monitoring for rotating machines (GTG/STG/pumps/fans) but no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser-specific mention.,True,,high,False,"Contains concrete vibration monitoring requirements, machine applicability thresholds, sensor types/mounting, DCS alarming, and ISO 10816 reference—useful for matching vibration monitoring and rotating equipment I&C documents.","The required vibration monitoring system shall be provided as per relevant standards for rotating machines wherever applicable. Vibration monitoring, analysing and diagnostic for GTG, STG, Boiler feed pumps and other critical pumps at 13.8kV levels shall be as per RFP and SFR requirements. All other rotating machines (e.g Pumps, fans, etc) with a rated motor capacity not less than 200 kW shall be equipped with vibration measurement devices with indication/warning/alarm in the DCS. All rotating machines shall be capable of satisfactory operation when delivering varying quantity from minimum to the design maximum and shall be constructed to ensure satisfactory operation under all climatic conditions prevailing at site.
Vibration measuring devices shall be selected for operation in their particular intended environment and duty. It shall use non-contacting transducers (proximity type) and shall be radially mounted in the same transverse plane perpendicular to the shaft axis and 90° apart. Absolute vibration measurement shall measure the bearing pedestal vibration and shall use seismic transducers (velocity or accelerometer type). The measurement of both relative and absolute vibration shall have sufficient bandwidth so that the frequency spectrum of vibration of the rotating plant is adequately covered.
For the protection, analysing and diagnosing of vibrations in major rotating machinery, thevibration monitoring system shall be provided in accordance with ISO 10816.
The vibration monitoring system shall be reliable, accurate, easy to maintain, and suitable for use in such environmental conditions appertaining to the intended plant installation."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_220,"127, 128",8.7.1.10._TRANSMITTERS,"8, 7, 1, 10",table,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.10._TRANSMITTERS,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Transmitters,,,"two-wire transmitters, 230 V AC analyser supply isolated output circuit, individual fusing for 230 V AC transmitters, SMART transmitters, local indicator with physical units, isolating/vent/blowdown valves, thermometer pockets, interconnecting pipework, independent vibration-free support, correct mounting attitude no stress on connections, in-situ calibration test connections, corrosion resistant wetted parts materials, DP/flow transmitter equalizing valve manifold close coupled, variable area flow transmitter all-metal analogue signal, grouping of transmitters on similar duties, on-site range changing, zero suppression/elevation zero shift ≥100% span, DP transmitter withstand full rated line pressure as differential input, pressure transmitter withstand 115% nominal input range, withstand 100% vacuum, thermocouple/RTD transmitters plug-in range cards, electrical isolation input/output",False,"General transmitter requirements (SMART, DP/flow, vacuum withstand) without any ACC reference.",True,,high,False,"Provides detailed transmitter design, power, mounting, calibration, materials, manifold, range/zero features, and overpressure/vacuum survivability requirements—strong retrieval anchors for transmitter specifications and installation standards.","Generally two wire transmitters shall be used. If or some special purposes (e.g. analyzers) 230 V AC analysers supply is required the output circuit shall be isolated. All transmitters supplied with 230 V AC shall be individually fused. All transmitters shall be of the SMART type.
All transmitters shall be fitted with and indicator displaying appropriate physical units which may be read clearly by the plant attendant from an easily accessible position. Transmitters shall be provided with all necessary isolating, vent and blowdown valves, thermometer pockets and interconnecting pipework.
All transmitters shall be supported in a vibration free manner independently of the plant connections and shall be mounted in the correct attitude. No stress shall be imposed through the connections between plant and the transmitter.
GT OEM's instruments shall be sufficiently protected from local conditions and are rated for outdoors however; it is acceptable only for GT OEM to not always have separate enclosures for instruments. Facilities shall be provided for the connection of test instruments to the input and output of each transmitter to enable in-situ calibration to be carried out.
Bodies, primary elements and wetted parts shall be of a material that is corrosion resistant to process fluid and environmental conditions.
Differential pressure/ flow transmitters shall be furnished with a close coupled equalizing valve manifold.
Variable area type flow transmitters, if applicable, shall be of all metal construction with appropriate means of analogue signal transmission.
On large installations locations for transmitters on similar plant duties shall be grouped unless this is economically infeasible.
To maintain minimum spares holding, the range of transmitters used for general pressure and differential pressure applications shall have facilities for simple on site range changing without changing of components. In addition each transmitter shall incorporate means of zero suppression elevation allowing zero shifts equivalent to at least 100% of maximum transmitter span.
All differential pressure transmitters shall be capable of withstanding without damage or calibration change, full rated line pressure as a differential input. Pressure transmitters shall withstand at least 115% of nominal input range without damage or calibration change.
All transmitters potentially subjected to vacuum shall be capable of withstanding 100% vacuum without damage.
Transmitters for use on electrical input signals such as thermocouples or RTDs shall allow range change on site by the use of simple plug-in range cards. They shall incorporate electrical isolation between input and output.
Transmitters shall meet the following minimum requirements unless agreed or specified otherwise by the Engineer."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_221,"128, 129",8.7.1.10._TRANSMITTERS,"8, 7, 1, 10",table,"table, list_item",8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.10._TRANSMITTERS,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Transmitters,,,"22–50 VDC, 4–20 mA, accuracy better than 0.2%, linearity 0.1% of span, repeatability 0.1% of span, stability 0.1% per annum, vibration effect +1.0% (5–300 Hz, 10 m/s2, 1g), humidity 5–95%, ambient temperature 0°C to 52°C, ambient temperature effect 0.5% per 55°C (-40°C to +85°C), design pressure rating ≥200% of maximum process static pressure, dead band ≤0.1% of span",False,"Transmitter technical specs (4–20 mA, accuracy, vibration, temp) with no ACC context.",True,,high,False,Provides explicit transmitter performance/environmental requirements and signal standards useful to match instrument datasheets/specifications.,"● DC Voltage Level:, 1 = Between 22 to 50 volts. ● signal current: Between, 1 = 4 to 20 mA. ● accuracy (of signal current with respect to measured variable): Better than 0.2%, 1 = ● accuracy (of signal current with respect to measured variable): Better than 0.2%. ● linearity:, 1 = 0.1% of span. ● repeatability:, 1 = 0.1% of span. ● stability:, 1 = 0.1% of nominal input range per annum. ● dead Band:, 1 = Not exceeding 0.1% of span. ● hysteresis:, 1 = Less than 0.1% of output span
- discrimination: A change on input of 0.02% of actual range shall produce a measurable change of output
- maximum permissible effect of vibration on output signal: +1.0% of output span between 5 and 300 Hz at l0 m/s2 (1g)
- humidity range:
- ambient temp. range:
- 5 - 95%
- 0°C -52°C
- ambient temp. Effect: Not exceeding 0.5% of maximum span per 55°C change between the limits of: -40°C and +85°C
- design pressure rating: Not less than 200% of maximum process static pressure"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_222,129,8.7.1.11._AMBIENT_CONDITIONS_MEASUREMENT_FACILITIES,"8, 7, 1, 11",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.11._AMBIENT_CONDITIONS_MEASUREMENT_FACILITIES,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Ambient Conditions Measurement,,,"ambient air temperature (dry bulb), barometric pressure, relative humidity, wind speed, Ambient Conditions Measurement Facilities, interface to Plant DCMS, monitoring and repeating data to DCMS, site location for ambient instruments",False,"Ambient conditions measurement (temperature, pressure, humidity, wind) repeated to DCMS; no explicit ACC mention.",True,,high,False,"Defines required ambient condition instruments and DCMS interface, supporting retrieval of met station/ambient measurement specs and I&C interface documents.","The Contractor shall install the Ambient Conditions Measurement Facilities at a suitable location within the Site.
The Ambient Conditions Measurement Facilities shall include all the instrumentation for monitoring the ambient air temperature (dry bulb), ambient barometric pressure, relative humidity and wind speed . Data shall be repeated to the Plant DCMS."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_223,129,8.7.1.12._CONTINUOUS_EMISSIONS_MONITORING,"8, 7, 1, 12",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.1.12._CONTINUOUS_EMISSIONS_MONITORING,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Continuous Emissions Monitoring (CEMS),,,"CEMS (Continuous Emissions Monitoring System), GT emissions monitoring, HRSG emissions monitoring, Reserve Boiler emissions monitoring, NOx, CO, O2, interface to DCMS for display and recording",False,CEMS requirements for GT/HRSG/boiler emissions; unrelated to ACC.,True,,high,False,States continuous monitoring/recording requirements and analytes plus DCMS interface—strong anchors for emissions monitoring system documents.,"The GTs, HRSGs and Reserve Boiler(s) shall be equipped to continuously monitor and record NOx, CO and O2 emissions. The continuous emissions monitoring system (CEMS) shall be interfaced to the DCMS for display and recording of the analytical measurements."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_224,"129, 130",8.7.2.1._SOLENOID_VALVES,"8, 7, 2, 1",list,"list_item, text",8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.2.1._SOLENOID_VALVES,Plant Instrumentation,Final Control Elements,Solenoid Valves,,,"5-way solenoid valves (bistable, 2 separated solenoids), 3-way solenoid valves, 24 VDC preferred, 230 VDC, 125 VDC acceptable, local manual override, position detectors / logic sensors for isolating valves without limit switches, adjustable retarders on inputs/exhausts, connector electrical connection on solenoids, diodes or varistors for overvoltage protection, pilot fluid external connections, filters and lubricators",False,Generic solenoid valve requirements for actuation; no ACC-related signal.,True,,medium,True,"Contains clear technical requirements for solenoid valves (types, voltages, protection, position feedback), but hierarchy_context appears inconsistent (8.7.2 under 8.7.1), so section placement may need verification.","- 5-way solenoid valves (bistable-2 separated solenoids), for actuation of bistable valves shall be provided by Contractor
- 3-way solenoid valves for actuation of fail open of fail closed valves shall be provided by Contractor
- operating voltage: shall be 24 VDC (preference) or 230 VDC, 125 VDC acceptable for the GTG package
- a built-in local manual operation allowing to force position whether solenoids are powered up or not shall be provided by the Contractor; however it is understood that local operating and monitoring of systems are not required for Plant normal operation, and for that, manual operation on solenoid valve shall not be necessary. Solenoid valves operating isolating valves without limit switches shall be provided with position detectors or with (logic) sensors on the process fluid in order to allow signaling of actual state (this point is not applicable only for the GTG package supplied by the GTG OEM wherein the GTG OEM's standard practice shall be followed)
- inputs and/or exhausts shall be fitted with built-in adjustable retarders
- electrical connection shall be made by connector on solenoids It is acceptable for the GT OEM only to use their standard terminal blocks inside junction boxes of the GTG Package
- DC Solenoids valves shall be fitted with diodes or varistors installed as short as possible to the coil to prevent overvoltage peaks due to power cut off or reverse polarity
Orifices for external connections of pilot fluid if necessary; filters and lubricators if appropriate, shall be included in Contractor's scope of supply."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_225,130,8.7.2.2._CONTROL_VALVES_AND_ACTUATORS,"8, 7, 2, 2",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.2.2._CONTROL_VALVES_AND_ACTUATORS,Plant Instrumentation,Final Control Elements,Control Valves and Actuators,,,"local control facility, local valve position indication, remote operation from CCR via DCMS, local/remote mode selection transmitted to DCMS, pneumatic actuated control valves for modulating duty, motorised control valves for inching duty, on/off valves with solenoid pilot valve, end-position detection by limit switches, valve position transmitters, LVDT for valve position feedback",False,Generic control valves/actuators requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention.,True,,high,True,Provides functional and instrumentation requirements for control valves/actuators and DCMS/CCR interface; hierarchy_context mismatch suggests section hierarchy should be checked.,"Irrespective of the specific function that a control valve performs in the control scheme, i.e. modulating, inching or on/off, all control valves shall have a local control facility and local indication of the actual position of the valve. The normal operating mode shall be 'remote' from the CCR via the DCMS, the selection of which shall be local to the valve and transmitted to the DCMS.
Control valves shall be supplied with suitable actuators matched to the operational and environmental requirements of the plant. In general, pneumatic actuated control valves shall be used for modulating duties. However, motorised control valves or hydraulic valves may also be used in specific control loops with the approval of the Owner. Control valves for inching duty shall be of the motorised type. On/off control valves may be motorised or pneumatically actuated with a solenoid pilot valve.
In general, all control valves shall have end-position detection by limit switches and indication in the CCR. Modulating and inching valves shall have transmitters or LVDT's to indicate the valve position in the CCR, the measurement of which shall be taken directly from the actual valve position."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_226,131,8.7.2.3._MOTORIZED_VALVES,"8, 7, 2, 3",table,"text, list_item",8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.2.3._MOTORIZED_VALVES,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Motorized Valves,,,"electric actuator unit (motor, gearbox, controls), limit switches and torque switches, lockable local/remote selector switch, hand wheel emergency operation; motor disengagement, torque adjustable 80–115% of rated torque, semiconductor power-electronic switches (no contactors), anti-condensate heater, DCMS control/supervision signals (open/close, position feedback, torque faults, local/remote, general fault), Gas Turbine package exceptions (lockable/hand wheel)",False,General motorized valve actuator requirements; no mention of ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or ACC-specific systems.,True,,high,False,"Contains detailed technical requirements for motorized valve actuators and DCMS interface signals suitable for matching to valve/actuator datasheets, I/O lists, and control system interface documents.","Motorized valves shall consist of self-contained actuator units with motor, gearbox, electric/electronic controls, limit/torque switches and all auxiliary devices installed in the actuator housing. A lockable local/remote selector switch with open/close controls shall be located on the actuator. Only for the Gas Turbine package, for safety reasons if some valves are not lockable it is acceptable. The actuator shall be provided with a hand wheel for operation of the valve in an emergency. Only for the Gas Turbine package, for safety reasons if some actuators do not have hand wheels it is acceptable. The operation of the hand wheel shall disengage the actuating motor to prevent damage.
Each end of the actuator travel shall be fitted with suitable limit and torque switches and correctly set up for preventing over travel and consequential damage to the actuator. The stopping of the operating mechanism on limit or torque switch shall be determined based on the specific application for which the valve is used. Adjustment of maximum torque shall be performed at works. Torque shall be adjustable from 80 to 115% of rated torque value demanded by valve;
Semiconductor power-electronic switches rather than contactors shall be used in the actuator for switching the motor supply.
Motorized valves shall be equipped with Anti-condensate heater.
Control and supervision signals to DCMS or Control System:
- OPEN and CLOSE control
- open and Closed position in feedback
- open and close torque faults
- motor in 'local/remote control'
- motor General 'fault'"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_227,"131, 132",8.7.2.4._PNEUMATIC_ACTUATORS/POSITIONERS,"8, 7, 2, 4",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.2.4._PNEUMATIC_ACTUATORS/POSITIONERS,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Pneumatic Actuators and Positioners,,,"electro-pneumatic converter 4–20 mA to 0.2–1.0 bar, solenoid pilot valve for on/off applications, fail-safe valve position on loss of signal/air (open/closed/stay-put), pneumatic control valve fail-safe position defined during detailed design, volume boosters for fast-acting loops, positioner and converter pressure gauges (supply, input, output)",False,Generic pneumatic actuators/positioners requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,False,Provides specific pneumatic actuator/positioner design and signal conversion requirements with numeric ranges and fail-safe philosophy useful for retrieving control valve/actuator specifications and instrument datasheets.,"An electro-pneumatic converter shall generate a 0.2 to 1.0 bar pneumatic signal from a 4 to 20 mA electric signal for use by the actuator positioner for modulating duties. On/off applications shall make use of a solenoid pilot valve with a suitable operating voltage to minimise volt drop on the connecting cable.
Control valve actuators shall be designed to drive the valve to a safe position from the process view point on failure of signal and/or air supply. For each valve application, due consideration shall be given to whether the safe state is open, closed or for the valve to remain in the last position prior to loss of control ('stay put' design). The fail-safe position for all pneumatic control valves shall be advised during the detailed design of the system.
Volume boosters shall be provided between the actuator and the control valve where necessary, e.g. on fast-acting loops.
All positioners and electro/pneumatic converters shall be furnished with pressure gauges for air supply, signal input and control air output."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_228,132,8.7.2.5._MANUAL_VALVES,"8, 7, 2, 5",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.2.5._MANUAL_VALVES,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Manual Valves,,,"manual valves critical to process, end position limit switches, CCR indication, DCMS HMI indication",False,Manual valve limit switch indication requirement; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.,True,,medium,False,"Defines instrumentation requirement (limit switches) and indication path (CCR via DCMS HMI) for manual valves, supporting retrieval of valve limit switch and I/O/interface documentation.",Manually operated valves critical to the process shall be provided with end position limit switches for indication in the CCR via the DCMS HMI.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_229,132,8.7.2.6._MOTOR_CONTROL_CENTRES,"8, 7, 2, 6",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.2.6._MOTOR_CONTROL_CENTRES,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Motor Control Centres,,,"MCC hardwired interface to control system, local-MCC / remote-DCMS control selection, on/off command inputs to MCC, feedback signals to DCMS (on/off/unavailable/fault), DCMS binary output module direct connection (no interposing relays), standardised MCC-control system interface, segregation for duty/standby pumps/fans (cabling and DCMS binary I/O modules)",False,Generic MCC interface for pumps/fans; does not reference ACC fans or Air Cooled Condenser scope.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit MCC interface and I/O segregation requirements that can match to existing control system interface diagrams, I/O lists, and MCC specifications.","Motor control centres (MCC) used for on/off control of pumps, fans etc. shall have a hardwired interface with the related control system. The MCC shall have the facility to select the point of control, i.e. local-MCC or remote-DCMS, on/off local controls, as well as local status indications.
The interface between the MCC and the control system shall be standardised. This shall include on/off command inputs to the MCC and on/off/unavailable/fault feedback signals to the DCMS. The command inputs to the MCC shall be electrically rated to allow a direct connection (i.e. without the use of external interposing relays) with the DCMS binary output module.
Segregation in terms of cabling and DCMS binary I/O modules shall be provided for duty/standby pumps/fans."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_230,"132, 133",8.7.2.7._VARIABLE_SPEED_DRIVES,"8, 7, 2, 7",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.2.7._VARIABLE_SPEED_DRIVES,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Variable Speed Drives,,,"VSD for flow control with large pumps/fans, cost-benefit analysis for energy saving/modulating control performance, critical signals hardwired (speed reference/feedback, trip command, fault, common alarm), non-critical signals via serial data link, standard fieldbus communication protocol, segregation for duty/standby pumps/fans (cabling and DCMS I/O modules)",False,Generic VSD requirements for pumps/fans; no explicit ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention.,True,,high,False,"Defines VSD application criteria and detailed DCMS signal/interface philosophy (hardwired vs fieldbus) useful for retrieving VSD interface specs, control narratives, and I/O documentation.","Electronic variable speed drives (VSD) shall be considered for flow control where large pumps/fans would be employed with modulating valves/dampers. The use of a VSD shall be based on a cost-benefit analysis in terms of energy saving and/or the need for achieving the required level of modulating control performance of the process.
The signals exchanged between the VSD and the DCMS shall be categorised into critical and non-critical. The critical signals (e.g. speed reference, speed feedback, drive trip command, drive fault, drive common alarm) shall be hardwired. The non-critical signals (e.g. internal drive status and warning signals) shall be communicated to the DCMS via a serial data communication link using a standard fieldbus communication protocol.
Segregation in terms of cabling and DCMS I/O modules shall be provided for duty/standby pumps/fans."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_231,133,8.7.2.8._GAS_DETECTION_SYSTEM,"8, 7, 2, 8",content,text,8.7._Plant_Instrumentation > 8.7.1._Field_mounted_instruments > 8.7.2.8._GAS_DETECTION_SYSTEM,Plant Instrumentation,Field Mounted Instruments,Gas Detection System,,,"intrinsically safe fuel gas leakage detection, international approvals, interface with DCMS, CCR alarm on gas concentration exceeding alarm limit, system fault alarms on DCMS workstation, gas turbine trip on gas concentration trip level",False,Fuel gas detection system for gas turbine; unrelated to ACC.,True,,high,False,"Clear technical requirements for fuel gas detection system, DCMS/CCR alarming, and gas turbine trip logic.","A field proven and intrinsically safe fuel gas leakage detection system with international approvals shall be supplied. .
The gas detection system shall interface with the DCMS to warn the CCR operator that a gas concentration exceeding the alarm limit has been detected. System faults shall also be alarmed on the DCMS workstation.
A gas turbine trip shall be implemented when the gas concentration exceeds the trip level setting."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_232,133,8.8._General_I&C_Requirements,"8, 8",content,text,8.8._General_I&C_Requirements,General I&C Requirements,,,,,"sun shades for outdoor instruments, isolating circuits insulation resistance 20 MΩ, insulation resistance measured at 500 VDC",False,"General I&C requirements (sun shades, insulation resistance); no ACC signal.",True,,medium,False,Contains specific I&C installation and electrical insulation resistance requirement useful for matching I&C specifications/standards documents.,"Sun shades shall be installed for outdoor instruments that are subjected to direct sunlight.
All isolating circuits and devices used in I&C equipment shall be designed to provide an insulation resistance of not less than 20 MΩ measured at 500 VDC."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_233,"133, 134",8.8.1._I&C_cabling,"8, 8, 1",content,text,8.8._General_I&C_Requirements > 8.8.1._I&C_cabling,General I&C Requirements,I&C Cabling,,,,"multi-pair screened cables, multi-core cables, optical fibre cables, rodent proof cables, flame resistant and fire retardant, copper conductors minimum 1 mm2, 20% spare conductors, separation from power cables, segregation for different systems, redundant communication network cables separate routes, volt drop considerations",False,General I&C cabling requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,True,,high,False,"Detailed technical cabling requirements (types, fire/rodent protection, conductor size, spares, segregation, redundancy routing) suitable for retrieving I&C cable specs and layouts.","I&C cabling shall use multi-pair cables with screens, multi-core cables and optical fibre cables as appropriate. The cables shall be of the rodent proof, flame resistant and fire retardant type. The protection method for I&C cables from physical abuse and rodent/insect attack shall be to the approval of the Owner/Engineer.
The multi-pair/multi-core cables shall be made up of copper conductors of not less than 1 mm2 cross-section except for those associated with interposing relay cubicles and power supplies where higher conductor cross-section shall be chosen based on volt drop considerations. I&C cables of less than 1 mm2 cross-section shall be subject to approval by the Owner/Engineer.
There shall be 20% spare conductors as a minimum in I&C cables.
Adequate separation of I&C cables from power cables and segregation of I&C cables for different systems shall be provided. Redundant cables (e.g. communication network cables) shall be installed over separate routes to minimise the possibility of a common failure."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_234,"134, 135",8.8.2._Cubicles_and_racks,"8, 8, 2",content,text,8.8._General_I&C_Requirements > 8.8.2._Cubicles_and_racks,General I&C Requirements,Cubicles and Racks,,,,"IEC 60297-3 cubicles/racks/panels dimensions, IP42 minimum indoor, IP54 minimum outdoor, anti-vibration mounting pads, I&C earthing scheme EMI/EMC reduction, cable screens terminated one end only, insulated clean earth bar, armoured cable armour isolated from cubicles, single-point connection to site earth, 10% spare capacity in cubicles/panels/racks/junction boxes, 100% redundant fans with failure detection alarm on DCMS, thermostatically controlled anti-condensation heaters, danger label for terminals 110 V and above",False,General cubicles/racks/earthing/spare capacity requirements; no ACC-related mention.,True,,high,False,Strong technical enclosure/earthing/IP/EMI and redundancy/spare capacity requirements that map to I&C panel/cubicle specifications and grounding details.,"Mechanical enclosures such as cubicles, racks and panels used for I&C systems shall conform to the relevant sections of IEC 60297: Part 3 for uniformity of dimensions and arrangements. The degree of protection for indoor cubicles and panels shall be IP42 minimum while those located outdoor in plant areas shall be IP54 minimum. The cubicles and panels shall be mounted in such a manner as to minimise external vibration being transmitted to internal components, for which suitable anti-vibration mounting arrangements (e.g. anti-vibration pads) shall be used.
The earthing scheme proposed for I&C systems shall reduce the effects of electrical/electromagnetic interference and ensure the safety of personnel. All metallic parts of cubicles and panels including doors shall be earthed by earthing conductors/braids of suitable cross-section using serrated washers to ensure a positive contact. Cable screens shall be terminated in such a way that earth loops are not introduced. Cable screens shall be terminated only at one end for which an insulated 'clean' earth bar shall be provided in cubicles. The earth bar shall be connected to the site earth via the cubicle safety earth at a single point. Armoured cables when used shall have their armours connected together externally, but shall be electrically isolated from the cubicles. The cable armours shall be connected to the site earth at a single point.
The layout of the interior of cubicles, junction boxes and panels shall facilitate easy access to components and terminals during commissioning and maintenance. In general, 10% of spare capacity shall be provided as a minimum in cubicles, panels, racks and junction boxes. This shall include unused module slots, unallocated wired I/O channels, unused terminals, space for additional external cables etc. The spare capacity of 10% shall be made available by Contractor after final commissioning which shall be pre-engineered, implemented, terminated and ready for use.
If forced ventilation is to be used in cubicles and panels, 100% redundant fans shall be employed with facilities for fan failure detection and alarming on the DCMS. Thermostatically controlled anti-condensation heaters shall be installed in cubicles and panels. Maintenance facilities such as internal lighting and a power socket shall also be provided in cubicles and panels.
Labels or other suitable identification methods shall be provided for sub-assemblies and terminals in cubicles and panels. This shall include modules, racks, power supplies, pre-fabricated cables, I/O termination boards, external cables and their conductors, terminals etc. A 'danger' label shall be provided with shroud for terminals connecting 110 V and above."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_235,135,8.8.2._Cubicles_and_racks,"8, 8, 2",content,text,8.8._General_I&C_Requirements > 8.8.2._Cubicles_and_racks,General I&C Requirements,Cubicles and Racks,,,,"external cables secured and supported, cable glanding for cable entry, outdoor I&C enclosures junction boxes plant-mounted control panels, sealed cable entry area, fire rating maintained at cable entry, dust/vermin-proof sealing",False,Generic cable entry/glanding requirements for I&C enclosures; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,medium,False,Technical requirements for cable entry/glanding and sealing/fire rating for outdoor I&C enclosures; useful for panel/junction box installation specs.,"External cables entering the cubicles and panels shall be adequately secured and supported by a suitable method, e.g. cable glanding. Outdoor I&C enclosures, e.g. junction boxes and plant-mounted control panels, shall only use cable glanding for this purpose. Alternate methods may be used to secure and support the external cables. In this case, the cable entry area shall be sealed by a suitable method to guarantee that the cubicle/panel has the required fire rating and is dust/vermin-proof."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_236,136,9.1._General_requirements,"9, 1",content,text,9.1._General_requirements,Civil Works,Buildings and Structures,Building Services (MEP),,,"integrated structural theme, international standards (buildings/structures), excavation, foundations, reinforced concrete, masonry, structural steel, HVAC, piping and ducting inside buildings, sanitary installations, power and lighting, earthing, lightning protection, enclosed building vs outdoor plant, containment to match main building, local planning authority requirements",False,"General civil/building services requirements (HVAC, power, lighting, foundations) with no mention of ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or related components.",True,,high,False,"Contains explicit civil/structural scope plus building services (HVAC, piping/ducting, sanitary, electrical, earthing/lightning protection) and enclosure/containment requirements useful for matching to existing design criteria/specifications/drawings.","Buildings and structures for the Plant shall form an integrated structural theme. They shall be designed to acceptable international standards and shall be fit for the purpose in each case. The Works shall include excavation, foundations, reinforced concrete, masonry and structural steel all as is necessary. HVAC, piping, ducting etc. inside the buildings, sanitary installations, power & lighting, earthing, lightening protection etc. shall all be provided to high standards and workmanship.
For a plant that requires to be enclosed, a suitable building shall be provided which meets the requirements of the local planning authority and the Owner. A plant or system which does not require to be enclosed may stand outdoors, although the Contractor shall judge for himself whether these items may need containment to match the main building and to meet Authority and Owner requirements."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_237,136,9.2._General_design_criteria,"9, 2",content,text,9.2._General_design_criteria,Civil Works,Buildings and Structures,General Design Criteria,,,"climatic conditions (site), minimum working life, aggressive conditions, safety and durability, building sizing for installation/operation/maintenance/replacement, structures stable at all stages of construction, design to facilitate erection",False,Generic building/structure design criteria; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,medium,False,"Provides general structural/building design criteria (climate, durability, maintainability, construction-stage stability) that can anchor retrieval of design basis/criteria documents.","The design shall reflect the climatic conditions that pertain to the site and which will be considered applicable during the minimum working life of the works. Special attention is to be paid at the effects of aggressive conditions, safe and durability of the facility.
All buildings housing the plant items shall be adequately proportioned to facilitate the installations, operation, maintenance and replacement of the plant.
All structures shall be designed to facilitate erection and so that they remain stable at all stages of construction."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_238,"136, 137",9.3._Foundations,"9, 3",content,text,9.3._Foundations,Civil Works,Foundations,Foundation Design and Construction Requirements,,,"building/equipment loads, ground conditions, reinforced concrete foundations, piled foundations (if necessary), soil investigations at exact locations, machinery foundations, static and dynamic loads, vibration isolation (adjacent equipment/structures), dynamic analysis of major machinery components, bench marks for settlement monitoring, bitumen based damp proofing",False,General foundation design requirements for buildings/equipment; no ACC-specific reference.,True,,high,False,"Clear foundation engineering requirements including piling, machinery dynamic/vibration considerations, settlement monitoring benchmarks, and damp proofing—strong retrieval anchors for geotechnical/foundation design documents.","Design and construction of all foundations shall be based on building/equipment loads and ground conditions on site.
Reinforced concrete foundations shall be provided and piled if necessary to transfer the loads satisfactorily to the supporting soils.
The final foundation scheme to be adopted shall be based on the soil investigations carried out by the Contractor in the exact location of the buildings and structures to ensure that the foundations shall be suitable to safely support the loads imposed on them.
Foundations for machinery shall be designed to withstand the imposed static and dynamic loads. Adjacent equipment and structures shall be isolated from the effects of vibration to acceptable level. The dynamic analysis of major machinery components will be provided for the review of the Owner.
The foundations shall be provided with appropriate bench marks for settlement monitoring purposes.
All concrete foundations and structures exposed to the ground shall be protected by bitumen based damp proofing."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_239,137,9.3.1._Geotechnical_/_Foundations,"9, 3, 1",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.3.1._Geotechnical_/_Foundations,Civil Works,Geotechnical and Foundations,Site Investigation and Piling Design,,,"subsoil investigations, BS 5930 Code of practice for site investigations, compressive static loading tests (piles), ASTM D 1143, maximum test load 2 Qn (pre-production pile) / 1.5 Qn (working pile), bearing capacities and safety factors, settlements, piling standards BS 8004, EN 1536, EN 12699, DIN 1054, ASTM C150, cement content 370 kg/m3, water/cement ratio ≤ 0.42, reinforcement cover 100 mm, aggregate max size 25 mm",False,Geotechnical/piling standards and concrete requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.,True,,high,False,"Highly specific geotechnical and piling requirements with named standards and numeric parameters suitable for matching to geotechnical reports, piling specs, and foundation calculations.","The Contractor is responsible for performing his own subsoil investigations to establish soil characteristics for detailed design. The campaign and techniques used will conform to the 'Code of practice for site investigations' - BS5930, or approved equivalent.
The Contractor shall execute compressive static loading tests on at least three piles belonging to different areas. The procedure to be followed is provided by ASTM D 1143. The maximum test load is equal to 2 Qn (if pre-production pile) or 1.5 Qn (working pile), where Qn is the net vertical load of the tested pile
Foundation type will be chosen by the Contractor, based on the geotechnical investigation report and a comprehensive calculation of the structure, bearing capacities, safety factors and settlements. When piling is proposed, the piling design and works shall be in accordance with internationally accepted standards such as BS 8004, BS 8008, BS 5930, BS 6031, ASTM D1143, DIN 1054, DIN 1626, EN 1536, EN 12699, DIN 4030, DIN 17100. Any cement to be used for concrete piles shall be in accordance with the CIRIA guide and ASTM C150 latest edition. The minimum content of cement shall be 370kg/m3, the maximum diameter of the aggregates shall be 25mm. The water/cement ratio shall not exceed 0.42. The cover on reinforcement shall be 100mm."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_240,138,9.4.1._General,"9, 4, 1",list,"text, list_item, table",9.3._Foundations > 9.4.1._General,,,,,,,False,Personnel facilities headcount planning; no ACC-related equipment or scope.,False,Primarily staffing headcount and welfare facility allowances (sanitary/showers/lockers) without technical design criteria or engineering requirements.,low,True,"Contains mostly administrative/personnel planning numbers; lacks technical parameters (e.g., HVAC rates, plumbing loads, space standards) needed for reliable MDL retrieval.","When designing the facilities for the plant personnel the following (preliminary) number of permanent people on site shall be considered:
- office staff (admin and plant management) app. 30
- maintenance staff
app. 50
- operational staff (CCR and field) app. 5 x 8 (5 shifts with 8 persons on each shift)
Also facilities (Sanitary & Showers, locker) for non-permanent staff shall be considered:
● Owner's engineers, 1 = 4. ● maintenance staff allowance, 1 = 40 to 50"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_241,138,9.4.2._Administration_and_social_building,"9, 4, 2",content,"text, list_item",9.3._Foundations > 9.4.2._Administration_and_social_building,Buildings,Administration and Social Building,Building Services and Fit-Out Scope,,,"Administration building rooms (offices, meeting room, data room, prayer room, server room, archive room), HVAC, electricity supply, IT and telecom connections, water and wastewater services, Central Control Room (CCR) furniture and appliances by Contractor, IT safety standards, Good Utility Practice",False,Administration/social building room list and services; no ACC mention.,True,,medium,False,"Defines functional room program and building-services scope (HVAC/electrical/IT/telecom/water/wastewater) and CCR furnishing responsibility, useful for matching building layout and building services documents.","Refer to Exhibit B, Part 1.
Administration building shall include at least:
- eight (8) offices (CEO, CFO, Plant Manager, Operations Manager, Maintenance Manager , HSE Manager, HR and Administration staff.);
- one meeting / conference room (20 people)
- one common office room for 10 people
- one data room
- one prayer room (minimum 20 people)
- server room and separate archive room which shall comply with all IT safety standards applicable in accordance with Good Utility Practice
- one equipped Kitchen and one Canteen for 15 people and for light meals (a combined canteen and kitchen may be proposed to the Owner's Representative for Owner's Representative's approval)
- sanitary (western type)
- first aid/medical room
The Administration and social building shall be fully equipped (HVAC, electricity, IT and telecom connections, water, waste water) without (tables or chairs) or any other equipment (computer systems, phones), except for the Central Control Room (CCR) in respect of which furniture, computer systems, phones, and other appliances and furniture shall be provided by the Contractor."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_242,"139, 140",9.4.3._Workshop_and_warehouse_buildings/areas,"9, 4, 3",content,"text, list_item",9.3._Foundations > 9.4.3._Workshop_and_warehouse_buildings/areas,Buildings,Workshop and Warehouse Buildings,Maintenance Facilities and Storage Requirements,,,"mechanical workshop welding bay power supply, fume extraction, instrumentation workshop, electrical workshop, GT spare parts storage (two complete sets including combustors), fenced outdoor heavy load storage area 2000 m2, hazardous/flammable materials storage segregation, bunding, ventilation, specialist fire protection, overhead travelling cranes, building height minimum 10 m at 80% of surface",False,Workshop/warehouse buildings requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit technical requirements for workshop/warehouse functions, hazardous storage provisions, cranes, ventilation/fire protection, and dimensional/area constraints—strong anchors for retrieving building, layout, and building-services documents.","New Workshop & stores building and Main stores building shall be provided.
Workshop and warehouse building shall be ready at Early Steam Operation Date.
In the mechanical workshop, power supply and fume extraction shall be provided for installation of a welding bay.
The workshops and warehouse building shall provide facilities for the CCGT Plant's day-to-day maintenance and operation. As a minimum the accommodation shall be provided for the following functions:
- mechanical workshop and stores
- instrumentation workshop
- electrical workshop
- the ware house shall accommodate at least two complete sets of GT spare parts including combustors
- warehouse and indoor storage facilities for spare parts and special tools.
- fenced outdoor area of 2000m 2 suitable for heavy load storage. Surface shall be adequate for truck access and heavy load storage
- applicable storage facilities for flammable materials and hazardous (chemicals) materials separated from other storage facilities
Warehouse shop should be large enough to accommodate all the spare parts storage. Chemical storage should be large enough for all the chemicals, and should have waste storage areas for both non-hazardous and hazardous material storage. Chemical storage tanks have to be big enough to make possible unloading with tankers.
Dedicated and suitable constructed areas for the storage of compressed and or flammable gases, chemicals and oils shall be integrated into the warehouse. Where required bunding, ventilation and specialist fire protection shall be provided.
Contractor shall provide the storage area (warehouse) for the storage of spare parts at their arrival on site.
In the case the workshop and warehouse is not ready on time, the Contractor shall provide temporary adequate storage for theses spare parts up to COD.
The workshop and storage buildings, including their associated offices, shall be:
- fully equipped (HVAC, electricity, IT and telecom connections, water, waste water )
- fitted with overhead travelling cranes
- without the specific workshop equipment (tools, benches) and without the storage racks or shelves
- without furniture ( tables, chairs )
- with hard flooring for movement of mobile crane/forklift (which mobile crane/forklift are not in the scope of Contractor)
The minimum requirements for the area of workshop and storage are 2,000m 2 accordingly, plus 400m 2 of common facilities (offices, toilets etc…). In storage and warehouse, elevation of the building shall be at minimum 10 meters high (suitable for storage shelves installation) at 80% of the surface.
The workshop and storage buildings shall comprise:
- one workshop area
- three offices
- one Hazardous Equipment and Gases Store Area
- one General Stores Area
- one Hazardous Materials Store Area
- one Hazardous Chemical Storage Area
- sanitary ( western type, including shower )
Note: All mechanical/electrical/instrument workshop equipment and hand tools for use after COD are excluded from Contractor scope of supply."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_243,140,9.4.4._Gate_house,"9, 4, 4",content,"text, list_item",9.3._Foundations > 9.4.4._Gate_house,Buildings,Gate House,Building Services Scope,,,"gate house, HVAC, sanitary (western type), electricity supply, IT and telecom connections, water and wastewater services",False,Gate house building services scope; no ACC reference.,True,,medium,False,"Defines gate house building-services scope (HVAC/electrical/IT/telecom/water/wastewater), useful for matching building services and utilities interface documents.","The gate house shall be:
- Fully equipped (HVAC, sanitary (western type), electricity, IT and telecom connections, water, waste water)
- Without furniture ( tables and chairs )"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_244,"140, 141",9.4.5._Central_Control_and_Switchgear_building,"9, 4, 5",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.4.5._Central_Control_and_Switchgear_building,Buildings,Central Control and Switchgear Building,CCR Layout and Access Requirements,Central Control Room (CCR),,"Annex 05a item 213 location reference, anti-static raised access floor, single-level CCR flooring, door sizing for panel installation and maintenance, ceiling height for future removal of panels/equipment, adjacent rooms for printers/engineering workstations/electrical cubicles, toilets, showers, messing facilities with lockers, outline layout drawings required (CCR and Central Control and Administration Building), Saudi Aramco standards",False,Central control/switchgear building and CCR layout requirements; no ACC-related terms.,True,,high,False,"Provides specific CCR/control building layout, access, flooring, and standards requirements plus drawing/layout deliverable references—strong retrieval signals for control building layouts and architectural/MEP requirements.","A Central Control and Switchgear building shall be constructed by the Contractor as per the location identified by item 213 on Annex 05a.
The Central Control Room (CCR) shall be provided with all necessary furniture, computer systems, phones, and other appliances as required.
The CCR flooring shall be at a single level, including accommodation of the anti-static raised access floor. The doors to the CCR shall be sufficiently sized to facilitate easy installation and maintenance of all panels and control room equipment. The doors and ceiling height shall be suitable for future removal of panels and equipment without the need for adjustment or removal.
Equipment such as work stations for non-operations related work, meeting rooms, printers, engineering work stations, electrical cubicles, etc. shall be located in adjacent or separate rooms on the same floor with easy access from CCR.
Amenities shall be provided suitable in size to accommodate the largest number of staff to change shift on site at any one time. The facilities shall include toilets, showers and messing facilities (with lockers).
All building layouts shall be provided for the approval of the Owner and the Contractor shall describe the proposed layout philosophy of the control room and equipment. Drawings of the outline layout of the CCR and the Central Control and Administration Building shall be included with the proposal.
The buildings shall meet the necessary Saudi Aramco standards as required."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_245,"141, 142",9.4.6._Laboratory,"9, 4, 6",content,"text, list_item",9.3._Foundations > 9.4.6._Laboratory,Buildings,Laboratories,Chemical Laboratory and Safety/Fire Protection Requirements,,,"laboratories (mechanical, I&C, electrical), HVAC, IT and telecom connections, water and wastewater services, water/steam cycle water quality monitoring, demineralised water quality, effluents quality (Exhibit B Part 1), oil analysis (transformer oil, lube oil, hydraulic oil), fuel oil quality testing, cathodic protection reference electrodes, NFPA requirements (smoke detection, fire extinguisher), emergency shower and eye shower, laboratory minimum size 6 m x 3 m",False,Laboratory requirements for water/steam cycle monitoring and oil analysis; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention.,True,,high,False,"Contains clear laboratory scope, analytical functions tied to plant systems (water/steam, demin water, effluents, oils, fuel oil, CP), plus NFPA fire protection and safety fixtures and minimum dimensions—strong anchors for lab design and safety/fire documents.","Laboratories (mechanical, I&C, electrical etc) shall be provided by the Contractor.
The Laboratories shall be fully equipped (HVAC, electricity, IT and telecom connections, water, waste water).
The chemical laboratory shall also include a chemical storeroom and shall enable the Operator to monitor among other things:
- the water/steam cycle water quality
- produced demineralised water quality
- effluents quality as per requirements in Exhibit B, Part 1
- oil analysis (transformer oil, lube oil, hydraulic oil)
- fuel oil quality testing
- cathodic protection systems implemented (reference electrodes)
The Contractor shall prepare a list considering the analysis requirements of the suppliers of the main equipment according to their operation and maintenance instructions.
The laboratory shall be at least 6m long and at least 3m wide.
An emergency shower, a flexible eye shower, a first aid box, safety lock and doors with inside panic opener/bars shall be installed in the laboratories. Concerning the fire detection and fire fighting system to be installed at the laboratories, the NFPA requirements shall be implemented (and as a minimum smoke detection and one fire extinguishers shall be installed).
Suitable clothing for laboratory works shall be provided.
The list and data sheets of the measuring instruments, of the chemicals and reagents and a list of glassware and other equipment shall be provided for Owner's Representative's approval. The list and characteristics of the furniture for the laboratory shall also be submitted to the Owner's Representative for approval.
Consumables for the laboratory after COD (specifically excluded furniture) shall be provided by the Owner."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_246,142,9.4.7._Other_buildings,"9, 4, 7",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.4.7._Other_buildings,Civil Works,Buildings,Other Buildings,,,"other buildings, HVAC, Heating Ventilating and Air Conditioning, electricity supply, IT and telecom connections, water services, waste water",False,"General requirement for other buildings (HVAC, electricity, IT, water, wastewater) with no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention.",True,,medium,False,"Defines building services/utilities to be provided for other buildings (HVAC, electrical, IT/telecom, water, wastewater), useful to retrieve building services and utilities design documents.","Other buildings shall be adequately sized and fully equipped (HVAC, electricity, IT and telecom connections, water, waste water)."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_247,142,9.4.7._Other_buildings,,list,"list_item, text",9.3._Foundations > 9.4.7._Other_buildings,Civil Works,Buildings,Other Buildings,,,"storage building, security building, fire brigade building, gate house, Exhibit B Part 1",False,"Lists generic site buildings (storage, security, fire brigade, gate house) with no ACC signal.",True,,medium,False,"Lists specific ancillary buildings, which can anchor retrieval of site layout/building architectural and civil/structural documents.","- storage building
- security building
- fire brigade building
- gate house
plus others as deemed necessary, refer to Exhibit B, Part 1."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_248,"142, 143",9.4.8._Design_criteria_/_structural_design,"9, 4, 8",content,"text, list_item",9.3._Foundations > 9.4.8._Design_criteria_/_structural_design,Civil Works,Structural Design,Design Loads and Codes,,,"worst load combination, erection loads, hydraulic testing loads, ASCE 7-10, basic wind speed ≥ 45 m/s, wind exposure C, seismic loading, temperature loading, 35°C, DIN 1072 SLW 60, minimum kerb radius 5 m, NFPA 850 Sections 5-7, fire rating 2 hours, cementitious fireproofing, Steam Turbine building steel structure protection",False,"General structural design criteria (wind/seismic/temperature loads, NFPA for steam turbine building) without ACC reference.",True,,high,False,"Contains explicit structural design criteria and code references (wind/seismic/temperature/special loads, road design standard, NFPA fireproofing for steam turbine building steel), strong anchors for retrieving structural design basis and fireproofing documents.","As per Codes and Standards (refer to section 4) and Exhibit B, Part 1 and Good Industry Practice.the design for the Plant shall incorporate the worst load combination (as per applicable code).
In particular:
- Erection loads (including loads during hydraulic testing of equipment);
- Wind loads shall be in accordance with ASCE 7-10 ""Minimum Design Loads for Buildings and Other Structures"". The basic wind speed for the site, as defined in ASCE 7-10 standard, shall be determined by the Contractor, but shall not be less than 45 m/s. The wind exposure C is to be considered for calculating the wind pressures in any direction;
- Seismic Loading - Refer to Exhibit B, Part 1.
- Temperature Loading -The temperature difference considered in the design, shall not be less than: 2/3 x (maximum ambient temperature in the shade - minimum ambient temperature) and 35°C.
- Special loads such as:
- -impact, braking and dynamic loads from cranes, hoists, elevators;
- -impact loads in silos, bins and hoppers due to arching from stored material;
- -dynamic loads from vortex shedding in wind fields;
- -dynamic loads from rotating and reciprocating machines;
- -short-circuit loads.
Roads shall be designed to DIN 1072 -SLW 60 and/or to accommodate trucks capable of delivering the heavy equipment. Minimum radius at kerb shall be 5 m.
In the case of no specific requirement in Exhibit B, Part1, the Contractor shall apply NFPA codes for the protection of steel structures for the Steam Turbine building as detailed below:
- Required area: The protection of the areas is subject to the systems contained within (i.e lube oil, electrical, fuel etc). Contractor shall refer to sections NFPA 850 Sections 5 -7 (particularly sections 5.3 and 7.7) for guidance
- Fire rating : 2 hours (for column, beam/girder only)
- Fire proofing type: There are no specific coating requirements. NPFA 850 requirements shall apply for the building materials e.g. cementitious fireproofing"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_249,"143, 144",9.5.1._Structural_framework_for_buildings,"9, 5, 1",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.1._Structural_framework_for_buildings,Civil Works,Buildings,Structural Framework,,,"reinforced concrete below ground floor, steel framed buildings, metal cladded roofs and walls, reinforced concrete framed structures, trafficable areas, concrete block perimeter walls 1.6 m high, corrosion protection coating system, decorative paint system, NFPA regulations, 15 years to first maintenance, colour coding system standardized",False,Generic plant building structural framework and corrosion/fire protection; no ACC equipment/area mentioned.,True,,high,False,"Provides building structural framework and materials requirements plus corrosion protection/painting life and NFPA fire detection/protection consistency, useful for retrieving civil/structural and coating/painting specifications.","All the structures below the ground floor level will be made of reinforced concrete.
Steel framed buildings shall be provided with metal cladded roofs and walls. Reinforced concrete framed structures shall be provided with metal cladded roofs and walls or with concrete roofs and concrete/concrete blockwork/brickwork walls. Steel framed buildings bounded by trafficable areas, shall be provided with concrete blocks perimeter walls at least 1.6m high.
All supporting structural steelwork, un-galvanized handrails and chequered plates shall be protected against corrosion by a coating system and decorative paint system.
The whole plant shall be designed and provided with adequate detection and protection of structures as far as required by the NFPA regulations and it shall be consistent with local fire authority and Insurer's requirements.
The paint protection shall be based on a minimum of 15 years to first maintenance. Colour coding system shall be standardized."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_250,144,9.5.2._Flooring,"9, 5, 2",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.2._Flooring,Civil Works,Buildings,Flooring,Central Control Room,,"structural ground slab on grade, suspended reinforced concrete slab, steel open grid flooring, falls and drainage outlets, CCR flooring single level, anti-static raised access floor, doors sized for panel installation and maintenance, ceiling height for future removal of panels and equipment",False,General flooring requirements and CCR (control room) flooring; no ACC-related terms.,True,,high,False,"Defines flooring types and CCR-specific requirements (raised access floor, access/clearance for panels), strong anchors for architectural/civil and control room layout/building services documents.","Floors shall be structural ground slab on grade, suspended reinforced concrete slab or steel open grid flooring, in accordance with the loading and requirements of usage. Adequate falls and drainage outlets to be provided to prevent the standing water to collect.
The CCR flooring shall be at a single level, including accommodation of the anti-static raised access floor. The doors to the CCR shall be sufficiently sized to facilitate easy installation and maintenance of all panels and control room equipment. The doors and ceiling height shall be suitable for future removal of panels and equipment without the need for adjustment or removal."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_251,144,9.5.3._External_Wall_Cladding,"9, 5, 3",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.3._External_Wall_Cladding,Civil Works,Architectural,External Wall Cladding,,,"coloured profiled metal sheeting, standard factory colours, acoustic insulation, finish selection, specified service life, site conditions",False,External wall cladding architectural requirements only; no ACC context.,True,,medium,False,"Defines architectural cladding material and performance considerations (acoustic insulation, service life under site conditions) useful for matching building envelope specifications.",The main cladding material shall be coloured profiled metal sheeting in standard factory colours to be selected by the Owner and/or Authority/architectural officer. Appropriate system may be utilised incorporating acoustic insulation as necessary. The choice of finish shall be made considering the specified service life under the specified site conditions.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_252,144,9.5.4._Roof_Cladding,"9, 5, 4",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.4._Roof_Cladding,Civil Works,Architectural,Roof Cladding,,,"roof cladding construction, rainwater roof outlets, prevent ingress of moisture, manually operated hatches, smoke ventilation, fire",False,Roof cladding and smoke ventilation hatches; no ACC mention.,True,,medium,False,"Contains roof envelope requirements including drainage/moisture ingress prevention and fire-related smoke ventilation hatches, which can map to architectural/civil specifications and roof details.","The Contractor shall make his proposals for the roof cladding construction. Rainwater roof outlets shall be fitted and detailed to prevent the ingress of moisture.
With regard to fire, manually operated hatches for smoke ventilation shall be located around the roof plan as necessary."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_253,144,9.5.5._Internal_Walls_and_Partitions,"9, 5, 5",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.5._Internal_Walls_and_Partitions,Civil Works,Architectural,Internal Walls and Partitions,,,"concrete block work, brick masonry, lightweight panel construction, sound transmission, fire prevention, fire partitioning walls and floors, penetrations sealing, fire endurance equal to structure",False,Internal walls/partitions and fire sealing requirements; no ACC signal.,True,,high,False,Provides clear requirements for internal wall types and firestopping/fire endurance for penetrations—strong anchors for architectural/fire protection details and specifications.,"Where required for structural reasons, sound transmission or fire prevention, internal walls shall be of concrete block work/brick masonry. Otherwise internal walls will be of appropriate lightweight panel construction. All penetrations through fire partitioning walls and floors shall be sealed to achieve fire endurance equal to the respective structure."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_254,145,9.5.6._Doors,"9, 5, 6",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.6._Doors,Civil Works,Architectural,Doors and Access,,,"personnel and escape doors, painted steel doors and frames, plastic coated steel roller shutter doors, folding gates, admin building office doors, control and electrical building, aluminium doors and frames, door furniture and closers, harmonised locks, confined spaces, 3 level master key system, lock proposal for approval",False,Doors/access and lock system requirements for buildings; no ACC reference.,True,,high,False,Specifies door types/materials by use case and buildings plus security/locking master key requirements—useful for architectural door schedules/specs and security hardware documentation.,"Personnel and escape doors and frames shall be mainly of painted steel. Vehicular access and erection purpose doors shall be plastic coated steel roller shutter doors or folding gates with personnel access doors. Internal office doors and frames in the admin building and in control and electrical building shall be of aluminium with necessary furniture and closers.
All locks shall be in harmonised/suited according to process areas and/or functional requirements or possible confined spaces. At least 3 level master key system is required. The Contractor is to provide a lock proposal for Owner's approval."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_255,145,9.5.7._Windows,"9, 5, 7",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.7._Windows,Civil Works,Architectural,Windows and Glazing,,,"permanent staff occupancy areas, metal framed windows, double glazing, daylight provision, permanent working places, acoustic considerations, aesthetic considerations",False,Windows/glazing requirements for occupied areas; no ACC-related terms.,True,,medium,False,"Defines window framing and glazing requirements and daylight/acoustic considerations, supporting retrieval of architectural glazing/window specifications.","Areas with permanent staff occupancy shall be provided with metal framed windows with double glazing. Consideration shall be given to the provision of daylight in all buildings with permanent working places consistent with function, acoustic and aesthetic considerations."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_256,145,9.5.8._Stairways,"9, 5, 8",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.8._Stairways,Civil Works,Buildings,Stairways,,,"main stairways, fire protected shaft, external emergency stairs, steel stairs, local regulations",False,Civil/building stairways requirement; no mention of ACC or ACC structures/equipment.,True,,medium,False,"Contains building safety/fire protection requirements for stairways (enclosed fire-protected shaft; external emergency steel stairs), useful for retrieving architectural/civil building design documents.",The main stairways shall be enclosed in fire protected shaft. External emergency stairs shall of steel according to local regulations.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_257,"145, 146",9.5.9._Internal_Finishes,"9, 5, 9",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.9._Internal_Finishes,Civil Works,Buildings,Internal Finishes,,,"internal surfaces, architectural finishes, control room finishes, raised flooring sealed against dust, hard floor finishes, ceramic tiling (toilets/kitchen/lockers), chemical/oil resistant floor finishes, suspended mineral fibre ceilings",False,"Generic building internal finishes (control room, floors, walls); no ACC signal.",True,,medium,False,"Provides architectural/internal finish requirements by area (control room, toilets, kitchens, lockers) including flooring/ceiling types and resistance to oil/chemicals—useful for matching building architectural specifications.","Internal surfaces of the buildings shall be selected to suit the particular requirements of each building, its function, requirements of occupancy and operation requirements.
Only the use of architectural finishes and materials with a proven track record of satisfactory installation in similar operational and climate conditions will be permitted.
The Control Room shall have better quality finishes to provide good wearing properties and visual appearance. Where raised flooring is provided the base structure shall be sealed against dust.
Hard finishes shall be provided for the majority of floor areas throughout the Plant. These shall provide durable surfaces that enhance the building environment and are easy to clean. Protective floor finishing shall be provided to Plant areas, ceramic tiling to toilets, kitchen and lockers. In the areas with constant movement of vehicles or risk of oil or chemicals spillage the floor shall be provided with surface hardening or chemical/oil resistant finishes.
Wall finishes shall conform to normal practice for each type of building and facility. Concrete block surfaces to be usually painted.
Offices, kitchen, locker room and control room shall have suspended mineral fibre ceilings on metal strip profiles.
General architectural finishes shall conform to the standards outlined in the table below. Furniture's will be supplied by Owner except for specialist areas such as control rooms and other technical buildings."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_258,146,9.5.9._Internal_Finishes,"9, 5, 9",table,table,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.9._Internal_Finishes,Civil Works,Buildings,Internal Finishes,,,"Workshop & Store finishes, heavy duty floor surface hardener, anti-dust finish, profiled metal insulated cladding, Battery Rooms acid resistant ceramic tiles, sealed batteries terrazzo tile, Demineralisation Building finishes (as applicable), Laboratory finishes (as applicable), Control Rooms/Computer Room anti-static vinyl tiles, raised computer type floor, Switchgear Rooms terrazzo tile, Air Compressor Building finishes, Gas Reduction Station finishes (only if applicable)",False,"Table of finishes for various buildings (workshop, battery room, switchgear, etc.); no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.",True,,high,False,"Room/building-specific finishes table with distinct technical anchors (acid resistant, anti-static, cladding types, raised floors) that can strongly match architectural/civil building finish schedules/specifications in the MDL.","Workshop &Store, Finishes.Floor = Heavy duty floor surface hardener Anti-dust finish. Workshop &Store, Finishes.Wall = Profiled metal insulated cladding with dado wall under. Cladding to have protected colour coating. Wall rendered externally, emulsion painted fairface block internally. Workshop &Store, Finishes.Ceiling = Not required. Battery Rooms, Finishes.Floor = Acid resistant ceramic tiles. For sealed batteries - Terrazzo tile. Battery Rooms, Finishes.Wall = Plaster and emulsion paint. Battery Rooms, Finishes.Ceiling = Suspended fibre cement board. Demineralisation Building (as applicable) Laboratory (as applicable), Finishes.Floor = Heavy duty floor surface hardener Anti-dust finish Acid resistant finish in chemical areas. Demineralisation Building (as applicable) Laboratory (as applicable), Finishes.Wall = Fairface block and emulsion paint. Acid resistant tiles in chemical areas. Demineralisation Building (as applicable) Laboratory (as applicable), Finishes.Ceiling = Not required. Admin / Office Building, Finishes.Floor = Vinyl tiles. Admin / Office Building, Finishes.Wall = Plaster and emulsion paint. Admin / Office Building, Finishes.Ceiling = Suspended acoustic mineral fibre board. Control Rooms/Computer Room Electronic Equipment, Finishes.Floor = Anti-static vinyl tiles on raised computer type floor. Control Rooms/Computer Room Electronic Equipment, Finishes.Wall = Plaster and emulsion paint. Control Rooms/Computer Room Electronic Equipment, Finishes.Ceiling = Suspended acoustic mineral fibre board. Switchgear Rooms, Finishes.Floor = Terrazzo tile, or Anti-static vinyl tiles on heavy duty raised floor system. Switchgear Rooms, Finishes.Wall = Plaster and emulsion paint. Switchgear Rooms, Finishes.Ceiling = Emulsion paint on concrete. Air Compressor Building Gas Reduction Station (only if applicable), Finishes.Floor = Heavy duty floor. Surface hardener, Anti-dust finish. Air Compressor Building Gas Reduction Station (only if applicable), Finishes.Wall = Fairface block and emulsion paint. Air Compressor Building Gas Reduction Station (only if applicable), Finishes.Ceiling = Not required"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_259,147,9.5.10._Furnishing_and_Fittings,"9, 5, 10",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.10._Furnishing_and_Fittings,Civil Works,Buildings,Maintainability and Handling Equipment,,,"Steam Turbine building overhead travelling crane, lifting capacity for maintenance, generator rotor removal without demolishing fixed structure, GT lay-down and service areas, ST lay-down and service areas, mechanical workshop crane (pumps and motors), electrical and instrumentation workshops manual chain hoist, warehouse at least 5 ton crane or hoist",False,"Maintainability/handling equipment for turbine building and workshops; no ACC-related equipment (fans, bundles, structure) mentioned.",True,,high,False,"Defines maintainability/handling requirements (cranes/hoists, laydown areas, rotor removal constraint) tied to ST/GT buildings and workshops—useful for retrieving building layout/handling equipment design documents.","The Steam Turbine building shall be furnished with an overhead travelling crane with sufficient lifting capacity in order to ensure effective maintenance. The removal of generator rotor shall be possible without need to demolish any fixed structure. Regarding the maintainability of various components of the Plant certain practical means will be considered:
GT area shall be provided with adequate lay-down and service areas during the major overhaul.
ST zone shall be provided with adequate lay-down and service areas during the major overhaul.
The mechanical workshop shall be equipped with a crane of sufficient capacity taking into account the possible heaviest component (certain pumps and motors) to be maintained.
For the electrical and instrumentation workshops a manual chain hoist shall be provided.
The stores shall be provided without racking system. The warehouse shall be equipped with at least a 5 ton crane or hoist."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_260,147,9.5.11._Fire_protec_t_ion_/_separate_fire_compartments,"9, 5, 11",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.11._Fire_protec_t_ion_/_separate_fire_compartments,Civil Works,Buildings,Fire Compartmentation and Means of Egress,,,"fire compartments, fire barriers, penetration seals, separating walls and floors, two exit routes, exit stairways, emergency and exit lighting, panic bars, marked exits, fire detection and fire fighting systems (section 6.8)",False,Fire compartmentation and egress requirements; no ACC context.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit fire compartmentation and egress requirements (barriers, penetration seals, exit routes, emergency lighting) and cross-reference to fire detection/firefighting systems—strong retrieval anchors for fire/life safety and architectural design docs.","The Plant shall be divided into fire compartments. Special attention shall be paid to fire barriers and penetration seals in separating walls and floors all over the Plant.
From every exit areas there will be two exit routes to the exit stairways or directly outdoor or to another fire compartment.
Exit routes will be provided with emergency and exit lighting and panic bars. The exits and the access to them shall be marked.
The fire detection and fire fighting systems are specified in section 6.8."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_261,147,9.5.11._Fire_protec_t_ion_/_separate_fire_compartments,,content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.11._Fire_protec_t_ion_/_separate_fire_compartments,Building Services,Plumbing and Drainage,"Sanitary, drainage and vent system",,,"sanitary drainage and vent system, local requirements, floor drains, toilets, tea and mess room areas, external sanitary sewer, waste disposal requirements",False,Sanitary drainage/vent system for toilets and mess rooms; no ACC signal.,True,,medium,True,"Technical plumbing/drainage requirements are clear, but section header appears mismatched (fire protection vs plumbing), so classification may need confirmation.","The sanitary, drainage and vent system shall be designed in accordance with the Local Requirements. All floor drains within toilets and tea and mess room areas shall discharge into the external sanitary sewer as per requirements of waste disposal. ."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_262,148,9.5.11._Fire_protec_t_ion_/_separate_fire_compartments,,content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.11._Fire_protec_t_ion_/_separate_fire_compartments,Building Services,Plumbing and Drainage,Domestic cold water system,,,"cold water plumbing, BS 6700:1997, design installation testing maintenance, local water supply regulations, Governmental Instrumentality approval, drinking water temperature below 20 ␀C, BS 6700:1997 clause 2.2 cold water service",False,Domestic cold water plumbing standard; unrelated to ACC.,True,,high,True,"Strong technical standard and temperature requirement for domestic cold water; however the stated section title suggests fire protection, so section placement may be incorrect.","The cold water plumbing shall be designed, installed and tested in accordance with BS6700 (1997): ""Specification for the design, installation, testing and maintenance of services supply water for domestic use within buildings and their curtilages"", and installed in accordance with local water supply regulations and approved by the Governmental Instrumentality. In general for drinking, cold water will be provided at a temperature below 20 ℃ according to BS 6700:1997 (2.2 Cold water service)."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_263,148,9.5.11._Fire_protec_t_ion_/_separate_fire_compartments,,list,list_item,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.11._Fire_protec_t_ion_/_separate_fire_compartments,Building Services,Plumbing and Drainage,Water supply for washing and safety eyewash,,,"washing water, WWC, urinals, shower, sink, ambient temperature water, safety eye washer, tepid flushing water, ANSI Z358.1, eyewash station piping insulation, exposed to direct sunlight",False,Washing water and safety eyewash water requirements; no ACC mention.,True,,high,True,Clear requirements for eyewash water and insulation plus ANSI standard; section title mismatch may indicate mis-indexed hierarchy.,"- For washing (to WWC, urinals, shower and sink) water at ambient temperature will be provided.
- For safety eye washer, tepid flushing water will be provided according to ANSI code (ANSI Z.358.1). All piping for the safety eye wash stations that are exposed to direct sunlight shall be insulated."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_264,148,9.5.11._Fire_protec_t_ion_/_separate_fire_compartments,,content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.11._Fire_protec_t_ion_/_separate_fire_compartments,Building Services,Plumbing and Drainage,Domestic hot water system,,,"hot water plumbing, CP 342 Part 2 (1974), centralised hot water supply, design installation testing, local water supply regulations, Governmental Instrumentality approval, electric water heater",False,Domestic hot water system requirements; no ACC signal.,True,,high,True,Explicit hot water design/testing standard and heater type; verify correct section placement due to fire-protection heading.,"The hot water plumbing shall be designed, installed and tested in accordance with CP342, Part 2 (1974): ""Code of Practice for centralised hot water supply to buildings other than individual dwellings"", and installed in accordance with local water supply regulations and approved by the Governmental Instrumentality. Hot water shall be supplied in accordance with local requirements for each building and its purpose of use. Water heater used shall be of electric water heater type."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_265,148,9.5.11._Fire_protec_t_ion_/_separate_fire_compartments,,content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.5.11._Fire_protec_t_ion_/_separate_fire_compartments,Building Services,Plumbing and Drainage,Floor drains and condensate drainage,,,"plant rooms, floor drains, general floor washing, condensate drainage, water seals, building perimeter drains",False,Generic plant room floor drains and condensate drainage; mentions condensate but no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.,True,,high,True,Clear drainage requirements for plant rooms including condensate and traps; section title mismatch suggests possible hierarchy/labeling issue.,"Plant rooms shall be provided with floor drains for general floor washing. In addition, individual floor drains shall be provided for condensate drainage. Floor drains for washing water and condensate shall be provided with water seals and connected to building perimeter drains."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_266,"148, 149",9.6.1._Transformer_compound_design,"9, 6, 1",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.6.1._Transformer_compound_design,Civil Works,Foundations,Transformer compound design,Transformer compound,,"reinforced concrete bases, NFPA fire walls (three sides), removable security palisade fence, lockable personnel access gate, explosion damage containment, safe working clearance for installation/maintenance/removal/cooling, transformer oil collection (full oil contents)",False,"Transformer compound civil design (fire walls, oil collection); no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.",True,,high,False,"Contains explicit civil/structural requirements for transformer compound: RC bases, NFPA fire walls, security fencing, explosion containment, and oil spill/collection provisions.","The transformer shall be supported on large reinforced concrete bases. Where recommended by NFPA the transformer shall be enclosed by fire walls on three sides.
The fourth side shall be provided with a removable security palisade type fence fitted with a lockable personnel access gate or as required under the SSDs.
In addition to normal loading design requirements, the transformer walls shall be designed to contain and confine damage caused by an explosion of the transformer, as far as practical.
The compounds shall be sized to permit safe working and provide room for installation, maintenance, removal and cooling of the transformer. Provision shall be made to collect the oil from any transformer in the event of a major explosion or other cause leading to the loss of the full oil contents."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_267,"149, 150",9.6.2._Turbine_foundations,"9, 6, 2",content,"text, list_item",9.3._Foundations > 9.6.2._Turbine_foundations,Civil Works,Foundations,Turbine foundations,Turbine-generator foundation block,,"DIN 4024 Parts 1 & 2, dynamic analysis (3D model), soil stiffness, structural resonant frequencies ±20% of machine operating frequencies, symmetrical structure (plan and elevation), vibration/misalignment limits, waves propagated through soil (adjacent structures/pipework/sensitive machinery), datum level monitoring plates, foundation settlement monitoring (key erection stages)",False,Turbine-generator foundation requirements; no mention of ACC or air-cooled condenser equipment.,True,,high,False,"Provides detailed turbine foundation design criteria including applicable standard, dynamic/resonance requirements, 3D soil-structure modeling, and settlement monitoring provisions.","The foundation design shall comply with the German Standard DIN 4024 Parts 1 & 2 or an equivalent international standard. The design concept of the foundation block shall ensure that there are no structural resonant frequencies closer than + or - 20% of the machine operating frequencies. For the pedestal foundation the model used for the dynamic analysis must be three dimensional and include the lower foundation and soil stiffness. As far as possible the structure shall be symmetrical about the centre lines both in plan and elevation.
The Foundation design shall be subject to the turbine supplier's requirements and approval of the Owner/Owner's Engineer, and will ensure that:
- the machine can operate efficiently and reliably without unacceptable vibration or misalignment
- the foundation itself suffers no damage or settlement sufficient to cause the machine to malfunction or to affect alignment
- the waves propagated through the soil by vibration of the foundation should not be harmful to persons or to adjacent structures, pipework and sensitive machinery or processes
The manner in which the machine parts are supported on their seatings in the structure top shall provide facilities to enable adjustments to be made, if found necessary, at a later date, for maintaining correct casing alignment and obtaining the required curvature of the shafting.
The supporting structures shall be set into position under the supervision of an appointed representative of the turbine generator manufacturer, who shall check and approve the settings and the relevant tolerances prior to and upon completion of casting the surrounding concrete.
The turbine-generator manufacturer is to supply a set of datum level monitoring plates which shall be grouted into the foundations. The number and spacing of these mounting points on the foundation block and on any other suitable surfaces shall be recommended by the turbine generator manufacturer and be subject to approval. When each block is complete, prior to the erection of the turbine, the Contractor shall record an agreed set of datum levels. Subsequent level measurements shall be recorded at key stages of turbine erection in order to monitor any foundation settlement."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_268,150,9.6.3._HRSG_foundations,"9, 6, 3",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.6.3._HRSG_foundations,Civil Works,Foundations,HRSG foundations,HRSG,,"reinforced concrete slab, equipment area drains containment and routing, discharge to waste water system, bunding to prevent overspill of blow-down water, HRSG manufacturer supervision for settings/tolerances, floor mounted equipment plinth (150 mm high), Plant buildings concrete plinths",False,HRSG foundation slab/drains/bunding; no ACC-related terms or context.,True,,high,False,Contains HRSG foundation and drainage/bunding requirements plus minimum plinth height—useful anchors for civil/structural and drainage documents.,"A reinforced concrete slab shall be provided under the HRSG. All drains from all equipment in the area shall be contained and routed properly so that they are discharged to the waste water system. Bunding shall be adequate to prevent any overspill of blow-down water under any conditions.
The supporting structures shall be set into position under the supervision of an appointed representative of the HRSG manufacturer, who shall check and approve the settings and the relevant tolerances prior to and upon completion of casting the surrounding concrete.
Any floor mounted equipment within the Plant buildings shall be mounted on at least 150mm high concrete plinth"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_269,"150, 151",9.6.4._Roads,"9, 6, 4",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.6.4._Roads,Civil Works,Roads and paving,Plant internal roads,,,"permanent roads within Plant Sites, loading capacities and clearances of existing bridges/connecting roads, pavement suited to construction traffic, concrete lining on either sides, subgrade compaction 95% MDD (top 600 mm), earthworks compaction 90% MDD, local load tests during construction, asphalt road surface, hardstanding areas for mobile cranes/maintenance, unhindered crane approach to GT, general plant layout including laydown areas (Owner approval)",False,"Plant internal roads, crane access, GT maintenance access; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.",True,,medium,False,Defines road/pavement construction and compaction/testing requirements and crane access/hardstanding needs—useful for civil works and site development document retrieval.,"Contractor shall provide all roads as per Exhibit B, Part 1.
Contractor shall take into consideration the loading capacities and clearances of existing bridges and connecting roads when carrying out transportation from the ports or from other places to the sites and viceversa.
The Contractor shall provide all permanent roads within the Plant Sites. The pavement construction shall be suited to the type of traffic likely to use the roads during construction and if applicable, during the lifetime of the Plants. All the internal roads shall be provided with concrete lining on either sides.
The sub grade earthworks for these roads shall be compacted to 95% maximum dry density for the top 600mm. All other associated earthworks shall be compacted to at least 90% maximum dry density. Local load tests will be performed during the construction of the roads
The road surface shall be asphalt. Roads for common and frequent use by the Plants shall be designed of sufficient width and load.
Hardstanding areas shall be provided around all plant, where mobile cranes or equipment will be used for maintenance. The compaction will be function of the load require for the erection.
As GT is located outside, Contractor shall also ensure unhindered crane approach for any major maintenance.
The general Plant layout, including laydown areas, shall be submitted for Owner's approval in the detailed design stage. Changes, if any, requested by Owner shall be carried out by the Contractor without any cost or time impact to the Owner."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_270,151,9.6.4._Roads,,content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.6.4._Roads,Civil Works,Roads and paving,Concrete paving (spill risk areas),,,"concrete paving, high risk fuel spillage/leakage areas, design for heaviest wheel loads, vehicles operating at power and water station",False,Concrete paving for fuel spill risk areas; no ACC scope signal.,True,,medium,False,"Provides specific paving material selection and wheel-load design requirement for spill-risk areas, supporting retrieval of paving/yard design documents.",Concrete paving shall be used for areas subject to high risks of fuel spillage/leakage. They shall be designed to support the heaviest wheel loads of the vehicles that will operate at or frequent the power and water station.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_271,151,9.6.4._Roads,,content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.6.4._Roads,Civil Works,Roads,Gravel paving,,,"gravel paving, 40 mm max crushed rock/gravel, approved grading, densely compacted, 100 mm thickness, driveway surface blinding, rock chippings",False,Gravel paving specification; no ACC-related content.,True,,high,False,"Provides explicit roadworks material and construction requirements (aggregate size, compaction, thickness, driveway blinding) useful for matching civil/roadwork specifications and details.","Gravel paving shall be constructed with 40mm max. crushed rocks or gravels of approved grading and quality, densely compacted to 100mm thickness. If the gravel paving is applied as driveway, the surface shall be blinded with rock chippings to form a reasonably smooth serviceable driveway."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_272,151,9.6.4._Roads,,content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.6.4._Roads,Civil Works,Roads,Unpaved area surfacing,,,"areas not to be paved, levelling, 75 mm minimum thickness, 20 mm single sized pebbles, 20 mm single sized stones",False,Unpaved area surfacing specification; no ACC-related content.,True,,high,False,"Defines surfacing/finishing requirements for non-paved areas with explicit thickness and aggregate size, useful for civil/siteworks specs.","The areas not to be paved shall be levelled, and finished with a 75mmminimum thickness of 20mm single sized pebbles or stones."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_273,151,9.6.6._Permanent_fences,"9, 6, 6",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.6.6._Permanent_fences,Civil Works,Permanent fences,Security fencing,FCHPP and ESF,,"security fences, FCHPP (Fuel Conditioning & Heating Preparation Package), ESF, Exhibit B Part 1",False,Security fencing for FCHPP and ESF; no ACC mention.,True,,medium,True,"Mentions provision of security fences for specific areas (FCHPP, ESF) but lacks technical fence type/specification details; still a useful retrieval anchor for fencing/site security scope.","All security fences for the FCHPP and ESF as required in Exhibit B, Part 1 shall be provided by the Contractor."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_274,"151, 152",9.6.7._Cable_trenches,"9, 6, 7",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.6.7._Cable_trenches,Civil Works,Cable trenches,Underground cable/piping trench design,,,"direct burial not suitable, underground cables and pipes in trenches, maintenance access, removable reinforced concrete cover slabs, PVC cable ducts, manholes, cable pull pits, tested for 100% water removal, pipe rack headroom 4.5 m minimum, trench bottoms laid to falls, drained at regular intervals, trench covers suitable for area loading, lifting equipment for covers heavier than 50 kg",False,Generic underground cable/piping trench design and pipe rack headroom; no ACC-specific signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains detailed civil/electrical interface requirements for cable trenches (covers, ducts, manholes, drainage, headroom, lifting limits) with multiple numeric anchors useful for retrieving trench/duct bank/manhole and pipe rack layout/spec documents.","Where direct burial is not suitable, underground cables and pipes shall be in trenches. The trenches shall be designed and constructed to provide adequate access for maintenance purposes. Trenches shall be provided with removable reinforced concrete cover slabs. Electrical cables may be laid in PVC-cable ducts. Routes shall be provided with suitable, appropriately located manholes. Electrical cable pull pits shall be designed and tested for 100% water removal.
The above ground pipe racks shall provide headroom of 4.5m as minimum in the trafficable areas.
Cable trench bottoms shall be laid to falls and drained at regular intervals along their lengths. Trench covers shall be suitable for the general area loading through which the trenches run. All necessary equipment for lifting shall be provided for single trench cover heavier than 50kg."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_275,152,9.6.8._Site_installation,"9, 6, 8",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.6.8._Site_installation,,,,,,,False,Temporary laydown areas and housing compounds/site installation logistics; no ACC scope signal.,False,"Primarily administrative/logistics requirements for temporary laydown areas, camps, offices, cleanup and restoration; no specific technical design criteria or engineering deliverable anchors.",low,True,Construction logistics and temporary facilities obligations are not strong MDL technical retrieval signals in this chunk.,"Contractor shall provide temporary off site lay-down areas and housing compounds and to bear the respective costs if the Plant Sites and the allocated temporary areas to be provided by Saudi Aramco/SEC are not sufficient for Contractor's camp, equipment lay-down, and office and/or administration requirements. In any case, Contractor welfare camp shall only be located outside Saudi Aramco area, location and cost of which shall be borne by the Contractor.
The Contractor shall optimize and include in its Proposal its requirement for the temporary and future use of the Plant Sites and shall propose corresponding time schedules for the use of these areas. Backfilling and grading of required areas shall be provided by the Contractor, as far as required.
Upon completion of construction, Contractor shall remove from the Contractor's camp site and other temporarily used areas all the buildings, rubbish, unused materials, storage tanks, temporary power distribution lines and all other temporary facilities. Contractor shall fill and dress all holes and cavities made for its convenience and leave all areas in good order and condition.
For avoidance of doubt, Saudi Aramco/SEC shall not be responsible for the provision of land for such off-site areas. The temporary installations include labour camps, offices and car parking for the Owner's Representative and for the Contractor workshop facilities, laydown areas, etc."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_276,"152, 153",9.6.9._General_levelling_and_grading_of_Site,"9, 6, 9",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.6.9._General_levelling_and_grading_of_Site,Civil Works,Site Preparation,Levelling and Grading,,,"grade and level plant area to MSL +30.60 m, final grading considering general layout, storm water drainage system interface, finished ground floor slab levels 0.20 m above ground level, remove unsuitable material and debris, excavate soft/unstable subsoil and replace with compacted fill, fill in 250 mm layers and compact each layer",False,Civil site grading/levelling and storm water drainage interface; no mention of ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or related equipment.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit civil/site grading requirements with numeric levels and compaction/layering criteria useful for matching to grading, earthworks, and drainage-related MDL documents.","As per Exhibit B, Part 1, Saudi Aramco shall grade and level the Plant area to MSL + 30.60 m. Any additional levelling and grading as required shall be done by the Contractor. The Contractor shall include for importing any further suitable material that may be required and for levelling and compacting the sites to acceptable standards and grades.
For the final grading of the Sites, the requirements of each complex, the requirements of the general layout of the Entire Plants, the storm water drainage system and other external installations shall be considered.
The finished ground floor slab levels of buildings shall basically be 0.20m above ground level.
Unsuitable material and debris shall be removed from the sites. Pockets of soft or unstable subsoil shall be excavated and replaced by compacted fill material. All filling work shall be carried out in 250 mm layers and compacted before proceeding with the next layer."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_277,153,9.6.10._Fuel_Oil_Storage,"9, 6, 10",content,text,9.3._Foundations > 9.6.10._Fuel_Oil_Storage,Civil Works,Foundations,Fuel Oil Storage,Fuel oil storage tanks and bunding,,"tank and bund foundations per soil investigations, structural analysis for tank/bund foundation, flammable liquids handling/storage regulations compliance, impervious bunded area at least 110% of total capacity of one tank, bund run-off drains to oil water separator",False,Fuel oil storage tanks and bunding foundations; no ACC-related terms or context.,True,,high,False,Defines foundation and containment/drainage requirements for fuel oil storage tanks including bund capacity (110%) and oil-water separator drainage—strong anchors for civil/structural and drainage/environmental MDL documents.,"Fuel oil storage tanks and bunding shall be founded in accordance with the soil investigations and structural analysis and shall comply with the current regulations for the handling, storage and use of flammable liquids. The impervious bunded area for the tanks will be at least 110 % of the total capacity of one tank. This area run-off shall drain to oil water separator."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_278,153,9.8.1._General,"9, 8, 1",content,"text, list_item",9.3._Foundations > 9.8.1._General,Building Services,HVAC,Ventilation and HVAC General Requirements,,,"fresh air to occupied areas, control room temperature and RH, remove excess heat from process equipment, remove noxious fumes and chemical vapours, pressure differences between rooms to prevent dust/fume ingress, forced ventilation sweeps total room volume, supply air taken from outside, filtered supply air to offices/control/electrical rooms/workshops, sound attenuators to prevent excess noise to neighbourhood, galvanised steel ductwork/materials, sound attenuation for control room air-conditioning, battery rooms have dedicated exhaust fans",False,General building ventilation/HVAC requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,False,"Provides HVAC/ventilation functional and design requirements (fresh air, filtration, pressure differentials, materials, noise attenuation, battery room exhaust) suitable for retrieving HVAC design criteria/specifications.","The functions of the ventilation/HVAC systems are following:
- to provide fresh air to occupied areas
- to control room temperature and RH
- to remove excess heat released from process equipment
- to remove noxious fumes and chemical vapours
- to keep proper pressure differences between certain rooms to prevent the ingress of dust and noxious fumes
Where forced ventilation is provided, the design shall ensure that the total room volume is swept. The supply air shall be taken from outside. Supply air to offices, control and electrical rooms and workshops shall be filtered. Forced mechanical exhaust ventilation can be used in process areas. Sound attenuators shall be installed to prevent excess noise to the neighbourhood (if required).
The main materials of the ventilation equipment and duct shall be galvanised steel.
Special care shall be taken in sound attenuation of the control room air-conditioning system.
The battery rooms have their own exhaust fans."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_279,154,9.8.1._General,,content,"text, list_item",9.3._Foundations > 9.8.1._General,Building Services,HVAC,Air Conditioning and Ventilation Design/Commissioning Requirements,Air handling unit (air cooled),,"PCC associated to GTs and STs indoor design conditions, no return air from battery room, commissioning performance test for air conditioning and ventilation systems, fresh air rate 30 m³/h per person for occupied areas, no forced fresh air supply required for non-occupied electrical/switchgear areas, air cooled AHU suitable for roof/wall/window balustrade mounting, galvanized sheet steel casing with sound and heat insulation, centrifugal fan double inlet (single inlet with approval), panel filter washable/removable; dust weight separation efficiency per ASHRAE Standard, direct expansion (DX) air cooler with copper tubes/aluminium fins; expansion valve; drain-pan with siphon and drain-pipe, hermetic or semi-hermetic compressor, internal switch/control panel with thermostat and fan selector, external louvre for air intake/discharge, fire dampers per NFPA guidelines",False,"Air handling unit (air cooled) for HVAC (DX cooler, compressor, filters); 'air cooled' here is HVAC, not Air Cooled Condenser (ACC).",True,,high,False,"Contains specific HVAC equipment configuration (AHU/DX unit components), ventilation constraints (battery room return air), fresh air rates, commissioning performance test, and standards (ASHRAE/NFPA) useful for matching to HVAC specs and commissioning/test procedures.","Power Control Centers (PCC) associated to the GTs and STs, the Contractor's proven standard shall be applied with respect to indoor design conditions; No return air shall be taken back into the system from battery room.
As a part of the commissioning, a performance test of the air conditioning and ventilation systems shall be carried out, based on a procedure agreed between the Owner's Representative and the Contractor.
The fresh air shall be provided in occupied areas (e.g. control room, offices, meeting rooms, prayer room, computer rooms, sanitary rooms, laboratory rooms, etc) with 30 m³/h person, For non-occupied areas like electrical & switchgear, etc, forced fresh air supply is not required to be provided.
The air handling unit (other than those specifically mentioned in the RFP, which shall follow the RfP requirements) shall be air cooled and suitable for mounting at the roof or wall or the window balustrade with horizontal air discharge. The unit shall comprise:
- casing of galvanized sheet steel, inside sound and heat insulation, outside stove enameled finish.
- centrifugal fan with double inlet. Single inlet may be provided with Owner's approval
- panel filter, filter material washable and removable without removing cabinet, dust weight separation efficiency according to ASHRAE Standard
- air cooler for direct expansion made of seamless copper tubes mechanically expanded to fix aluminium fins, including expansion valve and drain-pan complete with siphon and drain-pipe
- built-in compressor of hermetic or semi-hermetic type.
- complete internal switch and control panel with thermostat, on/off switch, fan state-selector switch
- external louvre for air intake and discharge
- protection of fire areas by means of fire dampers, according to the NFPA guidelines"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_280,"154, 155",9.8.2._HVAC_automation,"9, 8, 2",content,"text, list_item",9.3._Foundations > 9.8.2._HVAC_automation,Building Services,HVAC,HVAC Automation and Control System,,,"centralised computer-based HVAC control system, alarm printer, operator station in main control room, software for HVAC systems and building services operation, full-colour graphics, HVAC application schematics, equipment images (fans, dampers), temperature/humidity/pressure sensor and actuator symbols, damper position animation, reporting facilities: alarm histories, operator activity log",False,HVAC automation/control system description; no ACC-related equipment or scope.,True,,high,False,"Defines HVAC automation/BMS-style control system architecture, HMI/graphics, alarms, and reporting—strong anchors for control system/BMS/HVAC controls documents.","The control system of the HVAC equipment shall be a centralised computer based system with an alarm printer. The operator station shall be located in the main control room.
The system shall have appropriate software for operation of the HVAC systems and building services. Full-colour graphics shall include following:
- HVAC application schematics
- equipment images (fans, dampers, etc.)
- sensor, indicator and actuator symbols (temperature, humidity and pressure)
- animation symbols (damper position)
The system shall provide a reporting facilities, such as alarm histories, operator activity log etc."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_282,156,10.1._Uniformity_of_Supply,"10, 1",content,text,10.1._Uniformity_of_Supply,Standardization,Uniformity of Supply,Electrical and control room equipment manufacturer uniformity,,,"uniformity of supply, electrical equipment, motors, relays, main pumps, valves, instrumentation and control equipment, package control system, control room equipment, electrical rooms, recorders, indicators, push buttons, electronic modules, controllers, one manufacturer",False,Standardization/uniformity of supply for electrical/control room equipment; no ACC mention.,True,,medium,False,"Contains technical standardization requirements for equipment/manufacturer uniformity across electrical, I&C, pumps/valves and control/electrical room devices; useful to retrieve specifications for standardization and control room/electrical room equipment.","The Contractor will endeavour to obtain (together with its subContractors) a uniformity of supply for items such as electrical equipment, motors, relays, main pumps, valves, instrumentation and control equipment, as far as reasonable, to facilitate the training of the maintenance staff and reduce the number of spare parts.
With respect to the package control system when standard manufacturer packages are implemented, the package control system may be designed partly by different manufacturers in order to get the best subsystems and functionality.
All equipment placed in the control room (such as recorders, indicators, push buttons, etc) and electrical rooms (such as electronic modules, controllers) shall be as far as practicable of one manufacturer."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_283,156,10.2._Interchangeability,"10, 2",content,text,10.2._Interchangeability,Standardization,Interchangeability,Identical and interchangeable equipment of same type,,,"interchangeability, identical equipment, same type, adjustment work",False,Generic interchangeability requirement; no ACC-related signal.,True,,medium,False,"States a technical requirement for interchangeability/standardization of same-type equipment, which can map to equipment specifications and standardization criteria documents.","As far as possible, equipment of the same type shall be identical and interchangeable, with, eventually, the small necessary adjustment work with an item of the same type."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_284,"156, 157",10.3._Selection_of_Materials,"10, 3",content,text,10.3._Selection_of_Materials,Materials Engineering,Selection of Materials,Corrosion and erosion prevention requirements,,,"corrosion prevention, heterogeneous welding minimization, galvanic corrosion, electrostatic couples, material compatibility, design lifetime, strength and weldability, high corrosion/erosion resistant materials, abrasion wear, improper material combinations (carbon steel/stainless steel), circulating water-steam cycle, copper-based alloys not accepted, asbestos not allowed",False,General materials/corrosion prevention guidance for plant; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,True,,high,False,"Provides explicit materials selection and corrosion/erosion constraints (including prohibitions on copper-based alloys in water-steam cycle and asbestos), which are strong anchors for materials specifications and corrosion control documents.","Particular care shall be taken to corrosion prevention by, among others, choosing adequate materials, limiting heterogeneous welding to the minimum and protecting the equipment against galvanic and electrostatic couples.
All materials shall be new (subject to prior usage and storage as required under this Agreement) and of best quality, free from defects or damage and shall be of a class suitable for working under the local conditions.
The material shall withstand all expected physical working conditions without undue distortion, stress or deterioration.
Materials to be selected by the Contractor shall be proven to be eligible and sufficient for the complete design lifetime of the Plant.
In material choices, main attention shall be paid to compatibility of materials and operating conditions. Also mechanical, manufacturing and maintenance characteristics (e.g. strength and weldability) of materials shall be considered as important criteria of choice. The materials to be chosen shall be standardised and represent long-term operational experience.
Components of the CCGT Plant which are subject to abrasion or potential high rates of wear shall be selected from proven grades of high corrosion/erosion resistant materials.
To prevent galvanic corrosion, use of improper material combinations (e.g. carbon steel/stainless steel) shall be avoided.
In circulating water-steam cycle copper-based alloys are not accepted.
Asbestos is not allowed to be used."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_285,"157, 158",10.4.1._Overall_Maintainability,"10, 4, 1",content,"text, list_item",10.3._Selection_of_Materials > 10.4.1._Overall_Maintainability,Maintainability,Overall Maintainability,"Maintenance facilities, access, and standardization for CCGT plant",,,"ambient conditions (high temperature, sand storms), outdoor installation, accessibility for maintenance, dust and rain protection, weather and sun protection, steam turbine overhead travelling crane, heaviest lift, main and auxiliary hooks, gas turbine scheduled maintenance every year, service/dismantle/lay-down area, no building foreseen for gas turbines, steam turbine hall suitable for ST overhaul, GT overhaul maintenance, GT destacking, GT/HRSG maintenance while other unit running combined cycle, standardise manufacturer/size/type/rating/lubricants/control systems/materials, shut-off valves, actuators, field instruments, switchboards, electrical motors, platforms steps ladders standardisation, special tools for scheduled maintenance, routine inspection software equipment, strategic spare parts, 50 m of each type of HV cables",False,Overall maintainability requirements for GT/ST/HRSG and general platforms/ladders; no ACC-specific mention.,True,,high,False,"Contains detailed maintainability and maintenance-facility requirements (cranes, laydown area, GT/ST overhaul constraints, concurrent unit maintenance) plus standardization and spares/tooling requirements; strong retrieval anchors for maintenance philosophy, layout/handling studies, and equipment specs.","The ambient conditions (particularly high temperature and sand storms) and possible outdoor installation principles are to be taken into account when planning the maintenance and service arrangements for the Plant equipment and systems:
- valves, instruments and other equipment shall be easily and safely accessible for maintenance and operation.
- the main equipment and systems should have dust and rain protection.
- places requiring regular maintenance shall have proper weather and sun protection
The steam turbines shall have one permanent suitable overhead travelling crane for the heaviest lift with a weather protected 'parking' cover. The GTs will require scheduled maintenance every year and thus a permanent service/dismantle/lay-down area is to be provided. The Steam Turbine crane shall be equipped with main and auxiliary hooks.
As no building is foreseen for the Gas Turbines, the Steam Turbine hall and overhead crane shall be:
- suitable to perform ST overhaul and maintenance
- suitable to perform full overhaul GT maintenance (including GT destacking if required)
The weather protection of the service space is to be taken into account whether it is temporary or permanent one.
The maintenance of each GT/HRSG unit shall be possible when the other GT/HRSG unit(s) are running on combined cycle mode.
The Contractor shall, as far as practicable, harmonise and standardise on the manufacturer, size, type, rating, lubricants, control systems, materials of construction of equipment and components supplied under the Contract. This applies especially to shut-off valves, actuators, field instrument, switchboards and electrical motors. Such standardisation shall in no way compromise the suitability of each item to perform its required duty. The objective of standardisation shall be to rationalise the spares inventory, to minimise the maintenance requirements and to ensure that safety of operation is not compromised.
The requirement for standardisation shall extend to all platforms, steps and ladders to ensure their uniform function and appearance throughout the Plant.
The scope of Work shall include all necessary special tools for scheduled maintenance or scheduled inspections. This includes also required routine inspection software equipment.
Attention shall be paid to the proposed strategic spare parts by the Contractor: e.g. 50m of each type of HV cables applied in Plant shall be included in the Spare Parts proposal by Contractor."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_286,"158, 159",10.4.2._Maintenance_Access_Criteria,"10, 4, 2",content,"text, list_item",10.3._Selection_of_Materials > 10.4.2._Maintenance_Access_Criteria,Maintainability,Maintenance Access Criteria,Lifting/handling and clearance requirements,,,"maintenance access, 30 kg one person lift guideline, 45 kg two persons lift guideline, permanent monorail, lifting beams, hoists, chain block/chain fall, fork-lift with unicorn attachment, mobile crane placement plans, make and model rigs identified, no disassembly into sub-assemblies to meet weight criteria, control valve diaphragm operator and yoke sub assembly example, clear identified path to maintenance corridor, method to move elevated components to service platform/road, back clearance minimum 600 mm, preferred clearance >750 mm, pumps clearance each side of pump centreline, unobstructed path to plant maintenance corridor, lifting equipment international standards, material test certificates, dynamic and static load test on site",False,Generic maintenance access/lifting/clearance criteria; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.,True,,high,False,"Provides explicit quantitative maintenance handling criteria (lift weights, clearances) and lifting/rigging/testing requirements that can match layout, maintainability, lifting equipment, and access/clearance design documents.","It shall be assumed that all Plant mechanical, electrical and control equipment will require access for maintenance purposes periodically during the life of the Plant. The following general criteria shall be used as a guideline for locating devices either as singular components or packaged systems from vendors:
- one person without hindered access can lift about 30 kg
- two persons each without hindered access can lift about 45 kg
If access is hindered or weights are greater than 45 kg, means of lifting like permanent monorail, lifting beams and hoists with a chain block or via chain fall, or with fork-lift with unicorn attachment, or in the event of special cases, mobile (any mobile crane or fork-lift post COD is to be provided by the Owner) or permanent cranes shall be anticipated. In the case of some electrical equipment, trolleys or special handling gear are available from the Contractor and these should be included in the Work if other means are not available.
Disassembly of components into sub-assemblies shall not generally be accepted as a means around the weights above. For instance, while it is acceptable to take the diaphragm operator and yoke off a control valve as a sub assembly, it is not acceptable to break the diaphragm operator into several pieces for meeting the weight criteria.
A clear identified path from the device to a maintenance corridor for hauling/transport purposes shall be specifically identified if not obvious from the arrangement. If the device is at an elevated location in the Plant, a clear method of getting the component to a service platform or road shall be identified if not obvious.
Where mobile cranes are required for maintenance purposes, there shall be specific plans to identify placement of the crane and specific make and model rigs shall be identified for the duty. Use of greatly over capacity rigs for light components because of long reach shall generally be avoided.
Back clearance from the work face area shall be a minimum of 600 mm at a point, and preferably be greater than 750mm. In the case of pumps (or other rotating equipment), these clearances should be maintained on each side of the pump centreline. An unobstructed path from the work area to a plant maintenance corridor shall be established in the plant design.
The lifting equipment shall conform with the latest edition of the international standards.
Material test certificates shall be provided and dynamic and static load test shall be performed on site in the presence of the owner's representative."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_287,"159, 160",10.4.3._Operating_and_Troubleshooting_Criteria,"10, 4, 3",content,"text, list_item",10.3._Selection_of_Materials > 10.4.3._Operating_and_Troubleshooting_Criteria,Operating and Troubleshooting Criteria,Maintainability and Access,"Access to valves, panels, and sensors",,,"accessibility from floor level/stairway/permanent platform, remotely operated valves access, stairs vs inclined ladders vs vertical ladders (preference order), electrical/electronic panels sun/rain cover, relays and solid state circuitry troubleshooting access, primary control sensors access via extension ladder, extension ladder length < 5 m, man lifting platform access, rolling ladders/stairs in congested areas, maintenance corridors clearance for rolling stairs",False,"Generic maintainability/access requirements for valves, panels, sensors; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention or ACC-specific context.",True,,high,False,"Contains explicit maintainability/access design criteria for valves, control sensors, and electrical/electronic panels including ladder/platform constraints (5 m), useful for retrieving layout/GA, access/platform, and maintainability-related engineering documents.","Beyond the routine access for observation of equipment operating parameters and maintenance (including lubrication and chemical fills), the following specific criteria shall be followed:
- all control or remotely operated valves shall be accessible from floor level, stairway or permanent platform meeting the maintenance criteria above. elevated platforms shall be accessible from stairs, inclined ladders or vertical ladders (in order of preference)
- all electrical/electronic panels containing relays or solid state circuitry potentially requiring replacement or troubleshooting shall be protected with a local sun/rain cover to protect technicians
- primary control sensors and their sensor point valving do not need to be accessible to the above criteria as long as they can be reached via an extension ladder of less than 5 meter length or a man lifting platform. if access to these devices needs to be from a ladder from an elevated platform, the ladder length shall be limited to 5 meters and the platform from which it will be supported shall be accessible via stairway
In congested local areas (subject to Owner's review), it is permissible to assume that local rolling ladders/stairs can be used for access to elevated devices less than 5 meters above a permanent platform or floor for either operational or maintenance purposes. Maintenance corridors shall not restrict movement of such stairs."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_288,160,10.5._Plant_Operation_and_Manning_level,"10, 5",content,text,10.5._Plant_Operation_and_Manning_level,Plant Operation and Manning level,Facilities and Accommodation,O&M staffing considerations,,,"O&M manning level, automation concept, control room, training facilities, office rooms, meeting rooms, staff room, sanitary rooms, car parking slots, workshop, temporary office space during construction",False,Plant O&M manning/facilities text only; no ACC-related equipment or references.,True,,medium,False,"Defines operational staffing-driven facility requirements (control room, offices, workshop, parking, temporary offices) that can anchor retrieval of building layouts and operational facilities documents.","The Contractor shall take into account the O&M manning level throughout the Work including especially, but not limited to, automation concept, control room, training, office rooms, meeting rooms, staff room, sanitary rooms, car parking slots, workshop and temporary office space during construction."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_289,161,11._STUDIES_/_ENGINEERING,11,content,text,11._STUDIES_/_ENGINEERING,Studies / Engineering,,,,,"Codes and Standards (section 4), Exhibit B Part 1, Good Industry Practice",False,Generic reference to codes/standards and good industry practice; no ACC signal.,False,"Too generic; references codes/standards and exhibits without specifying any study type, system, or technical requirement to anchor MDL retrieval.",low,True,No concrete technical scope beyond generic compliance references.,"As per Codes and Standards (refer to section 4), Exhibit B, Part 1 and Good Industry Practice."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_290,161,12._FABRICATION/MANUFACTURING_/_CONSTRUCTION,12,content,text,12._FABRICATION/MANUFACTURING_/_CONSTRUCTION,Fabrication/Manufacturing / Construction,Engineering Studies,Electrical power system studies,,,"power system studies, load flow calculations, system design completeness",False,Electrical power system studies/load flow calculations in general; no ACC mention or interface.,True,,medium,False,"Explicitly calls for power system studies and load flow calculations, which are strong retrieval anchors for existing electrical study documents.","In addition to Exhibit B, Part 1 and Good Industry Practice, the Contractor shall also undertake any necessary studies (e.g. power system studies, load flow calculations etc.) to make the system design complete."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_291,161,"13._PACKAGING,_TRANSPORT,_STORAGE_AND_HANDLING",13,content,text,13._PACKAGING _TRANSPORT _STORAGE_AND_HANDLING,"Packaging, Transport, Storage and Handling",Preservation and Protection,Corrosion and moisture protection during transport/storage,,,"protection against shocks and weather during transport/outdoor storage, anti-rust paint temporary transport protection, air tight plastic sheets, SILICAGEL drying agent for moisture-sensitive equipment, plastic caps for tube/header/pipe ends, temporary coatings removal before/during commissioning, exception for insulated parts",False,"General packaging/preservation requirements (tube/header/pipe ends, corrosion protection); no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.",True,,medium,False,"Contains specific preservation/packing requirements (corrosion protection, silica gel, end caps, coating removal timing) that can match preservation, packaging, and commissioning-related specifications/procedures.","All parts shall be carefully packed to ensure their protection against shocks and weather conditions during their transport and outdoor storage if necessary before being put into place.
All metal parts subject to corrosion shall be protected by layer anti-rust paint/temporary transport protection or by air tight plastic sheets. Bags of SILICAGEL or equivalent drying agent shall be placed, in sufficient number, in cases containing equipment sensitive to moisture.
All tube, header and pipe ends shall be protected by plastic caps.
If temporary coatings should be necessary, they shall be provided and completely removed before or during commissioning, except for insulated parts."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_292,162,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT,14,content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT,,,,,,,False,Site organization/management structure requirement only; no ACC-related content.,False,"Administrative requirement for site organization chart, personnel qualifications and CVs; no technical design/equipment/system requirements.",low,True,Primarily organizational/management structure content; weak linkage to engineering MDL deliverables.,"The Contractor shall provide a Site Organization scheme with nominated personnel, their names, attached with qualifications and key personnel CVs with identification of responsibility and authority illustrating how the management structure/lines of authority will function. The organization shall be such as responsibilities are clearly and effectively divided to each organization level and project personnel. The Owner shall have clear understanding of Contractor's organisational responsibilities and decision process in Contractor's organisation."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_293,162,14.1.1._Contractors_Central_Administration_Area,"14, 1, 1",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.1.1._Contractors_Central_Administration_Area,,,,,,,False,Temporary office/storage compound location drawings; no ACC signal.,False,Request for outline drawings of temporary office/storage/car park locations is site logistics/admin; not a technical engineering deliverable for MDL matching.,low,True,Temporary facilities layout submittal is not a stable technical scope for retrieving existing engineering design documents.,"The Contractor shall submit outline drawings showing proposed locations of its and its sub-contractors central office area together with any other temporary buildings, storage compounds, car parks etc."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_294,162,14.1.2._Contractor's_Accommodation,"14, 1, 2",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.1.2._Contractor's_Accommodation,,,,,,,False,Temporary site accommodation approval/maintenance; no ACC-related scope.,False,Approval/maintenance/identification requirements for temporary accommodation; no technical system/equipment design criteria.,low,True,Mostly administrative controls over temporary site accommodation.,"Before the erection of any temporary site accommodation commences, the Contractor must obtain approval from the Owner of the size, type, condition, location, access and services. The Owner's approval shall be obtained before any accommodation is dismantled or removed from the Site. The Contractor's accommodation shall be reasonably maintained and suitably identified with the Contractor's name and that of his Site Engineer."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_295,"162, 163",14.1.3._Owner's_Accommodation_and_Facilities,"14, 1, 3",content,"text, list_item",14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.1.3._Owner's_Accommodation_and_Facilities,,,,,,,False,Owner temporary offices/facilities requirements; no ACC mention.,False,"Temporary office/facilities provisioning (rooms, staffing counts, utilities, internet) is site admin/logistics rather than engineering design deliverables.",low,True,"Contains some utility connection mentions (water, sewage, electricity, LAN/internet) but framed as temporary office provisioning, not technical design requirements tied to permanent plant documents.","The Contractor shall provide near to Contractor's site offices:
- project site temporary offices for the owner's project team and O&M
- -from NTP: five (5) -employees - and owner's engineer team - ten (10) employees, - and O&M representative -1 employee, including at least 2 conference rooms, 7 office rooms, , drawing office facilities with ample storage for records as well as accommodation for 2 clerical staff with filing and reproduction facilities. Washroom with 1 shower shall be included, as well as all facility connections (water, sewage, electricity, telephone, internet…)
- -12 month after NTP, 4 additional O&M employee in 2 additional office rooms
- access to contractor's food facilities will be available for the owner's and Owner's Engineer's site staff
- computers, telephones, fax, printers are not included however, the Contarctor shall provide necessary LAN network and internet connectivity (at least 8 Mbps) for the Owner and Owner's Engineer's site staff
- costs of consumables (water, electricity, phone…) and cleaning will be borne by the Contractor (international calls will be paid by the Owner)
- site preparation and access roads for temporary offices
Theses temporary offices will be made available to the Owner at the same time as Contractor's first site offices.
The quality of the Owner's temporary offices shall be equivalent to Contractor's site offices."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_296,163,14.1.4._Site_Records_Office,"14, 1, 4",content,"text, list_item",14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.1.4._Site_Records_Office,,,,,,,False,Site records office/document control requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,False,"Records/document control requirements (registers, permits, certificates, QA docs, O&M instructions) are administrative; no specific technical system design/performance requirements.",low,True,"Although it references commissioning documents and test certificates across disciplines, it is about maintaining records rather than defining technical requirements useful for MDL retrieval.","The Contractor will be required to maintain a suitably staffed records office to maintain updated records of all relevant documentation and test certificates and to see that these are available to meet obligations to all statutory bodies and to any commissioning committees, working parties or test teams which are established to meet the needs of the Contract. These records shall be maintained and retained until handed over on completion of all works on Site.
The relevant documents envisaged will include:
- site register for lifting appliances, cranes, slings, hooks, lifting beams and tackle for construction of the works either mobile or fixed.
- site register for lifting appliances, cranes, slings, hooks, lifting beams and tackle supplied as part of the permanent works
- scaffold register
- site works permit system records
- site labour records
- site working drawings
- commissioning documents
- test certificates from works and site.
- -civil
- -mechanical
- -electrical
- -control and instrumentation
- operation and maintenance instructions
- quality assurance documents"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_297,163,14.1.5._Telephones_&_IT,"14, 1, 5",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.1.5._Telephones_&_IT,,,,,,,False,Telephones/IT requirement only; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention or context.,False,Site management/admin requirement for contractor-provided telecom/IT facilities; no engineering design/technical deliverable scope.,low,True,Mentions LAN/telephone/fax but only as contractor logistics; insufficient technical retrieval anchors.,"The Contractor shall provide his own on-site/off-site telephone, fax and LAN facilities."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_298,164,14.1.6._First_Aid,"14, 1, 6",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.1.6._First_Aid,,,,,,,False,First aid and site security arrangements; no ACC-related equipment or scope signal.,False,"Primarily HSE/site administration (medical staff, first aid, security policy/procedures, access control) without technical engineering requirements or deliverables.",low,True,Could relate to HSE plans but lacks specific technical design criteria or document titles for MDL matching.,"The Contractor shall, in collaboration with and to the requirements of the local health authorities, ensure that medical staff, first aid facilities, sick bay and the ability to obtain ambulance services are available on the site at all times.
These arrangements are not intended to relieve the Contractor of his law and statutory obligations other than his obligations to provide such first aid post under 'Site Security'.
The Contractor shall arrange a security facility for the Plant and temporary storage areas, consisting of road access gates manned by uniformed security staff. The Contractor shall submit details of his proposed site security policy and procedures.
At a time of the Taking Over the security for all or part of the Site will be taken over by the Owner.
The Contractor shall ensure that only personnel necessary for ongoing work are permitted onto the site and that all admitted personnel are aware of all precautions to be observed."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_299,164,14.1.7._Site_security,"14, 1, 7",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.1.7._Site_security,,,,,,,False,Site security policy/procedures; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,False,Administrative/site security policy and responsibility statement; no technical engineering scope or deliverables.,low,True,Security gates/staff mentioned but only as site management; not enough for engineering document retrieval.,"The Contractor shall arrange a security facility for the Plant and temporary storage areas, consisting of road access gates manned by uniformed security staff to safeguard the materials and stores in the area allocated to him, together with the Work on the Site. The Contractor shall submit details of his proposed site security policy and procedures.
At a time of the Taking Over the security for all or part of the Site will be taken over by the Owner.
The Contractor shall ensure that only personnel necessary for ongoing work are permitted onto the site and that all admitted personnel are aware of all precautions to be observed.
The Owner or Saudi Aramco or SEC will not accept responsibility for any losses or damage which may occur during the execution of the Work."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_300,164,14.1.8._Contractor's_Storage_Areas,"14, 1, 8",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.1.8._Contractor's_Storage_Areas,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Temporary Facilities,Contractor Storage Areas (Laydown),,,"laydown areas, surfaced with self-draining materials, hardstanding for external storage, foundations for covered storage, drainage for covered storage, weather protection",False,Contractor laydown/storage areas and drainage for covered storage; generic site management with no ACC signal.,True,,medium,False,"Contains civil/temporary works technical requirements (surfacing, hardstanding, foundations, drainage) that can map to existing site temporary works/civil drawings/specs.","Contractors shall submit details of their requirements for laydown areas. These areas shall be surfaced with self-draining materials for weather protection.
The hardstanding for external storage together with foundations and drainage for the covered storage shall be provided by the Contractor."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_301,165,14.1.9._Sign_Boards,"14, 1, 9",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.1.9._Sign_Boards,,,,,,,False,Sign boards/posters approval; no ACC-related content.,False,Administrative approval requirement for signage; no technical engineering scope or deliverables.,low,True,"No design criteria, standards, or engineering document anchors beyond general signage approval.","The erection of sign boards or posters will not be allowed without the Owner's approval of the size, type, location, wording, etc."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_302,"165, 166",14.1.10._Site_Cleanliness_and_Waste_Disposal,"14, 1, 10",content,"text, list_item",14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.1.10._Site_Cleanliness_and_Waste_Disposal,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Site Cleanliness and Waste Disposal,Waste management and pollution prevention,,,"waste collection and transfer to disposal site, dust and dirt screening, pollution of site land drainage system, effluent discharge requirements, dangerous or noxious waste disposal (Owner approval), waste oils collection and disposal, no discharge of waste oils to surface water/groundwater/drainage, waste oil storage tanks / suitable vessels, oil-contaminated surface cleaning with solvents (uncontrolled discharge prohibited), air pollution limits for waste oil processing, burning of waste oils (written permission)",False,General site cleanliness and waste disposal requirements; no ACC-specific mention.,True,,medium,False,"Contains construction-phase environmental controls and specific waste oil/effluent disposal prohibitions that can map to environmental management, drainage, and waste handling requirements in existing project documents.","Throughout the period of construction of the Work the Contractor shall maintain the whole area of his operations in a clean, tidy and safe condition and shall arrange his materials in an orderly manner. The Contractor will be responsible for waste collection and transfer to a suitable disposal site.
All rubbish, etc. shall be systematically cleared from the working areas and, if not removed directly from the Site, shall be deposited at general collecting points provided by the Contractor pending the removal from the Site. Where practicable, screening shall be provided to prevent the ingress of dust and dirt to any part of the Work.
The Contractor shall ensure that during the Contract work he does not cause pollution of the Site land drainage system by either dumping rubbish, fill materials or waste products from any source whatsoever.
In the event of an incident whereby the pollution is attributable to negligence on the part of the Contractor then the Contractor shall clear this pollution as soon as possible and make early arrangements to remedy the cause.
The Contractor shall prohibit the committing of nuisance by his employees within the Site or upon either the Owner's land or others, and any employee found violating this provision shall be immediately discharged and not be employed again on this Contract.
In addition to his statutory obligations as stated in the Conditions of Contract the Contractor shall make himself familiar with all requirements relating to the discharge of effluent from the station and shall take all steps to ensure that these are not contravened by the disposal of effluent from the site.
No dangerous or noxious waste products, chemicals or materials shall be disposed of, on or off the Site without the Owner's approval.
The Contractor shall take all necessary measures to ensure the safe collection and disposal of waste oils pertaining to those Work or plant for which he has responsibility and to ensure the prohibition of:
- any discharge of waste oils into surface waters, ground waters or drainage systems of any kind
- any deposit and/or discharge of waste oils on to the ground or floors of buildings and any uncontrolled discharge of residues resulting from the processing of waste oils or the cleaning of oil contaminated surfaces with solvents
- in order to ensure compliance with this requirement the contractor shall provide suitable vessels for the collection of all waste oils. where large quantities of waste oils need to be stored prior to being disposed of in bulk, suitable storage tanks shall be provided by the contractor for this purpose
- any processing of waste oils causing air pollution which exceeds the level prescribed by existing statutory provisions. the burning of all waste oils should only be carried out following written permission being granted by the owner's site engineer"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_303,166,14.1.11._Sanitation_and_Drainage,"14, 1, 11",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.1.11._Sanitation_and_Drainage,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Sanitation and Drainage,Temporary foul drainage and sewage disposal,,,"foul drainage during construction, sewage disposal from site, consents and licences (local authority), no untreated discharge of foul water and sewage",False,Temporary foul drainage/sewage disposal during construction; no ACC-related term or context.,True,,medium,False,Defines construction-phase foul drainage/sewage handling constraints and untreated discharge prohibition—useful anchors for drainage/sanitation and environmental compliance documents.,The Contractor shall be responsible for the foul drainage and the disposal of all sewage from the Site during the construction period in accordance with all consents and licences or instructions from the local authority. Foul water and sewage shall not be discharged untreated from the site.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_304,166,14.1.12._Sanitary_Facilities,"14, 1, 12",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.1.12._Sanitary_Facilities,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Sanitary Facilities,Temporary welfare facilities,,,"temporary changing facilities, temporary sanitary facilities and amenities, operation and maintenance (clean and tidy condition)",False,Temporary sanitary facilities/amenities; no ACC scope signal.,False,Primarily administrative/construction welfare requirement with no engineering design/performance criteria or technical system details to retrieve MDL documents.,low,True,Mentions temporary amenities only; lacks technical specifications or system requirements beyond general cleanliness/operation.,"The Contractor shall provide temporary changing facilities, sanitary facilities and amenities. The Contractor shall maintain and operate these facilities in a clean and tidy condition for the duration of its use at the Site."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_305,166,14.1.13._Scaffolding,"14, 1, 13",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.1.13._Scaffolding,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Scaffolding,Inspection and certification requirements,,,"scaffolding erected by qualified scaffolders, supervision by experienced supervisors, weekly inspection and certification, competent engineer inspection, accredited representative of scaffold company, fault correction before use",False,Generic scaffolding erection/inspection requirements; no ACC mention.,True,,medium,False,Contains specific scaffolding inspection/certification frequency and competency requirements that can match HSE/construction method and temporary works documentation.,"Scaffolding shall be erected by qualified Scaffolders working under the supervision of experienced supervisors. Should the scaffold be required to remain in position for some time, it must be inspected and certified at weekly intervals by a competent engineer of the Contractor and the accredited representative of the scaffold Company. Should any fault be found on either the initial or subsequent inspections, these faults must be corrected before any personnel may work on the scaffold."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_306,"166, 167",14.1.14._Use_of_Motor_Vehicles_and_Parking,"14, 1, 14",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.1.14._Use_of_Motor_Vehicles_and_Parking,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Use of Motor Vehicles and Parking,Site traffic and parking controls,,,"parking compound within site boundary, outside security fence of construction area, no private vehicles in construction area, authorised vehicles only, vehicle pass issued by contractor, designated parking near main site office",False,Motor vehicles and parking controls; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,False,Primarily site logistics/administrative traffic control; no engineering design or technical system requirements for MDL retrieval.,low,True,Defines parking/vehicle access rules without technical design criteria or deliverable references.,"Parking facilities shall be provided by the Contractor in a compound within the Site boundary but outside the security fence of the construction area and as near to the Site access as possible. The Contractor's employee's private vehicles will not be permitted to enter the construction area. Only Authorised vehicles shall enter the construction area and these must display the vehicle pass issued by the Contractor.
Designated parking spaces shall be allocated local to the main site office for the Owners Project Manager, Owner's Engineer team and other senior project staff."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_307,167,14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements,"14, 2",content,"text, list_item",14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,HSE instructions and PPE requirements,,,"HSE instructions, Owner HSE guidelines (Exhibit T), EIA compliance, World Bank Group EHS Guidelines, PPE: helmet and protective shoes, safety harness with lifeline for work at height, hearing protection over 85 dB noise, no intoxicants at site",False,General site HSE/PPE requirements; no mention of ACC or air cooled condenser equipment/scope.,False,Primarily site HSE administrative/PPE rules and compliance statements; no specific engineering system/equipment technical requirements or design deliverables to retrieve.,low,True,Could relate to HSE plans/procedures but limited value for matching engineering MDL technical documents.,"The Contractor shall prepare health, safety and environmental (HSE) instructions for the project for Owner's review. The Contractor's activities at Site shall meet all requirements set out in the Owner's HSE guidelines as provided in Exhibit T to this Agreement, the EIA, the local legislation and World Bank Group Environmental, Health, and Safety Guidelines and shall be carried out according to a well-structured plan and under a comprehensive set of safety rules and procedures.
The Contractor shall familiarise all people working at Site with any HSE related aspects. In general terms the following instructions shall apply:
- each member of any site teams shall wear helmet and protective shoes
- goggles shall be used when entering to or working at areas where eye protection is required
- safety harness with lifeline shall be used when entering to or working at high level (safety belt is not accepted)
- each member of site team shall wear proper work clothing
- other personal protective devices shall be case by case, e.g. hearing protection over 85db noise
- no intoxicants are allowed or shall not be used at site or at any of project areas
- proper courtesy rules and good conduct shall be maintained in all relations between the owner's personnel and the contractor staff and his subcontractors and with others involved in the project
Each Site team member shall respect the HSE requirements and follow the project rules."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_308,"167, 168",14.2.1._HAZOP_study,"14, 2, 1",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.1._HAZOP_study,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Process safety studies (HAZOP and SIL),,,"HAZOP study, SIL assessment and calculation, all plant systems, interfaces with other parties (Saudi Aramco / SEC), P&IDs review at system component level, parameter excursions: pressure, temperature, flow, composition, electrical power, guide words: more/less/high/low/no/reverse, preliminary HAZOP after basic engineering, final HAZOP during detailed design",False,Generic HAZOP/SIL requirements for all plant systems; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser referenced.,True,,high,False,Explicit requirement for HAZOP and SIL assessment tied to P&IDs and design stages; strong retrieval anchors for existing safety studies and related engineering documents.,"An overall Hazard and Operability Study ('HAZOP') and SIL assessment and calculation shall be carried out by the Contractor for all plant systems by appointing a group (or groups) of multi-disciplinary specialists familiar with the HAZOP technique and the type of plant or system being assessed to evaluate problems that may represent risks to personnel, equipment, environment or prevent an efficient Plant operation. In addition, all interfaces with other parties (e.g. Saudi Aramco / SEC) shall also be subjected to detailed HAZOP studies.
P&IDs and other required documents shall be systematically and in order studied at the system component level for parameter excursions outside the normal design mode in terms of (but not limited to) radiation, pressure, temperature, flow, composition or electrical power. Each parameter shall be reviewed via guide words such as 'more', 'less', 'high', 'low' 'no' or 'reverse' to establish the causes and consequences of such variations. Then, current safeguards shall be analysed and, if required, additional measures should be proposed to reduce the risks of the analysed equipment or system. Results shall be recorded in an orderly manner.
HAZOP's shall be scheduled 8 weeks in advance with Owners and their Engineers and the Owners shall be represented at all HAZOP sessions. As a minimum, Contractor shall perform a preliminary HAZOP study after the basic engineering to incorporate its findings into the basic design and a final one during the latest stages of the detailed design.
HAZOP findings shall be followed up and design changes incorporated where deemed necessary. HAZOP computer based aids can be used to assist the HAZOP process."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_309,168,14.2.1.1._HAZARDOUS_AREAS_CLASSIFICATION,"14, 2, 1, 1",content,"text, list_item",14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.1._HAZOP_study > 14.2.1.1._HAZARDOUS_AREAS_CLASSIFICATION,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Hazardous area classification,,,"hazardous area classification, classification drawing, list of rooms/areas, temperatures (ambient/surface/internal), dust and water conditions, fire hazard, explosion hazard, ATEX requirements, IEC 60079, NFPA 70 Article 500, GT OEM alternative protection techniques, normal and exceptional conditions (weather, pipe breakage, operational errors)",False,Hazardous area classification requirements (ATEX/IEC 60079/NFPA 70) with no ACC-specific signal.,True,,high,False,"Clear technical requirement for hazardous area classification with explicit standards (ATEX, IEC 60079, NFPA 70) and deliverable types (drawing/list) useful for MDL matching.","The Contractor shall carry out a hazardous area classification of the Work, and comply with any mandatory norms and standards. The Contractor shall provide drawing and the list of rooms/areas defining the classification of the Plant and areas near equipment. The classification of the areas shall include all necessary information on:
- temperatures (ambient, surface, internal)
- dust and water conditions
- fire hazard
- explosion hazard (incl. analysis and descriptions in accordance with ATEX requirements)
- requirements for electrical equipment (IEC 60079)
It is acceptable only for the GT OEM's equipment to use alternative protection techniques as defined in NFPA 70 Article 500 for equipment in hazardous areas.
Both normal and exceptional conditions shall be considered including exceptional weather conditions, any equipment or pipe breakage as well as operational errors."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_310,"168, 169",14.2.2._Safety_Management,"14, 2, 2",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.2._Safety_Management,,,,,,,False,General industrial health & safety management text; no ACC-related terms or context.,False,"Primarily management/administrative H&S responsibilities, audits, and coordination; lacks technical design/system requirements.",low,True,May map to HSE management plans but weak linkage to engineering MDL technical documents.,"Industrial Health & Safety of the highest standard is required of all contractors. Safety must form an integral part of the line management control of the site construction works.
The Contractor has full responsibility for the management of health and safety on site (including Plant site and Share Facilities sites), and shall co-ordinate the activities of all sub-contractors to ensure that the requirements of the contract are satisfied, compliance with all aspects of Health & Safety legislation and authoritative guidance, and the establishment and maintenance at all times of a safe and healthy environment.
The Owner reserves the right to audit the management and control of health & safety on site at any time.
All contractors shall co-operate with the Auditor may be appointed by the Owner and shall make available to the Auditor such information records and access to places of work as required by the Auditor in pursuance of the Audit.
The Contractor shall bring the requirements of the contract to the attention of all sub-contractors."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_311,169,14.2.3._Safety_Plans_and_Method_Statements,"14, 2, 3",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.3._Safety_Plans_and_Method_Statements,,,,,,,False,Site Safety Plan and method statements requirements; not tied to ACC scope.,False,Primarily procedural/submittal requirements for site safety plan and method statements; not a technical engineering requirement for MDL retrieval.,low,True,Could relate to HSE documentation but generally outside engineering design deliverables targeted by MDL retrieval.,"The Contractor shall prepare a detailed Site Safety Plan and submit it to the Owner for review. The Contractor shall prepare method statements for work which they consider requires detailed instruction and will be responsible for agreeing these, where necessary, with their subcontractors. Before any site construction work begins, the Contractor shall submit several typical method statements, to provide an example to the Owner that a suitable standard will be achieved. Subsequently where deemed necessary by the Contractor and in order to meet his safety obligations, method statements will be produced and submitted to the Owner at least 30 days before work commences on the activity covered by the statement.
The Contractor shall require all sub-contractors to submit Site Safety Plans and method statements to him for approval before subcontract work commences. This process is open to Audit by the Owner's Auditor.
Site Safety Plans should include all the relevant subjects, and specify how safety is to be achieved in each case."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_312,169,14.2.4._Safety_Rules,"14, 2, 4",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.4._Safety_Rules,,,,,,,False,Generic compliance with safety documents and legislation; no ACC mention.,False,General HSE compliance statement referencing legislation and 'Safety Documents' without technical system/equipment requirements or engineering deliverables.,low,True,Primarily administrative HSE compliance language; weak MDL retrieval anchors.,"The Contractor shall comply with the requirements of the following documents and all relevant Country legislation where appropriate, and with the rules relating to the issue of 'Safety Documents' contained therein."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_313,170,14.2.4._Safety_Rules,,list,list_item,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.4._Safety_Rules,,,,,,,False,Lists general Saudi construction safety rules and Owner HSE guidelines; no ACC signal.,False,References general safety rules/guidelines (Saudi Arabia construction; Owner HSE Exhibit) without technical design/engineering content.,low,True,Administrative HSE reference list; no technical requirements to match to MDL engineering documents.,"- Safety Rules related to construction in Saudi Arabia.
- Owner's General HSE guidelines as per Exhibit T to this Agreement"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_314,170,14.2.5._Safety_Personnel,"14, 2, 5",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.5._Safety_Personnel,,,,,,,False,Safety personnel staffing requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,False,"Staffing/notification requirements (H&S officer, supervisor ratios, CV submission) are administrative and not tied to technical engineering scope/deliverables.",low,True,Site H&S resourcing requirements; not useful for retrieving engineering MDL documents.,"The Contractor shall notify the Owner of his Directors and Managers primarily responsible for implementation of the safety system.
The Contractor shall appoint a full time Site H&S Officer to coordinate the safety activities of his own and sub-contractors activities.
Contractor shall appoint one safety supervisor for every 50 workers.
Details of the proposed appointment together with curriculum vitae shall be submitted to the Owner before site work commences."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_315,170,14.2.6._Safety_Training,"14, 2, 6",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.6._Safety_Training,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Safety Training,,,"Safety Induction training (site-specific), sub-contractors included in training, grid interface safety rules, gas/liquid fuel operating system interface, scaffolders training, crane drivers training, Owner audit of training process",False,"General safety induction/training requirements (scaffolders, crane drivers, interfaces); no ACC mentioned.",True,,medium,False,"Contains specific HSE training requirements including interfaces with grid and gas/liquid fuel operating systems, which can help retrieve related interface/operational safety documentation if present in MDL.","The Contractor shall ensure that no individual commences work on site unless he has successfully completed a Safety Induction training course specific to that site. The training is to include all sub-contractors or personnel working on site (including CCGT plant SF).
The Contractor is responsible for ensuring that all required specific activity training, e.g. scaffolders, crane drivers, etc has been carried out.
Where work on site interfaces with the grid or gas/liquid fuel operating system, the Contractor is responsible for ensuring that relevant Safety Rules are applied, and that all such training and nomination as required by the said rules are completed.
The training process is open to audit by the Owner's Auditor."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_316,"170, 171",14.2.7._Safety_Meetings,"14, 2, 7",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.7._Safety_Meetings,,,,,,,False,Monthly site safety meetings and accident statistics; no ACC-related content.,False,Monthly safety meeting and accident statistics/audit reporting requirements are administrative management processes without technical engineering scope.,low,True,HSE management meeting process; weak linkage to engineering MDL deliverables.,"The Contractor shall establish and chair at senior management level a monthly site safety meeting, to which management representatives and safety officers of Contractor and his sub-contractor shall attend.
The purpose of the meeting is to discuss, co-ordinate and resolve all safety matters of concern on site, and examine current and future activities to ensure a consistently high standard of health & safety on site.
The Contractor shall be responsible for collating all site accident statistics, and these shall be considered and reviewed at the meeting.
The Contractor shall bring to the meeting's attention for comment and action, any reports arising from Audits undertaken by the Owners Auditors.
The Owner reserves the right to observe the proceedings at site safety meetings."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_317,171,14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,"14, 2, 8",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Site Safety Plan,,,"Site Safety Plan, safety requirements measures, guidance list not exhaustive",False,Generic site safety plan guidance; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention or ACC-specific context.,False,General guidance statement; no specific technical requirements or engineering deliverables to retrieve.,low,True,HSE admin guidance without concrete technical anchors.,"The list below is not intended to be exhaustive, and is provided for guidance purposes only. The Site Safety Plan should specify the measures proposed to meet the safety requirements in each areas below."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_318,171,14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,,content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,,,,,,,False,General safety management/training requirements; no ACC-related signal.,False,HSE management/training administrative content; not a technical design/performance requirement for MDL retrieval.,low,True,"Focuses on safety organization, reporting, and induction training rather than engineering scope.","Detail the management and supervisory structure responsible for safety, together with reporting procedures to monitor safety performance. Detail the arrangement for site safety training, especially induction training."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_319,171,14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,,content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Work in hazardous areas,,,"steel erection safety precautions, confined spaces, excavations, underground works, basements, work at height (above ground), access platform machines",False,Generic hazardous area/steel erection safety precautions; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,True,,medium,False,"Contains specific construction activity safety scopes (confined space, excavation, steel erection, access platforms) that can match existing method statements/procedures in an MDL.","Specify safety precautions for work in hazardous areas, i.e. above ground, steel erection, confined spaces, excavations, underground works and basements. State the preferred access system to be used, e.g. access platform machines."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_320,171,14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,,content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Scaffolding,,,"scaffold design and loading, scaffold management, Statutes Standards & Codes compliance, scaffolders qualification requirements, scaffold identification, scaffold inspection procedures",False,Scaffolding strategy and compliance requirements; no ACC-specific mention.,True,,medium,False,Includes concrete scaffolding technical/management requirements (design/loading/inspection/qualification) useful for retrieving scaffold procedures/specifications.,"State contract strategy and detail aspects of design, loading and scaffold management to ensure compliance with Statutes, Standards & Codes. Include scaffolders qualification requirements, identification and inspection procedures."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_321,171,14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,,content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Lifting equipment and site equipment safety management,,,"method statements for complex lifts, statutory test certification and inspection, equipment maintenance procedures, operator training, operator authorisation, competency requirements",False,General lifting equipment/site equipment safety management; no ACC-related terms.,True,,medium,False,"Provides specific requirements around lifting operations (complex lifts, statutory certification/inspection, operator competency) that can map to existing lifting plans/method statements/procedures in an MDL.","Detail the arrangements to ensure the management and safe use of all such equipment, e.g. method statements are required for complex lifts. Include provisions for statutory test, certification and inspection, and maintenance procedures. Specify operator training and authorisation to ensure competency."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_322,171,14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,,content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Site Safety Plan,,,"site voltages, fixed electrical equipment, portable electrical equipment, maintenance and inspection, systems of work, management control of equipment",False,"Generic site electrical safety (voltages, inspection); no ACC equipment/interface mentioned.",False,Health & safety plan administrative content; no engineering design/technical deliverable requirements to match MDL documents.,low,True,"Mentions electrical equipment safety management but lacks design criteria, specifications, or technical scope suitable for MDL retrieval.","Specify site voltages, maintenance and inspection of all fixed and portable electrical equipment. Describe systems of work and management control of equipment."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_323,171,14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,,content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Site Safety Plan,,,"gas welding equipment, electric welding equipment, gas cylinders control and storage, fire and explosion precautions, flashback arrestors (mandatory)",False,General welding equipment safety and gas cylinder controls; no ACC-related signal.,False,Construction site safety-plan requirements; not an engineering design/specification scope for MDL retrieval.,low,True,Contains safety precautions and mandatory flashback arrestors but does not point to specific engineering deliverables or design requirements.,"Describe arrangements to ensure safe use of gas and electric welding equipment. Include control and storage of gas cylinders, fire and explosion precautions. Note: flashback arrestors are mandatory on the Site."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_324,172,14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,,content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Site Safety Plan,,,"Country Regulations compliance, hazardous substances list, site-specific assessments",False,Country regulations and hazardous substances list; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.,False,Regulatory/admin HSE documentation requirement; not a technical engineering scope for MDL matching.,low,True,Could relate to HSE documentation but lacks technical design/performance requirements or explicit engineering deliverables.,"Detail how compliance with Country Regulations is to be achieved. Provide a list of all hazardous substances, as defined in the regulations, with a copy of the site specific assessments."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_325,172,14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,,content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Site Safety Plan,,,"Personal Protective Equipment (PPE), PPE wear/use/control policy",False,PPE policy requirements; no ACC-related mention.,False,PPE policy is HSE administrative content with no engineering technical retrieval anchors.,low,True,No equipment/system design or engineering document references; primarily site safety administration.,"Detail situations requiring the use of Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) and specify policy regarding its wear, use and control."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_326,172,14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,,content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Site Safety Plan,,,"health and welfare facilities, rescue arrangements, fire fighting facilities, first aid, site emergencies control",False,General site welfare/rescue/fire fighting/first aid; no ACC-specific signal.,False,"Site emergency and welfare arrangements are HSE/site management content, not engineering design scope for MDL retrieval.",low,True,Mentions emergency response facilities but without technical system requirements or references to engineering deliverables.,"Describe facilities provided for health and welfare, rescue, fire fighting, first aid and control of site emergencies."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_327,172,14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,,content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,,,,,,,False,Generic site safety plan addendum/proposal signing; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,False,Administrative proposal/submittal instruction for Site Safety Plan addendum; no technical design/engineering retrieval anchors.,low,True,HSE admin requirement (signed/dated copy with proposal) rather than technical scope tied to MDL engineering documents.,"This should be included as an addendum to the Site Safety Plan. A copy shall be provided with the Proposal, it should be signed and dated by the Director/Manager responsible for company safety."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_328,172,14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,,content,"text, list_item",14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.8._Typical_Items_for_Inclusion_in_a_Site_Safety_Plan,,,,,,,False,General construction site hazards list; no mention of ACC or related equipment.,False,Generic construction-site hazard list for safety plan; lacks project-specific technical requirements or engineering deliverables for MDL matching.,low,True,"Contains HSE hazard examples (e.g., compressed air, high voltage) but no specific plant system design/criteria/testing requirements to retrieve engineering documents.","The following list indicated some of the hazards commonly encountered on construction sites. Describe measures and precautions to achieve safety, this may be in the form of a company safety manual/booklet e.g.:
- Legionnaires disease
- abrasive wheels
- lead poisoning
- compressed air
- insulation materials
- buried services
- flammable liquids & gases
- glass containers
- airborne dust & particles
- waste products
- working near water fire
- noise poor illumination
- ionising radiation sources
- site transport
- high voltage apparatusexplosives
- Leptospirosis"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_329,173,14.2.9._Permit_to_Work_system,"14, 2, 9",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.9._Permit_to_Work_system,,,,,,,False,Permit-to-work system requirements for plant/site interfaces; no ACC-specific reference.,False,"Primarily procedural/administrative HSE management (permit-to-work, approvals, training/authorisations) without technical engineering scope for MDL retrieval.",low,True,"Interfaces with Owner/TSO safety procedures are noted, but no technical system requirements or document deliverables are specified.","The Contractor shall establish its own permit to work system applicable for the Plant until handing over to the Owner. This system is subject to Owner approval and shall as a minimum comply with the Owner's safety rules.
Where work on Site interfaces with the Owner's, Transmission System Operator's or Associated Works Contractors' operating systems, the Contractor is responsible for ensuring that the relevant safety systems and procedures (or other such safety procedures operated by the Owners or their Operations and Maintenance Contractor, Transmission System Operator or Associated Works Contractors) are applied, and that all necessary training, authorisations and nominations are completed. These do not absolve the Contractor for ultimate responsibility for all safety matters on the work area under its control."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_330,173,14.2.10._Obstruction_to_access,"14, 2, 10",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.10._Obstruction_to_access,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Site access and segregation,,,"temporary alternative access, unhindered access to work in progress, fencing and barriers, segregate construction areas from operational areas, avoid obstructing plant equipment operation",False,General access/segregation and obstruction avoidance on site; no ACC context.,True,,medium,False,"Contains site management/HSE requirements with concrete physical measures (fencing/barriers, segregation, temporary access) that could map to site logistics/access/temporary works documents.","All work at the Site shall be carried out in such a manner as not to obstruct the operation of any other Contractor on the Site, or in the latter stages of the Project the operation of the Plant equipment.
The Contractor shall also allow the Saudi Aramco, Owner, and Owner's representatives unhindered access to all parts of the Work in progress and where normal access is obstructed he shall provide temporary alternative access at his cost.
The Contractor shall provide fencing and barriers to segregate the construction areas from operational areas within the Plant."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_331,173,14.2.11._Clear_road_access,"14, 2, 11",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.11._Clear_road_access,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Road access and housekeeping,,,"temporary and permanent roadways, spillage from traffic, immediate clearing of spillage, earth on wheels or tracks",False,Housekeeping/roadway spillage clearing requirement; no ACC-related signal.,True,,medium,False,"Defines operational/site logistics requirement (roadway cleanliness/spillage control) that may align with construction method statements, site logistics, or temporary works/access documentation.","The Contractor shall take precautions to keep all temporary and permanent roadways clear of any spillage from his traffic to the satisfaction of the Owner's Engineer. All such spillage that occurs shall be cleared immediately at the Contractor's expense, including excessive earth brought in on wheels or tracks of the traffic, or other materials."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_332,173,14.2.12._Site_buildings,"14, 2, 12",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.12._Site_buildings,,,,,,,False,Site office/buildings administrative requirement; no ACC mention.,False,Administrative/site management requirement for contractor office/buildings and communications; no technical design/performance or engineering deliverable anchors.,low,True,"Primarily site administration (office availability and communication methods), unlikely to map to existing engineering MDL technical documents.","The Contractor shall provide and maintain an office on or near the Site and other buildings as necessary for the due performance of the Work. The office shall be open at all reasonable hours to receive instructions and other communications in the form of manual delivery, post, telephone, telefax and email."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_333,174,14.2.13._Site_labour,"14, 2, 13",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.13._Site_labour,,,,,,,False,Generic labour provision for erecting/testing/commissioning the Work; no ACC-specific signal.,False,"General staffing/labour provision statement (supervisory, skilled/unskilled, transport/accommodation) without technical requirements or document deliverables.",low,True,Construction resourcing language; does not provide technical retrieval anchors for MDL matching.,"The Contractor shall include for the provision of all necessary supervisory, skilled and unskilled labour for erecting, testing, commissioning, and maintaining the Work together with all necessary transport, accommodation where required, and other services necessary to support his supervisory and labour force."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_334,174,14.2.14._Sanitary_and_health_control,"14, 2, 14",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.14._Sanitary_and_health_control,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Sanitary and health control,,,"WHO Guidelines, sanitary facilities (site), refuse removal and disposal, health education programme, mosquito-borne disease precautions, approved insecticides spraying, avoid pools of water / mosquito breeding places, local regulations (waste disposal)",False,Sanitary/health control and mosquito precautions; no ACC relevance.,True,,medium,False,"Contains HSE/environmental control requirements (sanitation, waste disposal, vector control) and a standards anchor (WHO Guidelines) that could match existing HSE/environmental management documents.","The Contractor shall implement a programme of health education to ensure that all appropriate personnel working on the Site are aware of health risks associated with living and working together with necessary protective measures.
All persons connected with the Work shall be obliged to use the sanitary facilities provided and maintained on the Site by the Contractor.
The Contractor shall be responsible for the removal from the Site and ultimate disposal of his refuse in accordance with any local regulations.
The facilities provided and the standards maintained shall be in accordance with the WHO Guidelines as a minimum.
Special precautions shall be taken by the Contractor to keep the incidence of mosquito borne and other diseases to a minimum. These shall include spraying with approved insecticides the insides of all buildings under his control, and avoiding the formation of pools of water and other likely mosquito breeding places within and adjacent to the Works."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_335,174,14.2.15._Dumping,"14, 2, 15",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.2._Health_&_Safety_Requirements > 14.2.15._Dumping,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Health & Safety Requirements,Waste handling and disposal (dumping),,,"dangerous or noxious substances, handling and storage methods, pollution or contamination prevention, waste materials not dumped in rivers, approved off-site pit / disposal area, approved environmental procedures, approved disposal facilities, permissions for disposal facilities",False,Waste handling/dumping and environmental compliance; no ACC-related terms.,True,,medium,False,Defines environmental protection requirements for hazardous/noxious substances and waste disposal controls that can retrieve environmental procedures/plans in the MDL.,"The Contractor shall ensure that the methods employed in handling, storing and disposal of all dangerous or noxious substances are such that pollution or contamination of the Site or surrounding areas shall not occur. Waste materials shall not be dumped in rivers or on the Site but shall be deposited in an approved off-site pit or disposal area or otherwise disposed of in accordance with the approved environmental procedures. The Contractor shall be responsible for provision of the approved disposal facilities, including obtaining of all necessary permissions."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_336,"174, 175",14.3._Radio_Telephone_Systems,"14, 3",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.3._Radio_Telephone_Systems,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Radio Telephone Systems,Radio communications interference control,,,"radio telephone system permission (authorities), cordless telephones, cellular telephones, radio systems, interference with control and instrumentation equipment, commissioning phase, subsequent takeover, provide equipment details (construction phase)",False,Radio telephone systems and interference with control/instrumentation in general; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,medium,False,Includes technical interface constraint (avoid interference with control & instrumentation during commissioning/takeover) and radio communications scope that can match telecom/I&C interface documents.,"The Contractor shall obtain permission from the appropriate authorities before any radio telephone system is used on the Site. This includes cordless and cellular telephones as well as other radio systems.
The Contractor should ensure that the proposed system will not cause interference to control and instrumentation equipment installed on the station during commissioning or subsequent takeover.
The Contractor shall provide details of the equipment during the Project construction phase."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_337,175,14.4._Noise,"14, 4",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.4._Noise,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Noise,Construction noise control and measurement,,,"minimise noise emissions, noise levels measured according to ISO standards, permissible noise limits, silencers, lagging, acoustic enclosures, ventilation silencers, safety valve exhaust silencer, steam vent silencer, Ambient Noise distribution report",False,"Noise control requirements are generic for plant/construction; no mention of ACC, air cooled condenser, fans, bundles, or ACC-specific equipment.",True,,high,False,"Contains explicit technical requirements for noise measurement (ISO), mitigation features (silencers/acoustic enclosures), and a specific report deliverable (ambient noise distribution).","Construction processes shall be planned at the outset to minimise noise emissions.
The noise levels shall be measured according to ISO standards.
To meet the noise level requirements the Plant is required to be equipped with standard noise features.
All machinery which exceeds the permissible noise limits shall be equipped with silencers or with lagging or specially designed acoustic enclosures.
The ventilation systems of acoustic enclosures shall be provided with silencers before air rejection to the outside.
Exhaust to atmosphere for safety valves and steam vents shall be equipped with silencer.
Contractor will provide an Ambient Noise distribution report."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_338,175,14.5._Damage_to_Property,"14, 5",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.5._Damage_to_Property,,,,,,,False,Generic damage-to-property contractual clause; no ACC or air cooled condenser scope signal.,False,Primarily contractual/legal responsibility for property damage and compliance with local laws; no technical design/performance requirements.,low,True,Administrative/legal clause without technical retrieval anchors for engineering MDL matching.,"Damage to any property belonging to Saudi Aramco or SEC or any other contractors due to accident or exposure during and after construction activities and operation, shall be restored by, and for the account of, the Contractor. The Contractor shall be held responsible for any damage done to adjoining property.
The Contractor shall draw particular attention to the Laws of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia including applicable SSDs, concerning damage to property or disruption of services."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_339,176,14.6.1._Monitoring_of_Environmental_Impacts,"14, 6, 1",content,text,14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.5._Damage_to_Property > 14.6.1._Monitoring_of_Environmental_Impacts,Erection / Site Activities / Site Management,Environmental impacts,Monitoring and compliance with construction/environmental consents,,,"monitoring required by authorities, construction/environmental consents, conditions of consents, environmental impacts monitoring",False,Generic environmental monitoring/consents requirement; no ACC-related terms or context.,True,,medium,False,Defines requirement for environmental impact monitoring tied to authority consent conditions; useful to retrieve environmental monitoring/management documents if present in MDL.,The Contractor shall take care of all monitoring which is required to satisfy authorities' requirements and to fulfil the conditions of the any construction/environmental consents.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_340,"176, 177",14.6.2._Approvals_Procedure,"14, 6, 2",content,"text, list_item",14._ERECTION_/_SITE_ACTIVITIES_/_SITE_MANAGEMENT > 14.5._Damage_to_Property > 14.6.2._Approvals_Procedure,,,,,,,False,Generic approvals/licensing procedure text; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention or interface.,False,"Permitting/approvals administrative procedure (programmes, liaison, applications, deviations/variations) without specific technical scope or criteria.",low,True,Mostly administrative process text; mentions drawings/study reports generically but no technical requirements to anchor MDL retrieval.,"Subject to the provisions in the Contract the following shall be followed by the Contractor:
- The Contractor will prepare and maintain a detail programme for getting necessary approvals and supplementary consents for his project implementation. This programme will form the basis for liaison with all relevant authorities and will also describe the information to be provided.
- The Contractor will prepare all necessary material (drawings, descriptions, study reports etc.) which is needed for getting necessary approvals from the relevant authorities.
- The Contractor and his subcontractors are responsible of communicating directly with the authorities. In order to ensure well-coordinated communication with the different authorities, the Contractor has to name a person(s) in charge of coordinating the different tasks related to licensing between the Contractor and the authorities and the subcontractors.
- Prior starting of the design for licensing and permitting the Contractor shall liaise with proper local authorities to clarify the permitting procedures and detailed requirements for design documentation in order to secure fluent permitting procedure and compliance with statutory requirements.
- Prior sending any formal applications a mutual understanding should already be negotiated. Any dispute or further requirement by the authorities has to be reported to the Owner within a reasonable time. The Contractor will keep the Owner informed of meetings with the authorities and give the Owner the opportunity to participate.
- The Contractor will ensure that copies of the complete applications and agreements related to consents are handed over to the Owner for review.
- The Owner and the Contractor will formally request in due time a written confirmation from a relevant authority that the necessary approvals to start the works and progress the works are in place by target dates, if this has not become clear from the correspondence between the Contractor and the authority. The Owner will check that works subject to these approvals to the Contractor shall not commence before the approvals have been issued.
- The Contractor shall comply with the approved design information, and no deviation is permitted, unless a change is approved by the relevant authority and agreed with the Owner.
- The Owner will monitor but the Contractor shall be responsible for whether an approved design change or equivalent reason will lead to a need to change an existing consent condition. Discussions will be held with the Contractor and the relevant authority and thereafter the Variation Procedure shall be followed."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_343,178,17.1._General_requirements,"17, 1",content,text,17.1._General_requirements,Quality Management,Quality assurance and quality control,ISO 9001 quality system; audits; inspections and testing,,,"ISO 9001, quality management plans, quality audits (subcontractors), Owner quality audit rights, manufacturing facilities audit, documentation inspection system, Quality Assurance Program, quality control and inspection activities programme, factory/on-site inspections and tests, measuring apparatus, non-destructive tests (NDT), steel maker certificate (chemical and heat treatment)",False,Quality management/ISO 9001 requirements are general; no ACC equipment or ACC-specific QA/QC referenced.,True,,high,False,Contains explicit QA/QC standard (ISO 9001) and inspection/testing requirements including NDT and material certificates—strong anchors for QA/QC plans and inspection/test documentation in MDL.,"At all stages of the manufacture the Contractor shall make use of an effective control system for maintaining the agreed standard of quality. The quality system of Contractor and his subcontractors shall follow the requirements of ISO 9001 Standards (or equivalent).
Quality management plans shall include also the deliveries of the subcontractors.
The Contractor shall carry out quality audits to each of his subcontractors to ensure that the quality of the subcontractors will be compliant with the Contract requirements and Contractor's own quality requirements.
The Contractor or his subcontractors may be subject to quality audit by the Owner. The Owner or his nominated representative shall have the right of visiting the Contractor's or his subcontractors' manufacturing facilities in order to audit their quality control procedures, to observe the tests, inspections and works in progress and to review the test results. The Contractor shall provide any facilities, including access, which may be required by the Owner for monitoring activities.
The Contractor shall establish and maintain a documentation inspection system, which shall be capable of producing objective evidence that all manufactured parts, materials and assemblies comply with the quality requirements of the Contract.
The Contractor shall prepare his Quality Assurance Program arrangements defining a programme of quality control and inspection activities which he or his sub-contractors will perform in order to ensure that the manufacture and completion of the Plant complies with the specified requirements.
The Contractor shall provide necessary facilities and all necessary measuring apparatus for carrying out required factory/on-site inspections and tests.
If applicable, non-destructive tests of the various components, also mechanical tests on the materials thereof shall be carried out at the maker's works in accordance with the appropriate standard. Steel makers certificate of chemical and heat treatment shall be provided in all cases."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_345,"179, 180",17.3._Quality_Control_Plans_and_programmes,"17, 3",content,text,17.3._Quality_Control_Plans_and_programmes,Quality Management,Quality Control Plans and Programmes,Inspections and Tests,,,"Quality Control Plans (QC-Plans), manufacturing inspections and tests, acceptance criteria, statutory inspections and tests, nonconforming items identification and isolation, defects liability, Owner inspection arrangements, quality deficiencies reporting",False,Generic QC plans/inspections/testing requirements; no ACC or air cooled condenser scope signal.,False,"Primarily QA/QC administrative requirements (plans, notifications, responsibilities) without specific technical system/equipment requirements or engineering deliverables to retrieve.",low,True,Contains general QC plan obligations and inspection coordination language; limited technical retrieval anchors beyond generic inspections/tests.,"Quality Control Plans shall be prepared which identify the requirements for manufacturing, the necessary inspections and tests including complete information on the acceptance criteria and the records specific to the items covered by those QC-Plans.
The Contractor shall be responsible for identifying in a suitable manner, any inspections and test required for statutory purposes.
The Contractor shall keep the Owner informed in due time of starting and progress of the work so that the Owner will have sufficient time to make arrangements for the inspection.
The Contractor shall identify and isolate all items which do not comply with the required Quality Plan or Contract requirements. All such items shall be reported to the Owner immediately.
Inspections by the Owner do not relieve the Contractor from any responsibility regarding defects or any other failures which may be found and are subject to Contractor's defects liability.
The Contractor shall adhere to the approved Quality Control Plan. Any difficulties, deficiencies or other conditions adversely affecting quality shall be reported to the Owner.
The presence of the Owner or his representative does not release the Contractor from any responsibilities towards the Owner under the Contract.
The Owner will mark each Quality Control Programme to indicate:"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_346,180,17.3.1._Notification_Points_for_Inspections_and_Tests_(NPIT)_(or_'Witness_Point'),"17, 3, 1",content,text,17.3._Quality_Control_Plans_and_programmes > 17.3.1._Notification_Points_for_Inspections_and_Tests_(NPIT)_(or_'Witness_Point'),,,,,,,False,Generic inspection/test notification (witness point) procedure; no ACC mention.,False,Administrative notification procedure for inspections/tests (NPIT/Witness Point) with no technical scope or equipment/system requirements.,low,True,Only defines written notification and Owner attendance decision; no technical content for MDL matching.,"Notification of an inspection or test shall be given in writing by the Contractor to the individuals or organisations designated by the Owner.
If, following receipt of timely notification, the Owner decides not to attend, the inspection or test may proceed."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_347,180,17.3.2._Hold_Points_(HP),"17, 3, 2",content,text,17.3._Quality_Control_Plans_and_programmes > 17.3.2._Hold_Points_(HP),,,,,,,False,Generic hold point procedure for procurement/workmanship; no ACC-related content.,False,"QA/QC process definition (hold points, notification lead times) without technical requirements tied to specific systems/equipment or deliverables.",low,True,Defines HP/NPIT timing and approval gating; not useful for retrieving engineering MDL documents.,"A Hold Point is a stage in the material procurement or workmanship process which requires documented proof of approval and beyond which work shall not proceed without written approval by the designated individuals or organisations designated by the Owner.
Subject to the provisions in the Contract the Contractor shall notify the Owner two (2) weeks before the start of all HP and NPIT points. Verification shall be given five (5) working days prior to the start of the event."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_348,180,17.4._Quality_Records,"17, 4",content,text,17.4._Quality_Records,Quality Management,Quality Records,Inspection and Test Documentation,,,"quality records, inspection results, test reports, defect location charts/drawings, corrective measures documentation, Certificate of Conformity/Compliance, Quality Release Note, plant/equipment quality documentation",False,Generic quality records/certificates requirement; no ACC equipment or documentation specifically referenced.,False,"Primarily documentation/records requirements (test reports, certificates) without specific technical system/equipment design requirements.",low,True,Mentions test reports and certificates but as generic quality documentation; may weakly relate to turnover dossiers but lacks technical anchors.,"Quality records for the project consists of documents witnessing the effectiveness of the quality system (e.g. review protocols) and documents prepared for reaching the defined quality of the products, i.e. inspection results and test reports.
Test reports showing detected defects shall be provided with charts or drawings showing the exact locations and magnitudes of the defects. Complete information about the corrective measures must be provided to the Owner.
On completion of the manufacture or work and completion of inspections and tests the Contractor shall provide to the Owner and include with the Plant, equipment, or completed work a Certificate of Conformity/Compliance or a Quality Release Note."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_349,181,17.5._Third_party_certification,"17, 5",content,text,17.5._Third_party_certification,Quality Management,Third Party Certification,Equipment Certification,,,"manufacturers' certification, pressure vessels certification, lifting equipment certification, slings certification, design materials and construction certification, fabrication at manufacturer's works, site tests, legal and insurance compliance",False,Third-party certification for equipment (pressure vessels/lifting) is generic; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,medium,False,"Contains specific certified equipment categories (pressure vessels, lifting equipment/slings) and certification scope (design/materials/construction; factory and site tests) that can help retrieve existing certification/inspection/test documentation in the MDL.","The Contractor shall arrange for manufacturers' certification of the design, materials and construction of the equipment in his scope to support Owner to comply with legal requirements and for insurance purposes. The certification shall include all pressure vessels and lifting equipment, including slings, and shall cover both fabrication at the manufacturer's works and tests at the Site."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_350,181,17.6._Workshop_Tests_/_Inspections,"17, 6",content,"text, list_item",17.6._Workshop_Tests_/_Inspections,Workshop Tests and Inspections,Works Inspection,Workshop Tests,,,"works inspection, workshop tests, safety conditions per specifications, applied standards and regulations compliance, workmanship execution, detailed design conformity, intermediate and final certificates, test schedule, test certificates submission, 15 days notice for inspection/testing",False,Generic works inspection/workshop tests requirements; no mention of ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or related components.,True,,medium,False,Contains technical QA/QC requirement anchors for factory/workshop inspection and test certification tied to design/specifications and test schedules.,"The equipment to be supplied under the Contract shall be subject to works inspection and workshop tests.
The inspection and tests shall prove whether the equipment fulfils the requirements of the Contract in respect to:
- safety conditions according to respective specifications
- considerations of the applied standards and regulations
- execution of workmanship
- conformity with the present state of technology
- conformity with the requirements of the detailed design.
Whenever inspection or tests are carried out in the manufacturer's workshop, all intermediate and final certificates, in accordance with the agreed upon test schedule shall be made available to the Owner's Representative for the inspection. The latest versions of the related drawing and/or documents indicating the state of approval by the Owner's Representative shall be made available to the Owner's Representative.
All test certificates in respect of the final works inspection or workshop tests shall be submitted to the Owner's Representative.
At least fifteen (15) days' notice shall be given in writing to the Owner's Representative of the dates upon which the inspection or testing shall be carried out.
The approval by the Owner's Representative of any such inspection or test will not prejudice the right of the Owner's Representative to reject the Plant in accordance with the provisions of the main body of this Agreement."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_351,182,18.1._General_requirements,"18, 1",content,text,18.1._General_requirements,Documentation and Submittals,Document Submittal Schedule,Plant/Equipment Identification,,,"document submittal schedule, Exhibit B Part 1 / Part 2, plant-related calculations drawings schedules, KKS equipment numbering scheme, plant/equipment identification schedule, flow sheet or diagram identification numbers, Project Implementation Schedule, 21 days review period, 14 days objective review period",False,General document submittal schedule and plant/equipment identification (KKS); no ACC-specific signal.,True,,medium,False,"Provides retrieval signals around document control, KKS-based tagging/identification schedules, and review timelines that can map to existing MDL document control/tagging deliverables.","Contractor shall submit to the Owner, for the Owner's review, a detailed document submittal schedule listing all documents the Contractor will produce in line with the requirements specified in Exhibit B, Part 1 and this Exhibit B, Part 2 of the Agreement.
The Contractor shall be responsible for submitting all Plantrelated information, documentation, calculations, drawings, and schedules to the Owner on a schedule consistent with the Project Implementation Schedule. The documents shall employ an equipment numbering scheme based on the KKS, or an equivalent system. The Contractor shall prepare a comprehensive plant or equipment identification schedule using the above mentioned numbering system. The schedule shall include this numbering system and the respective flow sheet or diagram identification numbers.
The Contractor shall be responsible for submitting all documents in accordance with a program to be prepared by it for review by the Owner. This schedule shall provide enough time for the Owner to check, assess and comment upon the Documents within a period of twenty-one (21) days from initial submittal, unless otherwise specified in this Contract.
The Owner shall use its reasonable efforts to complete such activities in a period of less than twenty-one (21) days, with fourteen (14) days as the objective under normal circumstances.
The quality of all documents submitted by the Contractor shall conform to acceptable international practice and allow expeditious review by the Owner."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_352,"182, 183",18.1.1._Documentation_Review_Procedure,"18, 1, 1",list,"text, list_item",18.1._General_requirements > 18.1.1._Documentation_Review_Procedure,Documentation and Submittals,Documentation Review Procedure,Owner Review and Resubmittal Cycle,,,"contractor certification of documents, Document Review Comments Sheet, deemed approval if no response, 14 days resubmittal after comments, 14 days review of resubmitted documents, general arrangement and layout drawings review, key diagrams review, interconnections compatibility review, drawings reviewed before workshop tests, drawings reviewed before erection work",False,Generic documentation review procedure and drawing review timing; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser reference.,True,,medium,False,"Contains concrete technical-document review gates tied to GA/layout/key diagrams and prerequisite for workshop testing/erection, useful for matching to MDL drawings and review procedures.","When submitting Documents for review, including any prepared by a Sub-Contractor, the Contractor shall certify in each case that it has examined such Documents and that they comply with the requirements of the Contract.
Except as otherwise provided in the Contract, within the relevant period stated above, the Owner shall either:
a) return to the Contractor one copy of any Documents submitted to the Owner with no comments or queries endorsed thereon;
b) shall return one such copy with any comments or queries which it may have endorsed thereon; or c) a Document Review Comments Sheet (into an agreed format) shall be issued to facilitate rapid transfer of such comments or queries.
If the Owner fails to take any such action within the said period, then the said documents shall be deemed to have been returned to the Contractor with no comments or queries endorsed thereon.
The Contractor shall, within fourteen (14) days of receipt of any comments or queries on any document amend such document in order to implement and accommodate the Owner's comments or queries and resubmit such Documents for review as above, except that the period for review of resubmitted documents by the Owner shall be fourteen (14) days.
Review of a document by the Owner will be carried out according to the criteria set out in the Agreement, and will imply that:
- general arrangement and layout drawings and key diagrams have been examined and appear to be in accordance with the basic design concept of the Plant and meet the requirements of this specification
- other drawings of plant and equipment have been examined in relation to compatibility of the items and equipment with such specifications and in respect of interconnections with other items, equipment or systems
Any review by the Owner shall not relieve the Contractor of its obligations under the Contract.
Before submittal of any documents for the Plant, the Contractor shall submit to the Owner for the Owner's review a detailed document submittal schedule comprising all documents the Contractor will produce. Based on this list the Owner will decide which documents will have to be submitted for review, information or any other purpose. The document submittal schedule shall be maintained and updated during the course of the Works.
The Contractor shall ensure that documents for review are forwarded to the Owner in sufficient time to allow adequate time for examination of such documents. The Contractor shall also ensure that documents are submitted early enough prior to the date required for review to permit amendments to be made and the documents re-submitted for review without delaying the Project Implementation Schedule.
Drawings must be reviewed by the Owner before any equipment is workshop tested or before erection work is started at site."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_353,"184, 185",18.1.2._Drawings,"18, 1, 2",table,"text, list_item",18.1._General_requirements > 18.1.2._Drawings,Documentation and Submittals,Drawings,Drawing Standards and Electronic Submissions,,,"general arrangement and layout drawings, key diagrams, performance curves, data sheets, test reports, Quality Assurance documents, A0 (840 mm x 1190 mm) maximum, AutoCAD compatible CAD / PDF, as-built documentation black lines on white background, English language, SI units, electronic project file system for design review, searchable O&M manuals, MS Project Word Excel AutoCAD MicroStation native formats, CD-ROM submission, document identification code, plant identification system for component identification",False,General drawing standards/electronic submissions requirements; no ACC-related terms or equipment.,True,,high,False,"Strong retrieval anchors for drawing types (GA/layout/key diagrams), as-built requirements, CAD formats, and O&M manual searchable electronic system—useful to match existing MDL drawing/document standards and deliverables.","All drawings, including diagrams, schedules, performance curves, data sheets, test reports, Quality Assurance documents etc. submitted to the Owner shall comply with the requirements of the Agreement.
The drawings shall indicate that:
- general arrangement and layout drawings and key diagrams have been examined and appear to be in accordance with the basic design concept of the Plant and meet the requirements of the specifications set forth in this Minimum Functional Specification
- other drawings of plant and equipment have been examined in relation to compatibility of the items and equipment with such specifications and in respect of interconnections with other items, equipment or systems
- drawings shall be to scale and fully detailed with the maximum size of A0 (840 mm x 1190 mm). Major dimensions shall be given and the material of which each part is to be constructed shall be indicated
- drawings and as built documentation shall be black lines on a white background with revisions clearly marked and shall be prepared in AutoCAD compatible CAD system or pdf-format
- drawings shall bear the approved project references and title block drawings shall be prepared in the English language and with S.I. units
The Contractor shall comply with the following general requirements for electronic submissions:
- for design review purposes the contractor shall set up a dedicated electronic project file system which can be used as the delivery link for the documents to be reviewed for design review purposes and place of the latest and most up-dated project design documents and files where for both parties project members and the owner's engineer has a limited access to see and get those project documents as necessary
- contractor shall propose, apply and provide a system for a suitable 'intelligent' electronic system (searchable) for the O&M manuals and files
- all documents referred in this section (created using MS Project/ Word/ Excel, AutoCAD, Micro Station) shall be submitted also in CD-rom in native file format.
- the delivered documents, especially pictures and drawings, shall be in such high quality condition that a necessary amount of copies of good quality can be made of them
- all project documents will be easily traceable (e.g. by adding a cover sheet) to the subject involved and shall contain the following minimum information (final version to be agreed between the parties) :
- the name of the contractor
- the name of the project
- identification code of the document
- identification number or letter of the modification
- type or the name of the document (unit drawing, operation instruction etc.)
- when performing changes new versions of the documents will be issued and each change in the document will be clearly marked and identified
- in the drawing sizes the relevant standards shall be observed
- plant identification system shall be used for component identification.
- in addition to this coding the drawings shall include a short characteristic name/description of the content"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_354,185,18.2._Design_documentation,"18, 2",content,"text, table",18.2._Design_documentation,Documentation and Submittals,Design Documentation,Design Phase Calculations and Studies,,,"design data, design criteria, performance calculations, correction factor data sheet (correction curves), piping stress and elasticity calculations (main/essential systems), pressure loss calculations, electrical studies, short-circuit calculations, voltage drop calculations, equipment sizing calculations basis/details, electrical load summary (switchboard wise), control valve calculations, measuring orifice calculations, data sheets",False,"Generic design documentation list (calculations, electrical studies, piping stress, etc.); no ACC mention.",True,,high,False,"Explicit list of engineering calculations/studies and datasheets required during design phase; strong signals for retrieving corresponding existing MDL calculations, studies, and datasheet documents.","The Contractor shall submit documentation for the Owner's review taking into consideration logical phasing of design based on Good Industry Practice.
The Contractor shall submit to the Owner all such Documents as may be necessary for the Owner to verify that the equipment and materials to be included in the Plant complies in every respect with the requirements of the Contract.
Contractor shall submit the following information to the Owner, as a minimum, during the design phase of the Project Implementation Schedule:
1., Document = Documents to be submitted to Government Authorities. 1., Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A. 2., Document = Data and calculations:. 2., Owner/ Owner's Engineer = . , Document = ● design data, design criteria, performance calculations. , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A. , Document = ● correction factor data sheet (correction curves) plus those relevant to plant operation. , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A-. , Document = ● stress and elasticity calculations of the piping system (for main/essential systems only). , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A. , Document = ● pressure loss calculations. , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A. , Document = ● electrical studies, short-circuit and voltage drop calculations. , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A. , Document = ● equipment sizing calculations - basis / details. , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A. , Document = ● electrical load summary (switchboard wise). , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A. , Document = ● control valves and measuring orifice calculations. , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A. , Document = ● data sheets. , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A/I"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_358,189,18.2._Design_documentation,"18, 2",table,"table, text",18.2._Design_documentation,Design Documentation,,,,,"quality assurance manuals, list of Codes and Standards, lubricant list, testing procedures (factory and site tests), commissioning schedule and procedures, training manuals, MFS compliance review",False,Generic QA manuals/codes/lubricant list/testing procedures/commissioning schedule; no ACC signal.,True,,medium,True,"Mostly a submittal/review matrix; contains some technical retrieval anchors (codes & standards list, lubricant list, factory/site testing procedures, commissioning procedures) but limited system/equipment specificity.",", Document = diagrams ● quality assurance manuals (including quality. , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A. , Document = ● list of Codes and Standards. , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A. , Document = ● lubricant list. , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = I. , Document = ● testing procedures for factory and site tests. , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A. , Document = Commissioning schedule &procedures. , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A. , Document = 10. Training manuals. , Owner/ Owner's Engineer = A
A = submitted for review of compliance with MFS
I = submitted for information"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_359,189,18.3._Training_documentation,"18, 3",content,"text, list_item",18.3._Training_documentation,Training Documentation,,,,,"detailed training schedule, training course dates and places, training material",False,Training documentation requirements only; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.,False,Administrative training submittal requirements (schedule/material) with no technical system/equipment requirements or engineering deliverable anchors for MDL matching.,low,True,Training documentation is generally not an engineering MDL retrieval target unless tied to specific systems/equipment; this chunk is generic.,"The Contractor shall submit to the Owner for review documentation concerning training including items such as:
- Detailed training schedule with the final dates and places of training courses (for Owner's approval)
- Training material for each of the training courses"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_360,189,18.4._Quality_Assurance_documents,"18, 4",content,"text, list_item",18.4._Quality_Assurance_documents,Quality Assurance,,,,,"ISO 9001, Quality Assurance Programme, Quality Control Programmes and Plans, HSE plan, manufacturing Record Book, factory and on-site test results, test certifications, declarations of Conformity",False,ISO 9001 QA documentation and test certifications in general; no ACC-specific reference.,False,Primarily QA/QC and HSE administrative documentation requirements; lacks technical design/performance requirements to retrieve engineering MDL documents.,low,True,Contains standards/admin QA deliverables; may be useful for QA document control but weak for technical MDL retrieval.,"Quality assurance documents of Contractor and sub-suppliers shall be according to the relevant ISO 9001 standard and Contractor's QA instructions. Documentation to be handed over to the Owner for review covers items such as:
- project Quality Assurance Programme
- quality Control Programmes and Plans
- health & Safety and Environmental (HSE) plan (refer to Attachment 5:'HSE documents to be prepared by Contractor')
- manufacturing Record Book
- factory and on-site test results and certifications of the Plant and Goods
- declarations of Conformity"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_361,190,18.5._Documents_for_commissioning_and_testing_and_Contractor's_performance_Tests_and_SEC_acceptance_tests,"18, 5",content,text,18.5._Documents_for_commissioning_and_testing_and_Contractor's_performance_Tests_and_SEC_acceptance_tests,Commissioning and Testing,Performance Tests and Acceptance Tests,,,,"program for Commissioning and Testing, Contractor Performance Tests, Acceptance Tests, Exhibit I-1 of the Agreement",False,Program for commissioning/testing and acceptance tests in general; no ACC mention.,True,,medium,True,"Technical lifecycle scope (commissioning/testing, performance/acceptance tests) is explicit, but details and system specificity are not provided in this chunk.",The Contractor shall submit to the Owner for review the program for Commissioning and Testing including also the Contractor Performance Tests and Acceptance Tests as per the Exhibit I-1 of the Agreement.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_362,190,18.6.1._General_Requirements,"18, 6, 1",content,"text, list_item",18.5._Documents_for_commissioning_and_testing_and_Contractor's_performance_Tests_and_SEC_acceptance_tests > 18.6.1._General_Requirements,Operation and Maintenance,O&M Manuals,Power Plant (all systems and equipment),,,"O&M manuals, safe secure economical operation, operation instructions, maintenance instructions, equipment lists, settings, drawings and diagrams, spare parts, special tools",False,O&M manuals for whole plant (generic); no explicit ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or related equipment referenced.,True,,high,False,Clear requirement for O&M manuals covering the whole plant and listing specific content elements that can map to existing O&M/manual-related MDL documents.,"The Contractor shall provide the O&M manuals for review including all information necessary for the safe, secure and economical operation and maintenance of the Power Plant.
The content of O&M documents shall be in compliance with the relevant practice and industrial requirements and subject to Owner's approval.
The operational and maintenance documentation shall cover the whole Plant and all systems and equipment one by one within the scope of Work.
The manuals shall include items such as :
- operation instructions
- maintenance instructions
- equipment lists, settings, etc
- drawings, diagrams , etc
- spare parts, special tools"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_363,"190, 191",18.6.2._Operation_Manuals,"18, 6, 2",content,"text, list_item",18.5._Documents_for_commissioning_and_testing_and_Contractor's_performance_Tests_and_SEC_acceptance_tests > 18.6.2._Operation_Manuals,Documents for commissioning and testing and Contractor's performance Tests and SEC acceptance tests,Operation Manuals,Operational instructions content,,,"preparations for start-up, start-up, operation in power and heat generation, shut-down, disturbance situations, standstill and preparation for maintenance, preservation, operational limits for measured values, automation/instrumentation part, water and steam part, steam/water chemistry, electrical part",False,Generic operation manual content; no mention of ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or ACC components.,True,,medium,False,Defines required scope/content for operation instructions across operating situations and system parts; useful to retrieve existing O&M/operating instruction documents.,"The operation instructions shall include operational situation such as:
- preparations for start-up
- start-up
- operation in power and heat generation
- shut-down
- disturbance situations
- standstill and preparation for maintenance
- preservation
As far as applicable, the following items may also be included in the operational instructions:
- technical structure and functioning of the system in question
- theoretical background of functioning of the system concerned
- electrical part of the system/equipment
- automation/instrumentation part of the system/equipment
- water and steam part system/equipment
- steam/water chemistry
- material questions
- operational limits for measured values
- modes of operation to be avoided, if any, and the reason for restrictions
- precautions to be taken for normal and abnormal operating conditions
The operational instructions may include photographs, videos, drawings, pictures, functional diagrams and other material necessary for understanding and visualization of the instructions.
The operational instructions shall be divided according to the above operational situations of each individual system so that the above items are included in each operational situation."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_364,"191, 192",18.6.3._Maintenance_Manuals,"18, 6, 3",content,"text, list_item",18.5._Documents_for_commissioning_and_testing_and_Contractor's_performance_Tests_and_SEC_acceptance_tests > 18.6.3._Maintenance_Manuals,Documents for commissioning and testing and Contractor's performance Tests and SEC acceptance tests,Maintenance Manuals,Maintenance documentation and maintenance management data,,,"equipment register, preventive maintenance programs, annual maintenance programs, inspection system, test programs, spare parts and materials (lubricants), special tools, list of drawings, assembly drawings (parts list, dimensions, materials), manufacturing drawings (fragile and wearing parts), site erection protocols (tolerance limits), spare part lists and drawings, maintenance management system equipment records (data sheet), identification code, mechanical/electrical/instrumentation maintenance, safety and isolation instructions",False,Generic maintenance manuals/equipment records requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,True,,high,False,"Contains explicit technical documentation requirements for maintenance manuals, spare parts, equipment records/datasheets, and discipline-specific maintenance data—strong anchors for MDL retrieval.","The target of the maintenance documentation is to ensure that the Owner will be able to execute the maintenance of all equipment and systems, including tasks such as:
- design of equipment register
- design of preventive maintenance programs
- design of annual maintenance programs
- design of inspection system
- design of test programs
- determining the numbers of spare parts and materials, e.g. lubricants
- acquisition of special tools; dimensioning of the quantity of labour
The maintenance documentation to be supplied by the Contractor shall include items such as:
- list of drawings (summary list on drawings separately on each object)
- assembly drawings including a list of parts with data on dimensions and materials
- manufacturing drawings on fragile and wearing parts including data on dimensions and materials
- drawings, measurement and inspection charts, Site erection protocols, (including data on erection dimensions with tolerance limits), and installation instructions and documents needed for complete dismounting, control and mounting work.
- spare part lists and drawings
- data on maintenance is required separately for mechanical, electrical and instrumentation maintenance.
- all maintenance documents shall be delivered also in electrical form to the extent available
The Contractor shall provide in electronic form to be agreed equipment records for the Plant's maintenance management system and spare parts documentation as follows:
- the equipment record (data sheet) with exact identification code shall contain complete technical data on each process equipment (valve, pump, motor etc.) with reference of proper document and drawing numbers
- spare part lists, spare parts recommendation with technical data filled out is required for each equipment.
- maintenance instructions and schedule for preventive and periodic maintenance
- safety and isolation instructions
- templates and instructions for data input will be provided later by O&M Contractor"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_365,192,18.7._As-Built_Documentation,"18, 7",content,"text, list_item",18.7._As-Built_Documentation,As-Built Documentation,As-built contractor documents,Final installed locations/settings and execution records,,,"as-built locations, as-built sizes and details, design drawings with final process and equipment information, equipment/devices/signals final settings, certificates, manufacturer's manuals, erection dimension protocols, test protocols, QC documents",False,As-built documentation requirements are generic; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser referenced.,True,,high,False,"Specifies as-built documentation scope including final process/equipment info, settings, and test/QC protocols—useful for matching to as-built drawings and records in MDL.","The Contractor shall prepare, and keep up-to-date, and submit to the Owner for review a complete set of ""As-Built"" Contractor's Documents of the execution of the Work, showing the exact As-Built locations, sizes and details of the work as executed including documents such as :
- design drawings with final process and equipment information
- equipment, devices, signals, etc. with final settings
- certificates, manufacturer's manuals, erection dimension protocols, test protocols, QC documents"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_366,192,18.8._Required_documentation_for_proposal,"18, 8",content,text,18.8._Required_documentation_for_proposal,Required documentation for proposal,,,,,,False,Proposal documentation intro only; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.,False,Introductory administrative sentence referencing Exhibit B; no specific technical requirements or document types listed in this chunk.,low,True,Too generic to anchor retrieval; likely needs adjacent chunks for the actual list of required technical submittals.,"In addition to the requirements specified in Exhibit B, Part 1, the Contractor shall submit the following initial technical information for the Proposal."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_367,193,18.8.1._Cogeneration_plant_concept,"18, 8, 1",content,"text, list_item",18.8._Required_documentation_for_proposal > 18.8.1._Cogeneration_plant_concept,Required documentation for proposal,Cogeneration plant concept,Proposal technical information (plant concept),,,"cogeneration plant concept, complete data sheets (appendix within agreement), plot plan use (laydown areas), construction approach, phases of construction, commissioning and operation by phases, operational philosophy (power units, steam generation units, desalination units), start-up/shutdown/failure of individual units, maintenance philosophy, major plant overhauls, training, standards codes and regulations list, testing of expansion plant equipment",False,Cogeneration plant concept/proposal info is general; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mentioned.,True,,medium,False,"Lists concrete technical proposal submittal topics (data sheets, operational/maintenance philosophy, plot use, construction phasing, standards/codes) that can map to existing concept/design basis documents in the MDL.","The Contractor shall submit, as part of its Proposal, the following technical information:
Complete data sheets to be provided to the Owner as a separate Appendix within the Agreement.
Description of main features of the proposed plant such as:
- general description and main features of the plant and use of the plot including laydown areas
- detailed time schedule
- construction approach, phases of construction, commissioning and operation of plants constructed at the various phases
- operational philosophy of power units, steam generation units and desalination units (start-up, shutdown and failure of individual units)
- maintenance philosophy, maintenance schedule activities including major plant overhauls
- details of operation, maintenance and training
- standards, codes and regulations: listing or major standards, codes and regulations (international, supplier's own country) which will be applied for the design and construction of plant and equipment and facilities, for civil works and for testing of expansion plant equipment"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_368,"193, 194",18.8.1._Cogeneration_plant_concept,,content,"text, list_item",18.8._Required_documentation_for_proposal > 18.8.1._Cogeneration_plant_concept,Required Documentation for Proposal,Cogeneration Plant Concept,Plant systems and diagrams,,,"reserve boilers, steam turbine generator plant, water treatment plant, ESF and HV grid connections (protection, metering, control interface), instrumentation and control systems, Central Control Room (CCR), P&ID for main systems, single line diagrams (main and auxiliary electrical systems), protection diagrams, heat balance diagrams (reference site conditions), start-up and loading diagrams (cold/warm/hot), generator performance charts",False,"Lists general plant systems/diagrams (boilers, STG, water treatment, electrical, P&IDs) but no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.",True,,high,False,"Contains explicit technical systems and required engineering diagrams/curves (P&IDs, SLDs, protection, heat balance, performance/degradation curves) that can be used to retrieve matching MDL documents.","plant:
- main mechanical equipment and systems including auxiliary systems, such as: reserve boilers; steam turbine generator plant; water treatment plant, etc.
- electrical equipment and systems such as: generators; generator protection; electrical power system philosophy; main electrical equipment including auxiliary systems, etc.
- ESF and HV grid connections (protection, metering and control interface);
- instrumentation and control systems, such as: general control system philosophy; description of central control room; local control rooms; facility control system and control of common plant and auxiliary systems, etc
- emission control measures
- civil works, including site development buildings, seawater piping and discharge water systems
- plant drawings and diagrams
- plant location map with areas temporarily used during construction
- site plan, general layout and arrangement drawings for on- and off-site facilities indication of fenced areas
- equipment configuration in the area of the power plant, ccr, water treatment plant, etc.
- PID for main systems;
- single line diagrams for
- -facility main electrical systems
- -facility electrical auxiliary systems
- protection diagrams
- heat balance diagrams for the plant at the reference site conditions (guarantee)
- heat balance diagrams for the plant for the following operating points shall be provided but not limited to
- preliminary correction curves for part loads and deviating ambient conditions
- start-up and loading diagrams (from cold, warm and hot conditions) of units and plant
- operation curves of the units
- degradation curves of the units
- generator performance charts"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_369,194,18.8.1._Cogeneration_plant_concept,,content,"text, list_item",18.8._Required_documentation_for_proposal > 18.8.1._Cogeneration_plant_concept,Required Documentation for Proposal,Cogeneration Plant Concept,Environmental performance and discharges,,,"guaranteed air pollution emission rates, emission control measures, emission monitoring measures, wastewater quantities, wastewater treatment, wastewater disposal methods, ambient noise levels",False,Environmental performance/discharges requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser referenced.,True,,high,False,"Provides clear technical/environmental requirement anchors (emissions, wastewater treatment/disposal, noise) useful for retrieving environmental and performance-related MDL documents.","The Contractor shall submit, as part of its Proposal, the description and details of the following:
- guaranteed air pollution emission rates and type of emission control and monitoring measures
- nature and estimated quantities of waste water, waste water treatment and disposal methods
- ambient noise levels"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_370,194,18.8.1._Cogeneration_plant_concept,,content,text,18.8._Required_documentation_for_proposal > 18.8.1._Cogeneration_plant_concept,,,,,,,False,Subcontractors/suppliers schedule requirement is generic; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,False,Administrative/commercial proposal requirement (subcontractor/supplier details and country of origin) with no technical design/performance scope.,low,True,Does not contain technical requirements or engineering deliverable anchors suitable for MDL retrieval.,"The Contractor shall submit, as part of its Proposal, details and references of the nominated and/or proposed sub-contractors and suppliers/manufacturers (where applicable).
The Contractor shall submit a schedule of all major proposed subcontractors and suppliers detailing the type of work or services to be subcontracted and the country of origin."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_371,194,18.8.1._Cogeneration_plant_concept,,content,text,18.8._Required_documentation_for_proposal > 18.8.1._Cogeneration_plant_concept,,,,,,,False,Generic deviations/exceptions statement; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.,False,Contractual/administrative clause about declaring deviations; no technical retrieval anchors.,low,True,Purely administrative statement; not useful for matching to engineering MDL documents.,"The Contractor shall declare deviations/exceptions from this Exhibit B, Part 2, any undeclared deviations shall be deemed to be accepted by the Contractor."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_372,195,19.1._Introduction,"19, 1",content,text,19.1._Introduction,,,,,,,False,Generic contractor management responsibility; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mentioned.,False,General management responsibility statement; no technical scope or deliverable anchors.,low,True,Administrative introduction text without technical requirements; not suitable for MDL retrieval.,The Contractor shall be responsible for the management and control of all phases of his work and that of his sub-contractors and suppliers.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_373,195,19.2._Project_Schedule,"19, 2",content,text,19.2._Project_Schedule,,,,,,,False,Generic contract programme/schedule statement; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention.,False,Administrative schedule definition (contract programme key dates) with no technical design/performance requirements or engineering deliverables to retrieve.,low,True,Chunk is purely contractual scheduling language; weak/no MDL retrieval anchors.,The Contract Programme shall be the overall schedule of key dates agreed in Exhibit A of the Agreement.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_374,195,19.2.1._Project_schedule,"19, 2, 1",content,"text, list_item",19.2._Project_Schedule > 19.2.1._Project_schedule,,,,,,,False,"Project schedule key dates (milestones, submissions, site access, commissioning) with no ACC-specific signal.",False,"Project schedule/key dates list (payment milestones, submissions, access, services availability, testing/commissioning dates) without technical requirements or specific engineering document targets.",low,True,"Contains some technical nouns (water, fuel gas, electricity, testing/commissioning) but only in the context of schedule milestones; insufficient for MDL retrieval.","The Contract Programme shall be the overall schedule of key dates agreed prior to award of the Contract, and included in EPC Agreement. The schedule which shall not be altered except as otherwise agreed by Owner shall be broken down into major elements and shall clearly show the key dates relating to:
- payment milestones
- engineering submissions and reviews
- access to the site area for preliminary activities
- access to the site for construction activities
- availability of services (water, fuel gas and fuel oil, electricity)
- exchange of information with other parties concerning terminal points
- completion of all work relating to terminal points
- start of testing and commissioning
- completion tests
- take over of each unit"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_375,"195, 196",19.3.1._Levels_required_are:_Planning_Network_Programmes,"19, 3, 1",content,text,19.2._Project_Schedule > 19.3.1._Levels_required_are:_Planning_Network_Programmes,,,,,,,False,Planning network/bar chart requirements; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser terms or equipment context.,False,"Planning methodology requirements (critical path network, bar chart, tiered planning system) are administrative/project controls, not technical engineering scope.",low,True,No equipment/system technical requirements or explicit engineering deliverables; mainly project controls language.,"The Contractor shall prepare a detailed critical path network and bar chart that will fully describe the work to be carried out. The Contractor will use his standard systems for planning subject to their meeting the requirements stated herein.
The critical path network shall include all principal activities and milestones. The bar chart shall be based on the same information as the network and shall be capable of presentation in both monthly and weekly increments as may be required by the Owner.
The Agreement shall be monitored and controlled using the planning system, which shall be tiered to enable presentation at various levels of detail, as may be required for specific purposes during the Agreement, and whereby all activities can be uniquely identified and related to activities at other levels."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_376,196,19.3.1._Levels_required_are:_Planning_Network_Programmes,,content,text,19.2._Project_Schedule > 19.3.1._Levels_required_are:_Planning_Network_Programmes,,,,,,,False,Overall programme milestones description only; no ACC-related content.,False,Single-line programme description focused on milestones/payment milestones; no technical retrieval anchors.,low,True,Too generic and schedule-focused to match to MDL engineering documents.,"Overall programme showing the project milestones, payment milestones and containing major key dates and activities"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_377,196,19.3.1._Levels_required_are:_Planning_Network_Programmes,,content,text,19.2._Project_Schedule > 19.3.1._Levels_required_are:_Planning_Network_Programmes,,,,,,,False,Generic construction interfaces/tie-ins scheduling requirement; no ACC mention.,False,"Planning/network programme requirement about construction interfaces and tie-ins, but still project controls without technical design criteria or document targets.",low,True,Mentions interfaces/tie-ins but lacks system/equipment context and any technical requirements; primarily scheduling/planning.,A more detailed network programme including construction interfaces clearly identified as individual activities with both documentation and physical tie-ins
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_378,196,19.3.1._Levels_required_are:_Planning_Network_Programmes,,content,text,19.2._Project_Schedule > 19.3.1._Levels_required_are:_Planning_Network_Programmes,,,,,,,False,Project Master Schedule and subcontractor scheduling; no ACC-related signal.,False,"Project scheduling/programming administrative requirements (master schedule, look-ahead programmes) with no technical design/equipment requirements or engineering deliverables to retrieve from MDL.",low,True,Content is planning/programme management rather than technical scope tied to engineering documents.,Project Master Schedule. The Contractor's detail level of programming with a record for each activity. Each major sub-contractor shall maintain and update his own schedule and pass all updated information to the Contractor who shall keep an overall programme on which the 90 day look ahead programmes are based
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_379,196,19.3.1._Levels_required_are:_Planning_Network_Programmes,,content,text,19.2._Project_Schedule > 19.3.1._Levels_required_are:_Planning_Network_Programmes,,,,,,,False,Contractor working schedules for internal purpose; no ACC context.,False,Internal contractor working schedules statement; no technical requirements or MDL-retrievable engineering content.,low,True,Administrative scheduling text only.,"Contractor's working schedules produced and maintained by the Contractor, for internal purpose, if available, and;"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_380,196,19.3.1._Levels_required_are:_Planning_Network_Programmes,,content,text,19.2._Project_Schedule > 19.3.1._Levels_required_are:_Planning_Network_Programmes,,,,,,,False,Work definition/progress measurement level statement; no ACC mention.,False,Progress measurement/work definition level for internal purposes; no technical discipline/equipment requirements or engineering deliverables.,low,True,Project controls content not useful for matching to engineering MDL documents.,"Contractor's and sub-contractor's work definition and progress measurement level for internal purpose, if available."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_381,196,19.3.1._Levels_required_are:_Planning_Network_Programmes,,content,text,19.2._Project_Schedule > 19.3.1._Levels_required_are:_Planning_Network_Programmes,,,,,,,False,Issuance timing for Level 3 programme; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,False,"Programme submission timing (Level 1/2/3 programme within 60 days of NTP) is project controls/admin, not technical engineering scope.",low,True,Schedule deliverable timing does not map to engineering MDL technical documents.,The complete Level 3 programme together with updated versions of the Level 1 and Level 2 programmes shall be issued to the Owner within 60 days of the Notice to Proceed.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_382,"196, 197",19.3.2._Progress_Measurement,"19, 3, 2",content,text,19.2._Project_Schedule > 19.3.2._Progress_Measurement,,,,,,,False,Progress measurement system requirement; no ACC-related equipment or scope referenced.,False,Defines progress measurement system based on physical progress (not cost); project controls requirement without technical design/equipment anchors.,low,True,Project controls methodology; unlikely to retrieve engineering MDL documents.,"The Contractor shall develop and maintain a formalized progress measurement system based on physical progress (not cost).
It shall be the Contractor's responsibility to measure his own progress in line with and at the levels of detail defined within this Exhibit B, Part 2 and elsewhere in the Agreement."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_383,"197, 198",19.3.3._Progress_Reporting,"19, 3, 3",content,"text, list_item",19.2._Project_Schedule > 19.3.3._Progress_Reporting,,,,,,,False,Monthly progress reporting requirements only; no mention of ACC/Air Cooled Condenser or related equipment.,False,"Administrative project management requirement for monthly progress reporting (safety stats, schedules, correspondence, procurement status), not technical design/engineering requirements or deliverables for MDL matching.",low,True,Contains reporting format and management controls; mentions equipment/procurement only in status-reporting context without technical requirements.,"The Contractor shall submit a detailed progress report for each month according to the Agreement. The format of the monthly report shall contain but not be limited to:
- covering letter and executive summary
- an update of all safety statistics including a statement of injury or death of personnel engaged in work on site and the number of accidents or other incidents resulting in damage to equipment or plant, or incidents that represent a near miss to these events, together with details of such accidents and incidents, and statements of corrective actions
- document index marked up to show document status
- a statement of the number of site personnel engaged in the work during the reporting period including an update of manning histograms and details of erection equipment in use or held in readiness
- a listing of late activities compared to the respective originally planned (early finish) dates
- explanations for late activities and other problem areas which are having, or are likely to have, impact on the project schedule
- details of measures proposed to resolve problem areas and bring the overall project progress back on schedule or further improve on current progress
- outstanding interface data and measures proposed to expedite the issue of critical interface data
- demonstration of the achievement of all milestones
- purchasing schedule associated with major or significant items of equipment marked up to show status of procurement activities
- copies of those inspection reports which identify any deviations from the quality standards in the epc contract and a statement of corrective actions copies of quality assurance audit reports which call for corrective actions and evidence of the implementation of corrective actions
- colour photographs showing the progress of construction;
- update of progress 's' curves
- correspondence schedule listing all correspondence between contractor and owner and showing outstanding actions required
- a statement of progress made in the preparation of all documentation to be handed to the owner at milestone dates or substantial completion
- shipping documentation
- the level 3 'project master schedule' shall be marked up on a monthly basis. the contractor shall indicate the progress achieved against each activity and submit this as part of the monthly progress report which shall be issued to the owner one week prior to the monthly progress meeting
- the progress report shall address each of the following project phases, as appropriate:
- -engineering
- -procurement
- -expediting and fabrication
- -construction and erection
- -commissioning and tests on completion"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_384,198,19.3.4._Progress_Meetings,"19, 3, 4",content,text,19.2._Project_Schedule > 19.3.4._Progress_Meetings,,,,,,,False,Progress meetings logistics; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser scope signal.,False,Administrative meeting cadence/location requirements; no technical scope or engineering requirements to retrieve MDL documents.,low,True,"Progress meeting logistics (monthly/weekly, locations) are not useful technical retrieval anchors.","During the engineering and procurement phases, all progress meetings, which shall be held monthly and be chaired by Owner, and any other meetings deemed necessary to resolve technical and commercial problems, shall be held in Owner's offices or Owner's Representative offices, or at another location agreed by Owner. Once the main construction activities commence, weekly site management progress meetings will be held at Site. Additional meetings to resolve technical and commercial issues will continue to be held, as required, during the construction phase."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_385,198,19.3.5._Project_Management_and_Co-ordination_Procedures,"19, 3, 5",content,"text, list_item",19.2._Project_Schedule > 19.3.5._Project_Management_and_Co-ordination_Procedures,,,,,,,False,Project management and coordination procedures; no ACC-related terms or context.,False,Administrative requirement to submit project management/coordination procedures and organization details; not a technical engineering requirement for MDL retrieval.,low,True,"Focuses on management organization/procedures (reporting, planning, communications, administration) rather than technical systems or design criteria.","The Contractor shall, within thirty (30) days after the Notice to Proceed Date, submit to the Owner a comprehensive set of project management and co-ordination procedures that shall be mutually agreed upon, which describe both the Contractor's project management organization and procedures, and co-ordination and communication with the Owner. Based on the Contractor's project instruction manual, the procedures shall cover the items detailed below.
Contractor's project management organization including subcontractors (the status and responsibilities of Contractor staff (only) shall be included ):
- reporting
- planning and work control
- communications
- administration
- other topics needed to describe the project management and co-ordination"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_386,"198, 199",19.3.6._Communications,"19, 3, 6",content,text,19.2._Project_Schedule > 19.3.6._Communications,,,,,,,False,General communications/English language/receipts for handover items; no ACC signal.,False,Administrative communications language and handover receipt requirements; no technical design/performance content for MDL matching.,low,True,"Mentions goods/materials/spares/instruments only in the context of receipt/hand-over administration, without technical specifications.","All correspondence, communications and submissions relating to the Agreement shall be in English.
The Contractor shall have a Site Manager fluent in the English Language for routine administration with the Owner/Owner's Representative at the site.
For all documents, goods, materials, maintenance equipment, spare parts, instruments, etc. to be handed over to the Owner, the Contractor shall obtain a signed receipt by an authorized representative of the Owner that the goods, etc., have been received in a satisfactory condition as required under the Agreement."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_387,199,19.3.7._Diaries_for_Daily_Work_Measurements_for_construction_site,"19, 3, 7",content,text,19.2._Project_Schedule > 19.3.7._Diaries_for_Daily_Work_Measurements_for_construction_site,,,,,,,False,Construction site diaries and recordkeeping; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser mention.,False,Administrative site diary/record-keeping requirement; not technical engineering scope suitable for MDL retrieval.,low,True,"Describes daily records, verification/signature, and ownership of diaries; lacks technical requirements or document deliverables to match against MDL.","The Contractor shall keep suitable diaries in an approved form to which at all times the Owner and /Owner's Representative shall have access. The Contractor shall record all work progress as completed on the day on which the records are made, together with full particulars and details of all obstructions or modified or extra works or incidents, and the number of men employed in each of the several positions of the work in progress.
The diaries shall be available at all times for Owner/Owner's Representative to verify and record, and shall sign all entries therein which it verifies, and have full liberty to make entries therein but no such signature or entry shall imply acceptance of/or agreement with any area of work material used. The diaries shall, when signed by both Parties, be deemed to be a true record of the work executed and shall become the property of Owner."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_388,199,20.1._General,"20, 1",content,text,20.1._General,,,,,,,False,General spare parts requirements up to COD; no ACC-specific spares or ACC mention.,False,"Primarily commercial/administrative spare parts obligations (priced list, replacement, permissions, warranty free-of-charge) without technical design/performance requirements or specific engineering deliverables to retrieve.",low,True,Mentions spares and gas turbine generators but only in the context of commercial operation date/warranty and spare parts administration; weak MDL retrieval anchors.,"The Contractor shall provide all spares necessary for discharging his responsibilities in carrying out the Works, including commissioning and up to Commercial Operation Date. Any spares used during the period up to the end of the Commercial Operation Date shall be promptly replaced. The Contractor shall notify the Owner in advance on using the spares supplied before the commercial operation date and obtain permission from Owner for using them. However, any Spare Parts used by the Contractor to carry out warranty repairs including those for gas turbine generators shall be supplied free of charge and should be promptly replaced if used from Owner's stores.
The Contractor shall provide a priced list of spares which he recommends the Owner should hold. These recommended spares should be classified as follows:"
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_389,200,20.1._General,,content,text,20.1._General,,,,,,,False,Generic definition of normal-operation spares; no ACC-related content.,False,Fragment defining spare parts category for normal operation; no technical requirements or document/deliverable references.,low,True,Too generic and incomplete to anchor retrieval to specific MDL documents.,These are items for which the Contractor anticipates that demands will arise in normal operation of the plant.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_390,200,20.1._General,,content,text,20.1._General,,,,,,,False,Generic definition of overhaul spares; no ACC-related content.,False,Fragment defining spare parts category for minor/major overhauls; no technical requirements or document/deliverable references.,low,True,Insufficient technical specificity for MDL retrieval matching.,These are items for which the Contractor anticipates that demands will arise during the programmed minor and major overhauls.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_392,"201, 202",21.1._General_Requirements,"21, 1",content,"text, list_item",21.1._General_Requirements,Training,Operations & Maintenance (O&M) Training,Commissioning-phase participation and on-the-job training,,,"on-the-job training, O&M personnel (operations and maintenance), two training tracks for operation staff; one for maintenance staff, system walk-downs (groups of 6 or less), commissioning phase participation, remote/local control and monitoring, shift schedule agreed 180 days prior to start-up of commissioning, training documents based on plant instruction manuals, drawings/schematics used in training, English language training",False,General O&M training framework for the plant; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser referenced.,True,,medium,False,"Contains operational/commissioning training requirements and explicit anchors to instruction manuals and drawings/schematics that can help retrieve existing O&M manuals, commissioning documentation, and plant drawings in the MDL.","The details of the training program shall be agreed between the Contractor and Owner's.
Emphasize shall be on 'on-the-job' training and instruction for O&M personnel and building up the training programs (2 separate training tracks for operation staff and 1 for maintenance staff) accommodating O&M staff's mobilisation program.
All programs will be scheduled for a normal 5 day works week, excluding holidays observed by the Owner. Due to the technical content of the programs, instruction will be limited to 8 hours per day.
The Contractor shall advise the Owner of all sub-contractor representatives on site to allow the Owner's personnel to co-ordinate and receive on-the-job-training.
Here below is highlighted the overall framework and philosophy the O&M training should be based on when Contractor starts to plan the detailed training program for the Owner's O&M personnel.
O&M Operator's personnel shall be trained in due time by the Contractor to prepare them both in theory and practice for the management, operation and maintenance of all parts of the Plant.
Each site personnel shall be included in Overviews and Operation training.
English language shall be used for all training purposes.
The aim of the training is to teach the operation and maintenance personnel of the Owner to such an extent that after completion of the training they are able to:
- safely and economically operate the Power Plant with all its auxiliaries without the support of the Contractor
- carry out regular daily and weekly maintenance routines independently and in a correct way
- carry out maintenance and repair works independently and in a correct way
Also 'system walk-downs' with groups of 6 or less students shall be conducted to familiarize the student with the physical location of the equipment and acquaint the student with system an equipment features, controls, and displays. If possible actual operations, routine maintenance and routine checks of all equipment shall be demonstrated.
In addition to the above classroom training, an essential part of the training shall be the participation in the operation and maintenance during the commissioning phase of the Plant. The trainees will obtain real experience by attending the Plant operations under the supervision of the Contractor's commissioning engineer. The personnel will become familiar e.g. with remote/local control and monitoring, equipment operations as well as with the technical characteristics of the Plant. Detailed plan including shift schedule for the same shall be pre-agreed between the Contractor, Owner and Operator 180 days prior to start-up of any commissioning activities in the plant.
Training shall be provided by the Contractor's commissioning team and/or a nominated professional training team. The Contractor shall nominate a training coordinator. The training documents shall be based on the Plant instruction manuals. Drawings / schematics of the Plant shall be extensively used in the training."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_393,202,21.1._General_Requirements,"21, 1",content,text,21.1._General_Requirements,Training,Operations & Maintenance (O&M) Training,Training delivery methods and facilities,,,"classroom lectures, audio/visual training aids, hands-on exercises using supplied equipment, training split: general vs operating staff vs maintenance staff, training programme subject to Owner approval, training delivered in administration building or other suitable place",False,Training delivery methods/facilities; no ACC-related terms or scope.,True,,medium,False,"Defines training approach (classroom + hands-on) and program structure/approval, which can map to existing training plans/materials and O&M documentation referenced in the MDL.","The training shall consist partly of classroom lectures supported by audio and/or visual training aids. However, the practical 'hands on' exercises utilising the supplied equipment will form a major part of the training.
The above training duration shall be considered to be an adequate prerequisite for the Plant personnel to acquaint themselves with the correct operation and maintenance requirements of the installed Plant.
The training courses and programme shall be split between those which are general or dedicated for operating staff or for maintenance staff.
The proposed training programme as described in the following shall be taken into consideration by the Contractor when preparing the training programme. The training program and its content are subject to approval by the Owner.
Training courses may be delivered in the administration building or at any other place suitable for the purpose, provided by the contractor."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_394,202,21.1._General_Requirements,,content,text,21.1._General_Requirements,,,,,,,False,General training qualification requirement; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser signal.,False,Training staff qualification requirement; administrative/HR training content with no technical system/equipment requirements or engineering deliverables to retrieve.,low,True,No discipline-specific technical anchors for matching to MDL engineering documents.,"Trainers shall be suitably qualified, through experience and education, to teach their respective subject matter."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_395,203,21.1._General_Requirements,,content,text,21.1._General_Requirements,,,,,,,False,Training programme review/audit clause; no mention of ACC or related equipment.,False,Owner audit/review of training programme; administrative compliance text without technical requirements.,low,True,No technical scope beyond training programme oversight.,Owner will review and audit the Training Programme and individual performance at any time for Contractor's compliance before accepting the successful completion of any part of the Training Programme.
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_396,203,21.1._General_Requirements,,content,text,21.1._General_Requirements,,,,,,,False,Competency assessment and training records; generic commissioning/training text without ACC reference.,False,Competency assessment timing and training records ownership; administrative training management content.,low,True,Mentions commissioning timing but provides no commissioning technical requirements or system-specific criteria.,"Trainees will undergo competency assessment at two points within the Training Period. Owner will first undertake a competency assessment prior to commencement of commissioning and secondly at the completion of training.
The Contractor shall keep comprehensive records of all the personnel who have been trained in a format agreed by Owner and these records shall become the property of Owner, to be used in the final assessment of staff."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_397,203,21.1._General_Requirements,,content,text,21.1._General_Requirements,,,,,,,False,Training facilities/materials and supplier instruction; no ACC-specific scope or keywords.,False,Training facilities/materials and supplier representatives for instruction; focuses on training logistics and materials rather than technical design/engineering requirements.,low,True,References O&M manuals and major equipment suppliers but does not specify equipment/system scope or technical criteria suitable for MDL retrieval.,"Suitable facilities and training accommodation to support Owner's and Contractor's training shall be provided by the Contractor on site to meet the requirements of the jointly agreed training programme.
The Contractor shall provide for training materials, textbooks, manuals and other materials required for the Contractor's Training Programme. Classroom equipment such as overhead projectors, white/ blackboard, monitor etc. shall be provided by Contractor.
Training aids, such as films, slides, interactive videos, software packages materials and supplies considered necessary by the Contractor to support, maintain and successfully supplement the Contractor's Training Programme shall be furnished by the Contractor for the Training Period.
Training manuals provided by equipment suppliers, for operations and maintenance shall form part of the basic reference material in addition to any other references as required.
The Contractor shall arrange for representatives from selected major equipment suppliers to provide specific instruction concerning the construction, engineering, operation and maintenance of the equipment as specified in the Contractor's technical proposal."
Fadhili_ITB,test_temp_Fadhili_ITB_chunk_398,203,21.1._General_Requirements,,content,text,21.1._General_Requirements,,,,,,,False,Owner review of training materials; no ACC/Air Cooled Condenser context.,False,Owner review/discussion of training materials and schedules; administrative training governance.,low,True,No technical system/equipment requirements or engineering document targets stated.,"Owner reserves the right to review and discuss with the Contractor all training materials/ aids, course outlines, training schedules, steps, procedures, guidelines and other training matters."
